    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08003 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08003">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Scientific discipline that develops and applies strategies, instruments, and procedures to obtain information on the composition and nature of matter in space and time.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The definition was coined by the Working Party on Analytical Chemistry (WPAC) of the Federation of European Chemical Societies (FECS) and is known as the &quot;Edinburgh Definition&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;analytical science&quot; was coined in 1998 to emphasize the impact of informatics on analytical chemistry.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>analytical chemistry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08004"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08004 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08004">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Application of analytical chemistry.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:label>chemical analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08003"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08005 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08005">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Matrix effect that is independent of the measured quantity value of the measurand.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>background interference</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>translational matrix effect</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>An additive matrix effect affects the intercept, not the slope, of a linear calibration curve.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The effect is sometimes termed &quot;translational matrix effect&quot; or &quot;background interference&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>additive matrix effect</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08008"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08006 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08006">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Set of measurements of the same quantity performed under repeatability conditions of measurement.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>run</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>An analytical run may comprise measurements on one or more reference materials, blank materials, quality control materials, and analytical samples.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>analytical run</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08010"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08040"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08107"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08007 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08007">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Indication obtained from a phenomenon, body, or substance similar to the one under investigation but for which a quantity of interest is supposed not to be present or is not contributing to the indication.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In VIM 4.2, this concept is termed &quot;blank indication&quot; with &quot;background indication&quot; as an alternate term. However, since blank indication refers to the explicit use of a blank material in analytical chemistry, it should be distinguished from the concept &quot;background indication&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>background indication</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08003"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08009"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08010"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08008 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08008">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Step in a measurement procedure in which the effect of a blank indication is removed from an indication.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A blank indication may be subtracted from an indication, or, in the case of a transmittance measurement, the indication is divided by the blank indication.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The blank indication and the indication must be of the same kind of quantity.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>blank correction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08009"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08028"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08009 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08009">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Indication obtained from a sample of blank material under the measurement conditions for the measurand.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In VIM 4.2, &quot;background indication&quot; is given as an alternate term for &quot;blank indication&quot;. However, in analytical chemistry these terms refer to different concepts.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>blank indication</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08010"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08010 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08010">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Material which contains no, or as little as possible, of the analyte of interest used in measurement to establish a blank indication.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>blank</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A blank material to which a relevant component has been added is often termed &quot;spiked blank&quot; or &quot;fortified blank&quot;. Compare spike, internal standard, and measurement procedure with standard addition.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>If the term &quot;blank&quot; is used, for example, to denote blank indication or the related blank value or blank correction, this must be clarified by the context.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Terms such as &quot;solvent blank&quot;, &quot;reagent blank&quot;, or &quot;matrix blank&quot; are often used to specify the type of blank material.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Testing, processing, and measurement of blank materials are nearly always an essential part of chemical analysis and may be part of quality assurance and quality control.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The concept may be extended to more than one analyte.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;procedure (or procedural) blank&quot; is often used to denote a material that is carried through the entire measurement procedure. Terms such as &quot;field blank&quot;, &quot;calibration blank&quot;, and &quot;instrument blank&quot; refer to materials handled in specific parts of the measurement procedure.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>blank material</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08004"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08008"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08009"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08028"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08030"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08047"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08049"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08011 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08011">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Document issued by a technically competent organization providing information about a calibration.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Calibration certificates are often valid for a stated period of time, although this is not stipulated in ISO/IEC 17025.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The authority of the organization issuing the document comes from its demonstrated technical competence.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The document may include a statement describing the calibration procedure and the calibrators (calibrants) applied along with the calibration curve, calibration diagram, or other presentations of the calibration.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>calibration certificate</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08065"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08091"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08012 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08012">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Time period between calibrations over which the performance of the measuring system may be expected to meet specified requirements.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Guidelines for the determination of calibration intervals of measuring systems are given in ILAC-G24.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>ISO 14532 refers to a period &quot;between routine calibrations&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>calibration interval</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08122"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08013 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08013">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Property value of a reference material, accompanied by statements of associated uncertainty and traceability and identified as a certified property value in the reference material certificate.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In this definition, &quot;traceability&quot; covers both metrological traceability of a measurement result and examination traceability but not object traceability. Similarly, &quot;uncertainty&quot; covers measurement uncertainty and examination uncertainty.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>certified property value</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08036"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08117"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08014 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08014">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mass (amount of substance or number of entities) of a specified component divided by the mass (amount of substance or number of entities, respectively) of the system.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>purity</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Purity can be estimated as \(1 - \sum_{j=1}^{j=N} f_{j}\), where \(f_{j}\) denotes fractions of the same type (mass fraction, amount of substance fraction or number fraction) of all other components \(j = 1, \dots, N\). If the contributions are expressed as mass fractions, this estimation is sometimes termed &quot;mass balance&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Purity is usually related to a major component. The other components are termed &quot;impurities&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The numerical quantity value of purity is often expressed as per cent or per mille.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The quantity, component, and system must be specified.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>chemical purity</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08015 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08015">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Property value derived from a collection of results.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>consensus value</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A consensus property value could, through appropriate actions, become a certified property value; this is in analogy to the certification of a reference material.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A consensus property value is an example of assigned value.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A consensus property value may be obtained in a material-certification study or by agreement between appropriate organizations or experts.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The consensus property value, when it is a value of a unitary quantity, may be expressed as a mean, median, or mode, and is then termed &quot;consensus mean&quot;, &quot;consensus median&quot;, or &quot;consensus mode&quot;, respectively. The mode and the median also apply for ordinal quantities and the mode applies for nominal properties.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;consensus value&quot; is typically used to describe estimates of a measure of location, such as mean value, median or mode, and dispersion derived from results reported by participants in a proficiency testing round (see participant in an interlaboratory comparison), but may also be used to refer to values derived from results of a specified subset of such results or, for example, from a number of laboratories chosen for their expertise for the particular analysis. See performance score and standard deviation for proficiency assessment.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>consensus property value</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08013"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08091"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08093"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08095"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08118"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08130"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08016 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08016">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Quantity value attributed by agreement to a quantity for a given purpose.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>conventional value</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>conventional value of a quantity</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A conventional quantity value is generally accepted as being associated with a suitably small measurement uncertainty, which might be zero.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In quality assurance and quality control in chemistry, a conventional quantity value, which may be a consensus property value, is often termed &quot;assigned value&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Sometimes a conventional quantity value is an estimate of a true quantity value.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>conventional quantity value</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08015"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08057"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08081"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08017 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08017">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measured quantity value for a quantity of a component in a material, above which the component is declared to be present.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>critical level</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>decision level</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Another important concept in characterizing the capability of detection of measurement procedures is the limit of detection.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In some European legislations, the term &quot;decision limit&quot; (denoted as CCα) is used for the concept &apos;critical value&apos;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The critical value is chosen to give a probability α (usually 0.05) of a measured quantity value exceeding the critical value when the component is absent.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The critical value is usually considered to be a characteristic of a particular measurement procedure performed in a particular laboratory.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The detection decision is made by comparing a measured quantity value with the critical value.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The quantity measured is usually a mass fraction or a concentration but can also be, for example, a mass or an amount of substance.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>critical value</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08028"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08061"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08018 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08018">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Set of operations that are carried out on an object in order to provide qualitative or quantitative information about this object.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>&quot;Determination&quot; is a superordinate concept of measurement and examination, and so, the term &quot;determination&quot; should not be used when &quot;measurement&quot; or &quot;examination&quot; applies.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>&quot;Determination&quot; is a term in general usage and often implies a human decision.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>ISO 9000 defines determination as &quot;activity to find out one or more characteristics and their characteristic values&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>determination</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08049"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08019 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08019">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Component of the matrix that embodies an influence quantity.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:label>interferent</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08020 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08020">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement precision under intermediate precision conditions of measurement.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>intermediate precision</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>&quot;Intralaboratory precision&quot; or &quot;within-laboratory precision&quot; is sometimes used as a synonym of intermediate measurement precision.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>intermediate measurement precision</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08021 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08021">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Contribution to measurement bias that is attributed to systematic effects on measurement results made in a laboratory.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Measurement bias in analytical chemistry may be considered to include run bias, laboratory bias, and measurement procedure bias.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>laboratory bias</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08003"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08029"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08041"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08048"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08022 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08022">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Smallest or largest measured quantity value, obtained by a given measurement procedure, which fulfils a requirement of fitness for purpose.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>LOQ</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>quantification limit</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>If the LLOQ is estimated as a multiple of the standard deviation of measured values of a blank material (or one spiked with a small aliquot of the component) obtained under repeatability conditions of measurement, it is important to document the multiplication factor, which may be 5, 6, or 10, applied so that different values stated for the LLOQ can be compared.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The quantity measured is usually a mass fraction or a concentration but can also be for example, a mass or an amount of substance.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The requirement can, for example, be a standard deviation under repeatability conditions of measurement or a measurement uncertainty.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The smallest and largest measured quantity values correspond to the lower limit of quantification (LLOQ) and the upper limit of quantification (ULOQ), respectively. The interval between the LLOQ and ULOQ is the working interval.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>limit of quantification</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08010"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08028"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08040"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08051"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08073"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08122"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08022"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>LOQ</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08023 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08023">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ability of a measuring system to provide measured quantity values that are directly proportional to the quantity value of the measurand.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Linearity of a measuring system should not be confused with linearity of calibration.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The linearity of a measuring system is assessed during procedure validation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The set of measured quantity values for which linearity of a measuring system applies is usually termed &quot;linear interval&quot; or &quot;linear range&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>linearity of a measuring system</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08024"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08097"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08024 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08024">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Closeness of agreement between indications obtained using calibrators in the first step of a calibration and indications predicted by the calibration function for the calibrators’ reference quantity values.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>calibration linearity</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Calibration linearity should not be confused with linearity of a measuring system.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Linearity of calibration is assessed during procedure validation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Linearity of calibration may be expressed by measures of agreement (for example, correlation coefficient) or deviation (for example, standard error of regression), obtained by regression of calibration data or assessed from a residual plot.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The concept applies to calibration functions of any mathematical form. The term &quot;linearity&quot; is historical and refers to a time when calibration graphs were constructed on paper and were invariably considered to be linear.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>linearity of calibration</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08023"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08097"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08025 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08025">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Uniform structure or composition of a material with respect to one or more specified properties.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A material is said to be homogeneous with respect to a specified quantity if the quantity values measured using aliquots of a specified size do not fall outside a specified interval. See minimum sample size.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Detailed guidance for the assessment of homogeneity of reference materials is given in ISO Guide 35.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In the homogeneity study of a candidate reference material, it is distinguished whether the analytical samples are taken from different supply units or from a single supply unit (&quot;termed between-bottle homogeneity&quot; or &quot;within-bottle homogeneity&quot;, respectively).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Inhomogeneity is a source of measurement uncertainty.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>material homogeneity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08034"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08060"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08026 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08026">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mass (volume, amount of substance) of a specified component isolated from a system divided by the mass (volume, amount of substance) of the system prior to isolation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>recovery</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The measurement unit of material recovery is the measurement unit of the quantity related to the specified component divided by the unit of the quantity describing the system. When these units are the same, material recovery may be expressed as a percentage, and the quantity specified.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;recovery&quot; is also used to describe a recovered quantity value ratio. Therefore, the term &quot;recovery&quot; should not be used without qualification unless the meaning is clear from the context.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>material recovery</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08039"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08027 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08027">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Constancy of a property of a material over time.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>stability</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A material is said to be stable with respect to a specified property if its measured or examined property value does not fall outside a specified interval during storage under specified conditions over a specified period of time.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A reference material is assessed for the stability of an embodied property under conditions of transport (&quot;short-term stability&quot;) and storage (&quot;long-term stability&quot;).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;stability&quot; is also used for stability of a measuring instrument or process (see control limit).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The variation of the property value over time adds a contribution to the uncertainty budget or the examination uncertainty, as applicable. Regarding the assessment of stability of the reference material, detailed guidance is given in ISO Guide 35.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>material stability</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08069"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08028 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08028">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Detailed description of a measurement according to one or more measurement principles and to a given measurement method, based on a measurement model and including any calculation to obtain a measurement result.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A measurement procedure can include a statement concerning a target measurement uncertainty.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A measurement procedure is usually documented in sufficient detail to enable an operator to perform a measurement.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>An authorized measurement procedure is sometimes termed &quot;standard operating procedure&quot; (SOP) or &quot;recommended operating procedure&quot; (ROP).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In ISO/IEC 17025, the term &quot;method&quot; is used for measurement procedure. &quot;Examination procedure&quot; is defined for medical laboratories.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Measurement procedures in chemistry can be structured according to ISO 78-2 or Annex A of the Eurachem Guide &quot;The Fitness for Purpose of Analytical Methods&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The historical term &quot;assay&quot; is now largely obsolete as a synonym for metrological terms such as measurement procedure but still used in composite terms, for example, immunoassay and bioassay.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>measurement procedure</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08131"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08133"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08029 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08029">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Contribution to measurement bias that is attributed to systematic effects on measurement results made according to a measurement procedure.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>measurement method bias</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Contributions to measurement procedure bias are calculated during procedure validation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Measurement bias in analytical chemistry may be considered to include run bias, laboratory bias, and measurement procedure bias.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Measurement procedure bias covers instrumental bias.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>measurement procedure bias</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08003"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08021"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08028"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08041"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08048"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08097"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08030 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08030">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement procedure in which indications are obtained for an analytical sample as well as for analytical samples with addition(s) of a measurement standard.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>standard addition</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A measurement procedure with standard addition provides an unbiased measurement result if there is a multiplicative matrix effect.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>measurement procedure with standard addition</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08028"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08035"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08031 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08031">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement precision under reproducibility conditions of measurement.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>reproducibility</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Relevant statistical terms are given in ISO 5725-1 and ISO 5725-2.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;interlaboratory precision&quot; or &quot;between-laboratory precision&quot; is sometimes used as a synonym of measurement reproducibility.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>measurement reproducibility</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08032 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08032">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Property of a set of measurement results for a specified measurand, such that the absolute value of the difference of any pair of measured quantity values from two different measurement results is smaller than some chosen multiple of the standard measurement uncertainty of that difference.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>metrological compatibility</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>As required by the Mutual Recognition Arrangement (MRA) of the International Committee for Weights and Measures (CIPM), through which national metrology institutes demonstrate the international equivalence of their measurement standards, the concept &quot;degree of equivalence&quot; is applied in special interlaboratory comparisons termed &quot;key comparisons&quot;. The degree of equivalence of each national measurement standard is expressed quantitatively by two terms: its deviation from the reference quantity value of the key comparison and the measurement uncertainty of this deviation (at a level of confidence of approximately 95 %). See also: metrological equivalence of measurement results.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Correlation between the measurements influences metrological compatibility of measurement results. If the measurements are completely uncorrelated, the standard measurement uncertainty of their difference is equal to the root square sum of their standard measurement uncertainties, while it is lower for positive covariance or higher for negative covariance.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Metrological compatibility of measurement results replaces the traditional concept of &quot;staying within the error&quot;, as it represents the criterion for deciding whether two measurement results refer to the same measurand or not. If in a set of measurements of a measurand, thought to be constant, a measurement result is not compatible with the others, either the measurement was not correct (for example, its measurement uncertainty, was assessed as being too small), or the measured quantity changed between measurements.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>metrological compatibility of measurement results</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08033"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08081"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08033 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08033">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Property of two or more measurement results for a given measurand which have metrological compatibility of measurement results, so that they are each acceptable for the same specified intended use.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>equivalence of measurement results</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Measurement results are either metrologically equivalent or they are not.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>metrological equivalence of measurement results</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08032"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08034 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08034">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Lower limit of sample size stipulated in documentation taken for chemical analysis.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>minimum sample intake</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Examples of documentation include a measurement procedure, product information sheets (see reference material), and reference material certificates.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Values associated with performance characteristics of a measurement procedure and the property values stated in documentation are rendered invalid if the minimum sample size is not taken.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>minimum sample size</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08004"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08028"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08094"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08117"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08035 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08035">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Matrix effect that is proportional to the measured quantity value of the measurand.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A multiplicative matrix effect affects the slope, not the intercept, of a linear calibration curve.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A multiplicative matrix effect can be compensated for by following a measurement procedure with standard addition.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A multiplicative matrix effect may originate from non-analyte components of the measurement standard if these contribute to the signal attributed to the analyte.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The effect is sometimes termed &quot;rotational matrix effect&quot; or &quot;proportional interference&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>multiplicative matrix effect</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08030"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08036 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08036">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Property value that is provided for information only but is not certified by a reference material producer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A non-certified property value cannot be used as reference in a metrological traceability chain.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A non-certified property value may be included in the reference material certificate or supplied in other form.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In ISO Guide 30, &quot;indicative value&quot;, &quot;information value&quot;, and &quot;informative value&quot; are admitted terms.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>non-certified property value</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08117"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08120"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08037 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08037">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ability to trace the history, application, or location of an object.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>traceability (of an object)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In, the term defined is &quot;traceability&quot;. However, because of the potential confusion with metrological traceability, it is recommended to use the full term if there is ambiguity.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When considering a product or a service, traceability can relate to: the origin of materials and parts, the processing history, or the distribution and location of the product or service after delivery.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>object traceability</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08038 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08038">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Determination of property values obtained in the course of the production of a reference material.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>value assignment</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>property value assignment</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08018"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08057"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08039 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08039">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measured quantity value relating a component to a system divided by a reference value.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>analytical recovery</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>recovery</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Recovered quantity value ratio may be estimated from the measured change of the quantity value of the component of interest upon the addition of a known amount of substance or mass of the component. The added material containing the component is often termed &quot;spike&quot;. See also: blank material Note 5, measurement procedure with standard addition, and spike.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Recovery quantity value ratio is used in procedure validation to evaluate and correct for the measurement procedure bias.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The definition can be symbolized by \(R_{\rm{B}} = Q_{\rm{B,measured}}/Q_{\rm{B,reference}}\), where \(R\) denotes the recovered ratio of the quantity values \(Q\), and \(\ce{B}\) identifies the component.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The quantities involved are rational unitary quantities and of the same kind of quantity, usually either a concentration or content.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The respective measurement procedure must be specified.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;recovery&quot; is also used to describe material recovery. Therefore, the term &quot;recovery&quot; should not be used without qualification unless the meaning is clear from the context.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>recovered quantity value ratio</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08010"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08026"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08028"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08029"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08030"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08047"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08050"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08097"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08040 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08040">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Condition of measurement, out of a set of conditions that includes the same measurement procedure, same operators, same measuring system, same operating conditions, and same location, and replicate measurements on the same or similar objects over a short period of time.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>repeatability condition</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A condition of measurement is a repeatability condition only with respect to a specified set of repeatability conditions.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A set of measurements under repeatability conditions is often termed &quot;analytical run&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In ISO 3534-2, &quot;same operator&quot; (singular) is stipulated as a repeatability condition. The VIM request of &quot;same operators&quot; (plural) should be understood such that if two or more operators contribute to one measurement, they should be involved in the same way in repeated measurements.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In analytical chemistry, the phrase &quot;under repeatability conditions&quot; refers to the above specified set of conditions.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>repeatability condition of measurement</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08003"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08006"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08028"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08041 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08041">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Contribution to measurement bias that is attributed to systematic effects on measurement results made in a single analytical run.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Measurement bias in analytical chemistry may be considered to include run bias, laboratory bias, and measurement procedure bias.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>run bias</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08003"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08006"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08021"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08029"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08048"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08042 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08042">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Collective noun for all procedures used for conditioning a sample to a defined state which allows subsequent chemical analysis or long-term storage (see material stability).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>sample preparation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Sample pre-treatment includes, for example, mixing, splitting, drying, crushing, stabilization, dissolving, extracting, diluting, precipitating, and derivatizing.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sample pre-treatment</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08004"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08027"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08043 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08043">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Act of taking or constituting a sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Sampling often provides a contribution to the measurement uncertainty budget or the examination uncertainty, as applicable.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sampling</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08044 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08044">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Individual portion of material collected by a single operation of a sampling device.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>increment</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>sampling increment</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08045 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08045">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Portion of the material, at a particular time, that the sample is intended to represent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The sampling target may be defined by Regulations (for example, lot size).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The sampling target should be defined prior to designing the sampling plan.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sampling target</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08046 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08046">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Time interval during which a reference material producer warrants the material stability of the reference material.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The shelf life is equivalent to the &quot;period of validity&quot; of the reference material certificate and is ended by the &quot;expiry date&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>shelf life</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08027"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08117"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08120"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08047 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08047">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Material with known quantity values added to an analytical sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>&quot;Spike&quot; used as a verb is the addition of a spike to a sample.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A spike may be used to estimate the recovered quantity value ratio or compensate for systematic measurement error.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The known quantity value is often a fraction or concentration.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The material can be a reference material or a certified reference material.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>spike</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08039"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08048 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08048">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Recognized effect of an influence quantity on a measured quantity value.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A systematic effect can be compensated for by a correction.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>systematic effect</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08049 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08049">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Determination of one or more characteristics of an object of conformity assessment, according to a specified procedure.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>test</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In analytical chemistry, testing may be a measurement to obtain a quantity value or an examination, such as identifying a chemical substance (see qualitative analysis).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>testing</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08003"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08018"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08067"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08080"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08050 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08050">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Quantity with a magnitude expressed as a reference quantity multiplied by a number.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In the VIM, the concept is denoted as &quot;quantity expressed by a measurement unit&quot; in the concept diagram Figure A.1 and is referred to as &quot;quantities other than ordinal quantities&quot;, for example, in entry 1.21, and as &quot;non-ordinal quantity&quot; in Note 1 to entry 2.41.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>unitary quantity</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08051 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08051">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Set of quantity values over which a measuring instrument or measuring system provides measurement results with acceptable measurement uncertainty under defined conditions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>working range</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In some fields, the term is &quot;measurement range&quot;. In entry 4.7 of VIM, a similar concept termed &quot;measuring interval&quot; is defined.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The lower limit of a working interval should not be confused with the detection limit.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The working interval is bounded by the lower and upper limit of quantification.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>working interval</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08022"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08023"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08052 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08052">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Interval of permissible measured quantity values.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>acceptance zone</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>An acceptance interval is termed an &quot;acceptance zone&quot; in the Eurachem guide on compliance assessment.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Unless otherwise stated in the specification, the acceptance limits belong to the acceptance interval.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>acceptance interval</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08053"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08072"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08121"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08053 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08053">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Specified upper or lower bound of permissible measured quantity values.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Permissible measured quantity values are associated with items that conform to specified requirements with at least a predetermined conformance probability and lie within the acceptance interval.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>acceptance limit</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08052"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08066"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08067"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08129"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08054 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08054">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Third-party attestation related to a laboratory conveying formal demonstration of its competence to carry out specific conformity assessment tasks.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Examples of conformity assessment tasks for which accreditation can be granted are measurement, testing, inspection, provision of proficiency testing schemes, and production of reference materials.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The criteria for determining a laboratory&apos;s competence are based on relevant international standards, for example, ISO/IEC 17025, ISO 15189, or ISO 15195, and include adequate quality assurance and quality control procedures, such as qualifications, training and experience of staff, appropriate equipment that is properly calibrated and maintained, use of validated measurement procedures, participation in interlaboratory comparisons, and appropriate sampling practices.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>accreditation of a laboratory</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08003"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08028"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08049"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08065"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08067"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08080"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08081"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08104"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08055 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08055">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Selectivity of a measuring system in analytical chemistry.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>selectivity</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Selectivity should be qualified by &quot;analytical&quot; if there is potential confusion with selectivity in catalysis or in organic reaction mechanisms.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>analytical selectivity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08003"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08056 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08056">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Person, assigned by an accreditation body, with relevant professional expertise and experience who can determine the competence of a laboratory.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Depending on the role and responsibilities of an assessor, terms such as &quot;lead assessor&quot; and &quot;technical assessor&quot; are often used.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The assessor may work alone or as part of a team and be engaged in a voluntary or paid capacity.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The work of an assessor is often referred to as &quot;assessment&quot; and sometimes as &quot;peer review&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>assessor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08065"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08057 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08057">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Value attributed to a particular property of an item and that serves as an agreed reference for comparison.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Options for establishing an assigned value for some types of proficiency testing schemes are detailed in ISO/IEC 17043 and ISO 13528.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The value may be a reference quantity value or a value of a nominal property.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>assigned value</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08038"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08104"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08058 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08058">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Systematic, independent, and documented process for obtaining objective evidence and evaluating it objectively to determine the extent to which the audit criteria are fulfilled.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>An audit can be an internal audit (first party) or an external audit (second party or third party), and it can be a combined audit or a joint audit.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>External audits include those generally termed second and third-party audits. Second-party audits are conducted by parties having an interest in the organization, such as customers, or by other persons on their behalf. Third-party audits are conducted by external, independent auditing organizations such as those providing certification/registration of conformity or governmental agencies.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Internal audits, sometimes termed first-party audits, are conducted by, or on behalf of, the organization itself for management review and other internal purposes and can form the basis for an organization&apos;s declaration of conformity. Independence can be demonstrated by the freedom from responsibility for the activity being audited.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The fundamental elements of an audit include the determination of the conformity of an object according to a procedure carried out by personnel not being responsible for the object audited.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>audit</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08018"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08063"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08091"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08059 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08059">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reference material subjected to a process of reference material certification.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>candidate CRM</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>candidate certified reference material</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08118"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08060 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08060">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Material subjected to the procedures necessary to show its fitness for intended use (see fitness for purpose).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>candidate RM</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A candidate reference material for a given property may already be a reference material for other properties.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>candidate reference material</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08073"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08061 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08061">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ability of an object to realize an output that will fulfil the requirements for that output.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Process capability terms in the field of statistics are defined in ISO 3534-2.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>capability</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08122"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08062 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08062">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Diagram indicating the causes of a specific event or condition.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Ishikawa diagram</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>fishbone diagram</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>herringbone diagram</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In analytical chemistry, Ishikawa diagrams are used to indicate sources of measurement uncertainty. See Fig. 1. Figure 1: Ishikawa diagram for the titration of a hydrochloric acid solution with sodium hydroxide solution that has been standardized using potassium hydrogen phthalate (KHP). When used for identification of sources of measurement uncertainty, the diagram illustrates how the quantity value of the measurand (\(c_{\ce{HCl}}\)) depends on input quantities in a measurement model (symbols in bold), which in turn depend on other quantities. Symbols have their usual meanings. \(f_{\rm{precision}}\) is a is a factor in the measurement function to account for measurement precision with value 1 and standard measurement uncertainty.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>cause-and-effect diagram</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08003"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08063 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08063">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Third-party attestation related to products, processes, systems, or persons.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Certification is applicable to all objects of conformity assessment except for conformity assessment bodies themselves, to which accreditation is applicable.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Certification of a management system is sometimes also termed registration.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>certification</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08067"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08064 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08064">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Property of a reference material, demonstrated by the closeness of agreement between the relation among the measurement results for a stated quantity in this material, obtained according to two given measurement procedures, and the relation obtained among the measurement results for other specified materials.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Cases in which a reference material producer is required to conduct assessment of commutability are outlined in ISO 15194.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Guidance for assessment of commutability of reference material in laboratory medicine is provided by IFCC.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The material stability of commutable reference materials should be monitored regularly.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The measurement procedures referred to in the definition are the one preceding and the one following the reference material (calibrator) in question in a calibration hierarchy (see ISO 17511).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The reference material in question is usually a calibrator and the other specified materials are usually routine samples.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>commutability of a reference material</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08028"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08065 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08065">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ability to apply knowledge and skills to achieve intended results.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Demonstrated competence is sometimes referred to as &quot;qualification&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>competence</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08066 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08066">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Probability that an item fulfils a specified requirement.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:label>conformance probability</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08122"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08067 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08067">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Activity to determine whether specified requirements relating to a product, process, system, person, or body are fulfilled.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>&quot;Body&quot; refers to an organization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The expression &quot;object of conformity assessment&quot; or &quot;object&quot; is used to encompass any particular material, product, installation, process, system, person, or body to which conformity assessment is applied.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The subject field of conformity assessment includes activities, such as testing, calibration, carrying out an audit, inspection, and certification, as well as the accreditation of conformity assessment bodies.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>conformity assessment</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08052"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08058"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08063"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08072"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08076"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08080"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08091"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08121"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08129"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08135"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08068 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08068">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chart with control limits on which some statistical measure related to a series of samples is plotted in a particular order to steer the process with respect to that measure.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Control charts are commonly used in the regular monitoring of the performance of a measurement procedure, as part of the laboratory&apos;s internal quality control.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Examples of control charts are Shewhart means chart, Shewhart range chart, and cumulative sum control chart.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The control chart operates most effectively when the measure is a process variable which is correlated with an ultimate product or service characteristic.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The particular order is usually based on time or sample number order. Repeated sampling may be avoided where in-situ measurements are applicable.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>control chart</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08028"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08069"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08071"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08082"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08127"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08128"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08069 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08069">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Value defining an intended level of stability for a process.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A control limit may be a statistical value or be related to a predetermined target value, calculated, for example, from a target measurement uncertainty.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A typical control chart will consist of a centre line that reflects the level around which the plotted value can be expected to vary. In addition, this control chart will have two lines, called control limits, placed one on each side of the centre line, which define a band within which the value can be expected to lie randomly for a process in a state of statistical control.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A typical response to a value outside a warning limit is to monitor the process and, if this condition is repeated, to stop and investigate.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A value outside an action limit is normally taken as evidence that the process is no longer in statistical control. A typical response to such a value is to stop and investigate. (See process in a state of statistical control).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Control limits on a Shewhart control chart are placed at a distance of ±2σ and ±3σ where σ is the known or estimated standard deviation of the population. This gives approximate probabilities of 0.05 and 0.003, respectively, of finding values outside the control limits. These limits are known as the upper and lower warning limit (UWL and LWL) and upper and lower action limit (UAL and LAL), respectively. See Shewart means chart - Fig. 1.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>control limit</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08068"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08099"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08125"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08133"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08070 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08070">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Person(s) with responsibility for organizing and managing all of the activities involved in the operation of an interlaboratory comparison.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>coordinator</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>coordinator of an interlaboratory comparison</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08081"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08071 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08071">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Control chart where the cumulative sum of deviations of successive measured quantity values from a reference quantity value is plotted to detect shifts in the level of the measurand.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CUSUM chart</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>CUSUM charts are particularly sensitive to reveal a small measurement bias.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>More suited to spreadsheet analysis, the following quantities are calculated for N measured quantity values \(x_{i} (i=1; …, N)\) \(S_{\rm{hi}}(i) = {\rm{max}}(0,S_{\rm{hi}}(i - 1) + x_{i} - \mu - k)\); \(S_{\rm{hi}}(0) = 0\) \(S_{\rm{lo}}(i) = \rm{max}(0,S_{\rm{lo}}(i - 1) - {x}_{i} + \mu - k)\); \(S_{\rm{lo}}(0) = 0\). \(\mu\) is a suitably chosen target value and \(k\) is a reference quantity value such that only shifts away from the target value greater than \(k\) will add to the cumulative sum. \(k\) is usually taken as half the shift in the process mean that is required to be detected divided by the standard deviation (\(\sigma\)) obtained under repeatability conditions of measurement. An out-of-control criterion is when \(S_{\rm{hi}}(i)\) or \(S_{\rm{lo}}(i)\) becomes greater than a limiting value \(h\) (usually \(h = 4\sigma\)). (Note that the reference quantity value \(k\) is not a coverage factor).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The best discrimination of changes in the level is achieved when the reference quantity value is equal to the overall mean value.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The chart can be used for control, diagnostic, or predictive purposes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The ordinate of each plotted point represents the algebraic sum of the previous ordinate and the most recent deviation from the reference, target, or control value.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When used for process control, it can be interpreted graphically using a mask (for example, V-mask) superimposed on the graph. An out-of-control criterion is when the path of the cumulative sum intersects or touches the boundary of the mask.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>cumulative sum control chart</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08040"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08068"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08092"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08072 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08072">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Documented rule that describes how measurement uncertainty will be accounted for with regard to accepting or rejecting an item, given a specified requirement and a measured quantity value.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>decision rule</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Decision rules in conformity assessment give a prescription for the acceptance or rejection of an item based on the measured quantity value and the associated measurement uncertainty and limit(s) expressed or implied by specified requirements, taking into account the acceptable level of the probability of making a wrong decision. On the basis of the decision rules, an &quot;acceptance interval&quot; and &quot;rejection interval(s)&quot; are determined, such that if the measured quantity value lies in the acceptance interval, the item is declared as conforming and if in the rejection interval, it is declared as non-conforming.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>decision rule in conformity assessment</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08052"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08067"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08076"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08121"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08122"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08129"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08073 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08073">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ability of a product, process, or service to serve a defined purpose under specific conditions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>fitness for intended use</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>fitness for purpose</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08074 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08074">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Quality system concerned with the organizational process, and conditions under which non-clinical health and environmental safety studies are planned, performed, monitored, recorded, archived, and reported.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>GLP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A GLP approval should not be confused with accreditation of a laboratory.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>GLP is generally associated with the guidelines laid down by the Organization for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD). The use of the term GLP outside these guidelines is not recommended.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>good laboratory practice</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08054"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08074"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GLP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08075 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08075">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Combination of manufacturing and quality procedures aimed at ensuring that products are consistently manufactured to their specified requirements and to avoid contamination of the product by internal or external sources.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>GMP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>good manufacturing practice</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08129"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08075"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GMP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08076 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08076">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Interval between a tolerance limit and a corresponding acceptance limit. Figure 1: Guarded acceptance. Two-sided acceptance interval created by reducing the tolerance interval on either side by a guard band, thus reducing the probability of falsely accepting a non-conforming item.Figure 2: Guarded rejection. Two-sided acceptance interval created by increasing the tolerance interval on either side by a guard band, thus reducing the probability of falsely rejecting a conforming item.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A guard band is used to restrict or to extend an acceptance interval to reduce the probability of an unwanted decision on conformance due to the measurement uncertainty of the actual measurement. The width of a guard band is scaled with the measurement uncertainty. (See: decision rule in conformity assessment).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>If the acceptance limits lie outside the tolerance limits, the decision for conformity of the item is known as guarded rejection. The probability of falsely rejecting a conforming item is reduced. See Fig. 2.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>If the acceptance limits lie within the tolerance limits, the decision for conformity of the item is known as guarded acceptance. The probability of falsely accepting a non-conforming item is reduced. This is most often found in chemistry. See Fig. 1.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>guard band</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08052"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08053"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08067"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08072"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08135"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08136"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08077 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08077">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Empirical relationship providing a standard deviation under reproducibility conditions of measurement (\(s_{\rm{R,H}}\)) to be expected for typical measurements of the mass fraction (\(w\)) of a component in a material: \(s_{\rm{R,H}} = 0.02 w^{0.8495}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Horwitz curve</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Horwitz horn curve</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Horwitz trumpet curve</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The shape of the curve is called a trumpet when the relative standard deviation is plotted against the logarithm of the mass fraction as shown in Fig. 1. The reference justifies the mirror curve for negative ordinate values by &quot;… the lines are best regarded as confidence boundaries.&quot;</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Horwitz equation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08078 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08078">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Standard deviation of a mass fraction of a component under reproducibility conditions of measurement divided by the corresponding standard deviation calculated from the Horwitz equation. Figure 1: Horwitz curve (trumpet, horn). Expected relative standard deviation under reproducibility conditions of measurement, \(\pm s_{\rm{R,H}}/w\) plotted against logarithm of mass fraction, \(w\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>HORRAT</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Horrat</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In, the &quot;Horwitz ratio&quot; is defined as the ratio of the standard deviation under reproducibility conditions of measurement and standard deviation under repeatability conditions of measurement. This use is discouraged because of the possibility of confusion with the older usage.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The Horwitz ratio is used as a test of the fitness for purpose of methods of chemical analysis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The experimental measurement precision is better than to be expected from the Horwitz equation if the Horwitz ratio is less than 1 and poorer if greater than 1. In practice, ratios between 0.5 and 2.0 are acceptable.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Horwitz ratio</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08004"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08040"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08073"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08077"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08078"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>HORRAT</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08079 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08079">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reference material produced and used by an analytical laboratory.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>laboratory reference material</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The preparation, characterization, and use of in-house reference materials for quality control purposes are described in ISO Guide 80.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Various terms are used to distinguish among reference materials intended for different uses and with assigned values established applying different procedures.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>in-house reference material</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08016"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08038"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08057"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08080 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08080">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Examination of a product, process, service, or installation or their design and determination of conformity with specific requirements or on the basis of professional judgment, with general requirements.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>If the result of an inspection shows conformity, it can be used for purposes of verification.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Inspection of processes can include personnel, facilities, technology, or methodology.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Inspection procedures or schemes can restrict inspection to examination only.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The result of an inspection can show conformity or non-conformity or a degree of conformity, where conformity is defined as fulfilment of a requirement.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>inspection</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08018"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08122"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08139"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08081 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08081">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Organization, performance, and evaluation of measurements or tests on the same or similar items by two or more laboratories in accordance with predetermined conditions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ILC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Accredited laboratories and laboratories seeking accreditation are expected to participate in interlaboratory comparisons, for example, proficiency testing schemes, where available and appropriate.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Determination of performance characteristics of a measurement procedure as part of procedure validation or characterization of a candidate reference material can be done by means of an interlaboratory comparison. (See method performance study and material characterization study.)</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In some circumstances, one of the laboratories involved in an interlaboratory comparison may be the laboratory that provides the assigned value for the item. The assignment enables the determination of the metrological equivalence of measurement results of the participants but does not, by itself, establish metrological traceability.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Interlaboratory comparison is a generic term, and the purpose and detailed objectives of an interlaboratory comparison must be specified. Some types of interlaboratory comparisons have special names, for example, proficiency testing scheme and key comparison.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Interlaboratory comparisons are organized at all metrological levels and have the following steps in common. a) A coordinator of an interlaboratory comparison plans the interlaboratory comparison; b) One or more items are sourced by the coordinator, assessed as appropriate, and distributed to the participants in an interlaboratory comparison with instructions; c) The participants conduct measurements, tests, examinations, or other work on the item(s) and report the results back to the coordinator; d) The coordinator evaluates the results and provides feedback to the participants; and e) The coordinator and/or the participants act on the results.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Key comparisons and supplementary comparisons are organized by the International Bureau of Weights and Measures (BIPM) in support of the International Committee for Weights and Measures (CIPM) Mutual Recognition Arrangement (MRA) in which National Metrology Institutes demonstrate the international equivalence of their measurement standards and the calibration certificates and measurement certificates they issue.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The minimum number of laboratories participating in an interlaboratory comparison will depend on the metrological properties of the measurement procedures used, for example, measurement precision and analytical selectivity. See, performance characteristics of a measurement procedure.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The use of terms such as &quot;ring test&quot;, &quot;round robin&quot;, &quot;intercalibration&quot;, &quot;intercomparison&quot;, &quot;collaborative study&quot;, etc. for interlaboratory comparisons is not recommended in formal documents.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>interlaboratory comparison</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08011"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08018"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08028"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08033"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08049"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08055"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08057"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08060"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08070"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08085"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08090"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08093"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08094"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08097"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08102"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08104"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08131"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08081"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ILC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08082 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08082">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Quality control within a specified laboratory.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>IQC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>internal quality control</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08082"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>IQC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08083 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08083">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Organization, performance, and evaluation of measurements or tests on the same or similar items within the same laboratory in accordance with predetermined conditions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:label>intralaboratory comparison</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08049"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08084 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08084">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Information system which can provide services to one or more measuring systems.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>laboratory information system</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In laboratory medicine, the term &quot;Laboratory Information System&quot; is common.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>LIMS functionality can include sample registration and tracking (through barcodes, chips, etc.), electronic laboratory notebooks, report generation, quality control, and financial control.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Services include the control of automated analyses and collecting, processing, storing, and managing of data.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>laboratory information management system</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08085 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08085">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Intralaboratory comparison or interlaboratory comparison with the aim of assigning a reference quantity value to a quantity with stated measurement uncertainty or value to a reference nominal property value with stated examination uncertainty.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A material characterization study often utilizes selected (routine or reference) laboratories to measure quantity values of a candidate reference material by measurement procedure(s) judged most likely to provide the smallest associated measurement uncertainty.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Although proficiency testing is not primarily intended as a material characterization study, reference materials are sometimes certified on the basis of results from a proficiency testing scheme after appropriate quality assurance procedures.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>material characterization study</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08028"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08060"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08081"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08083"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08101"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08104"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08086 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08086">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reference material that is characteristic of a real sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A chemical substance dissolved in a pure solvent is not a matrix reference material.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Matrix reference materials are intended to be used in conjunction with the chemical analysis of real samples of the same or a similar matrix.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Matrix reference materials may also be prepared by a spike of the component(s) of interest into an existing material.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Matrix reference materials may be obtained directly from biological, environmental, or industrial sources.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>matrix reference material</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08004"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08047"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08122"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08087 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08087">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ability to measure a quantity in a specified interval of quantity values, embodied in a specified material, as demonstrated by measurement uncertainty.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A comparison of the measurement uncertainty in the measurement result obtained by one laboratory to that of the measurement result obtained by another laboratory for the same quantity in the same material compares their respective measurement capabilities.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>measurement capability</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08088 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08088">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Quality control material with or without reference quantity value used in quality control to assess measurement precision.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>precision control material</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>measurement precision control material</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08107"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08089 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08089">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Quality control material with reference quantity value used in quality control to estimate a systematic measurement error.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:label>measurement trueness control material</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08107"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08090 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08090">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Intra- or interlaboratory study of one or more performance characteristics of a measurement procedure.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Details regarding organizing and conducting the study, for example, type of samples and number of measurements, are specified in a validation plan or in a protocol for interlaboratory comparison.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The general term &quot;collaborative study&quot; should not be used for an interlaboratory method performance study.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The study is normally conducted in the frame of procedure validation or procedure verification.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>method performance study</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08094"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08097"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08105"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08138"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08091 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08091">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Person or group of people that has its own functions with responsibilities, authorities, and relationships to achieve its objectives.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In formal documents, the term &quot;body&quot; is often used to denote an organization, for example, &quot;accreditation body&quot;, &quot;certification body&quot;, &quot;inspection body&quot;, &quot;legislative body&quot;, and &quot;regulatory body&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The concept of &quot;organization&quot; includes, but is not limited to, sole-trader, company, corporation, firm, enterprise, authority, partnership, association, charity, or institution, or part or combination thereof, whether incorporated or not, public or private.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>organization</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08092 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08092">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Set of decision rules for identifying the presence of special causes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Decision rules may include those relating to measured quantity values inside and outside of control limits in a control chart. They cover runs, trends, cycles, periodicity, concentration of points near the centre line or control limits, unusual spread of points within control limits (large or small dispersion), and relationships among values within subgroups.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Examples of decision rules and guidelines for the daily interpretation of a control chart are provided by Nordtest.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>out-of-control criteria</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08068"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08069"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08093 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08093">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Laboratory, organization, or person that receives test items and submits results for review by the coordinator of an interlaboratory comparison.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>participant</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>participant in an interlaboratory comparison</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08070"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08091"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08094 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08094">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Aspect of a measurement procedure that may be quantitatively investigated and assessed as part of procedure validation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>performance characteristic</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>performance characteristic of a measurement method</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Measurement uncertainty is not a performance characteristic of a particular measurement procedure but a property of the measurement result obtained using that measurement procedure.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Performance characteristics that may be considered in procedure validation are analytical selectivity, sensitivity of a measuring system, working interval, ruggedness of a measurement procedure, linearity of a measuring system, linearity of calibration, critical value, limit of detection, limit of quantification, measurement trueness, and measurement precision.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>performance characteristic of a measurement procedure</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08017"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08022"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08023"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08024"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08028"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08051"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08055"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08097"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08123"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08124"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08095 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08095">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Statistic, derived from a result reported by a participant in a proficiency testing scheme, used in interpretation of participant performance and to allow comparison with defined objectives.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>performance statistic</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Names and defining equations of frequently used performance scores are listed in Table 1. Their definitions and interpretations are given in ISO/IEC 17043 and ISO 13528.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The purpose of a performance score is to express the deviation of the reported value from the assigned value in a proficiency testing scheme in a manner that allows comparison with performance criteria.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>performance score</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08101"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08104"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08096 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08096">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Quantity value, less than or equal to what the absolute difference between two measured quantity values obtained under specified conditions of measurement is expected to be with a given probability.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>ISO 3534-2 defines three kinds of precision limit: repeatability limit (\(r\)), intermediate precision limit, and reproducibility limit (\(R\)) obtained under repeatability conditions of measurement, intermediate precision conditions of measurement, and reproducibility conditions of measurement, respectively. The recommended probability is \(\pu{95\%}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Precision limit at approximately \(\pu{95\%}\) is estimated by multiplying the standard deviation obtained under the specified conditions of measurement by \(2\sqrt{2}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>precision limit</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08040"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08097 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08097">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process of defining an analytical requirement and confirming that the procedure under consideration has capabilities consistent with that requirement.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In ISO/IEC 17025, the term &quot;method&quot; is used as a synonym for measurement procedure.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Inherent in procedure validation is the need to evaluate performance characteristics of a measurement procedure.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Procedure validation is either done in a single laboratory or in the framework of an interlaboratory comparison.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The corresponding term in ISO 15189 is &quot;validation of examination procedures&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>procedure validation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08028"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08061"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08081"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08094"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08122"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08098 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08098">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Confirmation, through the provision of objective evidence, that the procedure fulfils specified requirements.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A laboratory that implements a procedure described in, for example, an international standard or in a manufacturer&quot;s documentation needs to verify that the procedure fulfils the specified requirements.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In ISO/IEC 17025, the term &quot;method&quot; is used as a synonym for measurement procedure.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The corresponding term in ISO 15189 is &quot;verification of examination procedures&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>procedure verification</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08028"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08122"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08099 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08099">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process subject only to random causes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>process in statistical control</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>stable process</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A control chart can be used to monitor a process and to determine whether the process is, or is not, in a state of statistical control. See out-of-control criteria and control limit.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The random variation is considered to follow a Gaussian distribution and is characterized by its variance (standard deviation)</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>This state does not imply that the random variation is large or small, within or outside of specification, but rather that the variation is predictable using statistical techniques.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>process in a state of statistical control</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08068"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08069"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08092"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08100 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08100">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sample, product, artefact, reference material, piece of equipment, measurement standard, data set, or other information used for proficiency testing.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PT item</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>proficiency test item</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08101"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08101 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08101">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Evaluation of the performance of a participant in an interlaboratory comparison against pre-established criteria.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PT</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Participation in proficiency testing schemes is not a substitute for internal quality control measures or vice versa.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Proficiency testing is achieved by the distribution of proficiency test items for unsupervised measurement or examination by the participants.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Some proficiency testing providers in the laboratory medicine area use the term &quot;external quality assessment (EQA)&quot; for their proficiency testing schemes or for their broader programmes, or both.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The evaluation of quantitative results of a participant in a proficiency testing scheme may be given as a \(z\) score, \(z&apos;\) score, \(\zeta\) score, or \(E_{\rm{n}}\) score (see performance score).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;proficiency testing&quot; includes, but is not limited to, a) quantitative scheme—where the objective is to quantify one or more measurands of the proficiency test item; b) qualitative scheme—where the objective is to identify or describe one or more characteristics of the proficiency test item; c) sequential scheme—where one or more proficiency test items are distributed sequentially for testing or measurement and results returned to the proficiency testing provider at intervals; d) simultaneous scheme—where proficiency test items are distributed for concurrent testing or measurement within a defined time period; e) single occasion exercise—where proficiency test items are provided on a single occasion; f) continuous scheme—where proficiency test items are provided at regular intervals; g) sampling—where samples are taken for subsequent chemical analysis; and h) data transformation and interpretation—where sets of data or other information are furnished
						and the information is processed to provide an interpretation (or other outcome).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>proficiency testing</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08004"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08043"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08049"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08082"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08093"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08095"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08100"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08102"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08104"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08101"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PT</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08102 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08102">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Organization which takes responsibility for all tasks in the development and operation of a proficiency testing scheme.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PT provider</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>proficiency testing provider</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08091"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08104"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08103 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08103">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Single complete sequence from distribution of proficiency test items to reporting of the evaluation results to the participants.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PT round</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>proficiency testing round</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08093"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08100"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08104 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08104">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Proficiency testing designed and operated in one or more proficiency testing rounds for a specified area of testing, measurement, calibration, or inspection.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PT scheme</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A proficiency testing scheme might cover a particular type or number of tests, measurements, calibrations or inspections on proficiency test items.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The use of terms such as &quot;ring test&quot;, &quot;round robin&quot;, &quot;intercalibration&quot;, &quot;intercomparison&quot;, &quot;collaborative study&quot;, etc. for proficiency testing schemes is not recommended in formal documents.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>proficiency testing scheme</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08049"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08080"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08100"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08101"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08103"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08105 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08105">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Detailed set of instructions describing the objectives, design, conduct, and reporting of an interlaboratory comparison.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:label>protocol for interlaboratory comparison</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08081"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08106 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08106">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Degree to which a set of inherent characteristics of an object fulfils requirements.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>&quot;Inherent&quot;, as opposed to &quot;assigned&quot;, means existing in the object.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Measurement uncertainty provides a quantitative measure of the quality of a measurement result.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;quality&quot; can be used with adjectives such as poor, good, or excellent.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>quality</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08122"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08107 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08107">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Material used for the purposes of quality control and subjected to the same or part of the same measurement procedure as that used for analytical samples.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>control material</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Blank materials, analytical samples, reference materials, and certified reference materials can be used as quality control materials.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>quality control material</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08010"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08028"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08108 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08108">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Part of quality management focused on increasing the ability to fulfil requirements for quality.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The requirements can be related to any aspect such as effectiveness, efficiency, or object traceability.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>quality improvement</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08037"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08106"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08109"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08122"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08109 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08109">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coordinated activities to direct and control an organization with regard to quality.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Quality management can include establishing quality policies and quality objectives.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>quality management</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08091"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08106"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08112"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08115"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08110 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08110">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Part of a set of interrelated or interacting elements of an organization to establish quality policies and quality objectives and processes to achieve those objectives.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A laboratory&apos;s structure, roles and responsibilities, planning, operation, practices, rules, and beliefs are also established within the quality management system.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Other common parts of a laboratory&apos;s management system are financial management and environmental management. These management systems should not be confused with a laboratory information management system.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>quality management system</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08084"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08091"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08112"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08115"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08111 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08111">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Specification for the quality management system of an organization.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Quality manuals can vary in detail and format to suit the size and complexity of an individual organization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>quality manual</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08091"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08110"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08112 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08112">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Result to be achieved related to quality.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In the context of quality management systems, quality objectives are set by the laboratory, consistent with the quality policy, to achieve specific results.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Quality objectives are generally based on the quality policy of the organization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Quality objectives are generally specified for relevant functions, levels and processes in the organization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>quality objective</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08091"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08106"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08115"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08113 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08113">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Specification of the procedures and associated resources to be applied when and by whom to a specific object.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A quality plan is generally one of the results of quality planning.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A quality plan often refers to parts of the quality manual or to procedure documents.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>These procedures generally include those referring to quality management processes and to product and service realization processes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>quality plan</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08109"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08111"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08114"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08114 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08114">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Part of quality management focused on setting quality objectives and specifying necessary operational processes and related resources to achieve the quality objectives.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Establishing quality plans can be part of quality planning.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>quality planning</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08109"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08112"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08113"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08115 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08115">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Intentions and direction of a laboratory as formally expressed by its top management related to quality.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Generally, the quality policy is consistent with the overall policy of the laboratory, can be aligned with the laboratory&apos;s vision and mission, and provides a framework for the setting of quality objectives.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>quality policy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08106"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08112"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08116 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08116">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Laboratory that applies a reference measurement procedure, a reference examination procedure, or performs a calibration.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In laboratory medicine, a reference measurement laboratory is defined in ISO 15195.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;reference laboratory&quot; may have other connotations in a national context.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>reference laboratory</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08117 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08117">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Document, issued by a reference material producer, containing the essential information for the use of a certified reference material and confirming that the necessary procedures have been carried out to ensure the validity and traceability of the stated property values.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A reference material certificate is usually valid for a stated period of time (&quot;period of validity&quot; or as described by its &quot;expiry date&quot;), which defines the shelf life of the certified reference material.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Documents containing the information described above may not, for legal or other (non-technical) reasons, be termed &quot;certificate&quot; (compare Note 1 to certified reference material).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>ISO Guide 31 is the guidance document to the required and recommended content of a reference material certificate.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In this definition, &quot;traceability&quot; covers both metrological traceability of a measurement result and examination traceability, but not object traceability.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>reference material certificate</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08037"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08046"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08120"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08118 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08118">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process that formally establishes the certified property value(s) of a candidate certified reference material.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>certification of a reference material</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Properties of a material include qualitative properties such as identities of chemical substances and quantitative properties such as mass concentration.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Reference material certification is a &quot;first-party attestation&quot; in accordance with the definition of the term &quot;declaration&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The outcome of the process is a reference material certificate.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The process is the responsibility of the reference material producer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>reference material certification</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08013"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08059"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08117"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08120"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08119 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08119">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Document giving detailed information, in addition to that contained in a reference material certificate.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>certification report</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The information may refer to, for example, the preparation of the reference material, measurement procedures, factors affecting measurement accuracy, statistical treatment of measurement results, and the way in which metrological traceability was established.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>reference material certification report</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08028"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08117"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08120 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08120">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Technically competent body that produces a reference material compliant with the appropriate guidance documents and issues the reference material documentation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RM producer</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>&quot;Body&quot; refers to an organization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>ISO 17034 provides the general requirements for the competence of reference material producers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The relevant guidance documents are the ISO Guides 31 and 35.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>reference material producer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08065"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08091"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08122"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08121 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08121">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Interval of non-permissible measured quantity values.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>rejection zone</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Non-permissible measured quantity values are associated with items that do not conform to specified requirements and lie outside the acceptance interval.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>rejection interval</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08052"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08067"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08129"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08122 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08122">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Need or expectation that is stated, generally implied or obligatory.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>&quot;Generally implied&quot; means that it is custom or common practice for the organization and interested parties that the need or expectation under consideration is implied.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A specified requirement is one that is stated, for example, in documented information.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>requirement</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08091"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08129"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08123 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08123">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ability of a measurement procedure to maintain acceptable performance under minor changes in operating conditions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>robustness of a measurement procedure</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A measurement procedure, the performance of which remains unaffected by minor changes in operating conditions, is usually said to be &quot;rugged&quot; or &quot;robust&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Ruggedness is normally investigated as a part of procedure development and may be part of procedure validation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The outcome of a ruggedness test provides objective evidence of the applicability of the measurement procedure during normal usage.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>ruggedness of a measurement procedure</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08028"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08049"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08097"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08124 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08124">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Quotient of the change in an indication of a measuring system and the corresponding change in a quantity being measured.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>analytical sensitivity</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>sensitivity</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Sensitivity of a measuring system can be obtained from the slope of the calibration curve.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Sensitivity of a measuring system can depend on the value of the quantity being measured.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Sensitivity of a measuring system should not be confused with diagnostic sensitivity in laboratory medicine.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Sensitivity of a measuring system should not be confused with limit of detection or limit of quantification.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The change considered in a value of a quantity being measured must be large compared with the resolution.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sensitivity of a measuring system</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08022"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08125 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08125">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Control chart with Shewhart control limits intended primarily to distinguish between the variation in the plotted measure due to random causes and that due to special causes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:label>Shewhart control chart</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08068"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08126"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08126 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08126">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Control limit determined statistically from the variation of the process due to random causes alone.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:label>Shewhart control limit</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08069"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08127 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08127">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Shewhart control chart in which the means of measured quantity values are plotted against time. Figure 1: Shewhart means chart of the means of duplicate analyses of a quality control material, twice per day (d) over 20 days. Each point is the mean of the day’s four results (\(n = 4\)). Upper and lower warning limits (UWL and LWL) are at \(\bar{x} \pm 2s/\sqrt{n}\), where \(\bar{x}\) is the process mean, \(s\) is the standard deviation under repeatability conditions of measurement, and upper and lower action limits (UAL and LAL) are at \(\bar{x} \pm 3s/\sqrt{n}\). A simulated measurement bias of one standard deviation is applied after day 10.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Nordtest terms a control chart with measured quantity values scaled by the mean or target value an &quot;X-chart&quot;. ISO uses the term &quot;X-control chart&quot; for a control chart which plots single values.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Shewhart means chart</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08040"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08107"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08125"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08128 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08128">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Shewhart control chart in which the ranges of measured quantity values are plotted against time.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Nordtest advises that for Shewhart range charts, the best samples to be used are test samples selected from among the samples to be analysed in that analytical run.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The range is the greatest measured quantity value minus the least measured quantity value.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Warning and action limits are given in tables (for example) as a function of the number of measured quantity values (\(n\)) taken to obtain the range and of the expected average range.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Shewhart range chart</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08006"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08125"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08129 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08129">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Need or expectation that is stated.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:label>specified requirement</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08122"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08130 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08130">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measure of dispersion used in the evaluation of results of proficiency testing.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Not all proficiency testing schemes evaluate proficiency based on the dispersion of results.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>standard deviation for proficiency assessment</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08101"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08104"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08131 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08131">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Authorized, documented procedure or set of procedures with detailed instructions for specified activities.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>recommended operating procedure</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>standard method</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Laboratory activities for which standard operating procedures are issued include sampling, measurement, testing, and examination.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>standard operating procedure</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08043"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08049"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08132 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08132">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Activities focused on the use of statistical techniques to reduce variation, increase knowledge about the process, and steer the process in the desired way.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SPC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Although statistical process control originally was concerned primarily with manufactured goods, it is also equally applicable to processes producing services, for example, a measurement procedure.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>statistical process control</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08028"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08132"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SPC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08133 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08133">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement uncertainty specified as an upper limit and decided on the basis of the intended use of measurement results.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Setting and using target uncertainty is described in.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>target measurement uncertainty</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08134 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08134">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Difference between upper and lower tolerance limits.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>specified tolerance</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Tolerance should not be confused with measurement uncertainty.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>tolerance</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08067"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08136"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08135 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08135">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Interval of permissible quantity values.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>specification zone</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A permissible quantity value of a specified quantity is required of an item fulfilling a specified requirement.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Permissible quantity values must not be confused with permissible measured quantity value of an acceptance interval.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;tolerance interval&quot; as used in conformity assessment has a different meaning from the same term as it is used in statistics.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Unless otherwise stated in a specified requirement, the tolerance limits belong to the tolerance interval.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>tolerance interval</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08052"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08067"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08129"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08136"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08136 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08136">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Specified upper or lower bound of permissible quantity values of a property.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>specification limit</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>tolerance limit</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08067"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08137 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08137">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Organization-wide efforts to install a permanent climate in which an organization continuously improves its ability to deliver high-quality products and services to customers.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>TQM</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>total quality management</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08091"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08106"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08137"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>TQM</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08138 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08138">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Confirmation, through the provision of objective evidence, that the requirements for a specific intended use or application have been fulfilled.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In the VIM, &quot;validation&quot; is defined as &quot;verification, where the specified requirements are adequate for an intended use&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The objective evidence needed for a validation is the result of a test or other form of determination such as performing alternative calculations or reviewing documents.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;validated&quot; is used to designate the corresponding status.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The use conditions for validation can be real or simulated.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>validation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08018"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08049"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08097"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08122"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08139 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08139">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Confirmation, through the provision of objective evidence, that specified requirements have been fulfilled.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In the VIM, &quot;verification&quot; is defined as &quot;provision of objective evidence that a given item fulfils specified requirements&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The activities carried out for verification are sometimes termed &quot;qualification process&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The objective evidence needed for verification can be the result of an inspection or of other forms of determination such as performing alternative calculations or reviewing documents.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;verified&quot; is used to designate the corresponding status.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>verification</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08018"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08080"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08122"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08140 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08140">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Graphical technique for evaluating an interlaboratory comparison when each participant has made two measurements on the same sample or one measurement on each of two different samples.Figure 1: Youden plot of the \(z\) scores of samples #9 and #10 of Round 5 of U39 of the proficiency testing program held by the RCPA Australia for the analysis of creatinine in urine by 218 laboratories. (Reproduced with permission).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0819"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The Youden plot is a simple but effective method for comparing both within-laboratory variability and between-laboratory variability.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The coordinates of each point may be the measured quantity values themselves or any transformation thereof such as a performance score, for example, a \(z\) score (see Fig. 1).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Youden plot</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08081"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08095"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08101"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08141 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08141">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In theories of unimolecular reactions an energized chemical species, often characterized by the superscript \(^{\ddagger}\), where the excitation is specific and the molecule is poised for reaction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Often used as a synonym for activated complex or transition state, but not restricted to transition-state theory.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>This is distinct from an energized molecule, often characterized by the superscript \(^{\ast}\), in which excitation energy is dispersed among internal degrees of freedom.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>This is not a complex according to the definition in this Glossary.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>activated state</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08142 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08142">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Both nucleophilic and electrophilic.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The distinction between ambi (Latin: both) and amphi (Greek: both) and the application to hydrophilic and lipophilic or to nucleophilic and electrophilic is arbitrary.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>ambiphilic</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08143 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08143">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>1) Designation applied to the carbon to which a functional group is attached. 2) In carbohydrate nomenclature, a stereochemical designation of the configuration at the anomeric carbon. 3) Parameter in a Brønsted relation expressing the sensitivity of the rate of protonation to acidity. 4) Parameter in Leffler’s relation expressing the sensitivity of changes in Gibbs activation energy to changes in overall Gibbs energy for an elementary reaction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>α</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08183"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08144 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08144">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Feature of a ligand with two potential binding sites.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>bidentate</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08145 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08145">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Feature on a potential-energy surface whereby a minimum-energy reaction path (MERP) emanating from a saddle point (corresponding to a transition structure) splits in two and leads to alternative products without intervening minima or secondary barriers to overcome. A bifurcation arises when the curvature of the surface in a direction perpendicular to the MERP becomes zero and then negative; it implies the existence of a lower-energy transition structure with a transition vector orthogonal to the original MERP.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>bifurcation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08146 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08146">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Standard molar enthalpy required to break a given bond of some specific molecular entity by homolysis.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Although \({\rm{D}}H^{\circ}\) is commonly used, \(\Delta_{\rm{diss}} H^{\circ}\) is more consistent with the notation for other thermodynamic quantities.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>bond-dissociation enthalpy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08147 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08147">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Average value of the gas-phase bond-dissociation enthalpies (usually at a temperature of \(\pu{298 K}\)) for all bonds of the same type within the same chemical species.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>mean bond energy</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>mean bond enthalpy</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>More commonly, tabulated mean bond energies (which are really enthalpies) are values of bond enthalpies averaged over a number of selected chemical species containing that type of bond, such as \(\pu{414 kJ mol-1}\) for \(\ce{C-H}\) bonds in a group of \(\ce{R3CH}\) (\(\ce{R = H}\), alkyl).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>bond enthalpy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08146"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08148 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08148">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Two (or more) molecules with the same spin multiplicity but with different lengths for one or more bonds.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>This feature arises because the potential-energy surface, which describes how the energy of the molecule depends on geometry, shows two (or more) minima that are not merely symmetry-related.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>bond-stretch isomers</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08149 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08149">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Parameter in a Brønsted relation expressing the sensitivity of the rate of deprotonation to basicity.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>\(\beta_{\rm{nuc}}\) and \(\beta_{\lg}\) are used to correlate nucleophilic reactivity and leaving-group ability, respectively.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>&lt;i&gt;β&lt;/i&gt;, &lt;i&gt;β&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sub&gt;nuc&lt;/sub&gt;, &lt;i&gt;β&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sub&gt;lg&lt;/sub&gt;</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08150 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08150">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Transformation of the general type \[\ce{XABY -&gt; A=B + XY (or X + Y, or X^{+} + Y^{-})}\] where the central atoms \(\ce{A}\) and \(\ce{B}\) are commonly, but not necessarily, carbon.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>1,2-elimination</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>β-elimination</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08151 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08151">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Quantitative measure of solvent polarity, based on the solvent’s hydrogen-bond-donor ability, hydrogen-bond-acceptor ability, polarizability, and dipolarity.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>Catalán solvent parameters</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08152 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08152">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Monoclonal antibody with enzymatic activity.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>abzyme</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A catalytic antibody acts by binding its antigen and catalyzing a chemical reaction that converts the antigen into desired products. Despite the existence of natural catalytic antibodies, most of them were specifically designed to catalyze desired chemical reactions.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Catalytic antibodies are produced through immunization against a transition-state analogue for the reaction of interest. The resulting antibodies bind strongly and specifically the transition-state analogue, so that they become catalysts for the desired reaction.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The concept of catalytic antibodies and the strategy for obtaining them were advanced by W. P. Jencks. The first catalytic antibodies were finally produced in 1986.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>catalytic antibody</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08153 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08153">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sequence of reaction steps in the form of a loop. One step is binding of a reactant to the active catalyst (sometimes formed from a precatalyst), and another step is the release of product and regeneration of the catalyst.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>catalytic cycle</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08154 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08154">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Noncovalent attractive force between a positive ion (metal cation, protonated Brønsted base, etc.) and a \(\ce{\uppi\ electron}\) system.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>cation-π interaction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08155 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08155">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Structural characteristic rendering a molecule chiral.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>chiral feature</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08156 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08156">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Attraction between molecules through noncovalent interactions that exhibit complementarity only between partners with specific chirality.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>chiral recognition</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08187"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08157 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08157">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Feature of a concerted transformation in which the primary changes in bonding occur within a cyclic array of atoms but in which two nonbonding atomic orbitals on an atom interchange roles with two bonding orbitals.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Because the atomic orbitals that interchange roles are orthogonal, such a reaction does not proceed through a fully conjugated transition state and is thus not a pericyclic reaction. It is therefore not governed by the rules that express orbital symmetry restrictions applicable to pericyclic reactions.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>coarctate</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08158 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08158">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Linear representation of the structure of a molecular entity in which bonds are omitted.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>This term is sometimes also called a line formula, because it can be written on a single line, but the line formula explicitly shows all bonds.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>condensed formula</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08160 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08160">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Symbols for depicting the flow of electrons in a chemical reaction or to generate additional resonance forms.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Single-headed curly arrows are used to depict the flow of unpaired electrons.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The tail of the curly arrow shows where an electron pair originates, and the head of the curly arrow shows where the electron pair goes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>curly arrows</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08166"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08205"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08161 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08161">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Large molecule constructed from a central core with repetitive branching and multiple functional groups at the periphery.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>arborol</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>cascade molecule</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The name comes from the Greek δενδρον (dendron), which translates to &quot;tree&quot;. Synonymous terms include arborols and cascade molecules.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dendrimer</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08163 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08163">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Method for analyzing activation energy as the sum of the energies to distort the reactants into the geometries they have in transition states plus the energy of interaction between the two distorted reactants.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>activation strain model</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>distortion interaction model</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08164 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08164">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Quantitative experimental measure of the Lewis basicity of a molecule \(\ce{B}\), expressed as the negative enthalpy of the formation of the 1:1 complex \(\ce{B.SbCl5}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>donor number DN (misnomer)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Donicity is often used as a measure of a solvent’s basicity.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>donicity</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08165 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08165">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>NMR spectroscopy of samples undergoing chemical reactions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Customarily, this does not apply to samples where the composition of the sample, and thus its NMR spectrum, changes with time, but rather to samples at equilibrium, without any net reaction. The occurrence of chemical reactions is manifested by features of the NMR line-shape or by magnetization transfer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dynamic NMR</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08166 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08166">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Method using curly arrows for showing the formal movement of an electron pair (from a lone pair or a \(\upsigma\) or \(\ce{\uppi\ bond}\)) or of an unpaired electron, in order to generate additional resonance forms or to denote a chemical reaction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The electron movement may be intramolecular or intermolecular.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When a single electron is transferred, a single-headed curly arrow or &quot;fish-hook&quot; is used, but the electron movement in the opposite direction is redundant and sometimes omitted.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electron pushing</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08160"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08205"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08167 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08167">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Heterolytic reaction in which the entering group adds to a nucleophile and in which the leaving group, or electrofuge, relinquishes both electrons to its reaction partner, whereupon it becomes another potential electrophile.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>It is arbitrary to emphasize the electrophile and ignore the feature that this is also a nucleophilic substitution, but the distinction depends on the electrophilic nature of the reactant that is considered to react with the substrate.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrophilic substitution</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08168 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08168">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Two-dimensional NMR technique producing cross peaks corresponding to site-to site chemical exchange. The cross-peak amplitudes carry information about exchange rates.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>EXSY</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>NMR exchange spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08169 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08169">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Quantity (omitted if zero) attached to each atom in a Lewis structure according to \[Z_{\rm{formal}} = N_{\rm{valence}} - N_{\rm{lonepairs}} - ½N_{\rm{bonds}}\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>This formalism assumes that electrons in bonds are shared equally, regardless of electronegativity.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>formal charge</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08170 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08170">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Acid and base for which adduct formation is prevented by steric hindrance.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>frustrated Lewis acid-base pair</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08171 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08171">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Function centered on an atom of the form \(\phi(r) \propto x^{i} y^{j} z^{k} \exp (-\zeta r^{2})\), used to approximate atomic orbitals in the LCAO-MO method.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>Gaussian orbital</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08172 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08172">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Allotrope of carbon, whose structure is a one-atom-thick planar sheet of \(\ce{sp^{2}{-}bonded}\) carbon atoms in a honeycomb (hexagonal) crystal lattice.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>graphene</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08173 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08173">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Association between a Lewis-acidic halogen atom in a molecular entity and a Lewis-basic region in another, or the same, molecular entity, which acts as an electron-pair donor, such that the balance of forces of attraction and repulsion results in net stabilization.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The interaction provides a stabilization of a few \(\pu{kJ mol-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>This is analogous to a hydrogen bond, in which the \(\ce{H}\) is the acidic atom.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Typical halogen-bond donors are \(\ce{I2}\), \(\ce{Br2}\), \(\ce{ICN}\), and \(\ce{IC#CH}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>halogen bond</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08174 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08174">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Topological description for the number of contiguous atoms of a ligand that are bonded to a central metal atom.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The hapticity is indicated as a superscript following the Greek letter \(\upeta\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>hapticity</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08175 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08175">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Strongly or weakly basic solvent capable of acting as a hydrogen-bond acceptor (HBA) and forming strong intermolecular solute-solvent hydrogen bonds.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>hydrogen-bond acceptor solvent</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>protophilic solvent</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A solvent that is not capable of acting as a hydrogen-bond acceptor is called a non-HBA solvent.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>HBA solvent</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08176 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08176">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Solvent with a sizable permanent dipole moment that bears suitably acidic hydrogen atoms to form strong intermolecular solvent-solute hydrogen bonds.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>dipolar HBD solvent</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>hydrogen-bond donating solvent</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>protic solvent</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A solvent that is not capable of acting as hydrogen-bond donor is called a non-HBD solvent (formerly aprotic solvent).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>HBD solvent</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08177 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08177">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Automated method to quickly assay large libraries of chemical species for the affinity of small organic molecules toward a target of interest.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Currently more than \(\pu{E5}\) different compounds can be tested per day.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>high-throughput screening</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08178 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08178">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Cycloaddition of 1,3 dipolar compounds.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>Huisgen reaction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08179 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08179">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Attachment of the ion \(\ce{H^{+}}\) either in natural abundance or where it is not desired to distinguish between the isotopes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>hydronation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08180 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08180">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Liquid that consists exclusively or almost exclusively of equivalent amounts of oppositely charged ions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ionic solvent</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>molten salt</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In practice, the ions are monocations and monoanions.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Ionic liquids that are liquid at or around room temperature are called room-temperature ionic liquids (RTILs).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term ionic liquid has been often restricted to those water-free liquids that have melting points (or glass-transition temperatures) below \(\pu{100^{\circ}C}\), following a definition given by Walden, who prepared the first ionic liquid, ethylammonium nitrate, \(\ce{CH3CH2NH3^{+}NO3^{-}}\), mp. \(\pu{13\!-\! 14^{\circ}C}\), for conductivity measurements.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The terminology for ionic liquids is not yet settled, as stated by Welton. Room-temperature ionic liquid, non-aqueous ionic liquid, molten salt, liquid organic salt, and fused salt are often synonymous.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>ionic liquid</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08181 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08181">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Interconversion of ketone and enol tautomers by hydron migration, accelerated by acid or base catalysis.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>keto-enol tautomerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08213"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08182 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08182">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Quantitative measures of solvent polarity, based on the solvent’s dispersion/induction interactions, electrostatic interactions between permanent multipoles, solute Lewis base/solvent Lewis acid interactions, and solute HBD/solvent HBA interactions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>Laurence solvent parameters</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08183 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08183">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In a series of elementary reactions, the changes in Gibbs activation energies are often found to be proportional to the changes in Gibbs energies for the overall reaction.\[\updelta \Delta^{\ddagger} G = \alpha \Delta_{\rm{r}} G^{\circ}\] This relation was interpreted in terms of the simple assumption that a small change in any transition-state property \(P_{\ddagger}\) is a linear combination of changes in reactant- and product-state properties, \(P_{\ce{R}}\) and \(P_{\ce{P}}\). \[\updelta P_{\ddagger} = \alpha \updelta P_{\ce{P}} + (1-\alpha)\updelta P_{\ce{R}}\] Within the limits of this assumption, the parameter \(\alpha\) is an approximate measure of the fractional displacement of the transition state along the minimum-energy reaction path from reactants to products.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Leffler’s assumption</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>There are many exceptions to the validity of Leffler’s assumption that \(\alpha\) is a measure of the position of the transition state.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Leffler’s relation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08184 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08184">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Statement that the velocity of a reaction depends on the active mass, i.e., the concentrations of the reactants.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>First recognized in 1864 as the kinetic law of mass action by Guldberg and Waage, who first introduced the concept of dynamic equilibrium, but incorrectly assumed that the rates could be deduced from the stoichiometric equation. Only after the work of Horstmann and van’t Hoff a mathematical derivation of the reaction rates considering the order of the reaction involved was correctly made.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>mass action, law of</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08185 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08185">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Rate constants for the reactions of \(\ce{sp^{2}{-}hybridized}\) electrophiles with nucleophiles can be expressed by the correlation \[\lg [k/(\pu{dm3 mol-1 s-1})] = s_{\rm{N}}(E + N)\] where \(E\) is the nucleophile-independent electrophilicity parameter, \(N\) is the electrophile-independent nucleophilicity parameter, and \(s_{\rm{N}}\) is the electrophile-independent nucleophile-specific susceptibility parameter.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Applications to \(\rm{S}_{\rm{N}}2\mbox{-}type\) reactions are possible if an electrophile-specific susceptibility parameter is introduced.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>As the \(E\) parameters of the reference electrophiles are defined as solvent-independent, all solvent effects are shifted into the parameters \(N\) and \(s_{\rm{N}}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The Mayr scale is available at https://www.cup.lmu.de/oc/mayr/DBintro.html.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The correlation should not be applied to reactions with bulky electrophiles, where steric effects cannot be neglected. Because of the way of parametrization, the correlation is applicable only if one or both reaction centers are carbon.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The equation transforms into the Ritchie equation for \(s_{\rm{N}} = 1\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>This equation is equivalent to the conventional linear Gibbs energy relationship \(\lg k = Nu + s_{\rm{N}}E\), where \(Nu = s_{\rm{N}}N\). The use of \(N\) is preferred, because it provides an approximate ranking of relative reactivities of nucleophiles.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Mayr–Patz equation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08203"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08186 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08186">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Principle that a chemical reaction is expected to occur with a minimum of bond changes or with a minimum redistribution of electrons (although more complex reaction cascades are also possible).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>minimum structural change, principle of</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08187 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08187">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Attraction between specific molecules through noncovalent interactions that often exhibit electrostatic and stereochemical complementarity between the partners.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The partners are usually designated as host and guest, where the host recognizes and binds the guest with high selectivity over other molecules of similar size and shape.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>molecular recognition</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08188 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08188">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Substance whose particles are in the size range of 1 to \(\pu{100 nm}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>This may have chemical properties different from those of the corresponding bulk material.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nanomaterial</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08190 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08190">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Compound with unpaired electrons for which no Lewis structures are possible with all bonding electrons paired in single or double bonds.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The isomer shown on the right (valence tautomer methylidenecyclopropane) is a Kekulé structure.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>non-Kekulé structure</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08191 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08191">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Heterolytic reaction in which an entering group adds to the electrophilic part of the substrate and in which the leaving group, or nucleofuge, retains both electrons of the bond that is broken, whereupon it becomes another potential nucleophile.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>It is arbitrary to emphasize the nucleophile and ignore the feature that this is also an electrophilic substitution, but the distinction depends on the nucleophilic nature of the reactant that is considered to react with the substrate.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Nucleophilic substitution reactions are designated as \(\rm{S}_{\rm{N}}1\) or \(\rm{S}_{\rm{N}}2\), depending on whether they are unimolecular or bimolecular, respectively. Mechanistically, these correspond to two-step and one-step processes, respectively. \(\rm{S}_{\rm{N}}1\) reactions follow first-order kinetics but \(\rm{S}_{\rm{N}}2\) reactions do not always follow second-order kinetics.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nucleophilic substitution</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08192 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08192">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Equilibrium concentration of a substance in octan-1-ol divided by its equilibrium concentration in water.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>partition ratio</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>water/octanol partition coefficient</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>This is a measure of the lipophilicity of a substance. It is used in pharmacological studies and in the assessment of environmental fate and transport of organic chemicals.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>octanol-water partition ratio</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08193 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08193">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Catalysis by small organic molecules, as distinguished from catalysis by (transition) metals or enzymes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Frequently used organocatalysts are secondary amines (covalent catalysis by generation of enamines or of iminium ions as reactive intermediates) and thioureas (hydrogen-bonding catalysis).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Organocatalysts are often employed for enantioselectivity.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The mechanisms employed by organocatalysts are examples of general acid catalysis, general base catalysis, nucleophilic catalysis, specific acid catalysis, specific base catalysis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>organocatalysis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08194 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08194">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Change of the position of the transition state upon stabilization or destabilization of a structure (or structures) along the assumed minimum-energy reaction path.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>parallel effect</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08195 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08195">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Characteristic of a bond between atoms of different electronegativity, such that the electrons in that bond are not shared equally.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>polarity</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08196 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08196">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Free energy as a function of a set of coordinates, the negative gradient of which gives the average force acting on that configuration averaged over all other coordinates and momenta within a statistical distribution. If the averaging is performed within a canonical ensemble (constant volume, temperature, and number of particles), the PMF is equivalent to the Helmholtz energy, but if it is performed within an isobaric-isothermal ensemble (constant pressure, temperature, and number of particles), the PMF is equivalent to the Gibbs energy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PMF</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Commonly, the PMF acting upon a selected geometric variable and averaged over the coordinates and momenta of all other geometric variables is evaluated for a succession of constrained values of the selected variable, thereby generating (generically) a free-energy profile with respect to the selected reaction coordinate (e.g., a bond distance or angle or a combination of internal coordinates); specifically, this is either a Helmholtz-energy or a Gibbs-energy profile, depending upon the choice of ensemble for the statistical averaging within a computational simulation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Molecular simulations often yield Helmholtz energies, not Gibbs energies, but for condensed phases the difference is usually neglected.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Selection of two geometric variables as reaction coordinates allows a free-energy surface to be computed as a two-dimensional PMF.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>potential of mean force</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08196"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMF</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08197 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08197">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Special case of nucleofugality, describing the relative rates of transfer of a proton (more generally: hydron) from a series of Brønsted acids \(\ce{H-X}\) to a common Brønsted base.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>This term has the advantage over the commonly used &quot;kinetic acidity&quot; that philicity and fugality are associated with kinetics, while acidity and basicity are associated with thermodynamics.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>protofugality</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08199"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08198 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08198">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Attachment of the ion \(\ce{^{1}H^{+}}\) of relative isotopic mass approximately equal to 1.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>protonation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08179"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08199 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08199">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Special case of nucleophilicity, describing the relative rates of reactions of a series of Lewis bases with a common Brønsted acid. This term has the advantage over the commonly used &apos;kinetic basicity&apos; that philicity and fugality are associated with kinetics, while acidity and basicity are associated with thermodynamics.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>protophilicity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08197"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08200 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08200">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Feature of an extended conjugated \(\ce{\uppi\ system}\) bearing an electron donor group at one end and an electron acceptor group at the other end.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>push-pull conjugation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08201 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08201">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Substituent capable of electron donation by resonance (e.g., \(\ce{OCH3}\)).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>π-electron donor</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08202 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08202">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Regression model to correlate chemical properties such as boiling point or chromatographic retention time with predictor parameters based on measured or calculated features of molecular structure.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>QSPR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>quantitative structure–property relationship</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08202"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>QSPR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08205 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08205">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>One of at least two Lewis structures, with fixed single, double, and triple bonds, that is a contributing structure to the valence-bond wavefunction of a molecule that cannot be described by a single Lewis structure.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>canonical form</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>contributing structure</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>resonance structure</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Although the valence-bond wavefunction is a linear combination of the wavefunctions of the individual resonance forms, the coefficients and the relative contributions of the various resonance forms are usually kept qualitative. For example, the major resonance forms for the conjugate base of acetone are \(\ce{CH2=C(CH3)-O^{-}}\) and \(\ce{H2C^{-}-C(CH3)=O}\), with the former contributing more.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Resonance forms are connected by a double-headed arrow (\(\leftrightarrow\)). This must not be confused with the double arrow connecting species in equilibrium (\(\ce{}\)).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>resonance form</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08206 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08206">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process whereby a system of single-molecule components spontaneously forms an organized structure, owing to molecular recognition.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>self-assembly</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08187"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08207 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08207">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Paramagnetic substance that induces an additional change of the NMR resonance frequency of a nucleus near any site in a molecule to which the substance binds.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>shift reagent</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08208 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08208">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Two-dimensional representation of a molecular entity in which bonds are indicated as lines between vertices representing octet carbon atoms with attached hydrogens omitted and in which other atoms are represented by their chemical symbols.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>bond-line formula</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>skeletal formula</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08209 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08209">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Isomers that differ in their solvation environment.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Because the solvation environment fluctuates rapidly, solvatomers interconvert rapidly.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Species that differ in the type of solvent molecules should not be called solvatomers because they are not isomers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>solvatomers</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08210 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08210">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Atom within a molecule bearing groups such that interchanging any two of them leads to a stereoisomer of the original molecule.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>stereogenic center</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08212 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08212">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Second Lowest Unoccupied Molecular Orbital (SLUMO).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SLUMO</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Subjacent and superjacent orbitals sometimes play an important role in the interpretation of molecular interactions according to the frontier orbital approach.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>superjacent orbital</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08212"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SLUMO</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08213 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08213">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Constitutional isomers that can interconvert more or less rapidly, often by migration of a hydron.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:label>tautomers</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08214 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08214">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Phenomenon whereby electrons can be delocalized from any of three (or more) groups to any other.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In Hammett-type correlations (linear Gibbs-energy relationships), this situation can lead to exalted substituent constants \(\sigma^{+}\) or \(\sigma^{-}\), as in solvolysis of \(\ce{p-CH3OC6H4C(CH3)2Cl}\) or acidity of \(\ce{p-nitrophenol}\), respectively.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>This may be contrasted with cross-conjugation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>through-conjugation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08215 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08215">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extent, in consequence of Heisenberg’s Uncertainty Principle, by which a particle or a set of particles has an energy greater than that of the minimum on the potential-energy surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1010"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A molecular entity with zero-point energy may even adopt a geometry with a potential energy greater than its total energy, a possibility that permits tunnelling.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Because of zero-point energy, a molecular entity has a nonzero probability of adopting a geometry whose energy is greater than that of the energy minimum.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Because the magnitude of zero-point energy increases with decreasing mass, it is significant in the context of isotope effects, especially of hydrogen isotopes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>zero-point energy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08216 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08216">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement principle of spectroscopy for the study of electromagnetic radiation absorbed and emitted by atoms.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Atomic spectra may be emission or absorption spectra.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>atomic spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08217 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08217">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement principle of spectroscopy for the study of rotational, vibrational, and electronic transitions of molecules.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Molecular spectra may be emission or absorption spectra.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>molecular spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08245"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement principle of spectroscopy to measure the precession of magnetic moments placed in a magnetic induction based on absorption of electromagnetic radiation of a specific frequency by an atomic nucleus.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NMR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Nuclei having a suitable magnetic moment include \(\ce{^{1}H}\), \(\ce{^{13}C}\), \(\ce{^{15}N}\), \(\ce{^{19}F}\), \(\ce{^{31}P}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The technique is used as a method of determining structure of organic molecules, or as a mechanism for quantification.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>NMR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08220 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08220">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Imaginary part of the complex refractive index describing absorption of electromagnetic radiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>imaginary refractive index</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>absorption index</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08221 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08221">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Condition that occurs when two or more normal transitions have the same energy as a matter of coincidence, as opposed to because of symmetry conditions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>accidental degeneracy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08222 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08222">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Angle between a beam of electromagnetic radiation and a line normal to a surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>angle of incidence</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08223 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08223">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Having properties that depend on direction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>nonisotropic</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>anisotropic</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08224 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08224">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Technical term for changing the shape of a mathematical function, electrical signal, or optical transmission to remove or smooth a discontinuity at the edges, literally meaning &apos;removing the foot&apos;.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Apodization is also used for manipulating FIDs (free induction decay) to enhance specific components of the signal, for example to improve signal-to-noise or resolution, and is achieved by application of specific shapes to the FID, e.g., in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy: exponential, Gaussian or sine-bell; in infrared spectroscopy: Boxcar, Happ-Genzel, Norton-Beer, cosine.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Originally, &apos;apodization&apos; described the procedure by which Fourier-transform infrared spectra are corrected for the side lobes that appear in the wings of spectral bands when the interferogram is not zero at its limits, by multiplying the interferogram prior to Fourier transformation by a weighting function that is zero at its limits (see Fourier-transform spectroscopy). Its use has broadened in recent years to include weighting functions that are not zero at their limits.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>apodization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08249"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08282"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08285"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08618"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08225 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08225">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mathematical function that effects apodization.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>window function</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>apodization function</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08224"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08226 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08226">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Photoelectric detector in which a large number of pixels are distributed, usually in regularly spaced lines and usually over a rectangular area.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>array detector</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08227 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08227">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Internal reflection from an absorbing material at angles of incidence at or above the critical angle.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ATR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>frustrated total internal reflection</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>attenuated total reflection</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08222"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08271"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08573"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08584"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08623"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08227"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ATR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08228 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08228">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Device to split a beam of electromagnetic radiation into two parts.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In an ideal two-beam Fourier-transform spectrometer, the beam splitter would transmit half of the radiation and reflect half of it to create the two beams.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>beam splitter</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08229 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08229">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Function \(y = a/(b^{2} + x^{2})\) where \(a\) and \(b\) are constants.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>If \(x\) is replaced by \(\tilde{\nu} - \tilde \nu_{0}\) this function yields a Lorentzian band centered at \(\tilde{\nu}_{0}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Cauchy function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08230 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08230">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Device that stores information in the form of charge packets in an array of closely spaced capacitors.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CCD</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In spectroscopy, the charge packets are created by interaction with electromagnetic radiation and the devices are extremely sensitive detectors for near infrared and visible radiation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The packets can be transferred from one capacitor to the next sequentially by electronic manipulation, so that the information contained in the array can be sequentially read electronically.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>charge coupled device</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08642"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08230"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CCD</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08231 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08231">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Difference between the refractive indices of an optically active medium for left, \(n^{\rm{L}}\), and right, \(n^{\rm{R}}\), circularly polarized radiation (\(n^{\rm{L}}-n^{\rm{R}}\)).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>&quot;Circular birefringence&quot; is also used to describe the phenomenon of having different refractive indices for left and right circularly polarized radiation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Like the refractive indices (\(n^{\rm{L}}\), \(n^{\rm{R}}\)), circular birefringence changes with wavenumber.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>circular birefringence</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08301"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08232 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08232">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Loss of coherence between the upper and lower states of a transition.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>phase relaxation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Dephasing leads to either homogeneous broadening or inhomogeneous broadening depending on the system. The continually changing intermolecular interactions cause a shift of the frequency of the intramolecular vibration. If these interactions change much more slowly than the amplitude of this shift, heterogeneous broadening results and causes a Gaussian band. If these interactions change much faster than the amplitude of this shift, homogeneous broadening occurs and the observed spectral band is a notionally narrowed Lorentzian band. In the general case, the broadening due to dephasing is neither homogeneous nor heterogeneous and the line shape is neither Gaussian nor Lorentzian.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &apos;dephasing&apos; arises from the density matrix formalism that is used in a phenomenological approach to simplify the description of the relaxation dynamics of a molecule coupled to its surroundings.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dephasing</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08252"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08285"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08611"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08619"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08233 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08233">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Time for signal integration after start of detection.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>detector integration time</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Optimization of gate width allows detection of species with different decay rates.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>detector gate width</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08234 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08234">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of spectroscopy in which spectral subtraction is used to help the study and identification of individual species in a mixture.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>difference spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08290"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08235 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08235">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Diffuse reflected (remitted) radiant power (\(P_{\rm{rem}}\)) divided by incident radiant power (\(P_{0}\)). \(R = P_{\rm{rem}}/P_{0}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>remission fraction</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>remittance</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Some authors distinguish between remittance and diffuse reflectance by excluding specular reflection in the latter.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The sum of remittance, transmittance, and absorptance equals one.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>diffuse reflectance</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08292"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08236 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08236">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reflection in which electromagnetic radiation incident on a scattering surface at a certain angle is reflected (remitted) over all angles.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>remission</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Diffuse reflection is a complicated process and involves transmission, reflection, and scattering.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>diffuse reflection</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08235"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08281"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08237 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08237">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process in which radiation is transmitted by a scattering sample and leaves the sample in directions other than that required by Snell’s law of refraction. The process is complicated and involves transmission, reflection, and scattering.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>diffuse transmission</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08238 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08238">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coupling of different motions through dipole-dipole forces.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>transition-dipole transition dipole coupling</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In vibrational spectroscopy of crystals, intermolecular vibrational coupling through interaction of resonant transition dipoles throughout the whole crystal.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dipole coupling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08708"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08239 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08239">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Fourier-transform spectroscopy using a Michelson or other two-beam interferometer with the sample placed in one arm of the interferometer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>asymmetric Fourier-transform spectroscopy</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>This method provides information about the phase change as well as the amplitude change caused by the sample.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dispersive Fourier-transform spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08240 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08240">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Spectrometer in which electromagnetic radiation is separated spatially into its component wavenumbers by a dispersive element, such as a prism or a diffraction grating.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>dispersive spectrometer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08301"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Flow of energy through space propagated as synchronized sinusoidal waves of the electric field, \(\boldsymbol{E}\), and the magnetic field \(\boldsymbol{H}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>EM</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Electromagnetic radiation is characterized by frequency \(\nu\), wavelength \(\lambda\), and speed \(c\), where \(\nu\lambda = c\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The wavelength and velocity change when radiation enters a medium. In its interaction with atoms and molecules, radiation behaves like particles, called photons, with zero mass, energy \(h\nu\), and momentum \(h/\lambda\), where \(h\) is the Planck constant \(h = \pu{6.62607015E-34 J s}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electromagnetic radiation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08299"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>EM</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08243 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08243">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Transition accompanied by the emission or absorption of a photon.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In general, an electromagnetic transition obeys certain rules called &quot;electromagnetic selection rules&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The frequency of the photon (\(\nu\)) is related to the energy difference of the transition by \(h\nu = \Delta E = |E_{2} – E_{1}|\), where \(h\) is the Planck constant.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electromagnetic transition</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08244 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08244">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement principle of spectrometry for the study of electrons emitted by atoms.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>electron emission spectrometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08219"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08245 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08245">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Transition in an atom, ion, or molecule from an electronic energy level \(E_{1}\) to another energy level \(E_{2}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>transition</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>electronic transition</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.N04081"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.P04386"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.S04387"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.S05435"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08246 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08246">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Set of one or more stationary quantum states of a free atom, ion, or molecule having a particular internal energy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>energy state</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{J}\). Common unit: electron volt, symbol \(\pu{eV}\), where \(\pu{1 eV} = \pu{1.602176634E-19 J}\). Energy levels are also expressed as \(\pu{kJ mol-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>energy level</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08247 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08247">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Decadic absorbance as measured with no corrections for other processes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Internal absorbance is used to term absorbance corrected for other processes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term emphasizes that radiation might be lost from the beam by reflection, luminescence, scattering, and possibly vignetting (e.g., spectral shifts introduced from changing a radiation beam diameter or shape), and not solely by absorption.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>experimental absorbance</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08261"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08281"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08248 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08248">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>The appearance of peaks at incorrect frequencies in a mathematically-processed spectrum due to insufficient density of datapoints in the time-domain spectrum or from peaks outside of the sampled frequency range.This process can be simulated by folding a spectrum that shows the correct wavenumbers back onto itself at \(\tilde{ u}_{\rm{M}}\) and its multiples.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>aliasing</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Folding is a consequence of undersampling in spectroscopy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In vibrational spectroscopy, when a spectrum is sampled correctly only up to a wavenumber \(\tilde{\nu}_{\rm{M}}\), radiation of wavenumber \(\tilde{\nu} \gt \tilde{\nu}_{\rm{M}}\) appears to have a wavenumber between 0 and \(\tilde{\nu}_{\rm{M}}\), per the table below:</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>folding</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08275"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08297"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08301"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08708"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08249 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08249">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Indication from a pulsed spectroscopy experiment that takes the form of damped sine waves arising from the excited system returning to equilibrium.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>FID</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy, the FID consists of sine waves oscillating at the Larmor angular frequency (\(\omega\)) and dampened by net dephasing time relaxation (\(T_{2}^{*}\)), \(\sin (\omega t) \exp(-t/T_{2}^{*})\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>free induction decay</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08375"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08380"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08249"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>FID</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08250 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08250">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reflection of radiation from a surface that is smooth and large with respect to the wavelength of the radiation. A reflected beam, of intensity governed by the Fresnel equations, is produced at an angle of reflection equal to the angle of incidence.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>May be termed specular reflection for non-scattering samples.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Fresnel reflection</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08281"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08292"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08299"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08252 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08252">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Spectral band with the shape \(H_{\rm{G}} \exp[-4\ln 2{(\tilde{ u} - \tilde{ u}_{0})}^{2}/W^{2}]\), where \(H_{\rm{G}}\) is the band maximum (peak height), \(W\) is the full width at half maximum, and \(\tilde{ u}_{0}\) is the peak wavenumber.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>Gaussian band</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08251"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08285"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08301"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08253 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08253">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Multiplexing spectrometer that uses the optical components of a dispersive spectrometer with a Hadamard encoding mask.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>Hadamard transform spectrometer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08240"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08255 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08255">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Optical filter that excludes radiation of wavenumber lower (wavelength higher) than a certain value and passes radiation of wavenumber above (wavelength lower) than this value. Electrical filter that blocks signal with a modulation frequency lower than a certain cut-on value and passes signals of frequency above this value.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>high-pass filter</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08268"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08300"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08256 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08256">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Functions that interrelate the real part, \(f&apos;\), and imaginary part, \(f&apos;&apos;\), of the refractive index, \(\boldsymbol{\hat{n}} = n + {\rm{i}}k\), or relative permittivity, \(\boldsymbol{\hat{\epsilon}} = \epsilon&apos; + \rm{i}\epsilon&apos;&apos;\), through \[f&apos;(\tilde{ u}_{\rm{a}}) - f_{\infty}&apos; = \frac{1}{\pi} P \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \frac{f&apos;&apos;(\tilde{ u})}{\tilde{ u} - \tilde{ u}_{\rm{a}}}\rm{d}\tilde{ u}\] \[f&apos;&apos;(\tilde{ u}_{\rm{a}}) - f_{\infty}&apos; = \frac{-1}{\pi} P \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \frac{f&apos;(\tilde{ u}_{\rm{a}}) - f_{\infty}&apos;}{\tilde{ u} - \tilde{ u}_{\rm{a}}}\rm{d}\tilde{ u}\] where \(P\) means that the principal part of the integral is taken at the singularity.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>Hilbert transforms</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08257 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08257">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Idealized form of a feature in a spectrometer corresponding to a transition.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ILS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>For Fourier-transform spectra, ILS varies with the apodization function used.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Ideal line shapes include Lorentzian, Gaussian, and Voigt functions, whose parameters are the line position, maximum height, and half-width.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>instrument line shape</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08225"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08252"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08254"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08298"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08257"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ILS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08258 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08258">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Area under a spectral band in an absorption spectrum where the absorption is linearly proportional to the amount of absorber in unit area.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The absorption quantity is usually a Beer-Lambert absorption coefficient, but recently \(\tilde{\nu} \alpha_{\rm{m}}&apos;&apos;(\tilde{\nu})\), the wavenumber times the imaginary part of local molar polarizability, has been used for neat liquids to correct for dielectric effects.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The area under a band in a transmittance spectrum is not linearly proportional to the amount of absorber.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>integrated intensity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08285"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08301"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08617"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08259 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08259">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Frequency or wavenumber range over which the integrated intensity of an absorption band is measured by numerical integration of the area under the band.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For Gaussian bands, integration over one full width half maximum to each side of the band centre yields \(\pu{98\%}\) of the area under the band.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Lorentzian bands integration over \(\pu{3.1}\) or \(\pu{15.9}\) full width half maximum to each side yields \(\pu{90\%}\) and \(\pu{98\%}\) of the band area.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>integration range</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08251"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08252"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08258"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08285"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08260 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08260">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Charge coupled device with a prior amplification system to improve sensitivity.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ICCD</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The intensifier used is based on a microchannel plate (MCP), which converts the incoming photons to electrons by means of a photocathode. Generated electrons are multiplied in a second step by the MCP and then reconverted to photons using a phosphorous screen. Finally, derived photons are detected with a CCD device.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>intensified charge coupled device detector</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08230"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08260"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ICCD</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08261 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08261">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Absorbance in the absence of reflection, scattering or luminescence.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Formally, \(A_{\rm{i}} = -\log_{10}(1 - \alpha_{\rm{i}})\), \(A_{\rm{i}} = -\log_{10}(T_{\rm{i}})\) for a sample that does not scatter or luminesce, where \(A_{\rm{i}}\) is the internal absorptance and \(T_{\rm{i}}\) is the internal transmittance.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Internal absorbance is a rarely used term in quantitative absolute intensity studies to emphasize that the necessary corrections of the transmittance for reflection and other cell effects have been made. Instrument manufacturers usually use the raw, uncorrected transmittance when calculating the absorbance. See experimental absorbance.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>internal absorbance</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08247"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08262"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08281"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08263 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08263">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Functions based on the physical principle of causality that interconvert the real and imaginary parts of complex optical quantities when they are known over a sufficiently wide (strictly infinite) wavenumber range. They are frequently used to interconvert the real part, \(f&apos;\), and imaginary part, \(f&apos;&apos;\), of the refractive index, \(\boldsymbol{\hat{n}} = n + {\rm{i}}k\), the dielectric constant (relative permittivity), \(\boldsymbol{\hat{\epsilon}_{r}} = \epsilon_{r}&apos; + \rm{i}\epsilon_{r}&apos;&apos;\), or the logarithm of the complex reflection coefficient \(re^{i\varphi}\) through \[f&apos;(\tilde{ u}_{\rm{a}}) - f_{\infty}&apos; = \frac{2}{\pi} P \int_{0}^{\infty} \frac{\tilde{ u} f&apos;&apos;(\tilde  u)}{\tilde{ u}^{2} - \tilde{ u}_{\rm{a}}^{2}} {\rm{d}}\tilde{ u}\] \[f&apos;&apos;(\tilde{ u}_{\rm{a}}) = \frac{-2\tilde{ u}_{\rm{a}}}{\pi} P \int_{0}^{\infty} \frac{f&apos;(\tilde{ u}) - f_{\infty}&apos;}{\tilde{ u}^2 - \tilde{ u}_{\rm{a}}^2} \rm{d}\tilde{ u}\] where \(P\) means that the principal part of the integral is taken at the singularity.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>All functions used to model vibrational spectra obey the Kramers–Kronig transforms as long as the real parts are even functions of wavenumber and the imaginary parts are odd functions of wavenumber, so that the Kramers–Kronig transforms are equivalent to the Hilbert transforms.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Kramers–Kronig transforms</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08256"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08708"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08264 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08264">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electromagnetic radiation visible to the human eye.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In general, spectroscopic usage, &apos;light&apos; is synonymous with &apos;radiation&apos; when the specific wavelength is not relevant.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>light</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08265 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08265">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Napierian absorbance (\(A_{\rm{e}}\)) divided by path-length (\(l\)). \(\alpha = A_{\rm{e}}/l\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Napierian absorption coefficient</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>absorption coefficient</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>\(\alpha\) is the reciprocal of the optical absorption depth.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>linear Napierian absorption coefficient</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08269"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08270"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08272"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08650"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08266 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08266">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Optical device based on polarization interference caused by transmission through a series of birefringent liquid crystal layers of different thicknesses that allow a narrow wavelength region to be selected and tuned over a broad spectral range.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>LCTF</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>liquid crystal tunable filter</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08299"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08266"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>LCTF</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08267 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08267">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Amplifier that amplifies only those signals that have, within a specified spectral bandwidth, the same frequency as, and a specified phase relation with, the selected reference signal.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>lock-in amplifier</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08286"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08268 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08268">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Optical filter that excludes radiation of wavenumber greater (wavelength lower) than a certain value and passes radiation of wavenumber below (wavelength greater than) this value. Electrical filter that blocks signal with a modulation frequency above a certain cut-on value and passes signals of frequency below this value.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>low-pass filter</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08255"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08269 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08269">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Decadic absorbance (\(A_{10}\)) divided by path-length (\(l\)) and amount concentration (\(c\)). \(\varepsilon = A_{10}/(cl)\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>molar absorption coefficient</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>&apos;Extinction coefficient&apos; or &apos;molar absorptivity&apos; have been widely used for the molar absorption coefficient, unfortunately often with values given in ill-defined units. Use of these terms has been discouraged since the 1960s, when international agreement with non-chemical societies reserved the word where the absorption is linearly proportional to the amount of absorber in unit area &apos;extinction&apos; for diffusion of radiation, i.e., the sum of the effects of absorption, scattering and luminescence.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>molar decadic absorption coefficient</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08265"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08270"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08270 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08270">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Napierian absorbance (\(A_{\rm{e}}\)) divided by path-length (\(l\)) and amount concentration (\(c\)). \(\kappa = A_{\rm{e}}/(cl)\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>absorption coefficient</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>molar Napierian absorption coefficient</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08265"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08269"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08272"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08271 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08271">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process in which attenuated total reflection occurs sequentially several times with the same sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>MATR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>frustrated multiple internal reflection (FMIR)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>multiple internal reflection (MIR)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>multiple attenuated total reflection</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08227"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08271"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>MATR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08272 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08272">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Absorbance calculated with logarithm base \(e\) (natural or Napierian logarithm).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Confusion with the decadic absorbance must be avoided.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Napierian absorbance</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08273 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08273">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Molar Napierian absorption coefficient divided by the Avogadro constant. \(\sigma_{\rm{net}} = \kappa/N_{\rm{A}}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>absorption cross-section ($\upsigma$)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>net absorption cross-section</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08270"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08274 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08274">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Spectral resolution setting on an instrument that a user sets to record a spectrum.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>nominal resolution</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>resolution</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The actual resolution achieved is related to this, to the width of the bands studied and to the alignment of the instrument.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nominal spectral resolution</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08286"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08289"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08291"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08275 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08275">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Result of information theory that a sine wave can be completely reconstructed if its ordinate is known at two or more points in a cycle.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>Nyquist criterion</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08276 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08276">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Highest frequency or wavenumber that can be characterized by sampling at a given rate in order to be able to fully reconstruct the signal without artefacts.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The Nyquist sampling theorem states that an FID or interferogram must be sampled at a rate at least twice the highest frequency in the FID or interferogram in order to reproduce the correct frequencies in an NMR or Fourier-transform infrared spectrum, respectively, without folding (folding (in spectroscopy)) artefacts. \(f_{\rm{Nyquist}} = &amp;half;\nu\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Nyquist frequency</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08248"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08275"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08277 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08277">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Volume in phase space, a function of the area of the emitting source (\(S\)) and the solid angle into which it propagates \((\mathit{\Omega}).{\rm{d}}^{2} G = {\rm{d}}S {\rm{d}}\mathit{\Omega}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>étendue</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>optical throughput</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08278 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08278">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Length of the optical path through the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>path length</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In a transmission cell the path-length is usually taken as the distance between the windows.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{m}\). Commonly used units: \(\pu{\upmu m}\), \(\pu{mm}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>path-length</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08653"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08279 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08279">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Frequency or band of frequencies in the range \(\pu{E4}\) to \(\pu{E12 Hz}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RF</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>radiofrequency</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08279"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>RF</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08280 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08280">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Radiant power incident on unit area.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>incident flux density</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>irradiance</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In atomic absorption spectroscopy incident flux density at a given wavelength is given the symbol \(\phi_{0}(\lambda)\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{W m-2}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>received radiant flux density</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08299"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08451"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08281 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08281">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process by which electromagnetic radiation is reflected by a sample. For non-scattering samples the term means Fresnel reflection; for scattering samples the term includes regular reflection and volume reflection or diffuse reflection.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>reflection</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08236"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08250"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08710"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08282 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08282">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Power of signal divided by power of noise.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Initialisms should not be used to denote a quantity in expressions and formulae. \(R_{\rm{S/N}}\) is recommended.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The value of signal-to-noise ratio may be expressed in decibel (\(\rm{SNR}_{\rm{dB}}\)) as ten times the logarithm to base 10 of signal-to-noise ratio.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When signal and noise are measured across the same impedance, SNR is often calculated as the root-mean-squared amplitude of the signal divided by the root-mean-squared amplitude of the noise.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>signal-to-noise ratio</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08283 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08283">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Output of a single beam spectrometer as a function of wavenumber or wavelength.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>intensity spectrum</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A single-beam spectrum is often termed an &apos;intensity spectrum&apos; (the measured radiant power is the intensity times the area of the detector) and does not have a flat baseline.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>single beam spectrum</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08291"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08299"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08284 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08284">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Distance between the blades of the entrance or exit slits in a monochromator.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Spectral slit width is the range of wavenumbers/wavelengths or frequencies that the physical slit widths allow to be passed by the monochromator and is often determined as the full width at half maximum measured by the instrument for a line that is known to be extremely sharp.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>slit width</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08251"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08299"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08301"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08285 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08285">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Region of the absorption/transmission spectrum in which the absorbance/transmission passes through a maximum/minimum.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The use of the term &quot;spectral band&quot; implies a wider region of the spectrum than &quot;spectral line&quot; (see spectral line of an atom).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>spectral band</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08550"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08286 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08286">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Upper frequency minus lower frequency in a continuous band of frequencies.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>bandwidth</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: hertz, \(\pu{Hz}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>spectral bandwidth</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08287 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08287">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Radiant energy density in unit wavenumber (wavelength) interval at wavenumber \(\tilde{ u}\) (wavelength \(\lambda\)).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{J m-2}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>spectral radiant energy density</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08299"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08288 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08288">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Wavelength range for which a spectrometer can be used.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Spectral range depends essentially on the radiation source, the optical components of the wavelength selector, and the detector.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>spectral range</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08299"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08289 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08289">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Smallest difference between wavenumbers (or wavelengths or frequencies) at which different spectral properties may be distinguished, measured as the full width at half maximum.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>resolution</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>An equivalent definition is the minimum separation of two infinitely sharp lines of equal intensity of radiation that allows the presence of two lines to be seen in the measured spectrum.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Resolution depends strongly on the instrument line shape &quot;function&quot; of the spectrometer and on the natural line width.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>spectral resolution</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08251"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08257"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08274"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08299"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08641"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08290 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08290">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ordinate of one spectrum minus ordinate of another spectrum.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Spectral subtraction may be used to improve the ability to detect of minor components in the spectrum of a mixture.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Successful use of the technique requires spectra, such as absorbance and Raman spectra, in which the ordinate is linear in concentration; a precisely agreeing wavenumber scale; and a very high signal-to-noise ratio.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>spectral subtraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08282"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08291"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08670"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08291 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08291">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Graph of a quantity derived from the radiant power at each wavelength (frequency or wavenumber) as ordinate plotted against the wavelength (frequency or wavenumber) of electromagnetic radiation as abscissa.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>vibrational spectrum</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In mid-infrared and Raman spectroscopy, wavenumber is mostly used today, while it is more commonly replaced by wavelength in near infrared spectroscopy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Quantities plotted include absorbance, absorption coefficient, transmittance, reflectance, luminescence.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>spectrum</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08300"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08617"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08642"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08669"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08292 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08292">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reflection of electromagnetic radiation from a surface such that each incident ray is reflected at the same angle to the surface normal as the incident ray, but on the opposing side of the surface normal in the plane formed by incident and reflected rays.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>regular reflection</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>An image reflected by the surface in this way is reproduced in mirror-like (specular) fashion.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>specular reflection</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08281"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08293 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08293">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electromagnetic radiation that does not follow the usual path through a spectrometer and consequently appears in a spectrum at a frequency or wavenumber different from its initial value. The term is also used to refer to radiation that passes around the sample instead of through it, and consequently is not modified by the sample and seriously affects absolute and relative intensities.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>stray light</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08291"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08294 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08294">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>General term for the spectrum of transmitted electromagnetic radiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>transmittance spectrum</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The ordinate quantity is usually the percentage of the incident radiation that is transmitted, usually called the percent transmission, with range \(\pu{0}\) to \(\pu{100\%}\). If the ordinate quantity is the fraction transmitted, i.e., the transmittance, the ordinate range is \(\pu{0}\) to \(\pu{1}\) and the spectrum may be called a transmittance spectrum.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>transmission spectrum</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08291"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08295 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08295">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement principle of spectroscopy in which spectra are recorded at different magnitudes of an applied external perturbation of the sample and are processed to yield a 2-D correlation spectrum.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>2-D correlation spectroscopy</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Correlation spectroscopy is used in infrared spectroscopy, nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy (COSY, TOCSY), fluorescence spectroscopy and others.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The external perturbation may be time dependent (chemical reactions, physical relaxation processes), or static (temperature change, concentration change).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>two-dimensional correlation spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08296"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08323"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08435"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08454"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08618"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08296 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08296">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Three-dimensional surface in which two of the dimensions show frequency axes (\( u_{1}\), \( u_{2}\)) and the third axis shows a correlation function of the spectral intensities observed at \( u_{1}\) and \( u_{2}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>2-D correlation spectrum</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The shape of the surface shows whether the bands at the two wavenumbers are or are not correlated, and hence allows deductions to be made about the extent to which the different parts of the molecule are linked in their response to the applied external perturbation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>two-dimensional correlation spectrum</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08590"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08297 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08297">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sampling less frequently than is required by the Nyquist criterion.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Undersampling occurs when an analogue signal is digitized with less than two data points per wavelength of the shortest wavelength (highest wavenumber) radiation that reaches the detector.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>undersampling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08248"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08275"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08299"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08298 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08298">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Convolution of Gaussian and Lorentzian (Cauchy) functions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>Voigt function</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08229"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08252"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08299 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08299">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Distance travelled by a wave of electromagnetic radiation in one cycle in a non-absorbing medium of real refractive index \(n\). \(\lambda = \lambda_{0}/n\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>wavelength</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In spectroscopy, the unqualified term &apos;wavelength&apos; frequently means the vacuum wavelength, and symbol \(\lambda\) frequently means \(\lambda_{0}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{m}\). Common unit: \(\pu{nm}\) or \(\pu{\upmu m}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>wavelength in medium</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08300"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08300 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08300">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Distance travelled by a wave of electromagnetic radiation in one cycle in vacuum.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>vacuum wavelength</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{m}\). Common unit: \(\pu{nm}\) or \(\pu{\upmu m}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>wavelength in vacuum</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08301 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08301">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reciprocal of wavelength of electromagnetic radiation. \(\sigma = 1/\lambda\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>wavenumber</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In spectroscopy, the unqualified term &apos;wavenumber&apos; (sometimes referred to as &quot;reciprocal centimetre&quot; \(\pu{cm-1}\)) often means vacuum wavenumber, and symbol \(\sigma\) often means \(\tilde{\nu}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{m-1}\). Common unit: \(\pu{cm-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>wavenumber in medium</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08299"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reciprocal of the vacuum wavelength of electromagnetic radiation. \(\tilde{ u} = 1/\lambda_{0}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>vacuum wavenumber</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{m-1}\). Common unit: \(\pu{cm-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The symbol \(\nu\) is often used instead of \(\tilde{\nu}\); this usage should be discouraged.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Wavenumber is related to the energy change, \(\Delta E\), induced when the radiation is absorbed by \(\Delta E = hc_{0}\tilde{\nu}\), where \(h\) is the Planck constant and \(c_{0}\) the speed of the radiation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Wavenumber should only be used when no confusion with frequency is possible.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>wavenumber in vacuum</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08300"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08303 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08303">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Range of absorbance or intensity which a spectrometer can measure with accuracy and precision.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Working range varies in different parts of the spectral range.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>working range</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08288"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08304 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08304">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Addition of zeros to a free induction decay or interferogram to decrease the frequency or wavenumber spacing between points in the Fourier-transformed spectrum.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>zero filling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08249"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08622"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08305 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08305">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In a Michelson interferometer or other two-beam interferometer, the position of the moving mirror at which the optical paths are equal.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>zero path difference</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08306 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08306">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with one carbon and two hydrogen dimensions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In the initial HMQC step, magnetization is transferred from \(\ce{^{1}H}\) to \(\ce{^{13}C}\) and back again. This is then followed by a NOESY step in which the magnetization is transferred to any other hydrogen nucleus close by before detection.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The spectrum is used to obtain restraints for structure calculations.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The technique requires \(\ce{^{13}C}\) labelling.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>&lt;sup&gt;13&lt;/sup&gt;C-HMQC-NOESY</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08387"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08432"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08307 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08307">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with one carbon and two hydrogen dimensions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Magnetization is exchanged between all hydrogens using the nuclear Overhauser effect before being transferred to neighbouring \(\ce{^{13}C}\) nuclei and then back to \(\ce{^{1}H}\) for detection.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The spectrum is used to obtain restraints for structure calculations.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>&lt;sup&gt;13&lt;/sup&gt;C-NOESY-HSQC</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08385"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08432"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08308 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08308">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with one nitrogen and two hydrogen dimensions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Magnetization is exchanged between all hydrogens using the nuclear Overhauser effect before being transferred to neighbouring \(\ce{^{15}N}\) nuclei and then back to \(\ce{^{1}H}\) for detection. This spectrum can be used to obtain restraints for structure calculations.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>&lt;sup&gt;15&lt;/sup&gt;N-NOESY-HSQC</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08385"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08432"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08309 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08309">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with one nitrogen and two hydrogen dimensions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>An isotropic mixing step transfers magnetization between \(\ce{^{1}H}\) spins. Then the magnetization is transferred to neighbouring \(\ce{^{15}N}\) nuclei and back to \(\ce{^{1}H}\) for detection. This can help identify amino acid types.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>&lt;sup&gt;15&lt;/sup&gt;N-TOCSY-HSQC</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08432"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08435"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08310 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08310">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Pulsed radiofrequency signal in NMR that rotates the bulk magnetization vector (\(\boldsymbol{M}_{0}\)) of a nuclear spin to its opposing direction, effectively inverting the signal, (e.g., from the \(+z\) to \(-z\) axis).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>180° pulse</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08279"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08311 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08311">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Pulsed radiofrequency signal that rotates the bulk magnetization vector (\(\boldsymbol{M}_{0}\)) of a nuclear spin to its orthogonal plane.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In a simple 1D NMR experiment, this is from the \(z\) axis to the \(xy\) plane.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>90° pulse</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08279"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08312 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08312">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Duration of the digitization of a free induction decay.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>AT</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>\(t_{\rm{a}} = &amp;#189N/f_{\rm{SW}}\) with number of data points \(N\) and NMR spectral width \(f_{\rm{SW}}\) .</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>acquisition time</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08249"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08336"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08417"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08312"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>AT</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08313 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08313">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Radiofrequency pulse that inverts spins by using a frequency sweep during the pulse.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Adiabatic pulse allows very high spectral bandwidth and accurate flip angles to be achieved with high tolerance to spatial variations in RF field intensity and is thus of value in excitation of nuclei with frequency ranges.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The sweep must be slow enough to satisfy the adiabatic condition \({\rm{d}}\theta/{\rm{d}}t \ll \gamma \boldsymbol{B}_{\rm{eff}}\) where \(\boldsymbol{B}_{\rm{eff}}\) is the effective radiofrequency magnetic flux density and \(\theta\) is the angle between \(\boldsymbol{B}_{\rm{eff}}\) and abscissa.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>adiabatic pulse</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08279"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08286"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08314 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08314">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Set of coupled differential equations which can be used to describe the behaviour of a magnetization vector under any conditions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>When properly integrated, Bloch equations yield \(X&apos;\), \(Y^{\prime}\), and \(Z^{\prime}\) components of magnetization within the rotating frame of reference as a function of time.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Bloch equations</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08315 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08315">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Frequency difference seen for a nuclear magnetic resonance signal when a radiofrequency field is applied during the acquisition time.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The shift arises from the effective magnetic field generated by the applied RF field. The resonances are always moved away from the frequency of the irradiating field and are inversely proportional to the difference in frequency between the irradiation and the resonance.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Bloch–Siegert shift</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08279"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08312"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08316 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08316">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Distribution of nuclear spins among their possible energy levels at thermal equilibrium.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For a positive magnetogyric ratio, produces population excess (polarization) in the direction of \(\boldsymbol{B_{0}}\). The population difference between the spin state can be expressed as \[\frac{N_i}{N} = \frac{e^{-{\varepsilon}_{i}/kT}}{\sum_{j=1}^{M}{e}^{-{\varepsilon}_{j}/kT}}\] where \(T\) is the thermodynamic temperature, \(k\) is the Boltzmann constant, \(N_{\rm{i}}\) is the number of spins in excited state \(i\), \(N\) is the total number of spins and \(\varepsilon_{i}\) and \(\varepsilon_{j}\) are the energies of states \(i\) and \(j\) respectively, and \(M\) is the number of possible energy levels.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Boltzmann distribution of nuclear spins</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08246"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08317 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08317">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with nitrogen, carbon and hydrogen dimensions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Magnetization is transferred from \(\ce{^{1}H\upalpha}\) and \(\ce{^{1}H\upbeta}\) to \(\ce{^{13}C\upalpha}\) and \(\ce{^{13}C\upbeta}\), respectively, and then from \(\ce{^{13}C\upbeta}\) to \(\ce{^{13}C\upalpha}\). From here it is transferred first to \(\ce{^{13}CO}\), then to \(\ce{^{15}NH}\) and then to \(\ce{^{1}HN}\) for detection. Along with CBCANH and HSQC this forms the standard set of experiments needed for backbone assignment.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>CBCA(CO)NH/HN(CO)CACB</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08318"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08358"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08432"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08318 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08318">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with nitrogen, carbon and hydrogen dimensions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Magnetization is transferred from \(\ce{^{1}H\upalpha}\) and \(\ce{^{1}H\upbeta}\) to \(\ce{^{13}C\upalpha}\) and \(\ce{^{13}C\upbeta}\), respectively, and then from \(\ce{^{13}C\upbeta}\) to \(\ce{^{13}C\upalpha}\). From here it is transferred first to \(\ce{^{15}NH}\) and then to \(\ce{^{1}HN}\) for detection. For each \(\ce{NH}\) group there are two \(\ce{C\upalpha}\) and \(\ce{C\upbeta}\) peaks visible. Along with the CBCA(CO)NH and HSQC this forms the standard set of experiments needed for backbone assignment.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>CBCANH/HNCACB</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08317"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08358"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08432"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08319 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08319">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with nitrogen, carbon and hydrogen dimensions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Magnetization is transferred from the side-chain hydrogen nuclei to their attached \(\ce{^{13}C}\) nuclei. Then isotropic \(\ce{^{13}C}\) mixing is used to transfer magnetization between the carbon nuclei. From here, magnetization is transferred to the carbonyl carbon, on to the amide nitrogen and finally the amide hydrogen for detection. The chemical shift is evolved simultaneously on all side-chain carbon nuclei, as well as on the amide nitrogen and hydrogen nuclei, resulting in a three-dimensional spectrum.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>This method is a useful spectrum for obtaining carbon side-chain assignment.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>CC(CO)NH</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08432"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08320 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08320">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chemical shift difference between isotropic and anisotropic states in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CSA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Chemical shift anisotropy also contributes to nuclear spin relaxation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Nuclei which are part of a specific functional group resonate at different frequencies depending on shielding by the local electronic environment and thus can give information on their relative orientation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>chemical shift anisotropy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08223"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08320"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CSA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08321 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08321">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for obtaining high-resolution solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance spectra on nuclei with high gyromagnetic ratio such as \(\ce{^{1}H}\) and \(\ce{^{19}F}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CRAMPS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>CRAMPS is based on coherent-averaging theory, which attempts to describe the time evolution of a system in terms of an average Hamiltonian derived from the density matrix formalism of quantum mechanics.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>combined rotation and multiple pulse spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08415"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08321"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CRAMPS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08322 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08322">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Segment of a pulse sequence in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy that allows evolution of chemical shift during a fixed time period rather than through the traditional symmetrical incremented delays.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>constant time evolution</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08396"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08323 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08323">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Homonuclear two-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy correlation experiment showing cross peaks representing spin-spin coupling between pairs of nuclei.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>COSY</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>COSY is often used to show the spin-spin coupling between hydrogen nuclei 2 or 3 bonds apart (\(\ce{H,H}\mbox{-}\rm{COSY}\)).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>COSY is the simplest and original 2D experiment, but still one of the most common along with its many variations including those with multiple quantum filters, solvent suppression and optimization for long range couplings.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>correlation spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08324"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08439"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08323"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>COSY</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08324 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08324">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Heteronuclear (typically \(\ce{^{13}C}\)) detected long range 2D correlation experiment in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy that has mostly been superseded by heteronuclear multiple bond correlation (HMBC) experiments and its variants.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>COLOC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>COLOC is often used to show spin-spin coupling between hydrogen and carbon nuclei 2 and 3 bonds apart.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>correlation spectroscopy through long-range coupling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08323"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08352"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08324"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>COLOC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08325 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08325">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Parameter related to the mean time during which a molecule maintains its spatial geometry.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For an internuclear vector, correlation time is approximately equal to the average time for it to rotate through an angle of one radian.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>correlation time</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08326 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08326">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Combined use of cross polarization with magic-angle spinning for solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CP/MAS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>cross polarization with magic angle spinning NMR</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08327"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08415"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08326"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CP/MAS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08327 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08327">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Technique in solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy that enables polarization from abundant spins such as \(\ce{^{1}H}\) or \(\ce{^{19}F}\) to be transferred to dilute spins such as \(\ce{^{13}C}\) or \(\ce{^{15}N}\) to enhance signal-to-noise ratio.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Cross polarization requires that nuclei are dipolar coupled to one another.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>cross polarization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08282"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08415"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08328 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08328">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Pulse sequence in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy often used for isotropic mixing in total correlation spectroscopy (TOCSY) experiments due to its efficiency over large spectral bandwidths.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DIPSI</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>decoupling in the presence of scalar interactions</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08286"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08396"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08435"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08328"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>DIPSI</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08329 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08329">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Pseudo-two-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment that resolves NMR signals of a one-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectrum in a second dimension through the diffusion times of their corresponding molecules.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DOSY</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>DOSY is used to differentiate signals from different molecules based on their effective sizes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>DOSY relies on spatially encoding the analyte by use of pulsed-field gradients.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>diffusion ordered spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08397"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08439"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08329"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>DOSY</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08330 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08330">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Direct though-space interactions of nuclear magnetic moment vectors proportional to the inverse cube of internuclear distance.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>magnetic dipole–dipole interaction in NMR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In isotropic solution, dipolar couplings average to zero as a result of diffusion but are present in solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy and their effect on nuclear spin relaxation are measurable through nuclear Overhauser effects (NOEs).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dipolar coupling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08385"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08415"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08331 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08331">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy used for determining the presence of primary, secondary and tertiary heteronuclei.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DEPT</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>DEPT is a 1D technique which is used typically for \(\ce{^{13}C}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The DEPT experiment for \(\ce{^{13}C}\) differentiates between \(\ce{CH}\), \(\ce{CH2}\) and \(\ce{CH3}\) groups by variation of the selection angle parameter (the flip angle of the final \(\ce{^{1}H}\) pulse): \(\pu{135\!^{\circ}}\) angle gives all \(\ce{CH}\) and \(\ce{CH3}\) in a phase opposite to \(\ce{CH2}\); \(\pu{90\!^{\circ}}\) angle gives only \(\ce{CH}\) groups, the others being suppressed; \(\pu{45\!^{\circ}}\) angle gives all carbons with attached protons with the same phase.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>distortionless enhancement by polarization transfer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08343"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08331"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>DEPT</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08332 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08332">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Pulse sequence in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy in which pulsed-field gradients are applied to a doubled spin echo sequence to cleanly and precisely excite particular resonances.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DPFGSE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>double pulsed-field gradient spin-echo excitation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08396"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08397"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08420"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08332"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>DPFGSE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08333 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08333">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment where excitation is applied independently to two distinct frequency ranges.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Double resonance is most commonly applied for spin decoupling.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>double resonance</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08419"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08334 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08334">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Shorthand for a protein which has been uniformly \(\ce{^{15}N{-}}\) and \(\ce{^{13}C{-}labelled}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>double-labelled protein</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08335 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08335">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Variant of the COSY experiment with a double quantum filter to remove, or at least reduce, impact of large singlet peaks on dynamic range and artefacts.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DQF-COSY</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>double-quantum filtered correlation spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08323"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08335"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>DQF-COSY</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08336 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08336">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Time interval between sampled data points.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DW</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>\(\tau_{\rm{d}} = 1/f_{\rm{SW}}\), where \(f_{\rm{SW}}\) is the NMR spectral width.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dwell time</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08312"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08417"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08336"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>DW</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08337 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08337">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy, time interval between the middle of the first radiofrequency pulse and the peak of the spin echo.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>TE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>echo time</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08279"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08420"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08337"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>TE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08338 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08338">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy that generates an electronic signal in a nuclear magnetic resonance spectrometer which is detected simultaneously to the sample free induction decay during the acquisition.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ERETIC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>This approach is used to simplify the quantification of signals in the resulting NMR spectra by avoiding the need for the use of internal or external reference materials.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electronic reference to access in vivo concentrations</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08249"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08338"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ERETIC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08339 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08339">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>When signal averaging in a single-pulse nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment, flip angle giving the best signal-to-noise ratio for a given combination of spin-lattice relaxation time and repetition rate.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Named after Richard Ernst. It is employed to allow the maximum signal to noise to be generated in a fixed amount of time, \(\cos (\theta_{\rm{E}}) = e^{-(t_{\rm{d}} + t_{\rm{a}})/T_{1}}\), where \(t_{\rm{d}}\) is the interpulse delay, \(t_{\rm{a}}\) is the acquisition time, and \(T_{1}\) is longitudinal relaxation time.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Ernst angle</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08282"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08343"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08423"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08340 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08340">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment, which is the same as a NOESY, but for the purposes of observing nuclei exchanging chemical environments during the course of the experiment through conformational or chemical exchange.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>EXSY</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>exchange spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08387"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08340"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>EXSY</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08341 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08341">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Robust method of generating selective excitation through combination of shaped pulses and pulsed-field gradients in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>excitation sculpting</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08397"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08342 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08342">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Relaxometry to measure the nuclear spin-lattice relaxation rate constant as a function of the applied magnetic field strength in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>FFC-NMR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>FFC-NMR is used to study molecular dynamics of molecules across a range of applications including molecular motion in solids and ligand-metal binding.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>fast field cycling NMR relaxometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08422"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08342"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>FFC-NMR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08343 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08343">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Rotation that the net magnetization experiences during the application of a radiofrequency pulse in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>tip angle</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>For a strong and rectangular RF-pulse of constant amplitude (\(B_{1}\)) and duration (\(t_{\rm{p}}\)), the resultant flip angle is approximately proportional to the frequency (\(f_{1}\)) of the \(B_{1}\) field: \(\alpha = \gamma B_{1} t_{\rm{p}}\), where \(\gamma\) is the gyromagnetic ratio.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>flip angle</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08279"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08344 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08344">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Application of spin decoupling only during selected time periods in a pulse experiment in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Gated decoupling is used to eliminate either \(\ce{^{1}H}\) – \(\ce{^{13}C}\) spin-spin coupling or the nuclear Overhauser effect from a 1D \(\ce{^{13}C}\) spectrum.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>If the decoupler is turned off during acquisition this is usually referred to as &apos;gated decoupling&apos; while decoupling turned on during acquisition is usually referred to as &apos;inverse gated decoupling&apos;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>gated decoupling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08385"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08419"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08345 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08345">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Spin decoupling pulse sequence in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy used to provide decoupling over a large frequency range.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>GARP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>GARP is typically used to remove \(\ce{^{13}C}\) sidebands in \(\ce{^{1}H}\) nuclear magnetic resonance spectra.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>globally optimized alternating-phase rectangular pulses</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08419"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08345"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GARP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08346 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08346">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment where pulsed-field gradients are employed to filter out unwanted resonances from a spectrum rather than the classic phase-cycling approach.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>gradient-selected experiment</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08291"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08397"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08347 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08347">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with one nitrogen, and two hydrogen dimensions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Magnetization is transferred from the side-chain hydrogen nuclei to their attached \(\ce{^{13}C}\) nuclei. Then isotropic \(\ce{^{13}C}\) mixing is used to transfer magnetization between the carbon nuclei. From here, magnetization is transferred to the carbonyl carbon, on to the amide nitrogen and finally the amide hydrogen for detection.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Requires \(\ce{^{15}N}\) and \(\ce{^{13}C}\) labelling.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Useful spectrum for obtaining hydrogen side-chain assignments.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>H(CCO)NH</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08432"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08348 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08348">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with one nitrogen and two hydrogen dimensions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Magnetization is transferred from \(\ce{^{1}H\upalpha}\) and \(\ce{^{1}H\upbeta}\) to \(\ce{^{13}C\upalpha}\) and \(\ce{^{13}C\upbeta}\), respectively, and then from \(\ce{^{13}C\upbeta}\) to \(\ce{^{13}C\upalpha}\). From here it is transferred first to \(\ce{^{13}CO}\), then to \(\ce{^{15}NH}\) and then to \(\ce{^{1}HN}\) for detection.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Requires \(\ce{^{15}N}\) and \(\ce{^{13}C}\) labelling.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Useful spectrum for obtaining \(\ce{H\upalpha}\) and \(\ce{H\upbeta}\) assignments.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>HBHA(CO)NH</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08432"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08349 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08349">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with one carbon and two hydrogen dimensions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Magnetization is transferred from the side-chain hydrogen nuclei to their attached \(\ce{^{13}C}\) nuclei. Magnetization is then exchanged between neighbouring \(\ce{^{13}C}\) nuclei via the J-coupling and finally transferred back to the side-chain hydrogen atoms for detection.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The nuclear magnetic resonance spectrum can be useful in aiding side-chain assignment.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>HCCH-COSY</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08432"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08350 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08350">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with one carbon and two hydrogen dimensions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Magnetization is transferred from the side-chain hydrogen nuclei to their attached \(\ce{^{13}C}\) nuclei. This is followed by isotropic \(\ce{^{13}C}\) mixing and finally the transfer back to the side-chain hydrogen atoms for detection.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The nuclear magnetic resonance spectrum is used for side-chain assignment.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>HCCH-TOCSY</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08432"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08435"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08351 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08351">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Spin decoupling in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy between two different nuclei (e.g., \(\ce{^{13}C}\) and \(\ce{^{1}H}\)) by appropriate excitation of the decoupled nucleus.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>decoupling</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Heteronuclear decoupling is normally performed to simplify spectra or to enhance signal-to-noise ratio by combining individual components of the NMR signal.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Notation: \(\ce{^{13}C\{^{1}H}\}\) denotes observation of \(\ce{^{13}C}\) nucleus with simultaneous decoupling of \(\ce{^{1}H}\) nuclei.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>heteronuclear decoupling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08282"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08365"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08419"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08352 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08352">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Two-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy that gives correlations between carbons and protons that are separated by two, three, and, sometimes in conjugated systems, four bonds.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>HMBC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Because of the wide range (\(\pu{0}\) to \(\pu{14 Hz}\)) of possible carbon-proton couplings, two experiments are often performed. One optimized for \(\pu{5 Hz}\) couplings and the second optimized for \(\pu{10 Hz}\). This gives the optimum signal-to-noise ratio. Alternatively, a comprise value of \(\pu{7}\) to \(\pu{8 Hz}\) can be used. There are also &quot;accordion&quot; versions that attempt to sample the full range of couplings.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Direct one-bond correlations are suppressed.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>HMBC gives connectivity information much like a proton-proton COSY.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The intensity of cross peaks depends on the spin-spin coupling constant, which for three-bond couplings follows the Karplus relationship. For dihedral angles near \(\pu{90\!^{\circ}}\), the coupling is near zero. Thus, the absence of a cross peak does not confirm that carbon-proton pairs are many bonds apart.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>heteronuclear multiple bond correlation NMR</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08282"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08439"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08352"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>HMBC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08353 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08353">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Variant of the heteronuclear single quantum correlation (HSQC) and heteronuclear multiple bond correlation (HMBC) experiment in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy that almost exclusively correlates \(\ce{^{1}H}\) nuclei with heteronuclei separated by two covalent bonds.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>H2BC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>heteronuclear multiple-bond correlation over two bonds</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08352"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08358"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08353"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>H2BC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08354 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08354">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Simplest implementation of an inverse detected \(\ce{^{1}H}\) heteronuclear correlation nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>HMQC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>heteronuclear multiple-quantum correlation NMR</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08354"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>HMQC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08355 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08355">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Variant of heteronuclear multiple bond correlation (HMBC) spectroscopy that shows correlations from a heteronucleus (e.g., \(\ce{^{13}C}\)) to not only its directly bound \(\ce{^{1}H}\) but also to all other \(\ce{^{1}H}\) nuclei within the spin system.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>HMQC-TOCSY</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>heteronuclear multiple-quantum correlation with additional TOCSY transfer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08352"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08421"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08435"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08355"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>HMQC-TOCSY</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08356 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08356">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Heteronuclear variant of nuclear Overhauser effect spectroscopy (NOSEY) showing through space interaction between \(\ce{^{1}H}\) and a heteronucleus, typically \(\ce{^{13}C}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>HOESY</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>heteronuclear Overhauser effect spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08387"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08356"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>HOESY</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08357 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08357">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Directly-observed one-bond heteronuclear correlation experiment in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>HETCOR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>HETCOR has largely been replaced by the indirectly observed heteronuclear single quantum correlation experiment due to its greater sensitivity. It does however have the advantage of greater resolution in the heteronuclear dimension.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>heteronuclear shift correlation NMR</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08358"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08357"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>HETCOR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08358 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08358">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>\(\ce{^{1}H}\) (inverse) detected two-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy heteronuclear experiment that correlates \(\ce{^{1}H}\) nuclei with their directly coupled nucleus through a single bond.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>HSQC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>heteronuclear single quantum coherence</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>heteronuclear single quantum correlation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08439"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08358"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>HSQC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08359 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08359">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Variant of the heteronuclear single quantum correlation (HSQC) experiment that shows correlations from a heteronucleus (e.g., \(\ce{^{13}C}\)) to not only its directly bound \(\ce{^{1}H}\) but also to all other \(\ce{^{1}H}\) nuclei within the spin system.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>HSQC-TOCSY</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>heteronuclear single quantum correlation with additional TOCSY transfer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08358"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08421"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08435"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08359"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>HSQC-TOCSY</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08360 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08360">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Variant of the heteronuclear single quantum correlation (HSQC) experiment that provides pure absorption, antiphase line shapes for precise, direct measurement of \(^{n}J(\ce{C,H})\) spin-spin coupling constants.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>HSQMBC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>heteronuclear single quantum multiple-bond correlation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08358"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08360"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>HSQMBC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08361 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08361">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with nitrogen, carbon and hydrogen dimensions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Each \(\ce{NH}\) group will show correlations to both their own and adjacent carbonyls group.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>the magnetization is transferred from \(\ce{^{1}H}\) to \(\ce{^{15}N}\) and then via the \(\ce{N-C\upalpha\ J{-}coupling}\) to the \(\ce{^{13}C\upalpha}\). From there it is transferred to the \(\ce{^{13}CO}\) via the \(\ce{^{13}C\upalpha\mbox{-}\!^{13}CO J\mbox{-}coupling}\). For detection the magnetization is transferred back the same way: from \(\ce{^{13}CO}\) to \(\ce{^{13}C\upalpha}\), \(\ce{^{15}N}\) and finally \(\ce{^{1}H}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>HN(CA)CO</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08432"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08362 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08362">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with nitrogen, carbon and hydrogen dimensions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The magnetization is passed from \(\ce{^{1}H}\) to \(\ce{^{15}N}\) and then to \(\ce{^{13}CO}\). From here it is transferred to \(\ce{^{13}C\upalpha}\) and the chemical shift is evolved. The magnetization is then transferred back via \(\ce{^{13}CO}\) to \(\ce{^{15}N}\) and \(\ce{^{1}H}\) for detection. This is similar to the HNCA, but is selective for the \(\ce{C\upalpha}\) of the preceding residue.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>HN(CO)CA</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08432"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08363 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08363">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with nitrogen, carbon and hydrogen dimensions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The magnetization is passed from \(\ce{^{1}H}\) to \(\ce{^{15}N}\) and then via the \(\ce{N-C\upalpha}\) \(\rm{J}\mbox{-}\rm{coupling}\) to the \(\ce{^{13}C\upalpha}\) and then back again to \(\ce{^{15}N}\) and \(\ce{^{1}H}\) hydrogen for detection. Each \(\ce{NH}\) group will show correlations to both their own and the previous residue’s \(\ce{^{13}C\upalpha}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>HNCA</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08432"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08364 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08364">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with nitrogen, carbon and hydrogen dimensions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Magnetization is passed from \(\ce{^{1}H}\) to \(\ce{^{15}N}\) and then selectively to the carbonyl \(\ce{^{13}C}\) via the \(\ce{NH-^{13}CO}\) \(\rm{J}\mbox{-}\rm{coupling}\). Magnetization is then passed back via \(\ce{^{15}N}\) to \(\ce{^{1}H}\) for detection.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>HNCO</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08432"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08365 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08365">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Spin decoupling in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy between two signals of the same nuclear isotope.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>decoupling</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Homonuclear decoupling is normally performed through selective excitation of specific NMR signals within a spectrum or through pure shift experiments such as PSYCHE.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>homonuclear decoupling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08351"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08398"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08419"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08366 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08366">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy, one of two equally sized blocks of frequency data \(\pu{90\!^{\circ}}\) out of phase to each other produced by Fourier transformation of the time domain signal.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>imaginary spectrum</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In simple 1D spectra this equates to the NMR signals being in dispersion mode.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Usually the imaginary frequency data is not displayed and is used for phase correction of the real spectrum.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>imaginary NMR spectrum</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08367 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08367">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment designed to show homonuclear spin-spin coupling correlations between low natural abundance nuclei such as \(\ce{^{13}C}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>INADEQUATE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>2D variant shows each coupled pair of nuclei gives a pair of peaks on the INADEQUATE spectrum which both have the same vertical coordinate, which is the sum of the chemical shifts of the nuclei; the horizontal coordinate of each peak is the chemical shift for each of the nuclei separately.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>incredible natural-abundance double-quantum transfer experiment</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08367"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>INADEQUATE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08368 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08368">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Pulse sequence in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy that involves the transfer of nuclear spin polarization from spins with large Boltzmann population differences to nuclear spins of interest with low Boltzmann population differences.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>INEPT</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>INEPT is a building block for many heteronuclear NMR experiments.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>INEPT uses  for the polarization transfer in contrast to nuclear Overhauser effect (NOE) which arises from dipolar cross-relaxation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>insensitive nuclei enhanced by polarization transfer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08385"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08396"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08368"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>INEPT</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08369 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08369">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Pulse in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy that completely inverts the magnetization of a spin, e.g., \(+z\) to \(-z\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The inversion pulse can be a simple \(\pu{180\!^{\circ}}\) or composite pulse.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>inversion pulse</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08310"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08370 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08370">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Spin echo sequence preceded by a \(\pu{180\!^{\circ}}\) inversion pulse used to determine spin-lattice relaxation times in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>inversion recovery</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The sequence is typically denoted as \(\pu{180\!^{\circ}} - \uptau - \pu{90\!^{\circ}}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>inversion recovery sequence</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08310"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08371"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08420"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08371 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08371">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Time between the inversion pulse and the sampling pulse(s) in an inversion recovery sequence.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>TI</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>\(T_{\rm{I,null}}\) is the value of \(T_{\rm{I}}\) when longitudinal magnetization (\(M_{\rm{z}}\)) is close to zero at the end of the \(\ce{TI}\) interval.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>inversion time</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08370"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08371"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>TI</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08372 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08372">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nuclear magnetic resonance pulse sequence that refocuses spin vectors that have fanned out due to field inhomogeneity or chemical shift differences.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>J-MOD</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>J-MOD is typically denoted as \(\pu{90\!^{\circ}} - \uptau - \pu{180\!^{\circ}} - \uptau(\rm{echo})\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>J-modulated spin-echo</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08396"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08420"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08372"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>J-MOD</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08373 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08373">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Two-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy resolving chemical shift information in one dimension and spin-spin coupling in the second.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>J-RES</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>J-RES exists as both homonuclear and heteronuclear variants.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The homonuclear variant can generate homonuclear decoupled projection when processed with a tilt function.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>J-resolved spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08439"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08373"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>J-RES</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08374 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08374">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Frequency shift in the nuclear magnetic resonance spectrum of a paramagnetic species that refers to the relative shift \(\boldsymbol{K}\) in NMR frequency for atoms in a metal (e.g., sodium) compared with the same atoms in a nonmetallic environment (e.g., as a salt).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The observed shift reflects the local magnetic field produced at the metal nucleus by the magnetization of the conduction electrons.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Knight shift</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08375 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08375">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Frequency at which nuclei with a nuclear spin precess (Larmor precession) around the direction of an external magnetic induction \(\boldsymbol{B}_{0}\). \(\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\rm{L}} = -\gamma \boldsymbol{B}_{0}\), where \(\gamma\) is the magnetogyric ratio of the nucleus.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Larmor frequency</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>Larmor angular frequency</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08376"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08376 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08376">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Precession of the magnetic moment (\(\boldsymbol{M}\)) of an object at an angular frequency, \(\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\rm{L}}\), about a static magnetic induction (\(\boldsymbol{B_{0}}\)), named after Joseph Larmor.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Larmor precession can be visually compared to the precession of a tilted gyroscope in an external torque-exerting gravitational field.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Larmor precession</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08394"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08377 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08377">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Technique in solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy to remove or reduce the influence of anisotropic interactions by rapid sample rotation about the magic angle, where \(\boldsymbol{B_{0}}\) is external magnetic induction and \(\uptheta\) is the axis of rotation of the sample at an angle of approx. \(\pu{54.74\!^{\circ}}\) to the external magnetic induction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>MAS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Much like in the case of solutions, MAS effectively averages orientation-dependent interactions and allow high resolution spectra.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The rate of spinning must be greater than or equal to the magnitude of the anisotropic interaction to average it to zero.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>magic angle spinning</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08223"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08415"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08377"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>MAS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08378 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08378">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy used to obtain high-resolution NMR spectra of quadrupolar nuclei by removal of the anisotropy of the quadrupole interaction, involving creating a triple-quantum (or \(5{\rm{Q}}\)) coherence.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>MQMAS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>multiple-quantum magic angle spinning NMR</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08378"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>MQMAS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08379 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08379">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Feature in a nuclear magnetic resonance spectrum that is split but is too complex to easily interpret.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A multiplet is distinguished from multiplicity of NMR peaks that give well-resolved doublet, triplet, quartet, quintet etc.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>multiplet</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08380 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08380">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measured time constant associated with spin-spin relaxation for loss of magnetization in the \(xy\) plane.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>T2-star</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>\(T_{2}^{*}\) includes losses due to \(\boldsymbol{B_{0}}\) inhomogeneity as well as spin-spin relaxation and is always less than or equal to the spin-lattice relaxation time (\(T_{2}\)).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>net dephasing time</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08423"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08425"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08381 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08381">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Technique employed within a high dimensionality nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment that acquires only a subset of the data points traditionally acquired linearly thus enabling a reduction in experiment time or an increased resolution in the resultant spectra.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NUS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>NUS uses reconstruction methods to allow the extraction of complete sets of chemical shift information.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>non-uniform sampling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08381"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>NUS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08382 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08382">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Parameter that describes the effective shape of the ellipsoid of nuclear charge distribution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>quadrupole moment of a nucleus</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>By convention, the value of \(eQ\) is taken to be positive if the ellipsoid is prolate and negative if it is oblate.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Non-zero quadrupole moment indicates that the charge distribution is not spherically symmetric.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nuclear electric quadrupole moment</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08383 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08383">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy to measure nuclear relaxation variables.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NMRR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>relaxometry</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>NMRR generally involves analysis of time domain NMR signals to generate simplified NMR data rather than conventional full frequency resolved NMR spectra.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nuclear magnetic resonance relaxometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08383"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>NMRR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Representation of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy data with at least one dimension a frequency domain.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NMR spectrum</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>An NMR spectrum typically involves the Fourier transform of a free induction decay. See Fourier-transform spectroscopy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nuclear magnetic resonance spectrum</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08249"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08385 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08385">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Change of intensity of one resonance when the spin transitions of another are somehow perturbed from their equilibrium populations.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>nOe</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>nuclear Overhauser enhancement</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The nuclear Overhauser effect at nucleus \(i\) for perturbation of spin \(S\) is expressed as a relative intensity change between the equilibrium intensity (\(I\)) and that in the presence of the nOe (\(I_{\rm{o}}\)). \(\eta_{i}({\rm{S}}) = (I - I_{o})/I_{o}\). The effect may be given as a percentage \(\eta \times \pu{100 \%}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>\(\eta\) can be positive or negative depending on the motional properties of the molecule and the signs of the gyromagnetic ratios.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>nOe allows a measure of through space rather than through bond interactions between nuclei and is proportional to the inverse \(6^{\rm{th}}\) power of the internuclear distance.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nuclear Overhauser effect</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08385"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>nOe</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08386 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08386">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>One dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy that yields, through subtraction of spectra generated with and without selective excitation of a specific resonance, the nuclear Overhauser enhancement of nuclei spatially close to a selectively excited nucleus.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>nOe difference spectroscopy</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>nuclear Overhauser effect difference spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08385"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08387 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08387">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy based on the nuclear Overhauser effect to observe nuclei that are close to each other in space.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NOESY</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>nuclear Overhauser enhancement spectroscopy</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A NOESY spectrum yields through space correlations via dipolar cross-relaxation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>For small molecules, NOE may be observed between protons that are up to \(\pu{0.4 nm}\) apart, while the upper limit for large molecules is about \(\pu{0.5 nm}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>NOESY also detects chemical and conformational exchange, when it is termed exchange spectroscopy (EXSY).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>NOESY is typically run as a homonuclear \(\ce{^{1}H}\) experiment.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nuclear Overhauser effect spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08340"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08385"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08387"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>NOESY</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08388 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08388">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy where splitting of the nuclear spin states is determined by the electrostatic interaction of the nuclear charge density with the external electric potential of the surrounding electron cloud.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NQR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Unlike NMR, NQR transitions of nuclei can be detected in the absence of a magnetic field.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nuclear quadrupole resonance spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08388"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>NQR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08389 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08389">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Frequency corresponding to the centre of the spectrum window for a given nucleus under observation and the nominal frequency used to generate the hard, full spectrum pulses.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>observation frequency</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08291"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08390 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08390">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Characteristic line shape seen in solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy arising from dipolar coupling between two nuclei.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>Pake doublet</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08415"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08391 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08391">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Linear combination of the real and imaginary parts of a 1-D nuclear magnetic resonance spectrum to produce peaks with pure absorption mode line shape.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The phase correction is normally described by the automatic or manual setting of a fixed zero order and a frequency dependent first order phase constant.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>phase correction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08392 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08392">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Component of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment that repeats a pulse sequence changing only the phases of the pulse(s) and the phase-sensitive detector reference.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The resultant FID’s are then added to suppress undesirable signal components and/or to produce the desired effect of a pulse sequence (e.g., multiple quantum filter).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>phase cycling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08249"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08396"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08393 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08393">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Very broad and distinctive line shape seen in a static solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment with a typical magnitude of \(\pu{E2}\) to \(\pu{E5 Hz}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A powder pattern is a summation of the multitude of signals arising from crystals with different orientations within the magnetic induction.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>powder pattern</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08415"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08394 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08394">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Classical description of the behaviour of nuclear magnetic moments, in which the vectors rotate about the \(\boldsymbol{B_{0}}\) axis at their Larmor angular frequencies.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>precession</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08375"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08395 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08395">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Technique to suppress a particular nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy signal where a long low-power pulse is applied to that specific resonance prior to the main pulse sequence.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Presaturation is typically used as a method for solvent suppression.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>presaturation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08396"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08396 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08396">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Timed series of radiofrequency pulses and magnetic field gradients that define a part or whole of a nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>pulse sequence</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08279"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08397 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08397">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Short, timed pulse in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy that momentarily destroys the magnetic field homogeneity within the sample through a spatial-dependent field intensity.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PFG</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>gradient pulse</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A PFG is characterized by its power, shape, duration and axis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>PFG techniques are used in magnetic resonance imaging, spatially-selective NMR, and diffusion ordered NMR spectroscopy (DOSY).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The net result of the pulse is that spins are dispersed in the transverse plane (defocussed) and produce zero net magnetization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>pulsed-field gradient</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08329"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08397"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PFG</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08398 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08398">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment generating homonuclear spin-spin decoupled spectra.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PSYCHE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The effect is achieved through the concatenation of partial free induction decays generated with a series of swept adiabatic pulses.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>pure shift yielded by chirp excitation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08249"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08398"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PSYCHE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08399 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08399">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Collection of time domain nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy data on both the \(x\) and \(y\ (\rm{g})\) axes of the rotating frame of reference to allow discrimination of positive and negative frequencies.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>quadrature detection</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08400 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08400">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nuclide with spin quantum number \(I \gt &amp;half;\) and a non-spherical distribution of charge in the nucleus giving rise to a nuclear electric quadrupole moment (\(eQ\)).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>quadrupolar nuclide</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08382"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08401 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08401">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Relaxation mechanism in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy arising from intramolecular quadrupolar interactions with electric field gradients.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>quadrupolar relaxation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08400"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08402 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08402">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Line-broadening effect on intense signals in high field nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Radiation damping can also produce other effects including changes to amplitude and phase of adjacent peaks.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Radiation damping occurs when the rotating transverse magnetization of the sample is intense enough to induce large enough electromotive force in the radiofrequency coil strong enough that it feeds back to the sample. This causes a rotation of magnetization back to the \(+z\) axis and results in line-broadening of the intense peak.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>radiation damping</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08279"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08403 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08403">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy, an inductor-capacitor resonant circuit used to set up \(B_{1}\) magnetic inductions in a sample and to detect the signal from the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RF coil</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>radiofrequency coil</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08404 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08404">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy, one of two equally sized blocks of frequency data \(\pu{90\!^{\circ}}\) out of phase to each other produced by Fourier transformation of the time domain signal.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Usually only the real frequency data is displayed and in simple 1D spectra equates to the NMR signals being absorption mode (in-phase).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>real spectrum</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08366"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08405 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08405">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Paramagnetic species added to a sample in a nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment to promote more rapid spin-lattice relaxation and allow a faster pulse repetition rate.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>relaxation reagent</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08422"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08406 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08406">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Time duration between repetitions of a nuclear magnetic resonance pulse sequence including the pulse sequence, acquisition time and relaxation delay.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>TR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>repetition time</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08312"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08396"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08422"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08406"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>TR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08407 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08407">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Dipolar coupling, but of a reduced magnitude to that seen in solids, observed in a liquid sample between spins in a molecule where there is incomplete averaging of spatially anisotropic dipolar couplings.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RDC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>RDC is typically achieved through partial alignment of molecules in the liquid state through spatially aligned gels or liquids.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>RDC is used to provide 3D structural information on molecules.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>residual dipolar coupling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08223"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08330"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08407"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>RDC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08408 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08408">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy yielding through-space correlations via spin-spin relaxation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ROESY</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>cross-relaxation appropriate for minimolecules emulated by locked spins (CAMELPSIN)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>ROESY has the advantage over the NOESY experiment that its response does not go through a null at specific correlation times and is thus preferred for molecules having molecular masses of a few thousand \(\pu{Da}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>ROESY is similar to the NOESY experiment (see nuclear Overhauser effect spectroscopy) and is useful for determining which signals arise from protons that are close to each other in space even if they are not bonded.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>rotating-frame NOE spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08387"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08425"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08408"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ROESY</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08409 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08409">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy allowing recoupling of heteronuclear dipolar coupling under magic angle spinning.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>REDOR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>rotational echo double resonance</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08377"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08409"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>REDOR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08410 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08410">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy that allows detection of transient binding of small molecule ligands to macromolecular receptors.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>STD</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Range of applicable dissociation constants is approximately \(\pu{E-3 M}\) to \(\pu{E-8 M}\). The experiment relies on ligand-signal attenuation through spin diffusion saturation propagating from the macromolecule.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>saturation transfer difference spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08410"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>STD</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08411 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08411">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Spin decoupling of a specific resonance by selective irradiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>selective decoupling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08419"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08412 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08412">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Application of a frequency selective radiofrequency pulse(s) to a single resonance or a discrete band of frequencies.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>selective irradiation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Selective excitation is usually achieved through the application of long, low power (soft) pulses.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>selective excitation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08279"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08413 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08413">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Radiofrequency pulse with a narrow frequency spectral bandwidth (long, low-power pulse) to excite nuclei in a limited chemical shift range (see selective excitation).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>selective pulse</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08279"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08286"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08412"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08414 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08414">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy on a single crystal in a similar manner to that used in X-ray diffraction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A large crystal is mounted on a &apos;tenon&apos;, which is in turn mounted on a goniometer head. If the orientation of the unit cell is known with respect to the tenon, then it is possible to determine the orientation of the NMR interaction tensors with respect to the molecular frame.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>single crystal nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08415"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08415 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08415">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Application of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy to solids.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SSNMR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>SSNMR is characterized by the presence of anisotropic (directionally dependent) interactions. Chemical shift anisotropy (CSA) and internuclear dipolar coupling result in severe line-broadening of the corresponding NMR signals and specific hardware and methods are required to perform SSNMR to mitigate some of these issues including magic angle spinning (MAS).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08320"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08377"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08415"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SSNMR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08416 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08416">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy reduction or removal of large, unwanted resonances from solvents to improve spectrum quality and dynamic range through selective excitation and exploiting differences in relaxation times between solvent and analyte signals.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>solvent suppression</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08291"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08417 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08417">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Frequency range that describes the width of the spectrum window of a specific nucleus in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>spectral width</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08291"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08418 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08418">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Intrinsic angular momentum of a nucleus (or other sub-atomic particle).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>nuclear spin</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy, only nuclei with spin \(I \gt 0\) are observable.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Nuclei with a spin of \(½\) generate the simplest nuclear magnetic resonance spectra and are thus the most commonly studied.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The magnitude of nuclear spin is \((h/2\pi)\sqrt{I(I + 1)}\) where \(I\) is the spin quantum number and \(h\) is the Planck constant.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The spin of a nucleus is dependent on the numbers and alignments of the spins of its individual protons and neutrons.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>spin</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08419 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08419">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Irradiation of a nucleus in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy to prevent spin-spin coupling.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>spin decoupling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08351"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08365"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08420 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08420">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Refocusing of spin magnetization usually by two consecutive radiofrequency pulses.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Hahn echo</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>SE is typically used within a pulse sequence to refocus chemical shift by pulses \(\pu{90\!^{\circ}}\), \(T_{\rm{E}}/2\), \(\pu{180\!^{\circ}}\), \(T_{\rm{E}}/2\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The simplest form of the spin-echo pulse sequence consists of \(\pu{90\!^{\circ}}\ \rm{pulse}\), a \(\pu{180\!^{\circ}}\ \rm{pulse}\), and then an echo. The time between the middle of the first RF pulse and the peak of the spin echo is called the echo time (\(T_{\rm{E}}\)).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>spin echo</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08279"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08310"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08311"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08337"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08343"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08396"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08420"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08421 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08421">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Group of magnetic nuclei that interact amongst themselves through spin-spin coupling but do not interact with any nuclei outside the spin system.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>spin system</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08422 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08422">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mechanism in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy by which the component of the magnetization vector along the direction of the static magnetic induction reaches thermodynamic equilibrium with its surroundings through loss of energy of the excited nuclear spins to the surrounding molecular lattice. The resultant exponential decay of signal is characterized by the spin-lattice relaxation time \(T_{1}\) (reciprocal of the rate constant of this mechanism).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>$T1$ relaxation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>$T_1$ relaxation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>longitudinal relaxation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>\(T_{1}\) dictates the time required between individual NMR scans to ensure the system has returned to equilibrium.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>spin-lattice relaxation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08423"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08423 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08423">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reciprocal of the rate constant of longitudinal relaxation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>spin-lattice time constant</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>spin-lattice relaxation time</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08380"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08422"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08426"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08424 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08424">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Satellite peaks in a nuclear magnetic resonance spectrum symetrically spaced either side of a main peak at a frequency offset related to the rate of spinning of the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In solid state nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy this occurs if the sample is spun at a rate less than the magnitude of the anisotropic interaction.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>spinning sidebands</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08223"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08415"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08425 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08425">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Loss of magnetization in the \(xy\) plane (in the absence of \(\boldsymbol{B_{0}}\) inhomogeneities) through the interchange of energy between nuclear spins resulting in some precessing faster than the Larmor angular frequency and some slower.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>T2 relaxation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>transverse relaxation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The resultant exponential decay of signal is characterized by the spin-spin relaxation time \(T_{2}\) (reciprocal of the rate constant of this mechanism).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>spin-spin relaxation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08375"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08380"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08426"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08426 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08426">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reciprocal of the rate constant of spin-spin relaxation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>transverse relaxation time</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>spin-spin relaxation time</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08380"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08423"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08425"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08427 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08427">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Flux density of the magnetic field of a nuclear magnetic resonance spectrometer about whose direction the nuclear magnetic moment precesses.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>magnetic flux density</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>static magnetic field</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Static magnetic induction is related to the magnetic field \(\boldsymbol{H}\) through the equation \(\boldsymbol{B} = \mu \boldsymbol{H}\) where \(\mu\) is the permeability of the material.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The value of \(\boldsymbol{B_{0}}\) is expressed in Tesla or as the nominal proton precession frequency (e.g., \(\pu{14 T}\) or \(\pu{600 MHz}\)).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>static magnetic flux density</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08394"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08428 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08428">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Method of removing artefacts from a two-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectrum that are symmetrical about the diagonal (e.g., HH COSY) or the \(\rm{f}1\) axis (e.g., tilted J-resolved spectra). Values equidistant from midline are compared and replaced the lower (or average) value of the two.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>symmetrization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08323"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08373"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08439"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08429 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08429">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Time domain arising from the regular increments of the delay period in two-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>$t_1$ in 2-D NMR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>&lt;em&gt;t&lt;/em&gt;&lt;sub&gt;1&lt;/sub&gt;</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08433"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08439"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08430 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08430">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Time domain in two-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy arising from direct free induction decay detection.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>$t_2$ in 2-D NMR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>&lt;em&gt;t&lt;/em&gt;&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08249"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08433"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08439"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08431 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08431">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Streaks of spurious artefact signals in a two-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectrum parallel to the \(\rm{f}1\) axis at the \(\rm{f}2\) of a strong resonance.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>$t_1$ noise in 2-D NMR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>&lt;em&gt;t&lt;/em&gt;&lt;sub&gt;1&lt;/sub&gt; noise</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08439"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08432 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08432">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy in which data are collected in three time domains.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>3-D NMR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A 3-D NMR experiment may be constructed from a 2-D NMR experiment by inserting an additional indirect evolution time and a second mixing period between the first mixing period and the direct data acquisition.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08438"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08433 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08433">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Condition in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy where the independent variable of all functions is time.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>time domain</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08249"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08434 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08434">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy run at low magnetic field strengths where data analysis is performed directly on the free induction decay, often through the extraction of relaxation time constants</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>TD-NMR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>time domain nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08249"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08434"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>TD-NMR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08435 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08435">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Two-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance homonuclear correlation experiment, which creates correlations between all protons within a given spin system, not just between geminal or vicinal protons as in COSY.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>TOCSY</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>homonuclear Hartmann-Hahn spectroscopy (HOHAHA)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>TOCSY uses a spin lock to allow propagation of magnetization through scalar couplings.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>total correlation spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08323"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08421"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08439"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08435"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>TOCSY</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08436 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08436">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Technique to suppress spinning sidebands in solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy with cross polarization in magic angle spinning experiments.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>TOSS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>total suppression of spinning sidebands</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08327"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08377"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08415"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08436"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>TOSS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08437 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08437">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy, usually in protein NMR, that improves peak sharpness for molecules having a molecular mass of greater than \(\pu{100 kDa}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>TROSY</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>TROSY relies on the cancellation of the dipolar coupling and chemical shift anisotropy (CSA) components of the transverse relaxation (see spin-spin relaxation).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>transverse relaxation optimized spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08320"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08425"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08437"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>TROSY</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08438 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08438">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy using three NMR-active nuclei.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>triple-resonance NMR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>For proteins the three nuclei are usually \(\ce{^{1}H}\) hydrogen, \(\ce{^{13}C}\) carbon, and \(\ce{^{15}N}\) nitrogen, which requires suitable labelling of the sample.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>triple-resonance nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08439 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08439">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy in which data are collected in two time domains: acquisition of the free induction decay (\(t_{2}\)) and a successively incremented delay (\(t_{1}\)).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>2-D NMR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The resulting data matrix is subjected to two successive Fourier transforms to produce a spectrum with two frequency axes, usually either chemical shift/chemical shift (correlation spectroscopy) or chemical shift/J-coupling (see J-resolved spectroscopy).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>two-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08249"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08373"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08433"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08440 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08440">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Cluster of pulses applied repeatedly for heteronuclear decoupling.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>WALTZ decoupling</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>WALTZ is commonly used for proton decoupling during the acquisition of \(\ce{^{13}C}\) spectra.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>wideband, alternating-phase, low-power technique for residual splitting</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08351"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08441 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08441">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Energy levels of a nucleus arising from the interaction of its magnetic dipole with an external magnetic field.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>Zeeman levels</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08246"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08442 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08442">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy in which nuclear magnetic resonance spectra are acquired in an environment carefully screened from magnetic fields, including from the Earth’s field allowing the direct detection of J-spectra in the absence of Zeeman interactions (see Zeeman levels).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>zero-field NMR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>zero-field nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08218"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08384"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08441"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08443 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08443">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Bombardment of a sample surface with ions resulting in the removal of atoms from their lattice sites, and thus an ongoing discharge process that causes continuous ablation of the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>ablation by sputtering</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08444 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08444">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mass of sample removed in laser-induced breakdown spectroscopy divided by the energy delivered by the laser beam.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>ablation efficiency</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08500"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08445 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08445">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Depth of sample layer removed in the glow discharge process per unit time.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Ablation rate is usually expressed in \(\pu{nm s-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>ablation rate</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08446 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08446">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Minimum fluence required to achieve ablation of the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>ablation threshold fluence</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08447 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08447">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Increase in signal equilibration time or in wash out time caused by a modification in inorganic acid concentration.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>acid transient effects</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08448 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08448">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Gases added to a combustion mixture.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>An inert diluent is non-reactive, an auxiliary gas is reactive.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>additional gases</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08449 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08449">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Processes occurring inside the spray chamber or desolvation system responsible for the modification of the primary aerosol to finally yield the tertiary aerosol.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>These phenomena include solvent evaporation, droplet coalescence or coagulation, inertial droplet losses, gravitational settling and turbulence losses.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>aerosol transport phenomena</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08529"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08551"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08450 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08450">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mass of analyte reaching the plasma per unit time.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>analyte transport rate</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08451 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08451">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of atomic spectroscopy for measuring the amount of a chemical element based on the measurement of the absorption of characteristic electromagnetic radiation by atoms in the vapour phase.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>AAS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>atomic absorption spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08216"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08451"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>AAS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08452 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08452">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of atomic spectroscopy for measuring the amount of a chemical element based on the measurement of the intensity of characteristic electromagnetic radiation emitted by atoms or molecules.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>AES</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>atomic emission spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08216"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08452"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>AES</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08453 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08453">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Atomizer intended to convert free atoms to an excited state.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>excitation source</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>atomic excitation source</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08454 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08454">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of atomic spectroscopy for measuring the amount of a chemical element based on the measurement of the re-emission of characteristic electromagnetic radiation by atoms, following the absorption of radiation in the vapour phase.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>AFS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The wavelengths of the absorbed and re-emitted radiation may be identical (atomic resonance fluorescence spectroscopy) or different.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>atomic fluorescence spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08216"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08454"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>AFS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08455 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08455">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Vapour containing free atoms of analyte.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>atomic vapor</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>atomic vapour</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08456 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08456">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Graph of absorbance against atomization temperature.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>atomization curve</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08457 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08457">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Inductively-coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer configuration in which the entrance slit is aligned with the main plasma axis.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Matrix interferences, which originate in the cooler plasma tail, can degrade precision and accuracy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The large amount of light captured in this method, includes information from the sample of interest plus background, which can be considerable.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>axial viewing mode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08491"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08458 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08458">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Intense continuous background usually observed in laser-induced breakdown spectra.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The continuum emission originates from Bremsstrahlung radiation, which predominates the first part of the plasma life time and decreases with further plasma evolution.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>background continuum emission</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08501"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08459 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08459">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Concentration of a given element measured at a given wavelength providing the same intensity as the background.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>background equivalent concentration</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08300"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08460 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08460">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Minimum fluence required to achieve measurable emission signals</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Usually the breakdown-threshold fluence is orders of magnitude higher than the ablation-threshold fluence.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>breakdown threshold fluence</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08446"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08461 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08461">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Plasma generated by a capacitor in presence of a microwave field.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>capacitive microwave plasma</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08462 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08462">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Spectral line of an atom used for the measurement of analyte concentration by atomic spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Characteristic lines include resonance and non-resonance lines.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>characteristic line</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08216"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08550"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08463 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08463">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Source that emits electromagnetic radiation with relatively constant intensity over a broad spectral region.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The use of such a source allows for correction of spectral interferences.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>continuum source</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08464 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08464">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process by which the sample is sputtered &apos;atomic layer by layer&apos; using suitable conditions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Depth profile analysis can be accomplished by time resolved measurement of the generated emission signals.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Depth profiling using signal versus time measurement requires knowledge of the ablation rate, which must be known, or has to be calculated, from measurements of suitable materials.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>depth-profiling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08445"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08465 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08465">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method using optical emission spectroscopy in which atomization and excitation occur in a glow electric discharge sustained by a direct current electric field.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DC GD-OES</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>DC GD-OES is only applicable for electrically conducting samples.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Generated argon ions impact with high energy on to the surface of the cathode, which can thus be ablated in a purely mechanical way (cathodic sputtering) or the cathode can be heated to assist evaporation (thermal volatilization).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>direct current glow discharge optical emission spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08482"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08513"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08465"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>DC GD-OES</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08466 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08466">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Conversion of the whole or part of a liquid or solid sample into a physical form sufficiently finely divided to allow it to be atomized (see atomization) upon introduction into the atomizer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>dispersion of a sample</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08467 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08467">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of laser-induced breakdown spectroscopy in which reactions are induced by the interaction of the evolved plasma and the remaining part of the laser pulse, or with a following laser pulse provided from a second laser system.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>double-pulse laser-induced breakdown spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08500"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08468 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08468">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Inductively-coupled plasma spectrometer that allows axial viewing mode or radial viewing mode.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>dual viewing mode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08489"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08469 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08469">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Transmission of electrical current by a plasma in an applied electric field through a normally non-conducting medium.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>discharge</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>An inductively-coupled plasma is a kind of electric discharge.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electric discharge</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08488"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08470 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08470">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of free electrons in vapour phase per volume unit.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{m-3}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electron number density</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08471 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08471">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of atomic absorption spectroscopy in which the sample is atomized by an electrothermal atomizer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ETAAS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>electrothermal atomic absorption spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08451"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08471"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ETAAS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08472 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08472">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Technique for sample introduction based on the use of a solid furnace heated at a controlled temperature to vaporize the sample prior to its introduction into an inductively-coupled plasma.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>electrothermal vaporization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08488"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08473 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08473">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Energy that must be supplied to an atom to transfer it from the ground atomic state to a given ionic excited state.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>&lt;em&gt;E&lt;/em&gt;&lt;sub&gt;sum&lt;/sub&gt;</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08474 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08474">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Spectrometer to make measurements by flame atomic spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>flame atomic spectrometer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08475"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08475 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08475">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of atomic spectroscopy that uses a flame to excite atoms.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>flame atomic spectrometry</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>flame atomic spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08216"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08476"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08476 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08476">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Continuously flowing mixture of hot gases with a stationary position that derives its heat content from a strongly exothermic, irreversible chemical reaction between a fuel and an oxidant.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The flame in general consists of a primary-combustion zone, a secondary-combustion zone and an interconal zone.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>flame</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08477 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08477">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Component of a flame atomic spectrometer for creating turbulence in the stream of mist in the spray chamber and removing the largest droplets from this mist by deposition.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>flow spoiler</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08474"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08478 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08478">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Time between the application of a laser pulse and the start of detection in laser-induced breakdown spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>time delay in laser induced breakdown spectroscopy</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The gate delay varies from some hundreds of nanoseconds to several microseconds.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Use of a suitable gate delay allows the high intensity background continuum emission present in the early stages of plasma formation to be removed.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>gate delay</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08500"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08479 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08479">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Spectrometers for glow discharge optical emission spectroscopy that allow simultaneous monitoring of selected spectral ranges or the whole emission spectra.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>GD-OES spectrometers mostly use the so called Paschen–Runge mounting, where the entrance slit, the curved grating and different detectors are aligned along the Rowland circle. Alternatively, Echelle spectrometers (see GD-OES spectrometers with simultaneous detection) are used, which use an Echelle grating in combination with a prism for wavelength separation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>GD-OES spectrometers with simultaneous detection</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08483"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08480 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08480">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Fraction of the energy generated being used for creating and maintaining the plasma in an inductively-coupled plasma spectrometer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>generator coupling efficiency</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08489"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08481 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08481">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Component of an atomic emission spectrometer employing a glow electric discharge used in atomic emission spectroscopy for qualitative and quantitative analysis of solid materials.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Glow discharge allows volatilization as well as excitation of analytes and sample matrices.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>glow discharge source</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08452"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08482"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08482 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08482">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electric discharge in gas at low pressure (\(\pu{100}\) to \(\pu{1000 Pa}\)).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>glow discharge</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In atomic emission spectroscopy argon is used as the inert working gas that generates the plasma.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In the prevailing electrical field the produced ions are accelerated towards the cathode, resulting in a continuous bombardment of the sample surface.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>glow electric discharge</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08452"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08469"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08483 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08483">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Optical emission spectroscopy in which a glow electric discharge excites atoms at the surface of a sample, which is made the cathode of the discharge.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>GD-OES</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>glow-discharge optical emission spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08465"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08482"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08513"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08537"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08483"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GD-OES</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08484 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08484">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Cathode for a glow electric discharge consisting of a flat sample, which is cooled during the discharge process so that thermal volatilization is suppressed and the sample material is removed by sputtering only.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>Grimm-type cathode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08482"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08557"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08485 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08485">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Glow discharge source with a graphite cathode, into which the sample is inserted in the form of drillings, a pressed pellet or a dry solution residue.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For this source, sample volatilization takes place mainly by thermal effects.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>hollow-cathode discharge source</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08481"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08486 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08486">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Glow discharge source of spectral lines of an atom in which the cathode is made of the desired element.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>line source</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>It is often referred to as line source, as opposed to a continuum source.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>hollow-cathode lamp</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08463"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08481"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08550"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08487 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08487">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Power supplied by a generator to the plasma.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>incident power</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08488 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08488">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Plasma produced by induction by means of a high-frequency (about \(\pu{2500 Hz}\)) electromagnetic field. The region of gas at high temperature and free from the field is taken as the region of observation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ICP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>inductively-coupled plasma</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08490"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08491"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08488"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ICP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08489 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08489">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of atomic spectroscopy that uses an inductively-coupled plasma to excite and ionize atoms.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ICP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>inductively-coupled plasma spectroscopy</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>ICP methods are described by their method of detection of ions in the plasma and include inductively-coupled plasma optical emission spectroscopy, and inductively-coupled plasma mass spectrometry.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>inductively-coupled plasma spectrometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08216"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08488"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08490"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08491"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08489"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ICP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08490 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08490">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of atomic spectrometry that uses an inductively-coupled plasma to excite and ionize atoms with measurement of the number and kind of ions in the plasma using mass spectrometry.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ICP-MS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>inductively-coupled plasma mass spectrometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08216"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08488"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08490"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ICP-MS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08491 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08491">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of atomic spectroscopy that uses an inductively-coupled plasma to excite and ionize atoms with measurement of the number and kind of ions in the plasma from their emission of electromagnetic radiation (see optical emission spectroscopy).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ICP-OES</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>inductively-coupled plasma optical emission spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08216"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08488"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08513"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08491"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ICP-OES</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08492 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08492">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ability of an inductively-coupled plasma to withstand slight modification in the operating conditions and/or matrix composition with no significant changes in its fundamental characteristics (i.e., temperatures, electron number density) or the analytical figures of merit (i.e., sensitivity, detection limits or signal-to-noise ratio)</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>plasma robustness</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>inductively-coupled plasma robustness</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08282"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08488"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08493 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08493">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Decrease in the inductively-coupled plasma volume normally caused when an organic volatile solution is delivered to it. Organic vapours diffuse towards the outermost area of the plasma and its volume decreases.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>inductively-coupled plasma thermal pinch</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08488"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08494 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08494">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Quartz component of an inductively-coupled plasma spectrometer where the plasma is generated and stabilized.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In ICP the torch is an open three concentric tubes assembly allowing the introduction of the three gas streams constituting the plasma. The inner tube of the torch can be made of a different material such as alumina and is usually called the injector.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>inductively-coupled plasma torch</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08489"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08495 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08495">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Inductively-coupled plasma volume in inductively-coupled plasma optical emission spectroscopy where the analytically-relevant light emission begins to be observed.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>initial plasma radiation zone</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08488"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08491"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08496 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08496">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Graph of absorbance or intensity plotted as a function of the concentration of an interfering element in an atomic spectroscopy experiment.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>interference curve</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08216"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08497 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08497">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Absorption of photon energy by free electrons.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Inverse Bremsstrahlung increases the kinetic energy of the electrons, which is important for heating the plasma.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>inverse Bremsstrahlung</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08498 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08498">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Gas phase containing free ions of analyte.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>ionic cloud</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08499 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08499">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Removal of material as an aerosol from a surface on irradiation by a laser beam.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>laser ablation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08500 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08500">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of atomic spectroscopy that uses a focused laser beam to ablate, atomize and excite atoms from a sample surface with subsequent measurement of the generated electromagnetic radiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>LIBS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>laser induced breakdown spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08216"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08500"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>LIBS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08501 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08501">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Emission spectrum generated as a consequence of the decay of the excited states generated in a laser-induced plasma that consists of a continuous background and element-specific atomic or ionic lines and molecular bands.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>LIBS spectra cover a wide wavelength range. Commonly, LIBS measurements are taken between the wavelengths of \(\pu{200 nm}\) and \(\pu{1000 nm}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>laser-induced breakdown spectrum</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08291"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08300"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08502"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08502 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08502">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Local plasma formed by the interaction between a laser beam and a solid sample and initiated by the production of primary electrons by multiphoton ionization and thermionic photoemission mechanisms.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>As well as electrons, laser-induced plasma contains ions, and neutral atoms as well as excited species of the ablated matter. Decay of these excited species generates the light emission analysed in laser-induced breakdown spectroscopy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>laser-induced plasma</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08500"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08503 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08503">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Increase in the theoretical width of a spectral line owing to thermal motion of emitting atoms (Doppler effect), to electric field (Stark effect), to self-absorption and to pressure (Lorentz effect).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Line-broadening increases the measurement uncertainty of a measurement.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>line-broadening</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08550"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08504 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08504">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Graph of the variation of emitted radiation intensity as a function of wavelength (emission line) or the variation of the absorption factor as a function of wavelength (absorption line).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>line profile</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08300"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08505 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08505">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Liquid-sample introduction system to an inductively-coupled plasma spectrometer adapted to work at sample flow rates below \(\pu{100 \upmu L min-1}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The system is normally composed of a micro-nebulizer and a low inner volume spray chamber.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>low sample consumption system</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08489"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08510"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08506 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08506">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of detector events per atom in the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>measurement efficiency</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08507 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08507">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Resonant cavity that permits focusing a microwave field inside a discharge tube thus giving rise to a standing wave.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>microwave cavity</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08508 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08508">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Plasma generated in a microwave cavity.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>microwave plasma</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The plasma results as a consequence of ionization of a given gas in presence of electrons and a microwave field.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>microwave-induced plasma</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08507"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08509 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08509">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Conversion of a liquid into an aerosol consisting of droplets suspended in a gas stream.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>nebulization</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08510 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08510">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Device for producing nebulization.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>nebulizer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08509"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08511 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08511">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electromagnetic radiation, within the bandpass filter used, emitted by, or absorption caused by, atoms, molecules and radicals that are subject to the atomizer by all components, apart from the analyte, that are present there during measurement.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>These effects include scattering or absorption effects by solid particles.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>non-specific emission or attenuation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08512 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08512">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Inductively-coupled plasma volume mainly responsible for emission of analytically-useful light because processes leading to excited species, such as droplet desolvation, element vaporization, atomization (or ionization) and excitation have been completed.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>normal plasma analytical zone</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08488"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08513 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08513">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement principle of atomic emission spectroscopy in which the emitted electromagnetic radiation is in the range of frequencies from the infrared, through visible, to the ultra-violet region.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>OES</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>OES is used as the detector in inductively-coupled plasma optical emission spectroscopy, and glow-discharge optical emission spectroscopy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>optical emission spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08452"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08483"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08491"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08513"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>OES</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08514 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08514">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In atomic spectroscopy, mass of analyte converted into free atoms in the atomizer divided by mass of analyte entering the dispersion device.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>overall efficiency of atomization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08216"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08515 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08515">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Collisional excitation and ionization mechanism of inductively-coupled plasma spectrometry involving analyte atoms and metastable argon atoms.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Penning ionization</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>Penning excitation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08489"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08516 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08516">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In atomic spectroscopy, flux of mass of sample divided by the flux of mass of solvent blank, measured under the same conditions, expressed as a percentage.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>percentage transmission</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08216"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08517 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08517">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Substance added together with the sample or standards in electrothermal atomic absorption spectroscopy that is refractory, or forms a refractory species (e.g., a carbide), and thus does not need to be renewed for several atomization cycles.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>This is a particular type of chemical modifier for electrothermal AAS.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>permanent modifier</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08471"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08518 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08518">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Plasma formed by an arc-discharge.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The discharge is blown through a suitable orifice to form a plasma jet. The high-temperature, electric field-free gaseous region is designed as the region of observation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>plasma arc</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08469"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08519 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08519">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Temperature governing the number density of atomic species in their ground state and excited states following a Boltzmann distribution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>plasma excitation temperature</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08520 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08520">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Gas streams used to maintain and stabilize the inductively-coupled plasma. The external stream is the main constituent of the plasma and confines the plasma avoiding melting of the torch walls; the intermediate gas stream keeps the plasma at a given height above the top of the torch and, the central stream carries the aerosol and promotes its injection in the plasma central channel.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>plasma gases</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08488"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08521 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08521">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Inductively-coupled plasma volume where there is a maximum interaction between the electromagnetic field and the plasma gas (mostly argon). This is the plasma’s hottest area and has a toroidal configuration.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>plasma induction zone</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08488"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08522 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08522">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Temperature associated with the energy level of plasma ions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>plasma ionization temperature</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08246"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08523 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08523">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Condition for which the temperatures estimated from different particles (atoms, molecules, ions, electrons) constituting a plasma are locally coincident.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>LTE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>plasma local thermodynamic equilibrium</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08523"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>LTE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08524 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08524">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Prevention of laser pulse energy from reaching a sample surface as a result of the presence of a laser-induced plasma.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The main process leading to plasma shielding is the absorption of the laser energy by electrons (inverse Bremsstrahlung) and multiphoton ionization (mainly relevant for shorter laser wavelengths).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>plasma shielding</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08497"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08502"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08525 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08525">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extinction of the plasma after a laser pulse as a consequence of self-absorption (quenching) and recombination of electrons and ions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The time elapsed between initiation and extinction of the plasma ranges from tenths of microseconds to a few milliseconds.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>plasma termination</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08526 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08526">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sample support made of graphite that is inserted into the atomizer tube in electrothermal atomic absorption spectroscopy. The sample, a liquid or a solid, is deposited onto this platform, from which it undergoes all the transformations leading to atomization. The use of a platform delays atomization, until a more stabilized temperature in the gas phase is reached.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>L’vov platform</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>platform atomization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08471"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08527 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08527">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nebulizers for which the aerosol generation principle is based on the exposure of the liquid sample to a high velocity gas stream.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Most common designs include cross flow, concentric, parallel path, entrained and Babington and V Groove.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>pneumatic nebulizers</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08510"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08528 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08528">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Inductively-coupled plasma volume where tertiary aerosols suffer from complete solvent evaporation, element salt vaporization and dissociation into atoms.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>preheating plasma zone</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>preheating inductively-coupled plasma zone</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08488"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08529 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08529">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Aerosol generated by a nebulizer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Usually, primary aerosols contain coarse droplets, they are polydisperse in terms of drop diameters and they are turbulent.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>primary aerosol</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08510"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08531 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08531">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Total optical energy content of a laser pulse. Integral of optical power over pulse duration.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Pulse energies range from microjoules to millijoules for Q-switched lasers, whereas mode-locked lasers achieve much lower pulse energies (picojoules, nanojoules or sometimes several microjoules).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>pulse energy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08530"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08532 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08532">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Laser system that emits electromagnetic radiation in the form of pulses of a defined and constant duration.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A pulsed laser produces no continuous optical wave.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>pulsed laser</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08533 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08533">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Graph of absorbance against pyrolysis temperature in electrothermal atomic absorption spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>pyrolysis curve</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08471"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08534 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08534">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Atomizer that is used to atomize those elements that form volatile species (e.g., hydrides) at relatively low temperatures (e.g., \(\pu{1000^{\circ}C}\) or below).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>These can be heated externally or positioned in a flame (see flame in flame atomic spectroscopy).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>quartz atomizer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08476"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08535 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08535">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Inductively-coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer configuration in which the plasma and the entrance slit are perpendicular and from which the signal is taken perpendicularly to the main plasma axis.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>This configuration yields lower sensitivities than the axial viewing mode.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>radial viewing mode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08457"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08491"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08536 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08536">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Component of an inductively-coupled plasma spectrometer responsible for the generation of alternating current in the radiofrequency portion of the electromagnetic spectrum (see table).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RF generator</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>radiofrequency generator</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08279"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08489"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08537 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08537">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Optical emission spectroscopy in which a radiofrequency-powered glow discharge is operated with frequencies in the low megahertz range.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RF-GD-OES</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The use of these frequencies establishes a negative direct current (DC)-bias voltage on the sample surface. The DC-bias is the result of an alternating current waveform that is centered about negative potential; as such it more or less represents the average potential residing on the sample surface. Radio-frequency has ability to appear to flow through insulators (non-conductive materials).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>radiofrequency glow discharge optical emission spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08481"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08513"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08537"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>RF-GD-OES</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08538 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08538">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Intensity transmitted by the reference medium in a double-beam spectrometer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>reference flux density</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08241"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08539 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08539">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Incident power that is not absorbed by the plasma.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>reflected power</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08487"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08540 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08540">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of laser pulses emitted by a pulsed laser system per unit time.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>pulse frequency</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>For laser systems with pulse duration in the nanosecond regime repetition rates range from \(\pu{1 Hz}\) to \(\pu{20 Hz}\), whereas for femtosecond laser systems increased repetition rates up to \(\pu{1000 Hz}\) are possible.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{Hz}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>repetition rate</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08530"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08532"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08541 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08541">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of inelastic X-ray spectroscopy that uses a hard or soft X-ray beam to excite transitions of core-level electrons into empty energy levels. The subsequent transition of an electron into the core state is accompanied by X-ray emission with characteristic momentum and energy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RIXS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>resonant X-ray Raman</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>resonant X-ray emission</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The incident photon energy is selected for resonance with an X-ray absorption edge of the system, such that the observed shifts in momentum and energy provide element-specific chemical sensitivity, with the capacity to distinguish the same element located at inequivalent sites.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>resonant inelastic X-ray scattering</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08246"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08541"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>RIXS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08542 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08542">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Equation that relates the ionization state of an element present in a gas in thermal equilibrium to the temperature and the pressure of the medium.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Saha–Langmuir equation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>For a gas composed of a single atomic species, the Saha equation is written: \[(n_{i+1}/n_{i}) n_{\rm{e}} = 2\lambda^{-3}(g_{i+1}/g_{i}) \exp[-(\epsilon_{i + 1} - \epsilon_{i})/kT]\] where \(n_{i}\) is the number density of atoms with \(i\) electrons removed, \(g_{i}\) is the degeneracy of the \(i^{\rm{th}}\) state, \(\epsilon_{i}\) is the ionization energy of the \(i^{\rm{th}}\) state, \(n_{\rm{e}}\) is the number density of electrons, \(\lambda\) is the thermal de Broglie wavelength of an electron, \(k\) is the Boltzmann constant, and \(T\) is the thermodynamic temperature of the gas.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The thermal de Broglie wavelength is given by: \[\lambda = \sqrt{\frac{h^{2}}{2\uppi m_{\rm{e}} kT}}\] where \(h\) is the Planck constant, \(k\) the Boltzmann constant, \(T\) temperature, and \(m_{\rm{e}}\) the mass of an electron.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Saha’s ionization equation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08543 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08543">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Intensity transmitted by the atomizer when the latter is supplied with the sample solution or a reference solution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>sample flux density</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08538"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08544 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08544">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Buffer containing interfering element(s) in sufficient quantity to reach the limit of enhancement or dispersion (i.e., saturation) of the interference curve.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>saturator</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08545 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08545">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sequential spectrometers use a dispersive element (grating) in combination with entrance and exit slits for selection of specific wavelengths. Geometric arrangements include the Czerny-Turner and the Ebert-geometry, both offering high resolution measurements. However, they do not permit simultaneous recording of different spectral lines, even for single emission line intensity, and adjacent spectral background has also to be measured sequentially.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>sequential detection</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08549"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08550"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08546 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08546">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Intensity transmitted by the atomizer when the latter is supplied with the solvent blank.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>solvent blank flux density</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08547 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08547">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Solvent condensation on solid particles or droplets contained in an aerosol.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>solvent nucleation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08548 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08548">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mass of solvent, both in liquid and vapour forms, reaching an inductively-coupled plasma per unit of time.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>solvent transport rate</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08488"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08549 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08549">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electromagnetic radiation, within the bandpass used, emitted by, and/or in the case of atomic absorption spectroscopy, absorbed in, the atomizer when nothing is supplied to the atomizer but the gases involved in its operation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>flame background</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In the case of flame atomic spectroscopy, the term &quot;flame background&quot; is used.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>spectral background</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08451"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08475"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08550 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08550">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In atomic spectroscopy, a very narrow band of frequencies of electromagnetic radiation emitted or absorbed by atoms, which undergo a single electronic transition.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>line</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>spectral line</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A distinction is made between lines corresponding to transition of neutral atoms (e.g., \(\ce{Ba I}\ \pu{553.548 nm}\) and \(\pu{577.762 nm}\)) and ions (e.g., \(\ce{Ba II}\ \pu{455.403 nm}\)).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The radiation is centred on a peak whose wavelength characterizes the line and which corresponds to the emission or absorption maximum.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>spectral line</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08216"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08245"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08300"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08551 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08551">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chamber of a nebulizer in which the sprayed liquid is converted to mist. Some of the droplets of this mist may evaporate, coalesce, or deposit in the chamber and subsequently drain away as waste.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>spray chamber</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08510"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08552 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08552">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mass of material removed from a sample surface in unit time.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Si unit \(\pu{kg s-1}\). Common unit \(\pu{\upmu g s-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sputter rate</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08553 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08553">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Stoppage to the otherwise constant flow of argon gas to the atomizer in electrothermal atomic absorption spectroscopy, that can be programmed to occur during atomization, for the purpose of increasing the residence time of the analyte atoms and thus the associated signal.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>stop-flow conditions</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08471"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08554 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08554">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>X-ray spectroscopy performed using a high intensity, continuous polychromatic synchrotron X-ray source.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>synchrotron X-ray spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08555 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08555">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Aerosol that reaches an inductively-coupled plasma.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Such aerosols are finer, less polydispersed and less turbulent than primary aerosols.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>tertiary aerosol</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08488"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08529"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08556 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08556">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Procedures whereby an aerosol is produced at a high temperature, for example by sparks, arcs, furnaces, lasers, cathodic sputtering or electron-beam.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>thermal dispersion</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08557 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08557">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process in glow-discharge atomic emission spectroscopy in which sample volatilization occurs by heating due to sample bombardment with argon ions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Although this technique offers highest detection power for most volatile elements its use in routine applications is still limited by the analytical challenges posed by the transient nature of the analyte signal.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The process causes evaporation of the sample constituents in accordance to their boiling-points.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>thermal volatilization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08483"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08558 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08558">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of laser-induced breakdown spectroscopy in which the emitted radiation is monitored with time resolution sufficiently high to resolve different stages in the lifetime of the laser-induced plasma.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>time-resolved laser-induced breakdown spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08500"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08502"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08559 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08559">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Liquid-sample introduction system able to achieve \(\pu{100\%}\) transport efficiency.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>total sample consumption system</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08560 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08560">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mass of analyte entering the atomizer of a flame atomic spectrometer or the plasma of an inductively-coupled plasma spectrometer divided by the mass of analyte entering the dispersion device.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>transport efficiency</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08474"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08561 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08561">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nebulizers whose aerosol generation principle is based on the transfer of the energy from a vibrating transducer to the liquid sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>ultrasonic nebulizers</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08510"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08562 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08562">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrothermal atomic absorption spectroscopy in which the sample is deposited directly onto the wall of the electrothermal atomizer tube, from which it undergoes all the transformations leading to atomization.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>wall atomization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08471"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08563 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08563">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Time required for the output signal of an inductively-coupled plasma spectrometer for a given analyte to fall back to baseline levels from the end of sampling time and/or after introducing a blank solution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>wash out time</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08489"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08564 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08564">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Spectrum comprising the continuum Bremsstrahlung and the most energetic characteristic spectral lines defined by differences in binding energy between electron energy levels.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>X-ray emission spectrum</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08246"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08291"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08550"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08565 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08565">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Amorphous glasses made from elements of groups 4, 5 and 6 of the Periodic Table that transmit infrared radiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>AMTIR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>AMTIRs are hard but can be brittle.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>amorphous material transmitting infrared radiation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08617"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08565"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>AMTIR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08566 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08566">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Modulation of the amplitude of a beam of electromagnetic radiation; by the movement of a moving mirror in a rapid scan Fourier-transform spectrometer or by a mechanical chopper in other spectrometers.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>amplitude modulation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08567 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08567">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Raman scattering by the anisotropic part of the derived polarizability tensor.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>anisotropic Raman scattering</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08667"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08568 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08568">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Changes in real refractive index of a material in the vicinity of an absorption band in which real refractive index decreases markedly with decreasing wavenumber on the high wavenumber side of an absorption band, then increases rapidly through the band centre and decreases markedly on the low wavenumber side of the band, before resuming normal dispersion at wavenumbers far from the band.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>anomalous dispersion</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08646"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08569 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08569">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Raman band with a depolarization ratio greater than \(\pu{0.75}\), as is frequently observed in surface-enhanced resonance Raman spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>anomalously polarized Raman band</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08586"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08665"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08690"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08570 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08570">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Raman scattering of electromagnetic radiation in which the scattered radiation has greater energy (greater wavenumber) than the exciting radiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>anti-Stokes Raman scattering</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08667"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08683"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08571 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08571">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Rotor whose three principal moments of inertia are all different.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>asymmetric top</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08693"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08572 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08572">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>3 × 3 tensor that gives the change in the components of the molecular dipole moment when the atom is displaced in three-dimensional space.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>APT</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>APTs have been widely used in the analysis of infrared absorption intensities.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{C}\). Common unit: \(\pu{D Å-1 \approx 3.33564E-30 C}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The rows correspond to different components of the dipole moment and the columns to \(x\), \(y\ (\rm{g})\) and \(z\) displacements.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>atomic polar tensor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08618"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08572"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>APT</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08573 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08573">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of vibrational spectroscopy based on internal reflection from an absorbing material at angles of incidence at or above the critical angle (attenuated total reflection).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>frustrated total internal reflection spectroscopy</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>internal reflection spectroscopy (IRS)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Attenuated total reflection occurs where the material absorbs. The resulting spectrum resembles an absorption spectrum of the sample.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>attenuated total reflection spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08227"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08623"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08708"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08574 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08574">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Description of the profile of a spectral band.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For a given band, the band shape is independent of the band height in Raman spectra, infrared absorption spectra and pATR spectra. The band shape changes if the band height changes in infrared transmission spectra or attenuated total reflection spectra.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>band shape</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08227"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08285"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08657"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08670"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08575 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08575">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Optical filter used, for example, to attenuate Rayleigh scattering while transmitting light subject to Raman shift.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Historically, a Bragg filter was a colloidal dispersion of spheres in a regular close packed array, which selectively diffracts/reflects wavelengths that satisfy the Bragg diffraction criterion for the lattice but transmits all other wavelengths. Now largely superseded by Volume Bragg Gratings, where photo-induced modulation of the refractive index of the filter medium is used to form a volume phase hologram with reflecting planes separated by a well-defined spacing. In each case, angle tuning is used to match the laser wavelength to the Bragg diffraction condition. The filter can be manufactured either as a notch filter, which transmits wavelengths either side of the laser line, or as an edge filter, which only transmits wavelengths longer than the laser line (i.e., Raman light subject to Stokes scattering).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Bragg filter</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08300"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08668"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08683"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08576 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08576">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Spectral region of an interferogram around zero-path-difference where intensity is greatest.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>center burst</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>centre burst</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08622"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08577 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08577">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Collections of solid particles that are transparent to electromagnetic radiation of a particular wavelength and are slightly larger than the wavelength of the radiation transmit poorly due to reflection and scattering from the interfaces. The transmission increases markedly at wavenumbers at which the refractive index of the particles is close to that of the surrounding medium.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The Christiansen effect frequently causes strong absorption bands from coarsely ground particles to be distorted due to the change in refractive index associated with the anomalous dispersion of the refractive index through the absorption region.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Christiansen effect</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08281"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08300"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08578 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08578">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Optical filter based on the Christiansen effect.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Such filters have been made from quartz particles in air, immersed in \(\ce{CCl4}\), or immersed in mixtures of \(\ce{CCl4}\) and \(\ce{CS2}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Christiansen filter</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08577"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08579 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08579">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of Raman spectroscopy that uses third-order susceptibility and is one of several four-wave mixing spectroscopies. The excitation is through lasers of wavenumber \(\tilde{ u}_{1}\) and \(\tilde{ u}_{2}\), which coincide spatially and temporally in the sample and produce an output laser of wavenumber \(\tilde{ u}_{3} = 2\tilde{ u}_{1} - \tilde{ u}_{2}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CARS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Experimentally, \(\tilde{\nu}_{1}\) is kept constant and \(\tilde{\nu}_{2}\) is scanned. A laser beam output is observed at the wavenumber for anti-Stokes Raman scattering \(\tilde{\nu}_{1} + \tilde{\nu}_{\rm{M}}\) when \(\tilde{\nu}_{1} - \tilde{\nu}_{2} = \tilde{\nu}_{\rm{M}}\), where \(\tilde{\nu}_{\rm{M}}\) is the wavenumber of an active vibration in the sample.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The Raman scattered radiation emerges as a laser beam instead of being scattered into three dimensions, thus greatly enhancing the sensitivity over that in normal Raman scattering.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>coherent anti-Stokes Raman spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08570"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08667"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08669"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08579"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CARS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08580 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08580">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Transition in which more than one vibration changes its degree of excitation; i.e., in which more than one vibrational quantum number changes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Combination band results from a combination transition.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Combination transitions are either sum or difference transitions, although some authors restrict them to sum transitions.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>combination transition</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08581 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08581">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Interferometer in which the optical path difference is continually changed.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Continuous scan interferometer is typically used for Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In rapid-scan interferometers, the rate of change of optical path difference is above \(\pu{0.05 cm s-1}\), high enough that no additional modulation is needed.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In slow scan interferometers, the rate of change of optical path difference is less than \(\pu{0.05 cm s-1}\) and a mechanical chopper or some other ancillary modulation is frequently used to provide an adequate modulation frequency.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>continuous scan interferometer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08605"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08653"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08681"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08582 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08582">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coupling between rotational and vibrational molecular motion caused by Coriolis forces.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>Coriolis coupling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08583"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08583 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08583">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In vibrational theory, the motion of a polyatomic molecule is usually initially regarded as the independent superposition of a rotation and a vibration, with the vibration considered in a coordinate system that rotates with the molecule. This initial treatment must be corrected for centrifugal forces and for Coriolis forces. The Coriolis force on an atom is given by \(F_{\rm{Coriolis}} = 2m v_{\rm{a}} \omega \sin \varphi\), where \(m\) is the mass of the atom, \(v_{\rm{a}}\) is its apparent velocity in the rotating coordinate system, \(\omega\) is the angular velocity of the coordinate system, and \(\varphi\) is the angle between the velocity vector and the axis of rotation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\ce{N}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Coriolis force</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08584 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08584">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Smallest angle of incidence at which total internal reflection occurs.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>If \(n_{2}\) and \(n_{1}\) are the refractive indices, and \(n_{2} &gt; n_{1}\), at the boundary, \(\sin \theta_{\rm{c}} = n_1/n_2\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>critical angle</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08585 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08585">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Vibration in which the dominant change from equilibrium is in one or more valence angles.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>deformation vibration</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08586 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08586">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Raman intensity with the electric vector of the scattered electromagnetic radiation perpendicular to that of the incident radiation divided by the Raman intensity with electric vectors parallel.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In normal Raman scattering with linearly polarized exciting radiation, \(\rho \le 0.75\) for totally symmetric vibrations and \(\rho = 0.75\) for all other vibrations.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>depolarization ratio</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08667"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08669"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08587 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08587">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Raman band with depolarization ratio \(\rho = 0.75\) for linear polarized incident radiation in normal Raman spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>depolarized Raman band</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08586"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08660"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08665"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08669"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08588 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08588">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Combination transition that does not start at the ground state and in which at least one vibration decreases and at least one vibration increases its degree of excitation; i.e., a transition in which more than one vibrational quantum number changes and at least one becomes smaller.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>difference band</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>difference tone</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A difference band or difference tone results from a difference transition.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>difference transition</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08580"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08589 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08589">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measuring instrument for Raman spectroscopy incorporating a dish-shaped cell that is divided by a vertical partition through its middle to allow different liquids in its two halves. It is rotated about a vertical axis through its centre and the Raman scattered radiation from each half is measured alternately. With a standard liquid in one half and a sample in the other it is used to determine Raman scattering intensities relative to the standard.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>divided spinning cell</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08667"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08669"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08590 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08590">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Spectrum of a material under the influence of an applied external perturbation represented by a variable \(\tau\), relative to a reference spectrum.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The reference spectrum may be chosen in many ways; e.g., it may be the average over \(\tau\), of the observed spectrum, or it may be zero.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dynamic spectrum</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08291"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08295"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08296"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08591 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08591">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Influence of terms that are of \(2^{\rm{nd}}\) and higher order in the normal coordinate \(Q\) in the expansion of the electric dipole moment.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>anharmonicity</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Electrical anharmonicity should not be confused with mechanical anharmonicity.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrical anharmonicity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08592"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08635"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08644"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08592 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08592">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Influence of the linear term, \(\Sigma_{k} (\partial \boldsymbol{\mu}/\partial Q_{k}) Q_{k}\), in the expansion of the electric dipole moment. \(Q\) is the normal coordinate.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>harmonicity</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Electrical harmonicity should not be confused with mechanical harmonicity.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrical harmonicity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08636"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08644"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08593 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08593">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of vibrational spectroscopy in which a constant-energy beam of electrons passes through or is reflected from a sample and the energy distribution in the transmitted or reflected beam is analysed to give a spectrum of number of electrons against electron energy loss.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>EELS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>EELS is used for the study of electronic states and has an energy resolution of \(\pu{0.25 eV}\) or better (corresponding to an approximate wavenumber of \(\pu{2000 cm-1}\)).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electron energy loss spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08291"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08708"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08593"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>EELS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08594 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08594">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Characteristic quantity used in the analysis of absolute infrared intensity data chosen on the assumption that the electric dipole moment of the molecule can be described as the vector sum of bond dipole moments.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>EOP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>equilibrium charge, charge flux (ECCF)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In Note 1, \(R\), are vibrational coordinates such as bond stretching, angle deformation, or torsion about a bond.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The original EOPs were \(\mu_{k}^{\circ}\), the equilibrium bond moment, and \(\partial \mu_{k}/\partial R_{t}\), the change in the electric dipole moment of bond \(k\) with change in internal displacement coordinate \(t\). A later and completely equivalent formulation uses equilibrium atomic charges, \(q_{\alpha}^{\rm{o}}\), and charge fluxes, \(\partial q_{\alpha}/\partial R_{t}\), which are also called EOPs as well as ECCF for equilibrium charge, charge flux.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electro-optic parameter</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08594"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>EOP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08595 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08595">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Degree to which linearly polarized incident electromagnetic radiation becomes elliptically polarized in an absorbing optically active medium. \[\tan\;\psi = (E_{\rm{R}} - E_{\rm{L}})/(E_{\rm{R}} + E_{\rm{L}}) = \tanh (k^{\rm{L}} - k^{\rm{R}})\pi l/\lambda\] where \(E_{\rm{R}}\) and \(E_{\rm{L}}\) are the electric vectors of right and left circularly polarized radiation, \(k^{\rm{L}}\) and \(k^{\rm{R}}\) are the absorption indices of the sample for left and right circularly polarized radiation, \(\lambda\) is the wavelength of the radiation and \(l\) is the path-length through the medium.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: rad = 1.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>ellipticity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08220"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08278"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08300"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08596 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08596">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Part of a standing-wave normal to a reflecting surface that extends beyond the reflecting surface when an electromagnetic wave undergoes total internal reflection.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>evanescent wave</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08700"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08597 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08597">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Unexpected minimum or hole in a broad absorption band due to a gap being created in a broad distribution of states by Fermi resonance.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>Evans hole</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08601"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08598 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08598">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Graph of Raman intensity at the desired Raman shift against wavenumber of the excitation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>excitation profile</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08668"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08599 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08599">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of spectroscopy in which radiation is reflected from a sample of higher refractive index than that of the incident medium.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>external reflection spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08600 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08600">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In a uniaxial crystal electromagnetic radiation with electric vector oblique to the optic axis and which does not obey the normal laws of refraction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>extraordinary ray</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In uniaxial crystals the optic axis coincides with the symmetry axis. The refractive index of the crystal is the same in all directions perpendicular to the optic axis but is different along the optic axis. Consequently, electromagnetic rays or waves that do not travel along the optic axis experience different refractive indices in different directions perpendicular to their direction of propagation. Two waves result, one has its electric vector perpendicular to the optic axis and forms the ordinary wave. The other is the extraordinary wave.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>extraordinary wave</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08655"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08601 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08601">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Cubic anharmonic resonance (see anharmonicity), i.e., any interaction caused by cubic terms in the vibrational potential energy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Fermi resonance is usually identified by the presence of more than one band of comparable intensity when only one fundamental is expected. Also seen as an Evans hole.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Traditionally, Fermi resonance was defined as the anharmonic interaction between a fundamental state and one or more overtone or combination transitions.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Fermi resonance</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08580"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08597"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08635"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08707"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08602 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08602">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Free spectral range divided by resolution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>finesse</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08607"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08603 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08603">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Infrared and Raman bands that are characteristic of a particular molecule rather than a functional group.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>fingerprint bands</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08665"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08604 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08604">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Region of a spectrum that contains fingerprint bands as well as bands with characteristic group frequencies.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The fingerprint region is often associated with the region of the mid-infrared or Raman spectrum of wavenumbers between \(\pu{1500 cm-1}\) and \(\pu{400 cm-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>fingerprint region</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08291"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08603"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08617"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08670"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08605 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08605">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Infrared spectroscopy in which a Fourier-transform spectrometer is used to separate the transmitted electromagnetic radiation into its component wavenumbers.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>FT-IR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Fourier-transform infrared spectrometry</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08618"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08605"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>FT-IR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08606 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08606">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Raman spectroscopy in which a Fourier-transform spectrometer is used to separate the scattered electromagnetic radiation into its component wavenumbers.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Fourier-transform Raman spectrometry</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>This technique usually uses near-infrared excitation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Fourier-transform Raman spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08642"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08669"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08607 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08607">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>For a Fabry-Perot or laser cavity, wavenumber interval between successive longitudinal cavity modes of vibration; \(\Delta \tilde{ u} = 1/(2nl)\), where \(l\) is the cavity spacing and \(n\) is the refractive index of the material in the cavity.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{m-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>free spectral range</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08637"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08608 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08608">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Angle of incidence of a beam of electromagnetic radiation greater than \(\pu{70\!^{\circ}}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Ideally the angle of incidence should be greater than \(\pu{80\!^{\circ}}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>grazing incidence</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08222"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08609 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08609">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Frequency in vibrational spectroscopy that is characteristic of a particular chemical functional group.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The frequency is usually given as a wavenumber.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>group frequency</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08708"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08610 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08610">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coefficient of (\( u + &amp;half;\)) in the vibrational term value, determined by the harmonic terms in the vibrational potential energy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>harmonic wavenumber</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{m-1}\). Common unit: \(\pu{cm-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>harmonic constant</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08707"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08709"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08611 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08611">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Line-broadening by mechanisms that affect every molecule of the same species in the sample in the same way.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>homogeneous broadening</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Such mechanisms do not require an ensemble of spectroscopically non-equivalent molecules of the same species. Such mechanisms are the natural line width, which is usually negligible in vibrational spectroscopy, and intra- or inter- molecular interactions, including anharmonic vibrational interactions (see anharmonicity), that reduce the lifetime of the excited state.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>homogeneous line-broadening</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08503"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08635"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08641"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08708"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08612 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08612">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electronic transition between an excited state and a state in which all vibrational quantum numbers are the same or greater.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A hot band arises from a hot transition.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A hot transition has the same changes in vibrational quantum numbers as a fundamental, overtone transition, or sum transition but it originates in an excited vibrational state not the ground state.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>hot transition</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08245"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08656"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08685"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08613 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08613">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Raman spectroscopy in which scattering occurs through hyperpolarizabilities. A two-photon technique in which an intense pulsed beam of electromagnetic radiation is focused onto the sample. When sufficient power is present in the pulse, two photons may interact with the one molecule to create a virtual state at double the frequency of the laser excitation. Raman scattering from this virtual state to an excited vibrational state of the ground state then occurs.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>HRS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>hyper-Raman spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08667"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08669"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08613"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>HRS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08614 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08614">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method to obtain information about the spatial composition of a sample by obtaining a spectrum at each spatially resolved point of the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>hyperspectral imaging</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08291"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08615 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08615">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In spectroscopy, a rotation-reflection axis, which is a symmetry element such that rotation by \(2\pi/n\) about the axis followed by reflection through a plane perpendicular to the axis transforms an object into itself.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In crystallography, a rotation-inversion axis, which is a symmetry element such that rotation by \(2\pi/n\) about the axis followed by inversion through the centre of symmetry transforms an object into itself.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>improper rotation axis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08616 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08616">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of vibrational spectroscopy to obtain spectra of molecules on metal oxide surfaces.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>IETS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>IETS yields vibrational spectra of adsorbates with high resolution (better than \(\pu{0.5 meV}\)) and low limit of detection (below \(\pu{E13}\) molecules are required to provide a spectrum).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>inelastic electron tunnelling spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08708"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08616"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>IETS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08617 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08617">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electromagnetic radiation of wavelength between approximately \(\pu{780 nm}\) and \(\pu{1000 \upmu m}\) or of wavenumber between approximately \(\pu{13000 cm-1}\) and \(\pu{10 cm-1}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>IR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>infrared</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>If there is no ambiguity the term &apos;infrared&apos; is used for the mid-infrared region.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The infrared range is sub-divided into three regions: near-infrared: \(\pu{780 nm}\) to \(\pu{2.5 \upmu m}\) (\(\pu{12800 cm-1}\) to \(\pu{400 cm-1}\)); mid-infrared: \(\pu{2.5 \upmu m}\) to \(\pu{25 \upmu m}\) (\(\pu{4000 cm-1}\) to \(\pu{400 cm-1}\)) and far infrared: \(\pu{25 \upmu m}\) to \(\pu{1000 \upmu m}\) (\(\pu{400 cm-1}\) to \(\pu{10 cm-1}\)).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The wavelength regions given are commonly used in chemistry and relate to working ranges of infrared spectrometers. Note that in other disciplines (e.g., astronomy) the terms are defined for different ranges.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>infrared radiation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08300"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08618"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08642"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08669"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08617"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>IR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08618 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08618">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement principle of vibrational spectroscopy using infrared radiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Without qualification, &apos;infrared spectroscopy&apos; usually refers to use of the mid-infrared (wavelength range \(\pu{2.5}\) to \(\pu{25 \upmu m}\), wavenumber range (\(\pu{4000 cm-1}\) to \(\pu{400 cm-1}\)) region of the electromagnetic spectrum (see table).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>infrared spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08300"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08617"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08708"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08619 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08619">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Line-broadening by mechanisms that arise from an ensemble of spectroscopically non-equivalent molecules of the same species. Such molecules exist, for example, when molecules occupy non-equivalent sites in a condensed phase, and when gaseous molecules have different velocities and, thus, shift the observed wavenumber differently through the Doppler effect.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>inhomogeneous broadening</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08503"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08620 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08620">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sinusoidal intensity variation due to interference of electromagnetic radiation that undergoes multiple reflection between two flat and parallel surfaces.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>channel fringes</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A spectrum of interference fringes is termed a channel spectrum.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Interference fringes are frequently observed in the transmission spectrum of a cell with flat windows and in non-scattering polymer films.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>interference fringes</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08621 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08621">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Record of the signal from the detector of a two-beam interferometer as the optical path difference between the two beams is varied.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>interference function</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The interference record contains a part that is constant and a part that varies with the path difference.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>interference record</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08653"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08622 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08622">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Pattern formed by wave interference produced by an interferometer, especially one represented on a screen or chart recorder.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>interferogram</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08623 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08623">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reflection of electromagnetic radiation from a material of lesser refractive index than that of the incident medium.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>internal reflection</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08624 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08624">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Transparent or semi-transparent material of high refractive index that carries infrared radiation to a sample in internal reflection and attenuated total reflection measurements.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The sample is mounted in optical contact with the element.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>internal reflection element</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08227"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08617"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08623"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08625 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08625">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Raman scattering showing absorption when Stokes Raman scattering exceeds anti-Stokes Raman scattering, in an experiment in which a sample is simultaneously illuminated by coincident beams from a continuum source and a pulse laser at \(\tilde{ u}_{0}\). Energy is absorbed from the continuum at \(\tilde{ u}_{0} + \tilde{ u}_{\rm{s}}\) where \(\tilde{ u}_{\rm{s}}\) is the wavenumber of a Raman-active vibration in the sample that is excited by this process, and this energy is emitted at \(\tilde{ u}_{0}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>inverse Raman scattering</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08570"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08667"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08683"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08626 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08626">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Variant of spatially offset Raman spectroscopy in which a sample is illuminated with a ring of laser light and the Raman scattering is collected from the centre of the ring.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>inverse spatially offset Raman spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08667"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08679"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08627 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08627">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Kinds of crystal transparent to infrared radiation used as windows and filters in infrared spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>IRTRAN 1 to IRTRAN 5 are hot-pressed forms of \(\ce{MgF2}\), \(\ce{CdF2}\), \(\ce{MgO}\), \(\ce{ZnSe}\) and \(\ce{ZnS}\), respectively.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>IRTRAN is a registered trade mark of Eastman Kodak Co.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>IRTRAN™</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08617"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08618"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08628 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08628">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Aperture in the optics of a Fourier-transform spectrometer, typically between the source and the interferometer, designed to be the limiting aperture when no other optical element such as the detector serves this purpose.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>J-stop</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>Jacquinot stop</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08629 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08629">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coupling of vibrational displacements through terms in the vibrational kinetic energy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Two displacement coordinates must share a common atom if they are to undergo kinetic coupling.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>kinetic coupling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08706"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08630 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08630">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Eutectic (\(\pu{42 \% TlBr, 58 \% TlI}\)) mixture of thallium bromide and thallium iodide transparent to infrared radiation used as window in infrared spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>KRS-5 transmits well down to \(\pu{200 cm-1}\). It is practically insoluble in water but its refractive index is rather high (approximately 2.35). It is rather plastic and deforms with time, and it is poisonous.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>KRS-5</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08617"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08618"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08631 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08631">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>\(f(R_{\infty}) = (1 – R_{\infty})^{2}/2R_{\infty}\), where \(R_{\infty}\) is the diffuse reflectance from a sample of infinite depth.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>remission function</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Theoretically \(f(R_{\infty})\) equals the ratio of the linear decadic absorption coefficient to the scattering coefficient. Under the assumptions of the Kubelka Munk theory, if the scattering coefficient at a given wavenumber is a constant for a given set of samples, \(f(R_{\infty})\) is directly proportional to the product of the absorption coefficient and concentration of each component of a mixture, analogous to the behaviour of absorbance under Beer’s law.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Kubelka–Munk function</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08235"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08710"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08632 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08632">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Homogeneous line-broadening due to the limited lifetime of energy states involved in a transition.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The lifetime may be limited by the natural lifetime, by collisions, as in pressure broadening, or by anharmonic vibrational interactions as in phonon-phonon collisions in solids.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>lifetime broadening</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08591"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08611"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08663"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08633 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08633">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mode of vibration localized in one type of bond.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>local mode</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>For highly excited \(\ce{CH}\) stretching vibrations anharmonic interactions mix the normal modes of vibration to the point that each observed mode of vibration behaves as a local mode.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>local mode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08637"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08648"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08634 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08634">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Defined under the assumption of the Lorentz local field by the Lorentz-Lorenz formula: \[\hat{\alpha}_{\rm{m}}(\tilde{ u}) = 3V_{\rm{m}}\epsilon_{0} \frac{\hat{\epsilon}(\tilde{ u}) - 1}{\hat{\epsilon}(\tilde{ u}) + 2} = 3V_{\rm{m}}{\epsilon}_{0}\,\frac{\hat{n}^{2}(\tilde{ u}) - 1}{\hat{n}^{2}(\tilde{ u}) + 2}\] where \(V_{\rm{m}}\) is the molar volume and \(\hat{\epsilon}\) and \(\boldsymbol{\hat{n}}\) are the complex dielectric constant and refractive index, respectively. To allow description of absorption, \(\hat{\alpha}_{\rm{m}}(\tilde{ u})\) is complex: \(\hat{\alpha}_{\rm{m}}(\tilde{ u}) = \alpha_{\rm{m}}&apos;(\tilde{ u}) + \rm{i}\alpha_{\rm{m}}&apos;&apos;(\tilde{ u})\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>molar polarizability</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{J-1 C2 m2 mol-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The imaginary molar polarizability shows the absorption band free from long range dielectric effects that distort the band shapes of very strong absorptions.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>local molar polarizability</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08574"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08635 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08635">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Influence of cubic and higher-order terms in vibrational potential energy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>anharmonicity</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Mechanical anharmonicity should not be confused with electrical anharmonicity.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>mechanical anharmonicity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08591"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08707"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08636 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08636">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Influence of quadratic terms in vibrational potential energy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>harmonicity</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Mechanical harmonicity should not be confused with electrical harmonicity.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>mechanical harmonicity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08592"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08707"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08638 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08638">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ellipticity divided by the amount concentration of the optically active absorbing material and by the path-length at temperature (\(t\)) and wavelength (\(\lambda\)).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{rad m2 mol-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>molar ellipticity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08300"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08639 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08639">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Decrease in measurement uncertainty obtained by measuring intensity at many different wavelengths simultaneously, as is done, for example, in Fourier-transform spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>multiplex advantage</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08300"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08641 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08641">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Line width of a molecular spectral band that arises from the probability of spontaneous emission in the absence of radiation fields and interaction with other molecules or entities.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>If the excited state \(n\) can only emit spontaneously to a single lower state \(m\) the natural linewidth is \(\pu{1.86E-38}\ \tilde{\nu}_{mn}^3 \langle m|\boldsymbol{\mu}|n \rangle^{2}\), when the transition wavenumber \(\tilde{\nu}_{mn}\) is in \(\pu{cm-1}\) and the dipole moment \(\mu\) is in Debye (\(\pu{1D \approx 3.335E-30 C m}\)). In the infrared, typical natural linewidths are approximately \(\pu{E-7 cm-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The natural linewidth, measured as the full width half maximum equals \(1/(2\pi c_{0} \Delta t)\), where \(\Delta t\) is the natural lifetime of the excited state.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>natural linewidth</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08251"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08285"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08640"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08642 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08642">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Infrared radiation of which vacuum wavenumbers range from approximately \(\pu{12800 cm-1}\) to \(\pu{4800 cm-1}\) and wavelengths range from approximately \(\pu{780 nm}\) to \(\pu{2500 nm}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>near-IR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The region is often divided into the silicon region between \(\pu{12800 cm-1}\) and \(\pu{9000 cm-1}\) (\(\pu{800 nm}\) and \(\pu{1100 nm}\)) and the lead sulfide region between \(\pu{9000 cm-1}\) and \(\pu{4000 cm-1}\) (\(\pu{1100 nm}\) and \(\pu{2500 nm}\)), where the names reflect the common detector for the region.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>near-infrared radiation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08299"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08617"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08643 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08643">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Kinds of infrared spectroscopy in which the effect of the sample on the radiation from the source is measured as a whole without exploring the dependence on wavenumber.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>These methods are mainly used to obtain adequate optical throughput for process measurement and monitoring.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>non-dispersive infrared spectroscopies</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08277"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08301"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08618"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08644 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08644">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Formal mathematical description of a normal vibration. When the potential energy, \(V\), is harmonic and the kinetic energy, \(T\), is calculated in the limit of infinitesimally small displacements, the normal coordinates are defined as independent entities such that &apos;(for non-linear molecules)&apos;\[V = &amp;half;\sum_{k=1}^{3N-6}\lambda_{k}Q_{k}^{2}\ {\rm{and}}\ T = &amp;half;\sum_{k=1}^{3N-6}\dot Q_{k}^{2}\]where \(\lambda_{k}\) is the \(k^{\rm{th}}\) eigenvalue and \(\dot {Q}\) is \(\partial Q/\partial t\). Normal coordinate \(Q_{k}\) is related to the internal displacement coordinates, \(R_{i}\), through the elements, \(L_{ik} = \partial R_{i}/\partial Q_{k}\) of the \(k^{\rm{th}}\) column of the eigenvector matrix \(\boldsymbol{L}\). Normal coordinates are not independent when the potential energy is anharmonic, or the displacements are not infinitesimal.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{kg^{-1/2} m}\). Common unit: \(\pu{u^{1/2} &amp;#8491; \approx 4.07497E-24 kg^{1/2} m}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>normal coordinate</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08645"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08705"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08645 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08645">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Calculation of the normal modes of vibrations of a molecule or crystal under the assumptions that the potential energy is harmonic and the displacements from equilibrium are infinitesimal.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>normal coordinate analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08644"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08648"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08705"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08646 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08646">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Slow decrease of real refractive index of a material in a non-absorbing spectral region far from regions of strong absorption as the wavenumber decreases.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>normal dispersion</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>normal dispersion of the refractive index</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08288"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08301"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08647 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08647">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Incidence of a beam of electromagnetic radiation when the angle of incidence \(\theta = \pu{0\!^{\circ}}\), i.e., the incident radiation beam is normal to the surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>normal incidence</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08222"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08648 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08648">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>One of the \(3N - 6\) (\(3N - 5\) for a linear molecule) modes of vibration that would add to give the total vibrational motion in a molecule if the interatomic potential were strictly quadratic in the displacements from equilibrium.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>normal mode</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>normal vibration</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>normal vibration mode</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Normal modes of vibration is an idealized concept that has proved extremely useful in analysing spectra in vibrational spectroscopy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>normal mode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08637"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08708"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08649 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08649">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Second differential of intensity with respect to bias voltage divided by first differential of intensity with respect to bias voltage.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NTI</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>constant resolution tunnelling intensity</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Normalized tunnelling intensity is plotted against the bias voltage to report inelastic electron tunnelling spectroscopy data.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>normalized tunnelling intensity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08616"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08649"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>NTI</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08650 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08650">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Depth in a sample at which the intensity of an incident beam is reduced to \(1/\rm{e}\) of its value at the surface of the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{m}\). Common unit: \(\pu{\upmu m}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>\(\mu_{\beta}\) is the reciprocal of the linear Napierian absorption coefficient.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>optical absorption depth</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08265"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08651 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08651">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Collective name for the real refractive index, \(n\) and the absorption index (imaginary refractive index), \(k\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>optical constants</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08220"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08652 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08652">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Spectrometer in which the ratio of the radiant power in the two beams at each wavenumber is measured opto-mechanically by inserting a linear optical attenuator into the reference beam until the two signals measured by the detector are equal, then measuring the displacement of the optical attenuator.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NTI double beam spectrometer</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>null-balance double beam spectrometer</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>optical null double beam spectrometer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08653 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08653">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Path-length difference between the arms of a two-beam interferometer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>OPD</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>optical retardation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{m}\). Common unit: \(\pu{cm}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term optical retardation is derived from &apos;phase retardation&apos;, which refers to the change in the phase of the electromagnetic radiation in the interferometer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>optical path difference</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08278"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08653"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>OPD</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08654 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08654">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Wavelength dependence of the angle of rotation of the plane of linearly-polarized radiation when transmitted through optically active materials.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ORD</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>optical rotatory dispersion</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08300"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08654"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ORD</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08655 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08655">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In a uniaxial crystal electromagnetic radiation with electric vector perpendicular to the optic axis and which obeys the normal laws of refraction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ordinary ray</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In uniaxial crystals the optic axis coincides with the symmetry axis. The refractive index of the crystal is the same in all directions perpendicular to the optic axis but is different along the optic axis. Consequently, electromagnetic rays or waves that do not travel along the optic axis experience different refractive indices in different directions perpendicular to their direction of propagation. Two waves result, one has its electric vector perpendicular to the optic axis and forms the ordinary wave. The other is the extraordinary wave.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>ordinary wave</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08600"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08656 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08656">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Transition between the ground state and a state in which a single mode of vibration is multiply excited.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>An overtone band results from an overtone transition.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>overtone transition</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08637"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08657 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08657">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>\(-\log_{10}\) of the spectrum obtained in attenuated total reflection spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The term is used to avoid the confusing, but common and incorrect, practice of calling it the absorbance spectrum.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>pATR spectrum</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08291"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08573"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08658 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08658">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Condition in photoacoustic spectroscopy in which all bands with greater than a certain absorption intensity appear to have the same intensity.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Photoacoustic saturation arises when the sample is optically opaque and thermally thin, i.e., when the thermal diffusion depth, \(L\), is greater than the optical absorption depth, \(\mu_{\beta}\), and less than the sample thickness. The condition may be relieved by increasing the modulation frequency sufficiently that \(L\) becomes less than \(\mu_{\beta}\), i.e., that the sample becomes thermally thick.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>photoacoustic saturation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08650"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08696"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08659 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08659">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of spectroscopy in which the absorption of electromagnetic radiation by a sample is detected as a result of the heat generated by the absorption.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>photothermal spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08660 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08660">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Raman band with depolarization ratio \(\rho \le 0.75\) for linear polarized incident radiation in normal Raman spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>polarized Raman band</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08586"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08587"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08665"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08669"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08661 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08661">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coupling of vibrational displacements through terms in the vibrational potential energy. The second-order (quadratic) terms cause harmonic potential coupling while the cubic and higher terms cause anharmonic potential coupling.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>potential coupling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08635"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08636"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08707"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08662 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08662">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polarization when electromagnetic radiation is incident upon a surface with its electric vector in the plane of incidence and, therefore, inclined to the reflecting surface unless the incidence is normal.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>TM polarization</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>transverse magnetic polarization</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>p-polarization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08663 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08663">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Increase in the line width of a spectral line of a gas due to molecular collisions and other intermolecular interactions that reduce the lifetime of an excited state.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>collision broadening</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>At pressures sufficiently low that the line width is far smaller than the line wavelength, the line width increases linearly with pressure, the broadening is homogeneous and the line shape is Lorentzian.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>pressure broadening</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08300"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08550"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08664 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08664">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Laser that emits mid-infrared radiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>QCL</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A QCL can be used as a source for infrared spectroscopy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>quantum cascade laser</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08617"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08618"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08664"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>QCL</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08665 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08665">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Spectral band in Raman spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>Raman band</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08285"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08669"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08666 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08666">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Phenomenon that vibrations in chiral molecules and materials Raman scattering right circularly polarized incident radiation to a different extent than left circularly polarized incident radiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ROA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In practice, the circular polarization of the exciting beam, or of the scattered beam, or of both beams simultaneously, may be selected. Raman optical activity is usually specified as &apos;right minus left&apos; circular polarizations, in unfortunate contrast to the &apos;left minus right&apos; specification that is universal in vibrational circular polarization and other forms of optical activity.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Raman optical activity</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08666"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ROA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08667 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08667">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Inelastic scattering of electromagnetic radiation by molecules that are excited to higher energy levels.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>normal Raman scattering</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Normal Raman scattering occurs through changes in polarizability of a molecule during a vibration, not the hyper-polarizabilities, and is excited by radiation that is not in resonance with electronic transitions in the sample.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The energy of the scattered photon can be lesser (Stokes Raman scattering) or greater (anti-Stokes Raman scattering) than that of the incident photon.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Raman scattering</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08245"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08246"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08570"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08683"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08668 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08668">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Difference in wavenumber between the incident and scattered electromagnetic radiation in Raman spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Raman wavenumber shift</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{m-1}\). Common unit: \(\pu{cm-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Raman shift</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08669"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08669 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08669">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement principle of vibrational spectroscopy based on Raman scattering.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Raman scattered light occurs at wavelengths that are shifted from the incident light by the energies of molecular vibrations.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Raman spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08667"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08708"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08670 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08670">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Graph of the intensity of scattered electromagnetic radiation in Raman spectroscopy against Raman shift.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Commonly a Raman spectrum is obtained between approximately \(\pu{100 cm-1}\) and \(\pu{3500 cm-1}\) and is a complement to its counterpart mid-infrared spectrum.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Raman spectrum</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08668"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08669"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08671 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08671">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Wavenumber of exciting electromagnetic radiation minus wavenumber of the scattered radiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Raman shift</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{m-1}\). Common unit: \(\pu{cm-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>\(\Delta \tilde{\nu}\) is positive for Stokes Raman scattering and negative for anti-Stokes Raman scattering.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Raman wavenumber shift</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08570"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08683"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08672 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08672">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy in which the optical path difference is continually changed at a rate of \(\pu{0.05 cm s-1}\) or greater.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>rapid-scan Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08581"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08605"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08653"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08673 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08673">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reflection-absorption with very thin layers of an absorbing material on a metallic substrate and the angle of incidence between \(\pu{70\!^{\circ}}\) and \(\pu{90\!^{\circ}}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>As the incident radiation beam travels nearly parallel to the sample surface, the electric vector of the radiation is then either parallel or nearly perpendicular to the surface.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>reflection-absorption at grazing incidence</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08222"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08674 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08674">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Raman scattering that occurs through the polarizability of a molecule, (not the hyperpolarizabilities) and is excited by electromagnetic radiation that is in resonance with electronic transitions or vibronic transitions in the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>resonance Raman scattering</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08667"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08674"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>RR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08675 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08675">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Spectral lines in the rotation-vibration spectrum of a gas that derive from the same change in rotation quantum number, \(J\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The most commonly observed branches for simple molecules are the \(\rm{P}\), \(\rm{Q}\) and \(\rm{R}\) branches in infrared spectroscopy and the \(\rm{O}\), \(\rm{Q}\) and \(\rm{S}\) branches in Raman spectroscopy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>\(\Delta J = J_{\rm{upper}} - J_{\rm{lower}} = -2, -1, 0, +1\ \rm{and}\ +2\) for the \(\rm{O}\), \(\rm{P}\), \(\rm{Q}\), \(\rm{R}\) and \(\rm{S}\) branches, respectively.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>rotational branch</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08550"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08618"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08669"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08676"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08676 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08676">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Spectrum that shows absorption or emission of radiation during transitions between rotational-vibrational states of a molecule.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>rotation-vibration spectrum</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08291"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08677 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08677">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Relation between the propagation directions, \(k_{\rm{i}}\), \(k_{\rm{s}}\), and the polarization directions, \(p_{\rm{i}}\), \(p_{\rm{s}}\), of the incident and scattered radiation, usually expressed in terms of laboratory-fixed or crystal-fixed Cartesian axes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A common notation, due to Porto, is \(k_{\rm{i}}(p_{\rm{i}}\,p_{\rm{s}})k_{\rm{s}}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>scattering geometry</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08678 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08678">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Appearance of more than one band in the spectrum of a crystal when only one is seen in that of the gas, when this splitting is attributed to either the molecule lying on a crystal site of lower symmetry than that of the gaseous molecule or to molecules occupying more than one type of crystal site. See crystal field splitting.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Site splitting arises from the static intermolecular forces in the crystal.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>site splitting</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08291"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08679 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08679">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of Raman spectroscopy in which the sample is illuminated at one location, and the Raman signal is collected from a different location on the sample surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SORS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>SORS allows chemical analysis of objects beneath diffusely scattering surfaces, such as tissue, powders and translucent plastics.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>spatially offset Raman spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08669"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08679"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SORS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08680 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08680">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Molecule whose three principal moments of inertia are all the same. An isotropic molecule.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>spherical top</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08681 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08681">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Interferometer (typically used for Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy) in which the optical path difference is changed by a certain amount, held constant, or modulated if phase modulation is desired, while the signal at the current path difference is recorded, then the process is repeated.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>step-scan interferometer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08605"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08653"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08682 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08682">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Two-photon Raman scattering occurs when a picosecond pump beam is used to create a virtual state and a probe beam of a frequency to match the frequency of Stokes Raman scattering from the virtual state to a specific vibrational level. The Stokes emission is stimulated thus depopulating the virtual state very quickly and increasing the scattering efficiency for the chosen vibration by orders of magnitude. SRS is a non-linear process, so sufficient photons from both the pump and probe beams must be present on the molecule at the one time.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SRS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>stimulated Raman scattering</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08667"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08683"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08682"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SRS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08683 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08683">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Raman scattering of electromagnetic radiation in which the scattered radiation has lesser energy (lesser wavenumber) than the exciting radiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Stokes scattering</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>Stokes–Raman scattering</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08570"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08667"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08684 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08684">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Band resulting from a sum transition.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>sum band</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08685"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08685 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08685">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Combination transition between the ground state and a state in which more than one vibration is excited; i.e., a transition from the ground state in which more than one quantum number increases.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>sum transition</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08580"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08686 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08686">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nonlinear optical technique in which pulsed lasers at frequencies \(\omega_{1}\) and \(\omega_{2}\) are overlapped at a surface and light emitted at the sum of the two frequencies, \(\omega_{1} + \omega_{2}\), is detected. One laser is normally in the mid-infrared and the other in the visible or near-infrared.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>sum-frequency spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08532"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08617"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08687 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08687">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of Raman spectroscopy by which the Raman spectrum is significantly enhanced simultaneously by both the hyper-Raman (see hyper-Raman spectroscopy) and surface-enhanced Raman (see surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy) effects.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SEHRS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Very high enhancement factors of \(\pu{E20}\) have been claimed.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>surface-enhanced hyper-Raman spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08613"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08669"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08670"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08689"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08687"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SEHRS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08688 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08688">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of infrared spectroscopy by which the intensity of absorption bands of molecules within a few nanometers of the surface of metal particles is increased by interactions with surface plasmons (the collective resonance of electrons near the surface of metal islands).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SEIRA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The metals that give the largest enhancement are silver and gold, but this phenomenon has been reported to occur with at least eight other metals.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>surface-enhanced infrared absorption</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08618"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08688"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SEIRA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08689 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08689">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of Raman spectroscopy by which the intensity of vibrational bands in the Raman spectra of molecules within a few nanometers of the surface of microscopically rough metals, metal colloids and metal nanoparticles is increased by several orders of magnitude.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SERS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Other mechanisms of enhancement are known but the common usage of SERS is with a metal or with related systems and implies enhancement through interaction with a surface plasmon.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The metals that give the largest enhancement are silver, gold and copper, but this phenomenon has been reported to occur with a range of other metals.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08669"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08670"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08689"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SERS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08690 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08690">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy (SERS) by which the Raman spectrum is significantly enhanced when the conditions occur that whilst undergoing SERS the molecules are excited by radiation that is in resonance with electronic transitions in the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SERRS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Enhancements of \(\pu{E14}\) to \(\pu{E15}\) have been measured.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>surface-enhanced resonance Raman spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08245"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08670"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08689"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08690"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SERRS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08691 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08691">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of Raman spectroscopy that combines spatially offset Raman spectroscopy and surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy (SERS) enabling the detection of a SERS signal deep within diffusely scattering samples.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SESORS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>surface-enhanced, spatially offset Raman spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08669"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08679"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08689"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08691"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SESORS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08692 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08692">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Term used in at least two senses in vibrational spectroscopy. In general usage, a symmetric molecule is a molecule with high symmetry. In more specific usage, a property is symmetric with respect to a symmetry element if it remains unchanged by the action of the corresponding symmetry operation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>symmetric</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08708"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08693 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08693">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Molecule that has two equal principal moments of inertia with the third one different.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>symmetric top</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08571"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08694 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08694">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Each \(S_{i}\) is a linear combination of general coordinates, usually internal coordinates, constructed to take advantage of the molecular symmetry. The \(S_{i}\) are elements of the vector \(\boldsymbol{S}\). They have the same units as the general coordinates from which they were constructed.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>symmetry coordinate</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08695 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08695">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Space group that does not contain screw-axes and glide planes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>symmorphic space group</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08696 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08696">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In photoacoustic spectroscopy, \(L = \sqrt{(D/\pi f)}\), where \(D\) is thermal diffusivity and \(f\) is modulation frequency of the electromagnetic radiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>thermal wave decay length</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{m}\). Common unit: \(\pu{\upmu m}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>\(\pu{63 \%}\) of the PAS signal originates within the thermal diffusion depth.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>thermal diffusion depth</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08697"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08697 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08697">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Thermal conductivity divided by the product of the specific heat and the density.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{m2 s-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>thermal diffusivity</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08698 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08698">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reciprocal of the thermal diffusion depth (\(L\)). \(a_{\rm{s}} = 1/L\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{m-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>thermal wave decay coefficient</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08696"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08699 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08699">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of Raman spectroscopy combining surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy and scanning using an atomic force microscope or scanning tunnelling microscope to give complementary maps of a surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>TERS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>tip-enhanced Raman spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08669"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08689"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08699"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>TERS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08700 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08700">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Internal reflection from a non-absorbing material at angles of incidence at or above the critical angle.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>total internal reflection</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08701 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08701">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Spectral intensity of a transflected beam divided by spectral intensity of the incident beam in a transflection experiment.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>&apos;Transflectance&apos; has also been used to mean transflection, and this usage is strongly discouraged.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>transflectance</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08702"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08702 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08702">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reflection absorption at near-normal incidence when the thickness of the absorbing medium is large enough to yield an interpretable spectrum. The substrate is usually a mirror for mid-infrared measurements and a ceramic disk for near-infrared measurements. The angle of incidence is typically between \(\pu{0\!^{\circ}}\) and \(\pu{45\!^{\circ}}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Transflection is widely used in IR microscopy and is particularly convenient for measuring transmission-like spectra in a near-infrared instrument configured for diffuse reflection measurements by mounting the sample on a non-absorbing diffusely reflecting substrate.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>transflection</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08222"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08236"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08291"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08647"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08703 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08703">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of Raman spectroscopy in which the sample is illuminated on one side and the Raman signal is collected from the opposite side.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>TRS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>text</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>transmission Raman spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08669"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08703"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>TRS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08704 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08704">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Circular dichroism, \(k^{\rm{L}} – k^{\rm{R}}\), for vibrational transitions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>VCD</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Vibrations in chiral molecules and materials absorb left circularly polarized radiation to a different extent than right circularly polarized radiation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>vibrational circular dichroism</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08704"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>VCD</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08705 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08705">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Part of the solution of the matrix equation of normal coordinate analysis, \(\boldsymbol{G\!F\!L} = \boldsymbol{L \lambda}\). Each element \(L_{ik}\) of \(\boldsymbol{L}\) is a vibrational eigenvector, and gives the change in internal coordinate \(R_{i}\) during unit change in the normal coordinate \(Q_{k}\), as shown in matrix form by \(\boldsymbol{R} = \boldsymbol{LQ}\), i.e., \(L_{ik} = \partial R_{i}/\partial Q_{k}\). Eigenvectors are sometimes expressed in terms of symmetry coordinates or Cartesian coordinates.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{kg^{-1/2}}\). Common unit: \(u^{-1/2} = \pu{2.45400E13 kg^{-1/2}}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>vibrational eigenvector</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08644"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08645"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08706 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08706">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Kinetic energy of the molecule as a function of the displacements of the atoms from equilibrium positions. For Cartesian displacement coordinates (\(x, y, z\)), \[T = \sum_{\alpha}&amp;frac12;m_{\alpha}(\dot x_{\alpha}^{2} + \dot y_{\alpha}^{2} + \dot z_{\alpha}^{2})\] where the sum is over all atoms and \(\dot{x}_{\alpha}\), etc., are the displacement velocities. For internal coordinates \[T = &amp;frac12;\boldsymbol{P}^{t}\boldsymbol{GP} = &amp;frac12;\dot{\boldsymbol{R}}^{t}{\boldsymbol{G}}^{-1}\dot {\boldsymbol{R}} = &amp;frac12;\sum_{ij}G_{ij}^{-1}\dot R_{i}\dot R_{j}\] where \(\dot {\boldsymbol{R}}^{t}\) and \(\dot {\boldsymbol{R}}\) are the row and column vector, respectively, of the \(\partial R_{i}/\partial t\), \(\boldsymbol{P}^{t}\) and \(\boldsymbol{P}\) are the row and column vector, respectively, of the momenta conjugate to the \(R_{i}\), \(\boldsymbol{G}^{-1}\) is the inverse of the \(\boldsymbol{G}\) matrix and \(G_{ij}^{-1}\) is the \(ij^{\rm{th}}\) element of \(\boldsymbol{G}^{-1}\). The elements of the \(\boldsymbol{G}\) matrix are defined by \[G_{ij} = \sum_{\alpha} \frac{1}{m_{\alpha}} B_{i\alpha} B_{j\alpha}\] where \(B_{i\alpha}\) and \(B_{j\alpha}\) relate the \(i^{\rm{th}}\) and \(j^{\rm{th}}\) internal coordinate to the \(\alpha^{\rm{th}}\) Cartesian coordinate through the equation \(\boldsymbol{R} = \boldsymbol{BX}\) in which \(\boldsymbol{X}\) is the column vector of the Cartesian coordinates.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:label>vibrational kinetic energy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08707 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08707">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Potential energy of the molecule as a function of the displacements of the atoms from equilibrium positions. The harmonic terms are quadratic in the displacements and the anharmonic terms are of cubic, quartic and higher orders in the displacements. \(Q\) are normal coordinates \[2V = \sum\limits_{k}\lambda_{k}Q_{k}^{2} + \sum\limits_{ijk}k_{ijk}Q_{i}Q_{j}Q_{k} + higher\,order\,terms\] In terms of internal coordinates, \[2V = \sum\limits_{i} F_{ii} R_i^{2} + \sum\limits_{i,j e i} F_{ij} R_{i} R_{j} + \sum\limits_{i,j,k} F_{ijk} R_{i} R_{j} R_{k} + higher\,order\,terms\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{J}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>vibrational potential energy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08644"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08708 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08708">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement principle of spectroscopy to analyse molecular properties based on vibrations (bond stretching or deformation modes) in chemical species.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Typically, vibrational spectroscopy uses electromagnetic radiation lower in energy than the visible spectrum i.e., infrared or near infrared (see table).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>vibrational spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08617"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08642"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08709 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08709">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Vibrational energy, \(E\), expressed in wavenumber units. \(G = E/(hc)\), where \(h\) is the Planck constant and \(c\) is the speed of light in vacuum.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{m-1}\). Common unit: \(\pu{cm-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>\(G\) is usually written with zero-energy at the minimum of the potential energy curve for a diatomic molecule as: \[G = \omega_{\rm{e}}(\nu +1/2) - \omega_{\rm{e}} x_{\rm{e}}(\nu +1/2)^{2} + \omega_{\rm{e}} y_{\rm{e}}(\nu + 1/2)^{3} + higher\ order\ terms,\] and for a polyatomic molecule \[G = \sum_{k} \omega_{k}(\nu_{k} + g_{k}/2) + \sum_{i\le j} X_{ij}(\nu_{i} + g_{i}/2)(\nu_{j} + g_{j}/2) + higher\ order\ terms,\] where \(\nu\), \(\nu_{k}\), etc. = 0, 1, 2, 3,··· , and \(g\) is the degeneracy of a vibration. \(X_{ij}\) are the anharmonic constants.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>vibrational term value</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08302"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08710 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08710">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Re-emergence of electromagnetic radiation from the surface of incidence after penetrating into a powdered sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0203"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Kubelka–Munk reflection</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>volume reflection</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08242"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08711 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08711">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of particles or photons counted by a radiation detector divided by the number emitted by a radiation source.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:label>absolute counting efficiency</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08712 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08712">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mass spectrometry technique in which atoms and molecules from a sample are ionized, accelerated to mega-electron volt (\(\pu{1 MeV} = \pu{1.602176634E-13 J}\)) energies, and separated according to their momentum, charge, and energy, allowing high discrimination for the measurement of nuclide abundances.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>AMS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>AMS is typically used for (but not limited to) the measurement of radionuclides with long half-lives, such as \(\ce{^{10}Be}\), \(\ce{^{14}C}\), \(\ce{^{26}Al}\), \(\ce{^{36}Cl}\), \(\ce{^{53}Mn}\), and \(\ce{^{129}I}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>accelerator mass spectrometry</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08712"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>AMS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08713 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08713">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Graph of the activity of a radioactive nuclide as a function of time and showing the increase of activity through the decay of the precursor or as a result of activation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:label>activity growth curve</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08714 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08714">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement principle in which the backscattering of nuclear radiation impinging on a sample is applied to measure the structure and composition of materials.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:label>backscattering analysis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08716 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08716">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Calibration method of neutron activation analysis in which the \(k_{0}\) proportionality factors, together with a value of the neutron flux (as a function of the neutron energy) and with a value of the radiation detector&apos;s response for gamma radiation (as a function of the gamma-ray energy) are used for establishing a relationship between the number of counts measured and the corresponding amounts of elements in a material.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>&lt;em&gt;k&lt;/em&gt;&lt;sub&gt;0&lt;/sub&gt; method of calibration</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The \(k_{0}\) proportionality factor is a ratio of the experimentally measured nuclear parameters of the (radio)nuclides and those parameters of the respective (radio)isotopes of a calibrator (often based on Au).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>calibration, k0 method</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08717"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08739"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08717 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08717">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measured amount of an element with stated measurement uncertainty that is simultaneously irradiated with the sample during activation analysis.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>comparator (obsolete)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>If one calibrator is used (single calibrator method, which is preferred over &apos;single comparator method&apos;), it is essentially identical to a flux monitor (except that the latter is not necessarily linked to activation analysis).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &apos;calibrator&apos; is preferred over &apos;comparator&apos;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>calibrator</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08726"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08718 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08718">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Range- (traveling distance) increasing effect caused by the decrease in interaction cross sections of the incident particles with atoms in a single crystal when a highly collimated particle beam impinges on a crystal along its principal axis or principal plane.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:label>channeling effect</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08719 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08719">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method to obtain a quenching correction by counting the spectrum in two separate channels, the ratio of which gives the degree of quenching.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:label>channels ratio method</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08720 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08720">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement principle in which wavelengths and angular distribution of Compton scattered electrons is applied in order to reconstruct X-ray and gamma-ray spectra.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Compton scattering analysis is applied, for example, in mammography.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Compton scattering analysis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08721 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08721">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ability of substances other than the analyte to bind to the binding reagent and ability of substances other than the binding reagent to bind the analyte in competitive binding assays.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The binding described here is known as cross reaction.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>cross reactivity</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08722 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08722">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Characteristic area related to the probability of a specified interaction or reaction between an incident nuclear radiation and a target particle or system of particles.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>microscopic cross section</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Cross section is the reaction rate per target particle for a specified process divided by the flux density of the incident radiation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In general, the type of nuclear radiation (e.g. neutron, photon), the energy of the incident radiation (e.g. thermal, epithermal, fast (see: neutron energy)) and the type of interaction of reaction (e.g. activation, fission, scattering) are specified.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>cross section</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08742"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08723 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08723">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Neutron activation analysis where neutrons are counted after a delay to allow interfering species to decay.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DNAA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>delayed-neutron analysis (DNA)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>delayed-neutron counting (DNC)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>delayed-neutron activation analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08739"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08723"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>DNAA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08724 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08724">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Beam of particles capable of removing one or more orbital electrons in a single quantum event from a specified atom resulting in an ion.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:comment>To have sufficient energy for direct ionization, most ionizing particles are electrically charged, for example, alpha particles, beta particles, electrons, positrons, protons. Photons can ionize atoms directly through the photoelectric effect or the Compton effect.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When considering the health effects of radiation, the distinction may be made between multiple ionizations by charged particles as they move through a material (called &apos;direct ionization&apos;) and a single event of ionization caused by a photon.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>directly-ionizing radiation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08730"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08725 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08725">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method to obtain a quenching correction through the use of a gamma-radiation source to generate a spectrum of Compton electrons within the sample vial.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:label>external standardization for quenching correction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08720"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08726 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08726">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Radiation detector to measure energy flux density.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A flux monitor may be a known amount of material irradiated together with a sample; the induced radioactivity is used to measure the flux density during the irradiation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>flux monitor</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08727 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08727">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Change of energy flux density or energy distribution of particles or photons in an object as a result of effects such as flux depression and/or self-shielding.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:label>flux perturbation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08728 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08728">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement principle of the quantitative study of the energy spectra of gamma-ray sources.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>gamma-ray spectroscopy (obsolete)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>gamma-ray spectrometry</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08729 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08729">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Thickness of a specified material that, when introduced into the path of a given beam of radiation, reduces the intensity of a specified radiation by one half.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>half thickness</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>half-value layer thickness</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>half-value thickness</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08730 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08730">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Beam of electrically neutral particles that cause ionization by interacting with atoms in a material, producing electrically-charged particles that subsequently cause direct ionization in the material.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:label>indirectly-ionizing radiation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08724"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08731 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08731">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of neutron activation analysis in which a living organism is exposed to neutrons to measure the concentrations of elements in that living organism.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Often prompt gamma-ray analysis is used to measure half-value thickness, rather than the activity of neutrons produced.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The neutrons are typically provided by a neutron beam.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>&lt;em&gt;in-vivo&lt;/em&gt; neutron activation analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08729"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08739"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08754"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08732 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08732">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement principle in which elementary particles resulting from nuclear reactions of charged particles with nuclei in a material are applied in order to measure the amount and depth distribution of elements in materials.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>IBA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>ion beam analysis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08732"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>IBA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08733 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08733">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Radioactive or stable isotope of a specified element distinguishable by the observer but not by the system used to identify an isotopic tracer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:comment>&apos;Isotopic labelling&apos; is the incorporation of an isotopic label in a substance. It may be qualified by the manner of introduction of the label, e.g. exchange labelling, conjugation labelling, recoil labelling.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In general usage, &apos;label&apos; and &apos;tracer&apos; are used synonymously.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>isotopic label</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08735 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08735">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chemical element having only one stable nuclide.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:comment>General usage of the term &apos;monoisotopic&apos; refers to the 21 elements with one isotope determining their relative atomic masses, i.e. \(\ce{^{9}Be}\), \(\ce{^{19}F}\), \(\ce{^{23}Na}\), \(\ce{^{27}Al}\), \(\ce{^{31}P}\), \(\ce{^{45}Sc}\), \(\ce{^{55}Mn}\), \(\ce{^{59}Co}\), \(\ce{^{75}As}\), \(\ce{^{89}Y}\), \(\ce{^{93}Nb}\), \(\ce{^{103}Rh}\), \(\ce{^{127}I}\), \(\ce{^{133}Cs}\), \(\ce{^{141}Pr}\), \(\ce{^{159}Tb}\), \(\ce{^{165}Ho}\), \(\ce{^{169}Tm}\), \(\ce{^{197}Au}\), \(\ce{^{209}Bi}\), \(\ce{^{231}Pa}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>There are 26 elements that follow the definition: \(\ce{^{9}Be}\), \(\ce{^{19}F}\), \(\ce{^{23}Na}\), \(\ce{^{27}Al}\), \(\ce{^{31}P}\), \(\ce{^{45}Sc}\), \(\ce{^{51}V}\), \(\ce{^{55}Mn}\), \(\ce{^{59}Co}\), \(\ce{^{75}As}\), \(\ce{^{85}Rb}\), \(\ce{^{89}Y}\), \(\ce{^{93}Nb}\), \(\ce{^{103}Rh}\), \(\ce{^{113}In}\), \(\ce{^{127}I}\), \(\ce{^{133}Cs}\), \(\ce{^{139}La}\), \(\ce{^{141}Pr}\), \(\ce{^{153}Eu}\), \(\ce{^{159}Tb}\), \(\ce{^{165}Ho}\), \(\ce{^{169}Tm}\), \(\ce{^{175}Lu}\), \(\ce{^{185}Re}\), \(\ce{^{197}Au}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>monoisotopic element</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08736 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08736">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method in which recoil-less resonance gamma ray scattering and absorption in solids is applied to measure the nuclear environment of atoms of elements in a material.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:label>Mössbauer spectrometry</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08737 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08737">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of X-ray analysis in which characteristic X-radiation emitted upon irradiation of a material with a beam of muons of certain energy is used to measure the chemical composition and chemical state of an element.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:label>muon induced X-ray emission analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08769"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08738 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08738">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of X-ray absorption analysis similar to X-ray absorption near edge structure, but usually reserved for soft X-rays with photon energy less than \(\pu{1 keV}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NEXAFS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>NEXAFS is generally used in surface and molecular science.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>near edge X-ray absorption fine structure</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08766"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08768"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08738"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>NEXAFS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08739 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08739">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Activation analysis using neutrons as the incident particles.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NAA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>neutron activation analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08723"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08731"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08739"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>NAA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08740 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08740">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of activation analysis for near-surface-depth light elements in which a thermal neutron or cold neutron (see: neutron energy) beam passes through a material with target nuclei that emit monoenergetic charged particles upon neutron absorption. The reduction of the energy of an emitted charged particle measures the depth of the target nuclei in a material.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NDP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>neutron depth profiling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08742"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08740"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>NDP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08741 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08741">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method in which the diffraction of neutrons is applied to measure parameters of the atomic and/or magnetic structure of a material.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:label>neutron diffraction analysis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08742 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08742">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Kinetic energy of a free neutron.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Neutron energy is usually given with unit electronvolt (\(\pu{eV}\)). \(\pu{1 eV} = \pu{1.602176634E-19 J}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Neutron temperature has unit kelvin, and the term should not be used for the neutron energy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Neutrons are classified according to their energies as follows:</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>neutron energy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08743 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08743">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method in which the elastic or inelastic scattering of neutrons by the target nuclei is applied to study the composition and structure of a material.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:label>neutron scattering analysis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08744 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08744">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Deactivation of an electronically-excited state by interaction with the external environment through a non-radiative process.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Non-radiative quenching may lead to spectral shift or counting losses.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The effects of quenching may be taken into account by a quenching correction.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>non-radiative quenching</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08745 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08745">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process in which an atomic nucleus acquires an additional particle.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>capture</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In general, a specification is added of the type of the captured particle or its energy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The captured particle may be an elementary particle and may be charged or neutral.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nuclear capture</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08746 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08746">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Radionuclide which precedes a nuclide in a decay chain.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:label>nuclide precursor</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08747 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08747">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of particles (or photons) incident on a plane perpendicular to the incident radiation per unit time per unit area.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>fluence rate (obsolete)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>photon flux density</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Often an indication of the type of incident particles is wrongly added to the unit of flux density, e.g., the neutron flux density is sometimes indicated as n \(\pu{cm-2 s-1}\). As &apos;n&apos; is not a unit, it is metrologically unacceptable and therefore not recommended.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Particle flux density is identical with the product of the particle density and the average speed of the particles.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The SI unit of flux density is \(\pu{m-2 s-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>particle flux density</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08748 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08748">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement principle in which the energies and intensities of characteristic prompt gamma-radiation emitted during nuclear reactions with charged particles other than electrons is used to measure the amounts of elements in a material.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PIGE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The particle that induces gamma-rays may be explicitly mentioned, e.g. &apos;proton-induced gamma-ray-emission analysis&apos;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>particle-induced gamma-ray-emission analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08753"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08748"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PIGE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08749 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08749">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of gamma-ray spectrometry in which coincidence counting is used for the measurement of parameters describing hyperfine interactions, with internal or external electrical or magnetic field gradients, of the spin of an intermediate level between two gamma-ray transitions in cascade emitted in the decay of a radionuclide.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PAC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>perturbed directional correlation spectrometry</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Perturbed angular correlation spectrometry can be performed both in a time-differential (TDPAC) and in a time-integrated (TIPAC) mode of measurement.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The parameters describing hyperfine interactions provide information on the chemical atomic environment of the decaying nucleus.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>perturbed angular correlation spectrometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08728"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08749"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PAC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08750 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08750">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement principle in which the annihilation of positrons is applied to study the microscopic structure of materials.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PAA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>positron annihilation spectroscopy for chemical analysis (PASCA)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Annihilation lifetime (see: positron lifetime spectrometry), Doppler broadening, and the angular correlation of annihilation radiation are measured in this technique.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In the most common case of positron annihilation, two photons are created, each with energy equal to the rest energy of the electron or positron (\(\pu{0.511 MeV} = \pu{8.187122600E-14 J}\)).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Techniques based on PAA have been used particularly for the study polymers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>positron annihilation analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08752"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08750"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PAA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08751 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08751">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Imaging method based on the detection of pairs of gamma rays emitted indirectly by a positron-emitting isotopic tracer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PET</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A commonly used nuclide is fluorine-18.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Three-dimensional imaging is obtained using computed tomography.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>positron emission tomography</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08751"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PET</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08752 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08752">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of positron annihilation analysis in which the time interval between the emission of positrons from a radioactive source and the detection of gamma rays due to the annihilation of these positrons with electrons from the surrounding matter is the lifetime of the positron or positronium.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PLS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>positron annihilation lifetime spectrometry (PALS)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>positron lifetime spectrometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08750"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08752"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PLS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08753 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08753">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Gamma radiation emitted during the de-excitation of a compound nucleus formed in a nuclear capture reaction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:label>prompt gamma radiation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08754 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08754">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement principle in which gamma radiation emitted during the de-excitation of the compound nucleus formed by neutron capture is applied to measure the amounts of elements in a material.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PGA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The measurement method is often denoted as prompt gamma-ray neutron activation analysis (PGNAA) or prompt gamma-ray activation analysis (PGAA), though the measurement method is not in agreement with the definition of activation analysis. The term prompt gamma-ray analysis (PGA) is therefore preferred.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>prompt gamma-ray analysis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08754"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PGA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08755 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08755">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Processing by a radiation counter of pulses resulting from the simultaneous absorption of independent particles or photons in a radiation detector, resulting in them being counted as one single particle or photon with energy between the individual energies and the sum of these energies.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:label>pulse pile-up</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08757 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08757">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Material interposed in the path of radiation to modify the energy distribution of the radiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:label>radiation filter</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08758 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08758">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Among the radionuclides of a decay chain, the state that prevails when the ratios between the activities of successive radionuclides remain constant.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>radioactive equilibrium</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>secular equilibrium</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>This is not equilibrium in the strict sense, since radioactive decay is an irreversible process.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>radioactive steady state</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08759 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08759">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Period of time after the dead time of a radiation counter during which the output pulses are smaller than the original.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Depending on the sensitivity of the counter, some pulses in this period will not be counted.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>recovery time</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08760 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08760">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Absolute counting efficiency of a given radiation counter divided by the absolute counting efficiency of a reference radiation counter.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:label>relative counting efficiency</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08711"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08761 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08761">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of X-ray fluorescence analysis in which protons are focused and collimated to form a micro beam to obtain an image of a surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SPM</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>scanning proton microscopy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08761"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SPM</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08762 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08762">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>An isotopically labelled compound is designated as selectively labelled when a mixture of isotopically substituted compounds is formally added to the analogous isotopically unmodified compound in such a way that the position(s), but not necessarily the number, of each labelling nuclide is defined.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A selectively labelled compound may be considered a mixture of specifically-labelled isotopic tracers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>selectively-labelled isotopic tracer</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08763 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08763">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Activity of a specified radionuclide (\(\ce{R}\)) per unit mass of that nuclide.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:comment>If the term &apos;specific activity&apos; is used without qualification, it should be made clear whether it is of a material or of a radionuclide. See: specific activity of a radionuclide in a material.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>specific activity (radionuclide)</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08764 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08764">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of X-ray fluorescence analysis in which synchrotron X-radiation is used to irradiate the substance.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:label>synchrotron radiation induced X-ray fluorescence analysis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08765 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08765">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of X-ray fluorescence analysis in which a collimated X-ray flux impinges on a smooth surface under a grazing angle; rendering total reflection is used to excite atoms in the top layers of the material for measurement of the amounts of elements.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>TXRF</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The method is highly sensitive because interfering X-rays of higher energies are refracted or adsorbed. Mass fractions of \(\pu{E-12}\:(\pu{ng kg-1})\) may be measured.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>total reflection X-ray fluorescence analysis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08765"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>TXRF</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08766 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08766">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement principle of X-ray analysis in which the absorption spectrum is used to measure the number of atoms, species, and other parameters of chemical elements.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>XAA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>XAS measures changes in the linear absorption coefficient of an element in a sample as a function of incident photon energy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>XAS requires a highly monochromatic (with \(\Delta E/E \approx \pu{E-4}\) to \(\pu{E-5}\)), high flux X-ray beam.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>X-ray absorption analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08768"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08769"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08766"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>XAA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08767 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08767">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Increase in X-ray absorption observed at the energy at which a strongly bound electron is released.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>absorption edge</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>X-ray absorption edge</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08768 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08768">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of X-ray absorption analysis in which the fine structure of the adsorption spectrum in the range \(\pu{30 eV}\) below to \(\pu{50 eV}\) above the absorption edge is used to measure parameters describing the chemical state, coordination environment, and local geometry distortion for the X-ray absorbing atom.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>X-ray absorption near edge fine structure</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>XANES</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The method uses synchrotron radiation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The wavelength of the emitted photoelectrons is longer than the interatomic distances between the absorbing atom and its nearest neighbours.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>X-ray absorption near edge structure</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08738"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08766"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08768"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>XANES</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08769 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08769">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement principle in which characteristic X-radiation, produced upon irradiation of a material with elementary particles or photons, is applied to measure amounts of elements in a material.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <rdfs:label>X-ray analysis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08770 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08770">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method based on the transmission of X-rays to obtain three-dimensional images of a sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>X-ray micro-tomography (XMT)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>XCMT</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Spatial resolution is in the range \(\pu{100 nm}\) to \(\pu{10 \upmu m}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>X-ray computed micro-tomography</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08770"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>XCMT</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08771 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08771">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method using diffraction of X-radiation to obtain the spatial arrangement of atoms in a crystalline sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>X-ray diffraction (XRD)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Bragg reflection follows \(n\lambda = 2 d \sin \theta\), where \(\lambda\) is the X-ray wavelength, \(d\) is the spacing between atomic planes, and \(\theta\) is the angle of diffraction.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Copper \(\ce{K}\upalpha_{1}\) radiation (\(\lambda = \pu{0.15406 nm}\), \(E = \pu{8.04 keV}\)) is typically used for routine XRD.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>X-ray diffraction analysis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08772 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08772">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of X-ray fluorescence to obtain quantitative and spatial information about elements in a sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>XRM</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Spatial resolution is typically in the range from \(\pu{200 nm}\) to \(\pu{10 \upmu m}\), but, using specialised probes at synchrotron facilities, the lower limit can be decreased to tens of nanometers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>X-ray beam energies of \(\pu{5 keV}\) excite core level vacancies and promote hard X-ray emission, for which the fluorescence yield is high.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>X-ray fluorescence microscopy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08772"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>XRM</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08773 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08773">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Transducer that converts the energy supplied or taken from the surroundings into some mechanical motion.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Human muscles, motors, loudspeakers and various piezoelectric devices are examples of actuators.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>actuator</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08774 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08774">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Soliton with the negative amplitude or opposite spin with respect to a given soliton.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>antisoliton</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Particle or quasiparticle capable of transporting an electric charge.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A charge carrier can be free (mobile) or trapped (immobile).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>charge carrier</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08787"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08826"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08863"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08776 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08776">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of charge carriers per unit volume.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>concentration of charge carriers</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A use of the term &apos;charge carrier density&apos; is depricated since it can easily be confused with the term charge density whose meaning is different, see charge density.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>charge carrier concentration</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08777 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08777">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Purely random, thermally induced motion of charge carriers.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>According to the type of charge carriers undergoing diffusion, it is subdivided into electron diffusion, hole diffusion, polaron diffusion, soliton diffusion, etc.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>charge carrier diffusion</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08797"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08826"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08778 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08778">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process whereby charge carriers are created.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>charge generation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The charge carrier generation in the low-permittivity organic semiconductors usually proceeds in two steps: (i) formation of an exciton, and (ii) the exciton dissociation to mobile charge carriers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The energy needed for the charge carrier generation can be gained from photon absorption (photogeneration), from thermal motion of lattice atoms (thermal generation), from an electric field discharge or from another charge carrier with sufficiently high kinetic energy (impact ionization).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>charge carrier generation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08815"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08831"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08835"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08779 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08779">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process whereby charge carriers in excess of the thermodynamic equilibrium level are introduced into a conductor, a semiconductor or an insulator from another material.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>charge carrier injection</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08774"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08792"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08801"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08853"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08877"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08780 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08780">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Drift velocity (\(v_{\rm{d}}\)) of charge carriers divided by the electric field strength (\(E\)). \[\mu = v_{\rm{d}}/E \quad (\rm{unit}{:}\ \pu{m2 V-1 s-1})\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>charge mobility</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>charge carrier mobility</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08794"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08781 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08781">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process whereby an electron in the conduction band (in LUMO level) loses its excess energy and re-occupies the energy state of a hole in the valence band (in HOMO level).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>charge recombination</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Based on the mechanism of the charge carrier recombination we distinguish: Auger recombination, geminate recombination, radiative charge carrier recombination, stimulated radiative charge carrier recombination, band-to-band recombination and localized-level-assisted (trap-assisted) recombination that dominate in organic semiconductors. See also doping.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In a thermally equilibrated material, generation and recombination of charge carriers are balanced yielding an equilibrium charge carrier concentration that is predictable from Fermi-Dirac statistics.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>charge carrier recombination</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08776"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08864"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08876"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08782 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08782">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Transfer of charge carriers between two sites of the same molecular entity (intramolecular CT) or between two molecular entities occurring in close proximity (intermolecular CT).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CT</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Electrons are implicitly considered as the charge carriers subject to a transfer. If other charge carriers (protons, quasiparticles) are considered, they should be explicitly mentioned.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The molecular entity or the entity region from which electron(s) is(are) released is an electron donor while the molecular entity or the entity region accepting electron(s) is an electron acceptor.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>charge transfer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08782"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CT</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08783 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08783">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Exciton formed by a charge-transfer transition.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>charge-transfer exciton</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08784 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08784">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Material having the ability to transmit energy or charge carriers or both.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The nature of the conductor is specified by the entity transmitted, e.g., electric (electron, hole, ion), thermal (heat) and acoustic.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>conductor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08785 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08785">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>A polymer composed of molecules whose backbone is a sequence of alternating single and multiple bonds:  where \(\ce{R^{1}}\) and \(\ce{R^{2}}\) are each hydrogen, alkyl, aryl or heteroaryl and Ar is arenediyl (formerly: arylene) or heteroarenediyl (formerly: heteroarylene).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Conjugated polymers are mostly linear or branched polymers. However, they can also have a dendritic, hyperbranched, network, rotaxane or metallo-supramolecular chain architecture.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Overlaps of neighboring \(\ce{\uppi\mbox{-}orbitals}\) across intervening \(\ce{\upsigma\mbox{-}bonds}\) in a conjugated molecule results in delocalization of π-electrons along the molecule backbone. This effect which, however, is limited by Peierls distortion gives the electric conductivity to conjugated polymers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Polymers such as polysilanes, polygermanes and polystannanes with significantly occupied LUMO orbitals due to low bandgap energy and thus show the delocalization of electrons sometimes called \(\ce{\uppi\mbox{-}conjugated polymers}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>conjugated polymer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08789"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08899"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08786 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08786">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Molecule or a part of a molecule, of which structure may be represented as a system of alternating single and multiple bonds, in which, eventually, a multiple bond can be replaced by an atom with a pair of non-bonding electrons or an atom carrying a negative charge.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Alternating arrangement of the single and multiple bonds allows the delocalization of electrons, which stabilizes conjugated systems. The most effective stabilization show planary cyclic systems with \(2n + 2\) delocalized electrons (where \(n\) is integer), so-called aromatic systems. The last two examples above are the species where the pair non-bonding electrons (thiophene) or electrons of negatively charged atom are delocalized together with \(\ce{\uppi\mbox{-}electrons}\) giving an aromatic system.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Due to the delocalization of electrons a formula drawn in terms of the valence bond theory does not fully describe the structure of a conjugated species, which actually is a combination of two or more limiting or intermediate resonance structures that are in a dynamic equilibrium. The molecular orbital theory which directly provides extended molecular orbitals spread over all contributing atoms allows better understanding of the electronic and optical properties of a conjugated system.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Examples of conjugated systems are conjugated polymers and molecule species whose formulas drawn in terms of the valence bond theory are as follows: </rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>π-conjugated system</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08785"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08789"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08841"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08878"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08787 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08787">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Distance between two oppositely charged charge carriers at which the energy of their Coulombic attraction is equal to \(kT\), where \(k\) is the Boltzmann constant and \(T\) the absolute temperature.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>coulomb radius</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08781"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08788 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08788">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electric conductivity of a material in the dark where the free charge-carrier photogeneration is excluded.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>dark conductivity</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The dark electric conductivity of a material in thermal equilibrium is proportional to the product of the concentration of free charge carriers and the charge carrier mobility.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dark electric conductivity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08823"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08789 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08789">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Redistribution of electron density in a molecular entity as compared with a model assuming individual atoms in the same valence states connected by localized bonds.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A delocalized electron is not associated with a particular atom or particular covalent bond but occupies an extended orbital spread over several to many atoms or the whole lattice.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Extensive delocalization of electrons is typical of metals, semiconductors, graphite, conjugated polymers, polysilanes, boranes, conjugated compounds such as organic dyes and others.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Topological modes of the electron delocalization include: ribbon (one-dimensional) delocalization of \(\ce{\uppi\mbox{-}}\) or \(\ce{\upsigma\mbox{-}electrons}\) or both, which is typical of linear conjugated polymers that are therefore termed one-dimensional conductors;surface delocalization of \(\ce{\uppi\mbox{-}}\) or \(\ce{\upsigma\mbox{-}electrons}\) or both through an overlap of radially oriented orbitals of a cyclic molecule such as a molecule of cyclopropane or benzene;volume delocalization of \(\ce{\upsigma\mbox{-}electrons}\) through an overlap of \(\ce{\upsigma\mbox{-}orbitals}\) directed inside a molecular polyhedron, e.g., in tetrahedrane or carboranes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>delocalization of electrons</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08774"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08785"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08818"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08835"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08841"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08899"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08790 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08790">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Material which polarizes when exposed to an external electric field, because it creates its own internal electric field oriented against the external field.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>dielectric</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Dielectrics are divided into polar and nonpolar. The polar ones contain dipolar groups and their poling (polarization process) consists in the orientation of these dipoles by an external electric field. It is the so-called orientation polarization, which can but may not be complete due to competitive thermal motion. Poling of some polar dielectrics such as fluoropolymers gives materials with a stabilized polarized structure known as electrets. Poling of a non-polar dielectric is always temporary since it consists in displacements of electrons (electronic polarization) or ions in crystals (ionic polarization) giving induced dipoles that decay immediately after the removal of the field.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Dielectrics are key materials for the construction of capacitors.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dielectric material</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08795"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08810"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08856"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08857"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08791 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08791">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Irregular charge transport in which charge carriers move with an ill-defined mean velocity owing to the high variety of dwell times spent at different transport sites.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>dispersive transport</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08829"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08792 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08792">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Simultaneous injection of both types of charge carriers (electrons and holes) into a material or a device.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>double charge carrier injection</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Double injection is used in production of electrets and function of light-emitting diodes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>double injection</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08795"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08826"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08836"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08793 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08793">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electric current caused by electric drift.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>drift current</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08777"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08797"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08794 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08794">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Average velocity that a charge carrier attains due to an applied electric field.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>If an electric field is absent, charge carriers move randomly. An applied electric field induces drift (net movement) of charge carriers in a preferred direction. Average net velocity of this movement is called the drift velocity \(v_{\rm{d}}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The drift velocity of a charge carrier is directly proportional to the applied electric field strength \(E\):  \[v_{\rm{d}} = \mu E\]  where constant \(\mu\) is the charge carrier mobility in the given material.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>drift velocity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08780"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08795 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08795">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Dielectric material with a permanent or quasi-permanent electric dipole.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A real-charge electret is obtained by the charge carrier injection onto the surface or into a parent dielectric material. It may contain either positive or negative excess charges or both, which must be trapped so that a good insulating parent dielectric material such as a fluoropolymer must be used.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>An oriented-dipole electret is obtained by poling a parent dielectric material with dipolar groups at a temperature above the glass transition temperature \(T_{\rm{g}}\), and eventual next stabilization of the structure by cooling the electret down below \(T_{\rm{g}}\) or by crosslinking.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Electrets are electric equivalents of magnets; electric magnets. Some oriented-dipole electrets are ferroelectric polymers and piezoelectric polymers. Quartz is an example of the natural electret.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electret</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08790"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08857"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08796 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08796">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Material that is able to transmit electric current.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>electric conductor</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08797 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08797">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Oriented flow of otherwise randomly moving charge carriers induced by an applied external electric field.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>electric drift</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08798 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08798">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Composite composed of a non-conducting polymer matrix in which irregular powdered or fibrous particles of an electrically conducting material are evenly dispersed in an amount exceeding the percolation threshold for electric conductivity and so the composite exhibits electric conductivity close to that of the conducting filler.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>conducting polymer composite</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Conducting polymer composites are extrinsically conducting polymeric materials since the matrix does not act as an electric conductor but only as a container for the active electric conductor.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Conventional rubbery polymers are mostly used as the insulating matrix and powdered metals or carbon black as the dispersed phase in commercialized conducting polymer composites.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrically conducting polymer composite</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08796"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08848"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08799 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08799">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymer that exhibits electrochromism.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>electrochromic polymer</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08800 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08800">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electroluminescence originated from singlet-state excitons.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>electrofluorescence</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08801 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08801">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process of charge carrier injection induced by an applied external electric field.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>electroinjection</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08779"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08802 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08802">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Physical field composed of two interconnected, mutually orthogonal fields, an electric field and a magnetic field, which mediates interactions of charged, dipolar and multipolar objects.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The electromagnetic field produced by one charged or polar object affects other charged or polar objects, which is the essence of electromagnetic interaction - one of the four basic forces in nature.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electromagnetic field</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08803 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08803">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymer showing a noticeable response of its physical or chemical properties during or upon exposure to an electromagnetic field or one of its components: electric field and magnetic field.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>electromagnetic field responsive polymer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08802"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08804 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08804">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Molecule that has fewer valence electrons than required for forming normal two-center two-electron chemical bonds and thus contains multicenter chemical bonds.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Binding of atoms into molecules, clusters and macroscopic bodies in electron-deficient compounds is mediated by delocalized electrons, each being shared by three or more atoms.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Boron, boranes, carboranes and related compounds are typical compounds with electron-deficient molecules in which boron and some other atoms are bound by multicenter two-electron bonds, such as by the two-electron three-center bond in which two electrons bind three atoms.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Metals are typical electron-deficient compounds in which extreme delocalization of electrons across the whole macroscopic body exists.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electron-deficient molecule</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08789"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08805"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08818"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08805 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08805">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Molecule that has more valence electrons than is needed to link its atoms by single covalent bonds.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Formally excess electrons present in a molecule containing conjugated multiple bonds are delocalized electrons. Non-bonding valence electrons present in molecules with heteroatoms, such non-bonding electrons of nitrogen, phosphorus and sulfur atoms, are also taken into account.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Molecules of unsaturated hydrocarbons or aromatic and heteroaromatic compounds are typical electron-rich molecules. Such compounds can be called electron-rich compounds.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electron-rich molecule</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08789"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08806 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08806">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Quantum mechanical effect of passing an electron through a potential energy barrier separating two energy equivalent regions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>electron quantum tunneling</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The electron tunneling effect is exploited, e.g., in scanning tunneling microscopes, pressure sensors based on quantum tunneling polymer composites and in touchable panels of electronic devices.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electron tunneling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08862"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08807 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08807">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Change in the optical properties of a material in response to a change in an external electric field strength.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Changes in the absorption coefficient, color, refractive index, permittivity or optical activity of a material are typical examples of electro-optic effects.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Electro-optic is not a synonym for optoelectronic.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electro-optic effect</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08846"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08808 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08808">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electroluminescence originated from triplet-state excitons.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>electrophosphorescence</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08809 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08809">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reversible change in the apparent viscosity of a liquid system such as suspension of polar or good polarizable microparticles in an oil, upon exposure of an electric field.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Interaction of electric dipoles induced on suspended particles produces strands of particles spanning between electrodes, which results in a giant increase in the viscosity of the suspension (up to five orders of magnitude) during its exposure to an electric field.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrorheological effect</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08810 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08810">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Quasi-continuous range of discrete energy levels that an electron is either allowed (an occupied band) or forbidden (forbidden band or band gap) to occupy in a molecule or solid.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>electronic band</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>energy band</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08811"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08812"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08811 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08811">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Theory describing the electronic structure of a solid in terms of energy intervals, called energy bands that are allowed or forbidden for occupation by electrons.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>electronic band theory</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The energy band theory stems from the solution of the Schrödinger equation for electron waves moving in a periodic crystal lattice where, due to the wave diffractions, only some directions of the wave propagation characterized by the wave vector (so-called \(k\mbox{-}\rm{vector}\)) are allowed.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When several atoms form a molecule, their atomic orbitals are transformed into a set of discrete bonding and antibonding molecular orbitals (energy levels), the number of which is proportional to the number of involved atoms. The energy levels that an electron is allowed to occupy are separated by energy intervals without any energy levels that an electron can occupy. If many atoms are linked together to form a lattice, the number of energy levels that the electrons are allowed to occupy becomes exceedingly large. Among these energy levels, large subsets of energy levels exist in which energy differences between individual levels are so small that they may be regarded as a quasi-continuum (individual energy levels are of no importance). The subset of energy levels that can be occupied by electrons is named allowed energy bands. The allowed bands are separated by energy intervals that electrons cannot occupy, which are called forbidden bands or energy bandgaps.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>energy band theory</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08835"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08812 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08812">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Energy interval in a lattice where no electronic state exists.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>bandgap</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>energy gap</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08835"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08813 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08813">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Interaction of two excitons resulting in their decay and radiative emission of the stored excitation energy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>exciton annihilation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08814 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08814">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Random movement of an exciton from one molecular entity to another or from one part of a molecular entity or a lattice to another part of the same kind.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>exciton diffusion</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08835"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08815 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08815">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Fission of an exciton into independent, Coulombically unbound mobile charge carriers: an electron and a hole, or a negative and a positive polaron.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>exciton dissociation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08826"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08816 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08816">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mean distance that an exciton travels from the site of its creation to the site where it radiatively or non-radiatively decays or dissociates into charge carriers.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>exciton mean pathway</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>exciton mean free path</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08817 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08817">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Interaction in which a part of the exciton energy is converted into the lattice vibrational energy and vice versa.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>exciton–phonon coupling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08835"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08818 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08818">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Magnitude of the region in a molecule over which a molecular orbital occupied with delocalized electrons is extended.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>effective conjugation length</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In a larger molecule or macromolecule, delocalized electrons are mostly divided over several to many extended orbitals of various magnitudes separated by orbitals where electrons are practically localized. Therefore, the average number of delocalized electrons per one extended orbital, or the average number of bonds or atoms or both over which a mean extended orbital is spread, are used as a measure of the extent of the electron delocalization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In a small molecule, the extent of electron delocalization is usually quantified by the number of delocalized electrons included in the extended orbital or by the number of bonds or atoms or both over which the extended molecular orbital is spread.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The extent of electron delocalization in a conjugated molecule is often termed effective conjugation length or effective extent of conjugation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>extent of electron delocalization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08789"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08819 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08819">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Quantum yield relating to the whole device or the overall process involved.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>external quantum yield</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08832"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08820 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08820">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Semiconductor whose electric properties are largely determined by the dopant &apos;impurities&apos; present.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Phosphorus-doped silicon, i.e., silicon, in which some Si atoms are replaced with phosphorus atoms, or iodine doped poly(acetylene) are examples of extrinsic semiconductors.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>extrinsic semiconductor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08778"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08833"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08882"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08821 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08821">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Transistor in which the flow of charge carriers from source to drain is controlled by the potential applied to the gate.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>FET</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>field-effect transistor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08821"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>FET</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08822 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08822">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ratio of the real maximum output power, \(P_{\rm{max}}\), that can be obtained with an actual solar cell to the product of the open-circuit potential, \(V_{\rm{oc}}\), and short-circuit photocurrent, \(I_{\rm{sc}}\): \[f_{\rm{FF}} = P_{\rm{max}}/V_{\rm{oc}}I_{\rm{sc}}\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The \(P_{\rm{max}}\) value is obtained from the cell volt-ampere characteristic as the maximum of the product \(V\!\cdot\!I = P\). Note that the product \(V_{\rm{oc}}\!\cdot\!I_{\rm{sc}}\) is not a theoretical maximum output power of the cell, since the product is zero at both these values as \(I = 0\) for the open circuit and \(V = 0\) for short circuit.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>fill factor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08875"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08823 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08823">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Generation of free charge carriers by photons.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>charge-carrier photogeneration</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>free charge-carrier photogeneration</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08824 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08824">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Constitutional isomerism of polymeric (oligomeric) chains composed of identical non symmetric repeating units consisting in different orientations of the units.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>HT-isomerism</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>regioisomerism</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In the repeating unit of a vinyl polymer: \(\ce{-CHR-CH2\bond{-}}\), the substituent-bearing group \(\ce{CHR}\) is denoted &apos;head&apos; (H) while the other one &apos;tail&apos; (T). There exist three modes how two such units can be linked in a chain: head-to-tail (HT), head-to-head (HH) and tail-to-tail (TT). </rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The uniformity/non-uniformity of repeating units’ orientation determines the regularity/irregularity of macromolecules.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>head-tail isomerism</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08868"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08825 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08825">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Interface between dissimilar crystalline or amorphous solid phases that have unequal bandgap levels.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Heterojunction allows efficient dissociation of charge carriers, which is utilized, e.g., in diode lasers, heterojunction bipolar transistors and high electron mobility transistors.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>heterojunction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08828"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08826 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08826">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Quasiparticle, a conceptual charge carrier opposite to electron, defined as the absence of electron at a position where it should be present if a lattice is in its ground state (valence band normally filled).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>electron hole</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>An electron hole is a conceptual (mathematical) opposite of an electron, while a positron is the antimatter analog of an electron.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In condensed matter, a hole is created if an electron originally occurring in the valence band is excited into conduction band thus leaving a positively charged site in its previous position.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The hole has a positive electric charge (\(+e\), where \(e\) is an elementary charge) and also an effective mass which, in most semiconductors is higher than the mass of the electron.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>hole</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08835"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08863"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08827 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08827">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Material, typically a semiconductor, in which holes are predominant mobile charge carriers.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>hole-transporting material</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08826"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08828 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08828">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Interface occurring between two similar semiconductors with equal bandgaps.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>pn-junction</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Homojunction mostly occurs at the interface between n-doped and p-doped semiconductor of the same nature, typically silicon. This type of homojunction is currently called a pn-junction.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>homojunction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08825"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08829 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08829">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Incoherent transport of charge carriers by hopping between adjacent energetically nearly equivalent localized sites.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A hopping charge carrier spends most of time sitting idle on a localized site and then hops (tunnel) to an adjacent site. As a result, the charge carrier mobility at hopping transport is low (typically below \(\pu{0.01 cm2 V s}\)) and is an increasing function of temperature.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Hopping transport is typical of molecular solids and polycrystalline and amorphous semiconductors. It takes place via electron tunneling or by overcoming energy barriers (typical of ions), and is the opposite of intra-molecular transport (band transport) of charge carriers in metallic conductors.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>hopping transport</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08780"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08791"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08806"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08830 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08830">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electron that is out of the thermal equilibrium with its environment and has enough kinetic energy to overcome potential barriers to travel between different regions of a molecule or lattice.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>hot electron</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08835"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08831 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08831">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Charge carrier generation by another charge carrier possessing a high kinetic energy, which knocks an electron out of the HOMO into the LUMO or still higher state so creating a new electron-hole pair.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>impact ionization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08778"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08826"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08837"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08832 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08832">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Quantum yield relating to a single component or a part of a device or a partial step of an overall process.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>internal quantum yield</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08833 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08833">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Semiconductor in which mobile charge carriers are generated solely by thermal excitation of electrons.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Intrinsic semiconductor is free of dopants, so that it has no energy states in the bandgap.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Pure conjugated polymers such as poly(ethene-1,2-diyl) or poly(pyrrole-2,5-diyl) and other polymers with delocalized valence electrons such as polysilanes are examples of intrinsic semiconductors.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>intrinsic semiconductor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08778"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08785"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08820"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08890"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08899"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08834 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08834">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Conducting polymer in which ionic species are mobile charge carriers.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Protons are typical charge carriers in ion-conducting polymers. Such polymers are currently referred to as proton-conducting polymers. Examples are polyanilines.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>ion-conducting polymer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08883"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08835 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08835">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Periodic grid-like ideal structure consisting of regularly arranged elementary repeating units.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Crystal (Bravais) lattices are the most famous examples of physical 3-D lattice models.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In the condensed matter physics, lattice models are currently used for modeling and theoretical studying of electric, magnetic and optical phenomena as well as materials properties. 1-Dimensional (1-D) lattice is used for modeling linear objects such as linear conjugated polymers (see Peierls distortion), 2-D and 3-D lattices for modeling planar and spatial objects, respectively.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>lattice</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08785"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08836 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08836">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Transducer-type device that converts an electric current into luminescence light.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>LED</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>light emitting diode</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08836"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>LED</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08837 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08837">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Lowest-energy molecular orbital that is unoccupied in the ground state but occupied by one or more electrons if the molecular entity occurs in its first electronically excited state.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>LUMO</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>lowest unoccupied molecular orbital</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08811"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08841"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08837"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>LUMO</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08838 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08838">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Material (polymer) that exhibits a luminescence.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>luminescent polymer</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Luminescent is in general a material, in which radiative charge carrier recombination represents a significant mode of the overall charge carrier recombination.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>luminescent material</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08781"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08864"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08839 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08839">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Change in the electric resistance of a material in response to an external magnetic field.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>magnetoresistance</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08840 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08840">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electric component that can be reversibly switched between stable low- and high-resistive states.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>memristive system</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The term memristor is derived from the phrase memory resistor.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>memristor</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08841 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08841">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Theory of quantum mechanics in which electrons in a molecule (lattice) are not assigned to particular bonds between atoms but move under the influence of all atoms.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>MO theory</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>MO theory is a counterpart of the valence bond theory in which electrons are assigned to individual bonds. MO theory fits perfectly for systems with delocalized electrons.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Within the MO theory, the wave function of \(i\mbox{-}\rm{th}\) MO, \(\varphi_{i}\), is expressed as a weighted sum of all atomic orbitals, \(\chi_{r}\) (method known as the linear combination of atomic orbitals, LCAO): \[\varphi_{i} = \sum_{r=1}^{N}c_{ri}\chi_{r}\] where the coefficient \(c_{ri}\) is the contribution of the \(r\mbox{-}\rm{th}\) atomic orbital to the \(i\mbox{-}\rm{th}\) MO.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>molecular orbital theory</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08835"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08878"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08842 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08842">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Molecule that can be reversibly switched between two or more stable states in response to a change in, e.g., its microenvironment (pH, the presence of a ligand, temperature etc.), illumination or electric current.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Molecules undergoing photoinduced reversible cis-trans isomerization or switching between redox state or protonation states (pH indicators) are examples of molecular switches.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>molecular switch</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08843 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08843">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Energy band occupied by electrons, typically the valence band.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>occupied band</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08810"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08844 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08844">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electric junction which exhibits linear and symmetric current-potential dependence.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The current is not rectified at an ohmic contact unlike the case of a Schottky barrier.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>ohmic contact</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08870"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08845 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08845">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymer that exhibits a nonlinear optical effect.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>optically nonlinear polymer</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08846 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08846">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Branch of physics that deals with the optical-to-electric and electric-to-optical transducers.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Optoelectronic effects are electroluminescence, photoconductivity and photovoltaic effect.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Optoelectronics should not be confused with electro-optics that deals with alteration of optical properties in response to an applied electric field (see electrooptic effect).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>optoelectronics</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08855"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08847 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08847">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Formation of the long-range (infinite) functional connectivity in a disperse system composed of small active particles evenly dispersed in an inactive matrix, which makes the system penetrable for a fluid or current.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Percolation is achieved at so-called percolation threshold and exists above it.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term percolation is derived from latin word percōlāre that means to filter. Porous materials in which percolation of pores exists can act as filters (pores are “active particles”). In hydrology and geology percolation relates to a slow penetration of water (liquids) through a porous material.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>percolation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08848"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08848 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08848">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Minimal volume fraction of functionally active small particles evenly dispersed in a non-active matrix, at which the long-range (infinite) functional connectivity in the system is achieved.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A desired transport through the composite material is not possible below the percolation threshold. Percolation threshold is about \(\pu{0.3}\) to \(\pu{0.35}\) (by volume) for composites with nearly spherical disperse particles while it can be even below \(\pu{0.10}\) for composites with short fibers, depending on the fibers’ aspect ratio (the length to diameter ratio).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Electrically conducting polymer composites composed of carbon black microparticles dispersed in a rubbery matrix are transposed in the electrically conducting state above percolation threshold.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>percolation threshold</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08798"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08849 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08849">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymer exhibiting photochromism.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>photochromic polymer</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08850 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08850">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymer exhibiting electrical conductivity or a clear increase in electrical conductivity under irradiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>photoconducting polymer</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08851 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08851">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Removal of charge carriers from traps in the bandgap by irradiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>photodetrapping</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08852 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08852">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Discharging of an electrically charged material by irradiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>photoinduced discharge</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08853 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08853">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Charge carrier injection by a process induced by irradiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>photoinjection</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08779"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08854 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08854">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Optically nonlinear polymer that changes refractive index when exposed to light.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>photorefractive polymer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08845"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08855 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08855">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Phenomenon consisting in converting light to electricity.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>photovoltaic effect</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08856 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08856">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Change in electric dipole moment per unit volume of dielectric material in response to its exposure to an electric field.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>dielectric polarization</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Polarization density (unit \(\pu{C m-2}\)) is defined as \(P = \varepsilon E - \varepsilon_{0} E\), where \(\varepsilon E {=D}\) is so-called electric displacement, \(E\) the electric field strength, \(\epsilon_{0}\) the vacuum permittivity and \(\varepsilon = \varepsilon_{\rm{r}}\varepsilon_0\) permittivity of the dielectric material. The ideal dependence of \(P\) on \(E\) is linear: \(P = \varepsilon_{0}\chi E\), where \(\chi = \varepsilon_{\rm{r}} - 1\) is electric susceptibility. Non-linearity of this dependence gives rise to non-linear optical effects.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The electric field causes limited reversible displacements of charged particles that cannot move freely in the dielectric material. Electronic polarization (displacements of electrons) takes a femtosecond. So it is in phase with an alternating electric field, such as that of light. Ionic polarization (of ions in ionic crystals) is also instantaneous. However orientation polarization based on reorientation of permanent dipoles is significantly slower (delayed) and thus often out of phase with the light field.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polarization density</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08790"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08857 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08857">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Orientation of dipoles or dipolar domains inside a material by using a sufficiently strong electric field.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Poling is usually achieved by applying a high strength electric field to the opposite surfaces of a polymer film to obtain piezoelectric polymer or ferroelectric polymer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>poling</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08858 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08858">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Increase in the electric conductivity of a semiconductor or insulator by exposing it to an electric field.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Valence electrons in an insulator or poor semiconductor that is exposed to an external electric field obtain a part of the excitation energy from an external electric field.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Poole–Frenkel effect</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08859 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08859">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ionic conductivity mediated by protons (more exactly by hydrogen cations properly called hydrons).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>hydronic conductivity</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>protonic conductivity</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08860 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08860">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Transformation of a semiconducting or insulating material to conducting state by protonation (hydronation) of its molecules.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>hydron doping</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The proton doping is crucial for polyanilines that are good conductors only in the protonated state. Deprotonation (dehydronation), as a reverse transformation is achieved by treating the doped polymer with an alkali.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>proton doping</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08883"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08861 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08861">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Effect occurring if at least one dimension of a structure is comparable to the de Broglie wavelength of the electron-hole pair and the motion of charge carriers is thus quantized by the structure boundary.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>quantum size effect</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A material composed of quantum-size structures exhibits electronic and optic properties different from those of bigger structures. A quantum size effect can be thus detected as an alteration of electronic and optical properties of a solid when reducing its particle size down to nanometer scale.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Based on the confinement dimensionality the following quantum-size objects are distinguished: quantum dot: three-dimensional confinement; example: nanoparticles of CdS;quantum wire: two-dimensional confinement; example: carbon nanotubes;quantum well: one-dimensional (linear) confinement; example: nanoscale layer of GaAs sandwiched between two layers of a wide-band-gap semiconductor such as AlAs.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>quantum confinement effect</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08862 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08862">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrically conducting polymer composite that, however, is conducting only when pressed due to thinning the insulating polymer matrix membranes so much that they allow electron tunneling through them.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Quantum tunneling polymer composites are utilized as pressure sensors and touchable control panels of electronic devices.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>quantum tunneling polymer composite</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08798"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08806"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08847"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08848"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08872"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08863 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08863">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Discrete disorder in a multi-particulate system that exhibits particle-specific behavior and properties.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A real particle can exist by itself, out of other real particles, while a quasiparticle cannot. It exists only within a multi-particulate system, like a gas bubble inside water or beer. It looks and behaves like a particle, but it does not exist outside liquid. Such a bubble is a macroscopic quasiparticle.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Characteristics typical of particles, such as the mass (referred to as effective mass), momentum, energy, velocity, mobility and ability to undergo collisions can be assigned to quasiparticles.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Examples of quasiparticles related to electromagnetic field responsive materials are electron hole, exciton, magnon, phonon, plasmon, polaron and soliton.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>There are two main classes of quasiparticles: those of the excitation type whose motion corresponds to a motion of individual particles interacting with other parts of the system; examples are exciton, hole, soliton and polaron;those that originate from a synchronized collective motion of the whole system, referred to as collective excitations or collective modes; examples are magnon, plasmon and phonon.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>quasiparticle</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08803"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08826"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08864 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08864">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Charge carrier recombination in which the energy released is emitted as a photon of corresponding energy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>radiative recombination</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Radiative charge carrier recombination occurs if a mobile electron meets a hole with which it forms an exciton that then decays by the radiative electron transition to the ground state.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>radiative charge carrier recombination</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08781"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08826"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08876"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08865 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08865">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Doping of a parent polymer consisting in oxidation or reduction of its molecules.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Redox doping can be accomplished by a chemical reaction or an electrode redox process.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>redox doping</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08866 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08866">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Regular polymer or an irregular polymer whose macromolecules contain non-uniformly oriented simple asymmetric repeating units.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The difference between regioirregular, irregular and regular chains is shown in the following chart.  </rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>regioirregular polymer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08824"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08868"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08867 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08867">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Regioirregular polymer with randomly oriented asymmetric constitutional repeating units in its chains.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>regiorandom polymer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08868"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08868 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08868">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Regular polymer whose chains are composed of uniformly oriented asymmetric constitutional repeating units.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The difference between regioregular and regioirregular poly(3-alkylthiophene) chains is as follows: </rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>regioregular polymer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08866"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08869 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08869">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Surface-conducting polymer composite obtained by crystallization of a low-molar-mass organic conductor on an insulating or semiconducting polymer support.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>reticulate doped polymer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08774"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08870 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08870">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Potential energy barrier at a semiconductor-metal junction with the current rectifying characteristics.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The diode based on the Schottky barrier is a Schottky diode or a hot carrier diode. Schottky diodes show switching times down to below \(\pu{1 ns}\) and a low applied potential drop (\(\pu{0.15}\) to \(\pu{0.45 V}\)).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Schottky barrier</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08871 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08871">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Transformation of the input monochromatic radiation beam into a double frequency (half wavelength) beam.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>frequency doubling</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Frequency doubling is a nonlinear optic effect typical of non-centrosymmetric crystals with a high second-order electric susceptibility. It is utilized in the laser and radio-communication techniques to obtain higher frequency signals, e.g., that of the wavelength \(\pu{533 nm}\) from the signal of \(\pu{1066 nm}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>second harmonic generation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08872 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08872">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Transducer-type device that responds to a specific property of its surroundings or to a coming stimulus by a visually readable change in its state or by generation of a signal that can be read by an observer or instrument.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>sensor</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08873 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08873">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Photocurrent flowing through a short circuited solar cell (if the electric potential across the cell is zero).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>short circuit photocurrent</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08875"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08874 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08874">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Effect of size on properties of the material or device which incorporates it.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>size effect</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08875 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08875">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Device that directly transduces electromagnetic radiation into electricity based on the photovoltaic effect.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>solar cell</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08855"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08876 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08876">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Radiative charge carrier recombination induced by incident photons of appropriate wavelength, which synchronize emission of other photons as to their wavelength, phase, polarization and direction of travel.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>If this process takes place in a system with a high population of excited states, it leads to stimulated emission, the principle of the function of lasers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>stimulated radiative charge carrier recombination</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08864"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08877 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08877">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Charge carrier injection by a thermally activated processes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>thermal injection</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08779"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08878 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08878">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Quantum mechanics theory of chemical bonding that treats electrons as being assigned to individual bonds between particular atoms.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>VB theory</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The VB theory views a chemical bond as a result of weak coupling (overlapping) of atomic or hybridized atomic orbitals or of both and describes delocalization of electrons (inherent in molecular orbital theory) as a result of resonance mixing of two or more possible energetically equivalent or nearly equivalent structures.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>VB theory and molecular orbital theory are closely related methods that become equivalent when extended enough. Increasing mixing of VB orbitals gives in the limit extended molecular orbitals.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>valence bond theory</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08789"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08841"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08879 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08879">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Minimum thermodynamic work (energy) needed to remove an electron from a solid to a point in the vacuum nearby the solid surface.\[\mathit{\Phi} = -e{\mathit{\Psi}} - \mu\]where \(e\) is the elementary charge (\(-e\) is charge of electron), \(\mathit{\Psi}\) is the electric potential nearby the surface and \(\mu\) is the energy of Fermi level.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Nearby the solid surface means that the released electron remains so close to the solid that it interacts with the surface. Hence the work function depends on the crystal face and contamination.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>work function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08880 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08880">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymers whose macromolecules are rich in fluorocarbon groups (structure-based polymer class name).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Fluoropolymers are often used as electrets. Membranes from ionomeric fluoropolymers are used in electrochemical cells.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>fluoropolymers</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08881 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08881">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Dynamic polymeric entity whose macromolecules are composed of alternating simple or oligomeric molecules with two or more chelate end-groups (so-called unimers) and metal ions (so-called ion couplers) that are linked to chains by reversible coordination bonds (structure-based polymer class name):</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>MSP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>An MSP is spontaneously formed upon mixing unimer(s) with ion couplers, because the activation energy of coordination binding is low. Therefore, the degree of polymerization of MSP is controlled by thermodynamics: it is low in a solution and/or at increased temperature but high in the solid state at room temperature. Due to these features, MSPs belong to the family of constitutional dynamic polymers, so-called dynamers. Constitutional dynamics gives to MSPs processing advantages and capability of self-healing.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Examples are MSPs formed by self-assembly of 2,5″-bis(-2,2′:6′,2″-terpyridin-4′-yl)-5,2′:5′,2″- terthiophene with metal ions \(\ce{M}\). The MSP with \(\ce{Fe^{2+}}\) ions exhibited electrochromism while that with \(\ce{Zn^{2+}}\) ions showed the singlet fission. </rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>metallo-supramolecular polymer</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08881"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>MSP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08882 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08882">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymers prepared from acetylene (ethyne), a substituted acetylene, or both in admixture by a polymerization involving one or more triple bonds (source-based polymer class name).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Although polyacetylenes opened the boom in organic electronics, they did not find commercial application due to insufficient stability under workload and in air. However, polymers of diarylacetylenes are stable and found applications as materials for gas separation membranes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Polymers of buta-1,3-diyne and its derivatives are a subclass of polyacetylenes referred to as polydiacetylenes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Polymers prepared by polycondensations from diols, triols, diamines, dicarboxylic acids etc. containing triple bonds (e.g., from but-2-yne-1,4-diol) are excluded. Such polymers are classified as unsaturated polyesters, unsaturated polyamides, etc.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polyacetylenes</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08883 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08883">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymers prepared exclusively from aniline, or substituted aniline, or both in admixture (source-based class name).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Examples of polyanilines (PANI) in the leucoemeraldine base form (see Note 2) are:  (the first name is the structure-based while the second one is the source-based name of the polymer).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>PANIs carrying strongly acidic group such as sulfonic or phosphonic group attached to benzene rings are referred to as self-doped PANIs, since the attached acidic groups afford protons ensuring proton doping of main chains.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Polyanilines exist in various forms (see scheme) differing in the degrees of oxidation (p-doping) and protonation (proton doping). Gem-based names (leucoemeraldine, emeraldine, pernigraniline) were given to particular PANI forms in 1910 by Green and Woodhead. </rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Redox doping of PANI-LB by the electron transfer (left branch in the scheme) directly gives PANI salts ES and PS, whereas dehydrogenation of LB (right branch in the scheme) gives PANI bases: EB and PB. PANI bases are reversibly transformed to corresponding PANI salts by proton doping. The intermediate oxidation state forms, protoemeraldine (product of one-electron oxidation) and nigraniline (product of three-electron oxidation of LB) are sometimes recognized in the literature.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The PANI ES form is an intrinsically conducting polymer (conductivity of \(\pu{1-10 S cm-1}\)) in which positive polarons and protons are major charge carriers. It is easy accessible by electrochemical or chemical oxidation of anilines in an acidic aqueous medium and it found many applications in electrochemical and photovoltaic cells, batteries, displays and others.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polyanilines</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08775"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08860"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08884 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08884">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymers composed of macromolecules containing exclusively arenediyl (formerly arylene) or heteroarenediyl (formerly heteroarylene) units in the backbone:  where Ar is a divalent arenediyl or heteroarenediyl group (structure-based polymer class name).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Polyphenylenes (Ar is phenylene or substituted phenylene) are a subclass of polyarylenes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polyarylenes</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08885 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08885">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymers composed of macromolecules containing exclusively alternating arenediyl or heterarenediyl (formerly arylene or heteroarylene, respectively) and ethynediyl (formerly ethynylene) constitutional repeating units in the backbone:  where Ar is a divalent arenediyl or heterarenediyl group (structure-based polymer class name).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A polymer for which Ar is phenylene is a polyphenyleneethynylene.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polyarylenethynylenes</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08886 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08886">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymers composed of macromolecules containing exclusively alternating arenediyl or heteroarenediyl (formerly arylene or heteroarylene, respectively) and ethene-1,2-diyl (formerly vinylene) or substituted vinylene constitutional repeating units in the backbone:  where Ar is a divalent arenediyl or heteroarenediyl group and \(\ce{R^{1}}\) and \(\ce{R^{2}}\) are each hydrogen, or an alkyl, aryl or heteroaryl group (structure-based polymer class name).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Polyarylenevinylenes are a subclass of conjugated polymers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Polyarylenevinylenes are of interest mainly due to their electroluminescent properties.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polyarylenevinylenes</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08785"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08897"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08887 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08887">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymers composed of macromolecules containing azomethine (azanylylidenemethanylylidene) linkages in the backbone: \[\ce{-N=CR-}\] where \(\ce{R}\) is hydrogen, or an alkyl or aryl group (structure-based polymer class name).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>poly (Schiff bases)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A polymer with only pendant azomethine groups is excluded.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The name azomethine is derived from the traditional names &apos;azo&apos; for azanylylidene group \(\ce{-N=}\) and methine&apos; for the methanylylidene group \(\ce{-CH=}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The original distinction between polyazomethines and poly(Schiff base)s, which is that a group \(\ce{R}\) in \(\ce{-N=CR-}\) linkage cannot be hydrogen in a polyazomethine but can be hydrogen in a poly(Schiff base), making polyazomethines a subclass of poly(Schiff base)s, has nearly completely disappeared.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polyazomethines</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08888 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08888">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymers composed of macromolecules containing benzimidazole groups in the backbone, such as groups (structure-based polymer class name).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Polybenzimidazoles are proton-conducting polymers with a high thermal stability that found applications as membranes for example in fuel cells or fibers for flame resistant clothes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polybenzimidazoles</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08834"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08889 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08889">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymer prepared from buta-1,3-diyne (diacetylene) or a substituted buta-1,3-diyne or both in admixture (source-based polymer class name).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>General formula of a regular polydiacetylene that is prepared by the photo-induced topochemical polymerization of a crystalline 1,3-diyne is as follows:  where \(\ce{R^{1}}\) and \(\ce{R^{2}}\) are each hydrogen or an alkyl, aryl or heteroaryl group.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Polydiacetylenes are a subclass of conjugated polymers and of polyacetylenes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polydiacetylenes</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08785"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08882"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08890 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08890">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymer composed of macromolecules exclusively consisting of ethene-1,2-diyl (formerly called vinylene) constitutional repeating units.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>poly(acetylene) (source-based name)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>poly(vinylene) (former structure-based name)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>poly(ethene-1,2-diyl)</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08882"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08891 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08891">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymer prepared from 3,4-(ethylenedioxy)thiophene (source-based polymer name).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PEDOT</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>poly{3,4-[ethylene-1,2-bis(oxy)]thiophene-2,5-diyl}</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>PEDOT is an intrinsically conducting polythiophene class polymer that is mostly used as a blend with sulfonated polystyrene (PEDOT-PSS), which is produced by oxidative polymerization of 3,4-[ethylene-1,2-bis(oxy)]thiophene (traditional name 3,4-(ethylenedioxy)thiophene, therefore abbreviated as EDOT) in an aqueous solution of partly neutralized sulfonated polystyrene.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The PEDOT-PSS is stable in ambient environment, water-dispersible thus processable from aqueous systems and considered as a non-toxic material for the flexible and printed organic electronics and photovoltaics. PEDOT-PSS belongs among the most often used materials for organic as well as organic/inorganic electronic and photovoltaic devices, for antistatic and electrochromic coatings and films, and also exhibits the thermoelectric effect.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>poly[3,4-(ethylenedioxy)thiophene]</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08891"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PEDOT</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08892 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08892">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymers composed of macromolecules containing fluorenediyl or substituted fluorenediyl (mainly 2,7-diyl) constitutional repeating units in the backbone (structure-based polymer class name).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Polyfluorenes are used as photoconducting, photoluminescent and electroluminescent materials for organic light-emitting diodes, organic thin film transistors and polymer solar cells.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polyfluorenes</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08893 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08893">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymers composed of macromolecules containing cyclic dicarboximide groupings in the backbone:  where \(\ce{R^{1}}\), \(\ce{R^{2}}\), \(\ce{R^{3}}\) and \(\ce{R^{4}}\) are groups with connectivity one, two, three, and four, respectively (structure-based polymer class name).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In electronics, polyimides are mainly used as highly flexible insulating and passivation layers in integrated circuits and microelectronic chips.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polyimides</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08894 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08894">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymers composed of macromolecules containing exclusively oxadiazole rings in the backbone (structure-based polymer class name).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Poly(1,2,3-oxadiazole-4,5-diyl) and poly(1,3,4-oxadiazole-2,5-diyl) are conjugated polymers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polyoxadiazoles</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08785"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08895 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08895">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymers composed of macromolecules containing exclusively \(\upsigma\)-bonded benzene or substituted benzene rings in the backbone:  where \(\ce{R^{1}}\), \(\ce{R^{2}}\), \(\ce{R^{3}}\) and \(\ce{R^{4}}\) are each hydrogen or an univalent group (structure-based polymer class name).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Polyphenylenes are a subclass of polyarylenes and also a subclass of conjugated polymers except for poly(1,3-phenylene)s which are not conjugated polymers. </rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polyphenylenes</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08785"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08884"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08896 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08896">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymer composed of macromolecules containing exclusively alternating phenylene or substituted phenylene and ethynediyl (formerly ethynylene) constitutional repeating units in the backbone:  where \(\ce{R^{1}}\), \(\ce{R^{2}}\), \(\ce{R^{3}}\) and \(\ce{R^{4}}\) are each hydrogen, alkyl, aryl, halogen, trialkylsilyl etc. (structure-based polymer class name).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Polyphenylenethynylenes are a subclass of polyarylenethynylenes and also a subclass of conjugated polymers (except for those containing 1,3-phenylene constitutional repeating units).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polyphenylenethynylenes</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08785"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08885"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08897 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08897">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymers composed of macromolecules containing exclusively alternating phenylene or substituted phenylene and ethene-1,2-diyl (formerly vinylene) or substituted ethene-1,2-diyl constitutional repeating units in the backbone:  where \(\ce{R^{1}}\), \(\ce{R^{2}}\), \(\ce{R^{3}}\), \(\ce{R^{4}}\), \(\ce{R^{5}}\) and \(\ce{R^{6}}\) are each hydrogen, alkyl, aryl, heteroaryl, halogen, trialkylsilyl, etc. (structure-based polymer class name).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PPV</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>PPVs found applications mainly in the fields of photovoltaic cells and electroluminescent devices.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Polyphenylenevinylenes are a subclass of polyarylenevinylenes and also a subclass of conjugated polymers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polyphenylenevinylenes</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08785"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08886"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08897"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PPV</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08898 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08898">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymers composed of macromolecules with backbones composed of pyrrole rings (structure-based polymer class name definition).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Ppy</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Polypyrroles belong among conjugated polymers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Polypyrroles found applications mainly in the field of sensors. Applications in electronic devices are still limited owing to insufficient stability of PPy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polypyrroles</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08785"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08898"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Ppy</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08899 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08899">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymers composed of macromolecules containing exclusively silicon atoms in the backbone:  where \(\ce{R^{1}}\) and \(\ce{R^{2}}\) are each hydrogen or an alkyl, aryl or heteroaryl group (structure-based polymer class name).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Polysilanes are \(\upsigma\)-conjugated polymers which show a good photoconductivity, however, they degrade when exposed to UV light.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Polysilanes were earlier named polysilylenes. A use of this term is discouraged.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polysilanes</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08785"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08900 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08900">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymers composed of macromolecules containing tetrazine or hydrogenated tetrazine rings in the backbone (structure-based polymer class name):</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A typical polytetrazine contains 1,2,4,5-tetrazine or hydrogenated 1,2,4,5-tetrazine rings since 1,2,3,4- as well as 1,2,3,5-tetrazine rings are unstable.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polytetrazines</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08901 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08901">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymers composed of macromolecules containing thiadiazole rings in the backbone (structure-based polymer class name).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Poly(1,2,3-thiadiazole-4,5-diyl) and poly(1,3,4-thiadiazole-2,5-diyl) are conjugated polymers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polythiadiazoles</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08785"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08902 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08902">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymers composed of macromolecules containing thiazole rings in the backbone:  where R is hydrogen, alkyl, aryl, heteroaryl, substituted alkyl, substituted aryl or substituted heteroaryl (structure-based polymer class name).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:label>polythiazoles</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08903 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08903">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymers composed of macromolecules containing exclusively thiophene rings in the backbone (structure-based polymer class name).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Poly(thiophene-2,5-diyl), often written as poly(thiophene), is the generic polythiophene.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Polythiophenes are a subclass of conjugated polymers. The highest practical applications has found poly[3,4-(ethylenedioxy)thiophene] (PEDOT). Regioregular poly(3-alkylthiophenes) are studied as materials for organic solar cells. They become conducting upon oxidative doping.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Regular polymers of a monosubstituted thiophene are prepared by catalytic coupling polymerization of the corresponding thiophene derivative or its defined dimers (dimers mostly provide regular but regioirregular polymers, see the chart below). Oxidative polymerization of an asymmetric thiophene monomer gives an irregular polythiophene with considerably worse functional properties. </rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polythiophenes</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08785"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08866"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08875"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08904 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08904">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymers prepared from a vinylcarbazole or a substituted vinylcarbazole or both in admixture (source-based polymer class name).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A typical polyvinylcarbazole is poly(N-vinylcarbazole) that is usually prepared by a radical or a coordination chain polymerization of N-vinylcarbazole.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Polyvinylcarbazoles are photoconducting polymers that transport charge carriers via carbazolyl pendant groups. They found wide applications, for example, in xerographic photocopying.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polyvinylcarbazoles</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08850"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08905 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08905">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymers composed of macromolecules containing exclusively ethene-1,2-diyl (formerly vinylene) or substituted ethene-1,2-diyl constitutional repeating units in the backbone: \[\ce{-CR^{1}=CR^{2}-}\] where \(\ce{R^{1}}\) and \(\ce{R^{2}}\) are each hydrogen or an alkyl, aryl, or heteroaryl group (structure-based polymer class name).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Delocalization of electrons in conjugated chains gives to main-chain single bonds a partial character of double bonds, which restrict free rotations around these bonds which, therefore, show the cis-trans like isomerism. Four basic stereoregular polyvinylene chains can be derived: </rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Polyvinylenes are almost exclusively prepared by polymerization of acetylene derivatives. Therefore, based on the source, they are mostly polyacetylenes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polyvinylenes</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08789"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08882"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08906 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08906">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymers composed of macromolecules consisting of zwitterionic repeating units (structure-based polymer class name).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>poly(inner salts)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>polyamphions</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>zwitterionic polymers</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>From the chemical structure point of view, a zwitterionic polymer is an ampholytic polymer containing ionic groups of opposite sign, commonly in the same pendant group.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Polybetaines are a subclass of polyzwitterions.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Polyzwitterions are mostly polyelectrolytes, since their zwitterionic groups are usually located in pendant groups (not in main-chain groups).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Unlike polyampholytes, polyzwitterions have anions and cations in the same monomeric unit.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polyzwitterions</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08907 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08907">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chain polymerization in which propagation involves reaction between a growing-chain end and an activated monomer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>AMP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The activator in activated-monomer polymerization should not be referred to as a catalyst unless it is established that it is a catalyst.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>activated-monomer polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08936"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08944"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08966"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08989"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08990"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08907"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>AMP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08908 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08908">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Term indicating control of a certain kinetic feature of a polymerization or structural aspect of the polymer molecules formed, or both.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Generally, the adjective &apos;controlled&apos; should not be used without specifying the particular kinetic or structural feature that is subject to control.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The expression &apos;controlled polymerization&apos; is sometimes used to describe a radical or ionic polymerization in which reversible deactivation of the chain carriers is an essential component of the mechanism, interrupting the propagation to secure control of one or more kinetic features of the polymerization or one or more structural aspects of the macromolecules formed, or both.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The expression controlled radical polymerization is sometimes used to describe a radical polymerization conducted in the presence of reagents that lead to, e.g., atom-transfer radical polymerization (ATRP), aminoxyl-mediated radical polymerization (AMRP), or reversible-addition-fragmentation chain transfer (RAFT) polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>controlled polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08912"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08913"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08917"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08924"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08935"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08973"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08985"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08909 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08909">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reversible-deactivation polymerization in which the deactivation of the active species involves degenerative chain transfer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DTP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>degenerate-transfer polymerization</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>This term has often been specifically associated with degenerative chain-transfer radical polymerization. However, there are also degenerative chain-transfer processes involving non-radical mechanisms (e.g., cationic, anionic, or coordination polymerization).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>degenerative-transfer polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08912"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08929"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08951"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08995"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08909"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>DTP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08910 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08910">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chain polymerization in which a specific atom or group is intramolecularly transferred to remain associated with the active chain-end during the course of polymerization.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>GTP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Group-transfer polymerization is most often a reversible-deactivation polymerization or a coordination addition polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Other forms of chain polymerization commonly referred to as group-transfer polymerizations include catalyst-transfer polymerization and rare-earth-mediated or lanthanide-mediated coordination polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term group-transfer polymerization is often specifically associated with polymerization of (meth)acrylates and acrylamides initiated by silyl ketene acetals such as 1-methoxy-2-methyl-1-(trimethylsiloxy)prop-1-ene (traditional name is methyl trimethylsilyl dimethylketene acetal) and catalysed by nucleophiles.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>group-transfer polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08912"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08977"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08996"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09008"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09010"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08910"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GTP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08911 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08911">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chain polymerization in which monomer units are sequentially inserted into a specific site within a polymer structure with retention of chain-end functionality.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>polyinsertion</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The term insertion polymerization is most commonly used in the context of coordination polymerization but is occasionally also used to describe some forms of reversible-deactivation radical polymerization such as RAFT polymerization and aminoxyl-mediated radical polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term polyinsertion is almost exclusively used in the context of coordination polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>insertion polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08916"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08917"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08935"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08995"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08997"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08912 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08912">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chain polymerization, propagated by chain carriers that are deactivated reversibly, bringing them into active-dormant equilibria of which there might be more than one.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RDP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>controlled reversible-deactivation polymerization.</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>reversible-deactivation polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08910"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08916"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08981"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08988"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08999"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08912"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>RDP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08913 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08913">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Term indicating control of a certain kinetic feature of a radical polymerization or structural aspect of the polymer molecules formed by radical polymerization, or both.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CRP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The adjective &apos;controlled&apos; should not be used without specifying the particular kinetic or structural feature that is subject to control.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The expression &apos;controlled radical polymerization&apos; is sometimes used as a synonym for reversible-deactivation radical polymerization (RDRP), e.g., atom-transfer radical polymerization (ATRP), aminoxyl-mediated radical polymerization (AMRP), or reversible-addition-fragmentation chain-transfer (RAFT) polymerization. This is acceptable subject to the proviso stated in Note 1.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term diffusion-controlled radical polymerization is sometimes used to indicate that the rate of termination is determined by the rate of diffusion of the active sites.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>controlled radical polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08908"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08916"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08917"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08924"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08935"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08913"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CRP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08914 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08914">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Radical polymerization in which chain termination and irreversible chain transfer are absent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Radical polymerizations, controlled radical polymerizations and reversible-deactivation radical polymerizations are not living radical polymerizations as there is a non-zero probability of chain termination.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term living radical polymerization can be used to refer to a hypothetical radical polymerization that occurs without chain termination.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Use of the adjectives pseudo-living, quasi-living, &apos;living&apos; (within quotation marks) or other terms that containing the word living to describe a radical polymerization with only some living characteristics is deprecated.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>living radical polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08913"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08916"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08946"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08966"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08915 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08915">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Radical polymerization in which a cyclic monomer yields a monomeric unit that is either acyclic or contains fewer rings than the cyclic monomer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:label>radical ring-opening polymerization</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08916 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08916">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chain polymerization, propagated by radicals that are deactivated reversibly, bringing them into active-dormant equilibria of which there might be more than one.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RDRP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>controlled reversible-deactivation radical polymerization</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Atom-transfer radical polymerization, reversible-addition-fragmentation chain-transfer polymerization, and stable-radical-mediated polymerization all fall into this polymerization category.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Names containing the word &apos;living&apos; are deprecated. Definition 84 in the kinetics document stipulates that chain termination and irreversible chain transfer must be absent if a polymerization is to be regarded as living. Examples of deprecated terms include living radical polymerization, controlled/living polymerization and quasi-living polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The abbreviated term controlled radical polymerization shall be permitted, provided that its context (i.e., the nature of the control) is specified at the first occurrence.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When the equilibria are established rapidly compared to the rate of propagation, the process may show many of the observable characteristics associated with living polymerization. One consequence of rapid equilibration is that it may become possible to exert control over the shape of the chain-length distribution, prepare polymers of low dispersity (i.e., polymers with a high degree of uniformity), and extend chains to form block copolymers, the block lengths of which are of low dispersity, by the sequential addition of monomers. Although some termination is inevitably taking place, the equilibrium between the remaining propagating radicals and dormant species is maintained.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>reversible-deactivation radical polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08913"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08914"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08921"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08924"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08935"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08952"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08916"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>RDRP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08917 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08917">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Stable-radical-mediated polymerization in which the deactivation of the radicals involves reversible coupling with aminoxyl radicals.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>AMRP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>aminoxyl-mediated polymerization</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &apos;nitroxide&apos; is deprecated in IUPAC nomenclature. The recommended term is &apos;aminoxyl&apos;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>aminoxyl-mediated radical polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08921"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08917"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>AMRP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08918 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08918">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reversible-deactivation radical polymerization mediated by a cobalt complex.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CMRP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The mechanism of cobalt-mediated radical polymerization may involve stable-radical-mediated polymerization (SRMP), degenerative chain-transfer radical polymerization, (DTRP), atom-transfer radical polymerization (ATRP) or some combination of these depending on the specific reagents and reaction conditions.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>cobalt-mediated radical polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08916"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08920"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08921"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08924"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08929"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08918"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CMRP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08919 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08919">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Stable-radical-mediated polymerization where the initiator and dormant species are iniferters.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In the iniferter process the dominant mechanism for control is stable-radical mediated polymerization (SRMP) but the control mechanism may also involve degenerate chain transfer radical polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>iniferter process</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08921"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08929"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08952"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08955"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08964"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08920 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08920">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reversible-deactivation radical polymerization mediated by an organometallic reagent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>OMRP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The mechanism of organometallic-meditated radical polymerization may involve stable-radical-mediated polymerization (SRMP), degenerative chain-transfer radical polymerization (DTRP), atom-transfer radical polymerization (ATRP) or some combination of these depending on the specific reagents and reaction conditions.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term organometallic-meditated radical polymerization is not usually used in describing an atom-transfer radical polymerization process.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>organometallic-mediated radical polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08916"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08918"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08921"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08924"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08929"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08920"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>OMRP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08921 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08921">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reversible-deactivation radical polymerization in which the deactivation involves reversible coupling with stable (persistent) radicals.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SRMP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Initialization may involve decomposition of an initiator in the presence of monomer and the stable radical. Polymerization then starts when there is complete consumption of the stable radical.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Processes known to involve stable-radical-mediated polymerization include aminoxyl-mediated radical polymerization and some forms of organometallic-mediated radical polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The species identified as stable radicals in stable-radical-mediated polymerization are not necessarily stable in a thermodynamic sense.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The use of the term &apos;free radical&apos; is deprecated in IUPAC terminology.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>stable-radical-mediated polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08916"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08917"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08920"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08956"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08970"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08921"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SRMP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08922 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08922">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reversible-deactivation radical polymerization in which the dormant species is a stable (persistent) radical and the deactivation step involves reversible fragmentation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RAFRP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In some cases, the dormant polymer may undergo reversible coupling.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Initialization usually involves decomposition of an initiator in the presence of monomer and the reagent. Polymerization commences when there is complete consumption of the mediator by initiator-derived radicals.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>reversible-addition-fragmentation radical polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08916"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08935"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08952"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08956"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08922"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>RAFRP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08923 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08923">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reversible-addition-fragmentation radical polymerization mediated by a carbonothioyl compound.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The commonly used term, thiocarbonyl, is not consistent with IUPAC nomenclature.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>carbonothioyl-mediated radical polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08922"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08924 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08924">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reversible-deactivation radical polymerization in which the deactivation of the radicals involves reversible atom-transfer or reversible group transfer to usually, though not exclusively, transition-metal complexes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ATRP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The activator is often incorrectly termed a catalyst. The term catalyst should only be used when referring to a species which is not changed as a consequence of reaction.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term ATRP (unqualified) usually refers to copper-mediated ISET-ATRP.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>There are various forms of ATRP that differ in details of the intimate mechanism, the reaction conditions and/or the reagents used. These include:Conventional, traditional or normal ATRP where the initial reaction mixture comprises an initiator (typically an alkyl halide) and an activator (the species on right-hand side of Figure).Reverse ATRP where the initial reaction mixture comprises a conventional initiator as a source of radicals (e.g., a dialkyldiazene or a peroxide) and a deactivator (the species on left hand side of Figure).AGET-ATRP (Activator Generated by Electron Transfer) where the activator is generated in situ by an electron transfer from a reducing agent at the commencement of the polymerization.ARGET-ATRP (Activator ReGenerated by Electron Transfer) where the activator is continuously regenerated by electron transfer from a reducing agent.lICAR-ATRP (Initiators for Continuous Activator Regeneration) - ARGET-ATRP where the reducing agent is a radical derived from a conventional initiator (e.g., a dialkyldiazene).SARA-ATRP (Supplemental Activators and Reducing Agents) where the polymerization commences with the activator (e.g., a zero-valent metal or an inorganic sulfite) which is a both a supplemental activator and a reducing agent for the deactivator.eATRP (electrochemically-mediated ATRP) where the activator is continuously regenerated by electrical current.photoATRP (photochemically-mediated ATRP) where the activator is continuously regenerated by photoreduction. The reduction can be facilitated by the presence of electron donors.mechanoATRP (mechanically-mediated ATRP) where the activator is continuously regenerated by mechanical forces through the use of the piezoelectric effect or ultrasound.PET-ATRP (photoinduced electron transfer ATRP) where activation involves electron-transfer from a photo-excited activator to an initiator which then dissociates to give an initiating species.O-ATRP (organic ATRP), a form of PET-ATRP where the activator is an organic photoredox catalyst.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>atom-transfer radical polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08916"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08925"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08926"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08927"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08949"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08959"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08924"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ATRP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08925 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08925">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Atom-transfer radical polymerization where the activation step involves direct atom or group transfer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ISET-ATRP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>ATRP is sometimes used as a synonym for ISET-ATRP. The term ISET-ATRP can be used to specify that the mechanism of ATRP involves direct atom or group transfer as distinct from single electron transfer followed by atom or group transfer or OSET-ATRP.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>inner-sphere electron transfer atom-transfer radical polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08924"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08927"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08925"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ISET-ATRP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08926 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08926">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Atom-transfer radical polymerization in which the deactivation of the radicals involves reversible atom transfer or reversible group transfer to a non-metallic reagent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NM-ATRP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>NM-ATRP may be referred to as ATRP.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The activator is often incorrectly termed a catalyst. The term catalyst should only be used when referring to a species which is not changed as a consequence of reaction being considered.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>There are various forms of NM-ATRP that differ in details of the intimate mechanism, the reaction conditions and/or the reagents used. These include: RCTP (reversible chain-transfer-catalyzed polymerization). The group transferred (\(\ce{X}\)) is often iodine and examples of the activator (\(\ce{R-X}\)) are certain nitrogen, phosphorus, germanium, or tin compounds, which include germanium tetraiodide and 1-iodopyrrolidine-2,5-dione (traditional name is N-iodosuccinimide). Use of the term RCTP is deprecated. The mechanism is proposed to involve inner-sphere electron transfer atom-transfer radical polymerization (ISET-ATRP).RCMP (reversible-complexation-mediated radical polymerization) where the activator (typically an alkyl amine) reacts with an initiator (typically an alkyl iodide) by molecule-assisted homolysis to form the initiating species and an activator-complex.O-ATRP (organocatalyzed atom-transfer radical polymerization). A photochemically-mediated ATRP in which an organic photoredox catalyst such as perylene, a 5,10-diaryl-5,10-dihydrophenazine or a 10-aryl-10H-phenoxazine is used as the activator. The mechanism is proposed to involve outer-sphere electron transfer atom-transfer radical polymerization (OSET-ATRP).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>non-metal-mediated atom-transfer radical polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08924"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08925"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08927"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08949"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08959"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08926"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>NM-ATRP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08927 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08927">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Atom-transfer radical polymerization where the activation step involves consecutive single electron transfer and atom or group transfer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>OSET-ATRP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>single-electron-transfer RDRP (SET-RDRP)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>OSET-ATRP may be referred to as ATRP.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Terms containing the phrase &apos;living radical polymerization&apos; are deprecated.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The terms SET-RDRP (single-electron-transfer reversible-deactivation radical polymerization), SET-LRP (single-electron-transfer living radical polymerization), and zero-valent metal-mediated polymerization (e.g., \(\ce{Cu^{0}}\)-mediated radical polymerization) are sometimes used to describe a process where the polymerization commences with the activator in the form of a zero-valent metal, e.g., \(\ce{Cu^{0}}\). The use of terms that might imply an outer sphere electron-transfer mechanism without evidence that the reaction proceeds by OSET-ATRP is deprecated.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>outer-sphere electron transfer ATRP</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08924"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08925"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08928 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08928">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reversible-deactivation radical polymerization mediated by transition-metal complexes or organometallic compounds.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>transition-metal-mediated reversible-deactivation radical polymerization</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Cobalt-mediated radical polymerization is usually a form of organometallic-mediated radical polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Transition metal mediated polymerization is an overarching term for atom-transfer radical polymerization (ATRP) and organometallic-mediated radical polymerization (OMRP). It is sometimes used as a synonym for transition-metal-mediated atom-transfer radical polymerization. This use of the term in that context without explanation is deprecated since it also includes organometallic-mediated radical polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>transition-metal-mediated radical polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08916"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08918"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08920"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08924"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08929 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08929">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reversible-deactivation radical polymerization in which the deactivation of the radicals involves degenerative transfer (DT) of a group (or atom).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DTRP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>degenerate chain-transfer radical polymerization</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Intermediate species may be present as, for example, in reversible-addition-fragmentation chain-transfer polymerization (RAFT polymerization).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>degenerative chain-transfer radical polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08916"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08930"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08931"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08932"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08933"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08935"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08951"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08929"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>DTRP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08930 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08930">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reversible-deactivation radical polymerization mediated by an iodo-compound.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ITP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Where the initial polymerization mixture comprises a conventional initiator (e.g., a dialkyldiazene or a peroxide) and molecular iodine, the term RITP is commonly used.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>iodine-transfer polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08916"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08934"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08930"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ITP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08931 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08931">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reversible-deactivation radical polymerization mediated by an organobismuthane compound.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>BIRP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>organobismuthane-mediated radical polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08916"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08931"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>BIRP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08932 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08932">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reversible-deactivation radical polymerization mediated by an organostibane compound.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SBRP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>organostibane-mediated radical polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08916"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08932"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SBRP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08933 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08933">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reversible-deactivation radical polymerization mediated by an organotellurium compound.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>TERP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The dominant mechanism of TERP thought to be degenerative chain-transfer radical polymerization (DTRP). However, at high temperature conditions or under photochemical irradiation the mechanism may involve stable-radical-mediated polymerization (SRMP).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>organotellurium-mediated radical polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08916"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08921"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08929"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08933"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>TERP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08934 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08934">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Iodine-transfer polymerization where the initial polymerization mixture comprises a conventional initiator (e.g., an azo-compound or a peroxide) and molecular iodine.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RITP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>reverse iodine-transfer polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08930"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08934"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>RITP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08935 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08935">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Degenerate-transfer radical polymerization in which chain activation and chain deactivation involve a degenerative chain-transfer process, which occurs by a two-step addition-fragmentation mechanism.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RAFT</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RAFT polymerization</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The term RAFT polymerization (unqualified) usually refers to polymerization mediated by a carbonothioylsulfanyl RAFT agent.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>There are various forms of RAFT polymerization that differ in details of the initiation mechanism, the reaction conditions and/or the reagents used. These include: Carbonothioylsulfanyl RAFT polymerization. The more commonly used term thiocarbonylthio RAFT polymerization is deprecated as it is inconsistent with IUPAC nomenclature. Examples of carbonothioylsulfanyl RAFT agents include certain dithioesters, trithiocarbonates, xanthates (dithiocarbonates), and dithiocarbamates with structure \(\ce{S=C(Z)-SR}\), where \(\ce{Z}\) is aryl, alkylsulfanyl, alkyloxy, or dialkylamino, respectively, and \(\ce{R}\) is a homolytic leaving group.Macromonomer RAFT polymerization where the RAFT agent is a &apos;macromonomer&apos; with structure \(\ce{CH2=C(Z)-CH2R}\), where \(\ce{Z}\) is usually \(\ce{-CO2alkyl}\) or \(\ce{-CO2aryl}\) and \(\ce{R}\) is a homolytic leaving group. This form of RAFT polymerization is also known as sulfur-free RAFT polymerization.MADIX (macromolecular design by interchange of xanthate) where the RAFT agent is a xanthate.PhotoRAFT polymerization where radical generation involves direct photolysis of a carbonothioylsulfanyl-RAFT agent to give an initiating species and a carbonothioylsulfanyl radical.PET-RAFT (photo-induced energy or photo-electron-transfer RAFT) polymerization where radical generation involves photo-induced energy or electron transfer from a photoredox catalyst or a photosensitizer, respectively, to a carbonothioylsulfanyl-RAFT agent which then dissociates to give an initiating species.redoxRAFT where radical generation involves ISET or OSET between the RAFT agent and a reducing agent agent to give an initiating species.eRAFT polymerization where radical generation involves electrochemical reduction of the RAFT agent either directly or through a redox mediator.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>reversible-addition-fragmentation chain-transfer polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08929"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08941"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08943"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08951"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08959"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08968"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08935"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>RAFT</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08936 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08936">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reactive species generated reversibly from a monomer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Most frequently, the activated monomer is an anionic or cationic species.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>activated monomer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08907"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08989"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08990"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08937 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08937">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chain-transfer process that proceeds by a propagating species adding to a chain-transfer agent to form an intermediate that then fragments to provide an initiating species.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:label>addition-fragmentation chain transfer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08959"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08966"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08938 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08938">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coordination compound that by reaction with an activator or cocatalyst provides an active catalyst during initialization of coordination polymerization.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>initiator (in coordination polymerization)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>precatalyst (in coordination polymerization)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>catalyst precursor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08956"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08995"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08939 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08939">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chain-transfer process involving transfer of an atom or group to a reagent (the catalyst) that in turn transfers that group to a monomer to reinitiate polymerization and regenerate the original reagent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CCT</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>An example of polymerization with catalytic chain transfer is radical polymerization in the presence of certain square-planar cobalt complexes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>catalytic chain transfer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08916"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08920"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08939"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CCT</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08940 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08940">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chain termination process involving transfer of a group to a reagent (the catalyst) that in turn transfers that group to a propagating species to terminate a chain and regenerate the original reagent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Catalytic chain termination may be observed as a side reaction in certain reversible-deactivation radical polymerizations.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>catalytic chain termination</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08916"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08966"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08941 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08941">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Unimolecular dissociation of a dormant species to form a mediating agent and a propagating species.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>activation (in SRMP)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>unimolecular activation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>unimolecular chain activation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Chain deactivation (in SRMP) is the reverse of chain activation (in SRMP).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In aminoxyl-mediated radical polymerization [nitroxide-mediated polymerization (NMP)], the dormant species is an alkoxyamine and the mediating agent is an aminoxyl radical.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In organometallic-mediated radical polymerization (OMRP), the domant species and the mediating agent are organometallic species.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In reversible addition-fragmentation radical polymerization (RAFRP), the dormant species is a stable radical and the mediating agent is a non-propagating olefin, e.g., 1,1-diphenylethylene.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>chain activation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08917"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08920"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08921"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08922"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08943"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08952"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08964"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08966"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08970"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08942 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08942">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reaction of an activator with a dormant species to form a deactivator and a propagating species.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>activation (in ATRP)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>bimolecular activation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>bimolecular chain activation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Chain deactivation (in ATRP) is the reverse of chain activation (in ATRP).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The activation and deactivation process may form part of a catalytic cycle. However, in general, the activator should not be called a catalyst.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>chain activation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08943"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08949"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08952"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08966"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08943 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08943">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Conversion of a chain carrier into an inactive species.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>deactivation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Chain deactivation, unlike chain termination, may be reversible.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The recommended symbol for the rate coefficient for chain deactivation in a polymerization is \(k_{\rm{deact}}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The reverse of chain deactivation is chain activation or chain reactivation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>chain deactivation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08941"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08945"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08946"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08944 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08944">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chemical reaction between a chain carrier and a monomer that results in the growth of a polymer chain and the regeneration of at least one chain carrier.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>propagation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The recommended symbol for the rate coefficient for chain propagation in a homopolymerization is \(k_{\rm{p}}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>chain propagation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08945 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08945">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Conversion of an inactive chain into a chain carrier.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>activation (in a chain polymerization)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>reactivation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Chain reactivation can be reversible or irreversible.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The recommended symbol for the rate coefficient for chain reactivation in a polymerization is \(k_{\rm{act}}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The reverse of chain reactivation is chain deactivation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term is usually used to refer to the reactivation of previously deactivated or dormant chains.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>chain reactivation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08941"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08943"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08952"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08953"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08946 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08946">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chemical reaction in which a chain carrier is converted irreversibly into a non-propagating species without the formation of a new chain carrier.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>irreversible chain deactivation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>termination</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The recommended symbol for the rate coefficient for chain termination in a polymerization is \(k_{\rm{t}}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &apos;chain deactivation&apos; is often used to stress that, in contrast to chain termination, the formation of non-propagating species may be reversible; see chain deactivation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>chain termination</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08943"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08948"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08953"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08947 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08947">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Substance able to react with a chain carrier by a reaction in which the original chain carrier is deactivated and a new chain carrier is generated.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CTA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>transfer agent</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In a polymerization, the common occurrence is that a new chain carrier of lower molar mass is generated.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>chain-transfer agent</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08947"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CTA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08948 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08948">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chain termination, in a chain polymerization, between two propagating macromolecules that gives rise to a single macromolecule of molar mass equal to the sum of the molar masses of the two macromolecules.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Combination is also called colligation, although this terminology is not commonly used in the context of chain polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Combination is often incorrectly called &apos;recombination&apos;. The term recombination should only be used to refer to the reaction, with each other, of two transient species produced from a common precursor.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>combination</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08946"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08949 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08949">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reagent that reacts reversibly with the initiating species or propagating species to form an initiator or a dormant chain, respectively, and an activator.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:label>deactivator</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08952"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08959"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08966"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08950 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08950">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymer chain which plays no active role in the polymerization.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>dead macromolecule (in RDP)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A dead polymer chain is a chain unlikely to be activated or reactivated to form a propagating species under the polymerization conditions within the timescale of the polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The products of chain termination and irreversible chain transfer comprise dead polymer chains.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dead polymer chain</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08946"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08966"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08951 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08951">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chain-transfer reaction that generates a new chain carrier and a new chain-transfer agent with the same reactivity as the original chain carrier and chain-transfer agent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>degenerate chain transfer</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>degenerate transfer</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>degenerative transfer</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>degenerative chain transfer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08947"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08952 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08952">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Temporarily deactivated chain carrier.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>dormant macromolecule (in RDP)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>dormant polymer chain</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A dormant polymer chain can be reactivated to a propagating chain under the polymerization conditions within the timescale of the polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dormant species</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08917"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08924"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08935"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08963"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08964"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08953 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08953">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Dead chains or dormant chains.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>inactive macromolecule (in RDP)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>non-propagating species</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>inactive chain</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08950"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08952"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08954 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08954">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Substance that combines the properties of an initiator and a reversible chain-transfer agent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:label>inifer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08947"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08955 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08955">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Substance that combines the properties of an initiator, a reversible chain-transfer agent and a chain terminator or reversible deactivator.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A substance that combines the properties of a photoinitiator, a reversible chain-transfer agent and a chain terminator or reversible deactivator is known as a photoiniferter.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Some iniferters are also RAFT agents.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>iniferter</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08919"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08947"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08949"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08968"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08956 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08956">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process for converting a starting coordination complex or precatalyst into an active catalyst.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:label>initialization</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08957 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08957">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Starting initiator or transfer agent in reversible-deactivation polymerization (RDP).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:label>initializer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08912"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08917"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08924"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08935"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08947"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08968"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08958 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08958">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Efficiency of conversion of a starting initiator or transfer agent into a macro-initiator or macro-chain-transfer agent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:label>initialization efficiency</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08947"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08960"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08962"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08963"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08959 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08959">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Species to which monomer adds or becomes inserted to start a chain polymerization.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>An initiating species may be formed from an initiator or be the initiator itself.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In radical polymerization, initiating species formed directly from an initiator are called primary radicals.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>initiating species</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08960 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08960">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of growing chains initiated divided by the number of active-centers generated from initiator molecules.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In RDP the efficiency of conversion of a starting initiator into a macroinitiator may be called the initiator efficiency (see initialization efficiency).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In a radical polymerization, the frequency of production of propagating radicals from an initiator that provides two similar radicals is \(2k_{\rm{d}} f_{\rm{i}}\), where \(k_{\rm{d}}\) is the rate coefficient for initiator decomposition.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The initiator efficiency for radical generation from initiators such as dialkyl diazenes and organic peroxides (common symbol \(f\), recommended symbol \(f_{\rm{g}}\) to distinguish from \(f_{\rm{i}}\)) is defined as the fraction of radicals that escape the cage (see cage effect) and are potentially available for reaction with a substrate (e.g., monomer). The value of \(f_{\rm{i}}\) will usually be less than \(f_{\rm{g}}\) as depending on the monomer and the monomer concentration, initiator-derived radicals may react to form products other than initiating radicals.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The number of growing chains initiated is the number of initiating radicals formed. The number of active centers generated is the number of active centers that could formed be in the absence of any cage effect.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>initiator efficiency</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08958"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08959"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08963"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08966"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08961 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08961">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymer chains that possess the potential for chain growth under the polymerization conditions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>living macromolecule (in RDP)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Living polymer chains comprise all active and dormant polymer chains.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>living polymer chain</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08952"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08962 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08962">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Macromolecule containing chain-transfer-agent functionality.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>macroCTA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>macroRAFT agent (in RAFT polymerization)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The dormant species in RAFT polymerization is often referred to as a macroCTA or macroRAFT agent.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>macro-chain-transfer agent</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08935"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08947"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08952"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08963 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08963">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Macromolecule containing initiator functionality.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The dormant species in RDRP such as ATRP or SRMP is often referred to as a macroinitiator.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>macroinitiator</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08916"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08921"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08924"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08952"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08964 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08964">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Species that mediates polymerization in reversible-deactivation polymerization.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>mediator</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>mediating agent</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08912"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08917"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08935"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08968"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08965 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08965">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Species that is formed from an initiator or monomer molecule and that is capable of initiating polymerization.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In radical polymerization a primary radical may be formed by the action of heat, irradiation or electron-transfer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In radical polymerization, recombination of primary radicals and their reactions with other radicals leads to reduced initiator efficiency.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &apos;primary species&apos; is also used to designate a species centred on a primary carbon atom, for example, an ethyl radical.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>primary species</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08960"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08969"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08966 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08966">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Active species formed by addition to monomer that is capable of adding further monomer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>active macromolecule (in RDP)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>active polymer chain</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>growing chain</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The propagating species are chain carriers in chain polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>propagating species</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08967 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08967">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Diminished rate of polymerization brought about by the action of a retarder.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:label>retardation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08968 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08968">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chain-transfer agent that provides chain transfer by a reversible-addition-fragmentation mechanism.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RAFT agent</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>chain-transfer agent (CTA) (in RAFT polymerization)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>RAFT agents are mediating agents in RAFT polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>reversible-addition-fragmentation chain-transfer agent</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08935"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08947"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08964"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08969 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08969">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Species formed by the rearrangement or fragmentation of a primary species that is capable of initiating a polymerization.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &apos;secondary species&apos; is also used to designate a species centred on a secondary carbon atom, e.g., a propan-2-yl radical.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>secondary species</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08965"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08970 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08970">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mediating agent in stable-radical-mediated radical polymerization with sufficient kinetic stability such that it is essentially unreactive towards addition to monomer under the conditions of the experiment.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The stable radical mediating agent need not be thermodynamically stable</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>stable radical</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08921"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08941"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08964"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08971 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08971">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Species comprising only one monomer unit.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The term is commonly used to designate a dormant species in reversible-deactivation radical polymerization (RDRP) that contains only one monomer unit.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>unimer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08916"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08952"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08972 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08972">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chain-transfer process that proceeds by a one-step substitution reaction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In radical polymerization the process is called homolytic substitution chain transfer and reaction is by a \(\rm{S}_{\rm{H}}2\) mechanism. An example of a chain-transfer agent that reacts by substitution chain transfer is a thiol.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>substitution chain transfer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08947"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08973 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08973">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Term indicating control of a certain kinetic feature of an ionic polymerization or structural aspect of the polymer molecules formed by ionic polymerization, or both.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Generally, the adjective &apos;controlled&apos; should not be used without specifying the particular kinetic or structural feature that is subject to control.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term controlled ionic polymerization is sometimes used to indicate that the initiation is complete within a period that is short with respect to that for propagation such that dispersities are very low and the molar mass can be precisely predicted based on the initial reagent concentrations.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>controlled ionic polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08908"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08974 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08974">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ionic polymerization in which a cyclic monomer yields a monomeric unit that is either acyclic or contains fewer rings than the cyclic monomer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:label>ionic ring-opening polymerization</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08975 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08975">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ionic polymerization in which chain termination and irreversible chain transfer are absent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Ionic polymerizations, controlled ionic polymerizations and reversible-deactivation ionic polymerizations should not be described as living if there is a non-zero probability of chain termination or irreversible chain transfer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>living ionic polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08946"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08976"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08976 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08976">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reversible-deactivation polymerization in which the kinetic-chain carriers are ions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RDIP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>reversible-deactivation ionic polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08912"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08976"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>RDIP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08977 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08977">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Anionic polymerization in which a specific atom or group is intramolecularly transferred to remain associated with the active chain-end during the course of polymerization.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>group-transfer polymerization (GTP)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>anionic group-transfer polymerization</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08978 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08978">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Anionic polymerization in which a cyclic monomer yields a monomeric unit that is either acyclic or contains fewer rings than the cyclic monomer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:label>anionic ring-opening polymerization</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08979 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08979">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Term indicating control of a certain kinetic feature of an anionic polymerization or structural aspect of the polymer molecules formed by anionic polymerization, or both.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Generally, the adjective &apos;controlled&apos; should not be used without specifying the particular kinetic or structural feature that is subject to control.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &apos;controlled anionic polymerization&apos; is sometimes used to indicate that the initiation is complete within in a period that is short with respect to the time for propagation such that dispersities are very low and the molar mass can be precisely predicted based on the initial reagent concentrations.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>controlled anionic polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08908"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08980 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08980">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Anionic polymerization in which chain termination and irreversible chain transfer are absent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Anionic polymerizations, controlled anionic polymerizations and reversible-deactivation anionic polymerizations should not be described as living if there is a non-zero probability of chain termination or irreversible chain transfer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>living anionic polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08946"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08981"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08981 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08981">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reversible-deactivation polymerization in which the kinetic-chain carriers are anions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RDAP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>reversible-deactivation anionic polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08910"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08912"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08981"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>RDAP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08982 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08982">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chain polymerization in which the active centres are nucleophilic species.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Anionic polymerization is an example of nucleophilic chain polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nucleophilic chain polymerization</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08983 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08983">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nucleophilic chain polymerization in which a cyclic monomer yields a monomeric unit that is either acyclic or contains fewer rings than the cyclic monomer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:label>ring-opening nucleophilic chain polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08982"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08984 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08984">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Cationic polymerization in which a cyclic monomer yields a monomeric unit that is either acyclic or contains fewer rings than the cyclic monomer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:label>cationic ring-opening polymerization</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08985 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08985">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Term indicating control of a certain kinetic feature of a cationic polymerization or structural aspect of the polymer molecules formed by cationic polymerization, or both.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Generally, the adjective &apos;controlled&apos; should not be used without specifying the particular kinetic or structural feature that is subject to control.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>controlled cationic polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08908"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08986 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08986">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chain polymerization in which the active centres are electrophilic species.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Cationic polymerization is an example of electrophilic chain polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrophilic chain polymerization</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08987 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08987">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Cationic polymerization in which chain termination and irreversible chain transfer are absent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Cationic polymerizations, controlled cationic polymerizations and reversible deactivation cationic polymerizations should not be described as living if there is a non-zero probability of chain termination or irreversible chain transfer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>living cationic polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08912"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08946"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08985"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08988 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08988">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reversible-deactivation polymerization in which the chain carriers are cations.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RDCP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>reversible-deactivation cationic polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08912"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08988"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>RDCP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08989 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08989">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Activated-monomer polymerization where the propagating species is an electrophile and activating species is anionic.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>anionic activated-monomer polymerization</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>anion-activated-monomer polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08907"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08966"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08990 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08990">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Activated-monomer polymerization where the propagating species is a nucleophile and the activating species is cationic.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>cationic activated-monomer polymerization</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>cation-activated-monomer polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08907"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08966"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08991 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08991">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ring-opening metathesis polymerization in which chain termination and irreversible chain transfer are absent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Ring-opening metathesis polymerizations should not be described as living if there is a non-zero probability of chain termination (e.g., due to side reactions consuming some part of the catalyst) or irreversible chain transfer (e.g., due to backbiting).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>living ring-opening metathesis polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08933"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08946"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08992 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08992">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymerization in which unsaturated monomers are transformed into unsaturated polymers by a process of bond redistribution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Metathesis polymerization is most often metal-catalyzed and the kinetic-chain carriers alternate between metal-carbene complexes and metallacyclobutanes or between metal-carbyne complexes and metallacyclobutenes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The two most common forms of metathesis polymerization are ring-opening metathesis polymerization (ROMP) and acyclic diene metathesis polymerization (ADMET). ADMET is not a chain polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>metathesis polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08933"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08993 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08993">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Metathesis polymerization in which an unsaturated cyclic monomer is converted into an unsaturated monomeric unit that is either acyclic or contains fewer rings than the cyclic monomer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ROMP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In general, ring-opening metathesis polymerizations that show some living characteristics should not be called living ring-opening metathesis polymerization when there are side reactions leading to loss of catalyst or irreversible chain transfer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>ring-opening metathesis polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08991"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08992"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08993"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ROMP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08994 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08994">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ring-opening metathesis polymerization (ROMP) proceeding through one-electron redox processes in the absence of a metal carbene initiator.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ET-ROMP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>ET-ROMP may be initiated by single electron-transfer to a vinyl ether from a photoredox catalyst or by electrochemical processes to create a radical cation as the kinetic-chain carrier.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electron-transfer ring-opening metathesis polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08933"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08994"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ET-ROMP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08995 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08995">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chain polymerization that involves the preliminary coordination of a monomer molecule with a chain carrier.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Coordination polymerization often occurs by pseudo-ionic polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &apos;metal-catalyzed polymerization&apos; without explanation to designate coordination polymerization is deprecated.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>coordination polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09007"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08996 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08996">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coordination polymerization of \(\ce{\upalpha\!,\!\upbeta\mbox{-}unsaturated}\) amides or esters that proceeds by sequential monomer coordination and Michael addition events.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:label>coordination-addition polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08995"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08997 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08997">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coordination polymerization of olefins that proceeds by insertion of the olefin into a metal–carbon bond</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>insertion polymerization</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>polyinsertion</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>coordination-insertion polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08911"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08995"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08998 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08998">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coordination polymerization in which chain termination and irreversible chain transfer are absent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Coordination polymerizations, controlled coordination polymerizations and reversible-deactivation coordination polymerizations should not be described as living if there is a non-zero probability of chain termination or irreversible chain transfer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The use of the adjective living to denote systems with an extended catalyst lifetime is deprecated.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>living coordination polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08946"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08995"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08999"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08999 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08999">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reversible-deactivation polymerization in which the chain carriers are coordination complexes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RDCP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>reversible-deactivation coordination polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08912"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09001"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09003"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08999"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>RDCP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09000 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09000">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coordination polymerization in which a cyclic monomer yields a monomeric unit that is either acyclic or contains fewer rings than the cyclic monomer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:label>coordination ring-opening polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08995"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09001 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09001">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coordinative chain-transfer polymerization that involves two different catalysts showing different specificity, where chain propagation occurs sequentially on the two catalysts, allowing for the formation of segmented or multiblock copolymers.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CSP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Chain-shuttling polymerization is a form of degenerative chain-transfer polymerization (DTP).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>chain-shuttling polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08909"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08944"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09003"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09001"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CSP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09002 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09002">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coordination polymerization in which the chain carrier migrates along the formed polymer chain through a series of \(\ce{\upbeta\mbox{-}hydrogen}\) eliminations and reinsertions with inversion of stereochemistry.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CWP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>CWP of \(\ce{\upbeta\mbox{-}olefins}\) generally leads to less branched polymers than those formed by classical coordination polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In contrast to classical coordination polymerizations, which provide linear polyethylene, CWP of ethylene leads to a branched polymer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>chain-walking polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08995"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09002"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CWP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09003 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09003">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coordination polymerization in which a propagating chain is able to transfer reversibly between the catalyst and a chain-transfer agent, thereby converting the active species into a dormant species.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CCTP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Coordinative chain-transfer polymerization is a form of degenerative chain-transfer polymerization (DTP).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>coordinative chain-transfer polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08909"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08947"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08952"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08966"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08995"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09001"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09003"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CCTP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09004 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09004">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coordination polymerization making use of a heterogeneous catalyst.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>heterogeneous coordination polymerization</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Heterogeneous catalysis coordination polymerization of polyolefins is sometimes known as Ziegler-Natta polymerization. Named reactions are deprecated in IUPAC terminology.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When using the term heterogeneous coordination polymerization, the context must allow distinction from a coordination polymerization in a heterogeneous medium.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>heterogeneous catalysis coordination polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08995"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09005 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09005">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coordination polymerization making use of a homogeneous catalyst.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>homogeneous coordination polymerization</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Metallocene polymerization is one form of homogeneous coordination polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When using the term homogeneous coordination polymerization, the context must allow distinction from a coordination polymerization in a homogeneous medium.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>homogeneous catalysis coordination polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08995"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09006"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09006 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09006">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coordination polymerization making use of a metallocene catalyst.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:label>metallocene polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08995"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09007 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09007">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymerization proceeding by insertion of a monomer into a polar bond without the generation of ions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Most pseudo-ionic polymerizations involve concerted (e.g., four- or multi-centre) mechanisms of propagation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term pseudo-ionic polymerization has been most often used in connection with ring-opening polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>pseudo-ionic polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08995"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09008 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09008">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coordination-addition polymerization with propagation occurring within the coordination sphere of a rare-earth metal complex.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>lanthanide-mediated group-transfer polymerization</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>rare-earth-metal-mediated coordination polymerization</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>rare-earth-metal-mediated group-transfer polymerization</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The process may also involve non-rare-earth metal complexes with a sufficiently large coordination sphere (e.g., zirconium).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>rare-earth-metal-mediated coordination-addition polymerization</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09010 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09010">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Catalysed condensative chain polymerization where the catalyst is transferred from reactant polymer to product polymer with high efficiency.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CTP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>catalyst-transfer chain polymerization</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>It is recommended that the adjective &apos;chain&apos; be included in the term catalyst-transfer chain polymerization in circumstances where there may be confusion with respect to whether a chain mechanism is involved.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>catalyst-transfer polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09009"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09011"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09010"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CTP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09011 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09011">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Catalyst-transfer polymerization where the propagation steps comprise cross-coupling reactions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:label>catalyst-transfer cross-coupling polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09010"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09012 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09012">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Condensative chain polymerization in which chain termination and irreversible chain transfer are absent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1211"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Condensative chain polymerizations should not be described as living if chain termination or irreversible chain transfer are evident.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>living condensative chain polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.08946"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09009"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09013 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09013">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymer composed of brush macromolecules.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0103"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The term should not be confused with polymer brush, which is commonly used to describe polymers attached to a substrate.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>brush polymer</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09014 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09014">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Numeral or letter or a combination of both that identifies position(s) in a structure.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0103"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A locant appears in a name or general expression such as ‘2-substituted’.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>locant</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09015 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09015">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>More (most) senior CRU of a structure of a macromolecule or oligomer molecule containing two or more different types of CRUs.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0103"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>S-CRU</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>If CRUs begin with an identical subunit, the choice depends on the seniority of the remaining subunits and the number and position of substituents.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The CRU beginning with the subunit of highest (higher) seniority is chosen as the S-CRU.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>senior constitutional repeating unit</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09015"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>S-CRU</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09016 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09016">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Constitutional repeating unit (CRU) written with the subunits in a linear series from the highest (higher) seniority to the lowest (lower) or as close as possible by the shortest path.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0103"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>P-CRU</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The P-CRU is chosen with the lowest possible locant(s) for the substituents.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>This definition is based on descriptions of methods to reach the P-CRU.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>preferred constitutional repeating unit</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09016"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>P-CRU</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09017 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09017">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Largest main-chain (backbone) segment of the constitutional repeating unit (CRU) that can be named a single unit under organic nomenclature rules. This may be a ring or a ring system, a heteroatom or a heteroatom chain, or an acyclic carbon chain.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0103"/>
        <rdfs:label>subunit</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09018 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09018">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Amount concentration \(c_{\rm{l}}\) of a species in the liquid phase divided by the amount concentration \(c_{\rm{g}}\) of that species in the gas phase under equilibrium conditions at infinite dilution: \[H_{\rm{s}}^{cc}: = \underset{c_{\rm{l}} \to 0}{\rm{lim}}\frac{c_{\rm{l}}}{c_{\rm{g}}}\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>dimensionless Henry solubility</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Instead of the amount concentrations \(c_{\rm{l}}\) and \(c_{\rm{g}}\), the mass concentrations \(\gamma_{\rm{l}}\) and \(\gamma_{\rm{g}}\) might be used here. This yields the same dimensionless Henry solubility because the molar mass \(M\) of the solute cancels out:\(\gamma_{\rm{l}}/\gamma_{\rm{g}} = (c_{\rm{l}}M)/(c_{\rm{g}}M) = c_{\rm{l}}/c_{\rm{g}}\)</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dimensionless Henry’s law solubility constant</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09021"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09019 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09019">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Amount concentration \(c_{\rm{g}}\) of a species in the gas phase divided by the amount concentration \(c_{\rm{g}}\) of that species in the liquid phase under equilibrium conditions at infinite dilution: \[H_{\rm{v}}^{cc}: = \underset{c_{\rm{l}} \to 0}{\rm{lim}}\frac{c_{\rm{g}}}{c_{\rm{l}}}\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>dimensionless Henry volatility</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Instead of the amount concentrations \(c_{\rm{g}}\) and \(c_{\rm{l}}\), the mass concentrations \(\gamma_{\rm{g}}\) and \(\gamma_{\rm{l}}\) can be used here. This yields the same dimensionless Henry volatility because the molar mass \(M\) of the solute cancels out: \(\gamma_{\rm{g}}/\gamma_{\rm{l}} = (c_{\rm{g}}M)/(c_{\rm{l}}M) = c_{\rm{g}}/c_{\rm{l}}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dimensionless Henry’s law volatility constant</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09022"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09020 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09020">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Proportionality factor describing the equilibrium distribution of a chemical species between the gas phase and a liquid solution at infinite dilution of the solute according to Henry’s law. It is an umbrella term for the reciprocal quantities Henry’s law solubility constant and Henry’s law volatility constant. Because of its ambiguity, it is strongly recommended to specify whether a solubility or volatility constant is intended.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0302"/>
        <rdfs:label>Henry’s law constant</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09021"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09022"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09021 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09021">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Umbrella term for all variants of Henry’s law constants that are defined in such a way that the numerical value increases with increasing solubility of the solute. Variants include \(H_{\rm{s}}^{cp}\), \(H_{\rm{s}}^{xp}\), \(H_{\rm{s}}^{bp}\), and \(H_{\rm{s}}^{cc}\), as discussed elsewhere in this section.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Henry solubility</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>Henry’s law solubility constant</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09022"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09022 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09022">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Umbrella term for all variants of Henry’s law constants that are defined in such a way that the numerical value increases with increasing volatility of the solute. Variants include \(H_{\rm{v}}^{pc}\), \(H_{\rm{v}}^{px}\), \(H_{\rm{v}}^{pw}\), and \(H_{\rm{v}}^{cc}\), as discussed elsewhere in this section.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Henry volatility</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>Henry’s law volatility constant</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09021"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09023 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09023">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Partial pressure \(p\) of a species in the gas phase divided by the amount concentration \(c_{\rm{l}}\) of that species in the liquid phase under equilibrium conditions at infinite dilution: \[H_{\rm{v}}^{pc}: = \underset{c_{\rm{l}} \to 0}{\rm{lim}}\frac{p}{c_{\rm{l}}}\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0302"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The coherent SI unit for \(H_{\rm{v}}^{pc}\) is \(\pu{Pa m3 mol-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Henry’s law volatility constant (via p and c)</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09022"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09024 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09024">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Amount concentration \(c_{\rm{l}}\) of a species in the liquid phase divided by the partial pressure \(p\) of that species in the gas phase under equilibrium conditions at infinite dilution: \[H_{\rm{s}}^{cp}: = \underset{c_{\rm{l}} \to 0}{\rm{lim}}\frac{c_{\rm{l}}}{p}\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Henry solubility (defined via amount concentration and partial pressure)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The coherent SI unit for \(H_{\rm{s}}^{cp}\) is \(\pu{mol m-3 Pa-1}\). Another commonly used unit is \(\pu{mol L-1 bar-1}\), equivalent to \(\pu{10E-2 mol m-3 Pa-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Henry’s law solubility constant (via c and p)</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09021"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09025 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09025">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Amount fraction \(x\) of a species in the liquid phase divided by the partial pressure \(p\) of that species in the gas phase under equilibrium conditions at infinite dilution: \[H_{\rm{s}}^{xp}: = \underset{x \to 0}{\rm{lim}}\frac{x}{p}\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0302"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The coherent SI unit for \(H_{\rm{s}}^{xp}\) is \(\pu{Pa-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Henry’s law solubility constant (via x and p)</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09021"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09026 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09026">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Molality \(b\) of a species divided by the partial pressure \(p\) of that species in the gas phase under equilibrium conditions at infinite dilution: \[H_{\rm{s}}^{bp}: = \underset{b \to 0}{\rm{lim}}\frac{b}{p}\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0302"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Here, the symbol \(b\) is used for molality (instead of \(m\)) to avoid confusion with the symbol \(m\) for mass.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Molality refers to the mass of solvent and should not be confused with content which refers to the mass of solution.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The coherent SI unit for \(H_{\rm{s}}^{bp}\) is \(\pu{mol m-3 Pa-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Henry’s law solubility constant (via b and p)</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09021"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09027 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09027">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Partial pressure \(p\) of a species in the gas phase divided by the amount fraction \(x\) of that species in the liquid phase under equilibrium conditions at infinite dilution: \[H_{\rm{v}}^{px}: = \underset{x \to 0}{\rm{lim}}\frac{p}{x}\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0302"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Henry volatility (defined via partial pressure and liquid-phase amount fraction)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The coherent SI unit for \(H_{\rm{v}}^{px}\) is \(\pu{Pa}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Henry’s law volatility constant (via p and x)</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09022"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09028 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09028">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Partial pressure \(p\) of a species in the gas phase divided by the mass fraction \(w\) of that species in the liquid phase under equilibrium conditions at infinite dilution: \[H_{\rm{v}}^{pw}: = \underset{w \to 0}{\rm{lim}}\frac{p}{w}\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0302"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The coherent SI unit for \(H_{\rm{v}}^{pw}\) is \(\pu{Pa}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Henry’s law volatility constant (via p and w)</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09022"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09029 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09029">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Constitutive part of a conjugation that binds an active species to a substrate.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0502"/>
        <rdfs:comment>When release of the active species is desired, the link must be cleavable.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>link</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09030 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09030">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Conjugate in which at least one conjugation includes a bioactive active species to be released by cleavage of the link where its action is desired.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0502"/>
        <rdfs:label>prodrug conjugate</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09031 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09031">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Link used in a conjugation to distance the substrate from the active species that otherwise would have its activity affected by steric or other influences.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0502"/>
        <rdfs:label>spacer arm</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09029"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09032 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09032">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Materials chemistry is the scientific discipline that designs, synthesizes, and characterizes materials with particular interest on both their processing and the understanding of useful or potentially useful properties displayed by the materials designed and synthesized for specific applications.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For a series of further details on both the structure-property relation and functionality by design, read the source.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The aspects of design, synthesis, and function (useful or potentially useful properties) are all needed for the topical item of material research/development/production to be part of materials chemistry.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The keywords &quot;useful&quot; and &quot;properties&quot; emphasize functionality as a major driving force of the practitioners of materials chemistry.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>materials chemistry</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09033 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09033">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Weak attractive interaction between an electrophilic region on a pnictogen atom in a molecular entity (wherein the pnictogen is involved in other stronger bonds) and a nucleophilic region in another, or the same, molecular entity.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-1002"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PnB</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A typical PnB is denoted by the three dots in \(\ce{R–Pn\bond{...}A}\), wherein \(\ce{R}\) is collectively representing the groups covalently bonded to the pnictogen atom Pn, Pn (the PnB donor atom) is any atom (possibly hypervalent) of Group 15 having at least one electrophilic (electron poor) region (the PnB donor region) involved in a bond with \(\ce{A}\), \(\ce{R–Pn}\) (the PnB donor) is a molecular entity containing the electrophilic pnictogen atom, and \(\ce{A}\) (the PnB acceptor) is a molecular entity having at least one nucleophilic (electron rich) region (the PnB acceptor region).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>An electrophilic region (typically associated with a \(\ce{\upsigma\mbox{-}}\) or \(\ce{\uppi\mbox{-}hole}\)) on a pnictogen atom in a molecular entity and a nucleophilic region in another or the same molecular entity can form bonds spanning a range of separations, energies, and other experimental or theoretical parameters; the use of the term PnB has to be limited to cases where separations, energies, and/or other experimental/theoretical parameters differ from those of corresponding covalent/ionic bonds.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Bonds wherein a pnictogen atom acts as the nucleophile and those wherein its electrophilic role cannot be identified must not be named PnBs.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Pnictogen atoms can concurrently form one or more than one PnB; the PnB donor and/or PnB acceptor can be charged molecular entities, thus forming charge-assisted PnBs.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The polar character of the pnictogen atom in the interaction (i.e., the electrophilic role in pnictogen bonded systems or its nucleophilic role in the other systems) is established by considering the physical and chemical properties of the starting molecular entities before the interaction formation and/or of the system assembled by the interaction (see onward).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>pnictogen bond</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09033"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PnB</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09034 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09034">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measure of the capacity of aqueous media to react with hydrogen ions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Alkalinity is often expressed as an equivalent mass concentration of calcium carbonate, for example &quot;alkalinity equals \(\pu{3.2 mg L-1}\) as \(\ce{CaCO3}\).&quot;</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Alkalinity is used to assess the buffering capacity of natural waters and is measured by titration using methyl red (pH 4.5, total alkalinity) or phenolphthalein (pH 8.3, composite alkalinity).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>alkalinity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09038"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09035 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09035">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Titration of remaining reagent after addition of excess reagent to the titrand.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>back titration</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09051"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09036 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09036">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Titration performed with titrand containing no analyte.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A blank titration is used to estimate systematic error such as indicator consumption error.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>blank titration</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09046"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09051"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09037 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09037">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Redox titration using cerium(IV), in which an iron(II)-1,10-phenanthroline complex (ferroin) color change indicates the end-point.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>cerimetry</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>cerimetric titration</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09038 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09038">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement by titration to the phenolphthalein end-point (pH 8.3) of that portion of alkalinity arbitrarily attributed to all the hydroxyl content and half the carbonate content of a titrand.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>phenolphthalein end-point alkalinity</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>By definition, composite alkalinity is zero for solutions which have a pH value of 8.3 or less.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>For the carbonate system, with no other oxoanions, \(A_{\rm{P}} = [\ce{CO3^{2-}}] + [\ce{OH-}]\) where the concentrations refer to the solution before the titration \(\ce{CO3^{2−} + H+ -&gt; HCO3−}\) and \(\ce{OH− + H+ -&gt; H2O}\) performed to discoloration of the visual indicator.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>composite alkalinity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09034"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09051"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09039 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09039">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mass of a body that is balanced by a standard mass under conventionally chosen conditions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The International Organization of Legal Metrology recommends that the conventionally chosen conditions are: reference temperature, \(t_{\rm{ref}} = \pu{20^{\circ}C}\), density of air as a reference value, \(\rho_{0} = \pu{1.2 kg m−3}\); reference density of standard weight, \(\rho_{\rm{c}} = \pu{8000 kg m-3}\). In this case, the conventional mass is related to the mass (\(m\)) and density (\(\rho\)) of the object by \(m_{\rm{c}} = m \frac{1 - \rho_{0}/\rho}{1 - \rho_{0}/\rho_{\rm{c}}}\)</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The base unit of the quantity &quot;conventional mass&quot; is the kilogram.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>conventional mass</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09040 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09040">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Titration in which titrant is added directly to titrand.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>direct titration</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09035"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09051"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09041 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09041">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Difference in the volume or mass of titrant, or the corresponding difference in the volume or mass of titrand, between the end-point value and equivalence-point value.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>end-point error</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09042"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09042 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09042">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Stage of a titration at which the titrant has completely reacted with the titrand according to the stoichiometry of the reaction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;equivalence-point&quot; is also used for the quantity that expresses the volume or mass of titrant at the equivalence-point.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>equivalence-point</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09051"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09043 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09043">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement principle of quantitative chemical analysis based on measurement of mass.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>gravimetry</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The analyte of interest is separated from the sample in a form that allows measurement of mass (e.g., by precipitation), and its mass or amount of substance is calculated from the mass of the weighed compound whose stoichiometric composition must be exactly known.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>gravimetric analysis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09044 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09044">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mass fraction of analyte in a precipitate (see precipitation) obtained in a gravimetric analysis.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>GF</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Gravimetric factor is calculated by \(g_{\rm{F}} = (M_{\rm{A}}v_{\rm{A}})/(M_{\rm{P}}v_{\rm{P}})\), where \(M_{\rm{A}}\), \(M_{\rm{P}}\) are molar masses of analyte and precipitate, respectively, and \(v_{\rm{A}}\) and \(v_{\rm{P}}\) are the stoichiometric numbers in the precipitation reaction.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Historically, the gravimetric factor is given the initialism GF, but to conform to the IUPAC convention that quantities should have a single symbol, it is recommended that \(g_{\rm{F}}\) is used in equations.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The gravimetric factor is used to calculate the mass fraction of an analyte in a sample (\(w\)) by \(w = m_{\rm{P}}/m_{\rm{sample}} \times g_{\rm{F}}\), where \(m_{\rm{P}}\) is the mass of precipitate and \(m_{\rm{sample}}\) the mass of sample.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>gravimetric factor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09043"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09044"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GF</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09045 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09045">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Precipitation in gravimetric analysis in which the precipitating agent is produced from a homogeneously dissolved precursor to minimise local excesses and supersaturation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>homogeneous precipitation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09043"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09046 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09046">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Systematic measurement error from the reaction of a visual indicator with a reactant in a titration.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A significant compensation of this error usually takes place because the standardization of the titrant is carried out in similar conditions to the analysis titration.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Indicator consumption error may be estimated by performing a blank titration.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Indicator consumption error may be expressed as a volume or mass of titrant.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The magnitude and sign of this error depends on the amount of substance of indicator used and the nature of the interaction between the indicator and the analyte.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>indicator consumption error</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09036"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09047 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09047">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Redox titration in which the course of the reaction is followed by potentiometry.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>potentiometric titration</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09048 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09048">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Visual indicator precipitated, with concomitant color change, by reaction with excess titrant at the end-point of a titration.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>precipitation indicator</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09049 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09049">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Visual indicator that reacts with excess titrant or titrand with a color change at the end-point of a redox titration.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>redox indicator</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09051"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09050 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09050">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Solution prepared by weighing an appropriate portion of a solid or by measuring out an appropriate volume of a liquid and dissolving it in the weighed mass of solvent or by adding solvent to a given volume.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A stock solution is usually of a greater concentration than is needed for the chemical purpose and is diluted to give the required concentration before use.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>stock solution</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09051 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09051">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reactant solution being titrated in a titration.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>titrand</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09052 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09052">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Methods of quantitative analysis employing titration.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>titrimetry</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The titrant added is usually measured by volume (volumetric titration) but it can be weighed.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>titrimetric analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09055"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09053 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09053">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Potential at the end-point of a redox titration.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The transition potential is a function of the formal redox potential, the total concentration of the indicator (especially for one color indicators), the depth of the color layer, the minimal observable absorbance change (which depends on wavelength and eye sensitivity), and the absorption coefficient.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>transition potential</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09054 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09054">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Visual indicator composed of a solution of several pH indicators (main components are thymol blue, methyl red, bromothymol blue and phenolphthalein) that changes color through a range of pH values, usually from 1 to 14.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Universal indicators are commercially available in the form of a solution or in paper strips accompanied by a color matching chart.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>universal indicator</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09055 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09055">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Titration in which the titrant is added by volume.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>volumetry</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Titrant is usually added from a calibrated burette.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>volumetric titration</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09056 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09056">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Difference between the concentration of the supersaturated solution (see supersaturation) required for nucleation (\(Q\)) and its solubility (\(S\)), divided by the solubility, \((Q − S)/S\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>relative supersaturation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The particle size of a precipitate increases as its relative supersaturation decreases, so \(Q\) should be kept low and \(S\) should be high during gravimetric analysis by precipitation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The units of the quantities \(Q\) and \(S\) must be the same.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>von Weimarn ratio</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09043"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09057 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09057">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Algebraic sum of faradaic currents flowing through an electrode.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:label>net faradaic current</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09058 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09058">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>System that consists of at least two electron conductors (electrodes) in contact with ionic conductors (electrolytes).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>If processes of interest occur at both the anode and the cathode of a cell (as in differential amperometry or controlled-current potentiometric titration with two indicator electrodes), the cell should be said to comprise two indicator or two working electrodes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In electrochemistry the term “cell” is commonly qualified to describe the particular device on which electrochemical reactions take place. Examples of galvanic and electrolytic cells are standard cells, electrochemical sensor cells, conductivity cells, spectroelectrochemical cells, fuel cells, batteries, electrochemical measuring cells, and two- and three-electrode cells.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The current flow through the electrochemical cell may be zero or non-zero. An electrochemical cell with current flow can operate either as a galvanic cell or as an electrolytic cell.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The two electrodes may be a working electrode and an auxiliary electrode or, for zero-current measurements (potentiometric mode), an indicator electrode and a reference electrode. There may be a third electrode, the cell having a separate auxiliary electrode (to carry current) and reference electrode (for measurement of electrode potential) in addition to a working electrode (See three-electrode cell).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrochemical cell</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09061"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09062"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09066"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09072"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09081"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09132"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09059 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09059">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In May 2019, certain fundamental constants that had been measured in SI units have had their numerical values fixed, with no uncertainty, thus defining the units. Of importance to electroanalytical chemistry are the values of the Boltzmann constant, the elementary charge, and the Avogadro constant. These are given in the table below, with calculated values of the Faraday constant (\(F = e·N_{\rm{A}}\)) and gas constant (\(R = k\cdot\!N_{\rm{A}}\)).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:label>electrochemical constants</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electron conductor in an electrochemical cell connected to the external circuit.  Half-cell consisting of at least one electron conductor and at least one ionic conductor (electrolyte).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In some electroanalytical techniques (conductometry and differential potentiometry), two indicator electrodes are used. For many analytical measurements in cells with current flow, a three-electrode cell is used.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Many types of electrodes are used. These may be classified into groups according to their composition, form and size, and according to their operating mode.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The current flow through electrochemical cells may be zero or non-zero. An electrochemical cell with current flow can operate either as a galvanic cell or an electrolytic cell.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09058"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09061"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09062"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09066"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09078"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09081"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09127"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09132"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09166"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09061 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09061">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Conducting medium in which the flow of electric current is accompanied by the movement of ions.  Substance that provides ions on dissolution in a solvent or on melting.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A solid electrolyte is a solid material where the predominant charge carriers are ions. For example: NASICON (Na Super Ionic Conductor), which has the general formula \(\ce{Na_{1+x}Zr2P_{3-x}Si_{x}O12}\), \(0 \lt x \lt 3\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>An ionic liquid is an electrolyte composed of a salt that is liquid below \(\pu{100^{\circ}C}\). Ionic liquids have found uses in electrochemical analysis, because their unconventional properties include a negligible vapor pressure, a high thermal and electrochemical stability, and exceptional dissolution properties for both organic and inorganic chemical species.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrolyte</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09062 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09062">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrochemical cell in which electrical energy is converted into chemical energy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:label>electrolytic cell</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09058"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09063 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09063">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of electrons transferred in a charge transfer reaction between an electrode and a single entity (ion, radical-ion, or molecule) of an electroactive substance, whose identity must be specified.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>charge number</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>number of electrons transferred</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Charge number is used in in the IUPAC Green Book.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Symbol \(n\) is commonly used when there is no risk of confusion with an amount of substance.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The apparent number of electrons transferred, \(z_{\rm{app}}\), represents the experimentally measured number of electrons transferred between an electrode and one electroactive substance species, whose identity must be specified. When the electrooxidation or electroreduction of a substance \(\ce{B}\) is accompanied by chemical processes, such as the catalyzed or induced reduction of a second substance, or a side reaction that consumes \(\ce{B}\) or an intermediate, the value of \(z_{\rm{app}}\) will differ from that of \(z\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electron number of an electrochemical reaction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09064 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09064">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Potential of an electrode when no electric current flows through the cell and all local charge transfer equilibria across phase boundaries that are represented in the cell diagram (except at possible electrolyte-electrolyte junctions) and local chemical equilibria are established.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Nernst potential</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>reversible potential</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Temperature and activities of relevant species must be specified.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>\(E_{\rm{eq}}\) is related to the standard electrode potential by the Nernst equation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>equilibrium electrode potential</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09068"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09065 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09065">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Equilibrium electrode potential under conditions of unit concentration of species involved in the electrode reaction. \[E_{\rm{eq}} = E^{\circ\prime} - (RT/zF) \mathop \sum \limits_{i}  u_{i} \ln(c_{i}/c^{\circ)\] where \(E_{\rm{eq}}\) is the equilibrium electrode potential, \(R\) the gas constant, \(T\) the thermodynamic temperature, \(F\) the Faraday constant, \(z\) the electron number of an electrochemical reaction, \( u_{\rm{i}}\) the stoichiometric coefficients (numbers of species) in the equation of the electrode reaction (positive for products and negative for reactants), and \(c_{\rm{i}}\) are the amount concentrations of the species involved (most usually ions).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>formal potential</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>It is \(E^{\circ\prime}\) which is calculated in electrochemical experiments when the concentrations of the various species are known, but their activities are not. Its value depends on the composition of the electrolyte solution. The argument of ln is dimensionless, while the concentration \(c_{\rm{i}}\) is normalized through division by the standard concentration, usually \(c^{\circ} = \pu{1 mol dm-3}\) for soluble species..</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>formal electrode potential</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09063"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09064"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09066 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09066">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrochemical cell in which chemical reactions occur spontaneously and chemical energy is converted into electrical energy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:label>galvanic cell</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09058"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09067 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09067">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Notation giving electrodes, electroactive substances, and other relevant information for an electrochemical cell. The notation starts and finishes with an electrode. Phase boundaries are denoted with a solid vertical bar \(|\), boundaries between miscible liquids by a vertical dashed bar \(¦\), and a liquid junction by double vertical dashed bars \(¦¦\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The potential difference of an electrochemical cell is measured between a metallic conductor attached to the right-hand electrode of the notation and an identical metallic conductor attached to the left-hand electrode.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>line representation of electrochemical cells</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09058"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09068 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09068">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Fundamental equation in electrochemistry that describes the dependence of the equilibrium electrode potential on the composition of the contacting phases, written as a reduction: \[E_{\rm{eq}} = E^{\circ}\!-\!(RT/zF)\mathop \sum \limits_i  u_i \ln(a_i)\] where \(E_{\rm{eq}}\) is the equilibrium electrode potential, \(E^{\circ}\) the standard electrode potential of the reaction, \(R\) the gas constant, \(T\) the thermodynamic temperature, \(F\) the Faraday constant, \(z\) the electron number of an electrochemical reaction, and \( u_{\rm{i}}\) are the stoichiometric coefficients (numbers of species) in the equation of the electrode reaction, positive for products and negative for reactants, while \(a_{\rm{i}}\) represents the activities of the species involved (most usually ions).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For a solution containing oxidized (ox) and reduced (red) forms of a redox couple at activities \(a_{\rm{ox}}\) and \(a_{\rm{red}}\), respectively, the equilibrium electrode potential is \[E_{\rm{eq}} = E^{\circ}\!-\!(RT/zF)\ln(a_{\rm{red}}/a_{\rm{ox}})\] where \(E^{\circ}\) is the standard electrode potential of the redox couple.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In analytical chemistry, concentrations, rather than activities, are often considered (see Note to formal electrode potential) \[E_{\rm{eq}} = E^{\circ\prime}\!-\!(RT/zF)\ln(c_{\rm{red}}/c_{\rm{ox}})\] where \(E^{\circ\prime}\) is the formal potential which can differ from the standard electrode potential owing to the influence of real conditions (pH, ionic strength, concentration of complex forming substances, etc.), and \(c_{\rm{red}}\) and \(c_{\rm{ox}}\) are the amount concentrations of reduced and oxidized species, respectively. At \(\pu{25^{\circ}C}\), and for practical purposes, this equation is often written as \[E_{\rm{eq}} \approx E^{\circ\prime}\!-\!(0.0592/z)\log_{10}(c_{\rm{red}}/c_{\rm{ox}})\] where the factor \(0.0592 \approx (RT/F)/{\rm{log}}_{10}(\rm{e})\) at \(\pu{298.15 K}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Nernst equation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09063"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09064"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09065"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09069 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09069">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Equation for diffusion current \(I_{\rm{d}}\) at time \(t\) at a large planar electrode in contact with a semi-infinite layer of unstirred solution containing excess supporting (inert) electrolyte and an electroactive substance of bulk concentration \(c\) when, at the instant \(t = 0\), the potential of the electrode is suddenly changed to a value at which a mass-transport limited electrochemical reaction occurs and the concentration of the electroactive species at the electrode is zero, \[I_{\rm{d}} = zFA{D^{1/2}}c/(\pi^{1/2} t^{1/2})\] where \(z\) is the electron number of an electrochemical reaction in the charge transfer step of the species (being positive for an oxidation and negative for a reduction), \(F\) the Faraday constant, \(A\) the electrode surface area, and \(D\) the diffusion coefficient of the electroactive substance.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>This equation shows that the current decays from an initial infinitely large value, is proportional to \(1/t^{1/2}\), and that no steady-state current (current independent of time) is ever achieved. In practice, diffusion-limited transport is eventually limited by convection.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Cottrell equation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09063"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09074"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09070 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09070">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Plot of current versus applied potential (\(I\!{-}\!E\)) for a given system.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>I-E curve</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In voltammetry the curve is called a voltammogram.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The curve is sigmoidal in hydrodynamic voltammetry, voltammetry at microelectrodes and polarography, or peak-shaped in ac voltammetry, differential pulse voltammetry, square-wave voltammetry, stripping voltammetry, and derivative techniques.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>current-potential curve</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09130"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09136"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09138"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09147"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09148"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09149"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09155"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09071 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09071">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrochemical sensor that has a biological recognition element.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>bioanalytical sensor</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Electrochemical biosensor may be shortened to ‘biosensor’ only if there is no ambiguity concerning, or interest in, the principle of measurement.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrochemical biosensor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09072"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09072 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09072">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measuring system in which the response to an electrochemical reaction is converted, via a suitable transducer, into a measurable electrical signal.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Among the most important general criteria which must be obeyed in the design of a useful electrochemical sensor are: For amperometric and voltammetric sensors, the species to be determined are electroactive within the sensor’s potential range.For potentiometric sensors, there is an adequate electrode material, free from interferences.The concentration of electroactive species can be determined with sufficient accuracy and precision.Measurements are sufficiently reliable and repeatable.The response time of the sensor is sufficiently short.The drift or decrease of sensor response with time owing to electrode degradation or surface fouling is sufficiently low.Calibration is simple and easy to perform, or not necessary.The limit of detection is sufficiently low for the purpose envisaged.The relative importance of these factors depends on the requirements for monitoring, as well as on the technique employed and the electrode and cell configuration. It is important to bear in mind the possibilities and advantages arising from self-contained test modules, specific assays, miniaturization, and no necessity for external pre-treatment or reagent addition.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Measurement principles of electrochemical sensors are given in the table below. When used in the description of cells, the measurement principle is written in place of ‘electrochemical’; thus, potentiometric sensor, amperometric sensor, conductometric sensor.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrochemical sensor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09127"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09128"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09166"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09073 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09073">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Amount of a chemical modifier at an electrode surface per unit area.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>electrode coverage</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit of \(\mathit{\Gamma}\) is \(\pu{mol m−2}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The total coverage may be distinguished from coverage that is electrochemically reactive or accessible on some given timescale.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrode coverage density</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09074 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09074">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Area of electrode–solution interface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The electroactive area is the area calculated from experiments with model electroactive species and may be different from the real surface area in cases where not all of the surface is electrochemically active or accessible.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The geometric area, \(A_{\rm{geom}}\), is the interfacial area, determined on the assumption that the interface is truly flat (two-dimensional) and calculated using the geometric data of the involved surfaces.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The real (true) area, \(A_{\rm{real}}\), takes into account non-idealities of the interface (roughness, porosity, etc.) and can be measured by a variety of electrochemical methods.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrode surface area</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09075 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09075">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Faraday’s First Law of electrolysis: Mass \(m\) of electrochemically-transformed substance is proportional to the charge \(Q\) passed, \(m\! \propto\! Q\). Faraday’s Second Law of electrolysis: When the same electric charge (quantity of electricity) \(Q\) is passed through several electrolytes, the mass \(m_{\rm{i}}\) of the substances deposited are proportional to their respective chemical equivalent molar mass, \(M_{\rm{i}}/z_{\rm{i}}\). \[m_{1}/m_{2} = (M_{1}/z_{1})/(M_{2}/z_{2})\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>If reference is made only to ‘Faraday’s Law of electrolysis’ the first law is meant.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Measurement of charge using a coulometer relies on Faraday’s Law.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The relationship may be formulated as \(m=MQ/zF\), where \(M\) is the molar mass of the substance, \(z\) the number of electrons transferred (see: electron number of an electrochemical reaction), and \(F\) is the Faraday constant.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Faraday’s laws of electrolysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09063"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09157"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09076 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09076">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Instrument which controls the electric current between the working electrode and the auxiliary electrode.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:label>galvanostat</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09079"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09132"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09077 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09077">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrode potential of working electrode relative to the reference electrode when no potential or electric current is being applied to the electrochemical cell.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>OCP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In the case of a reversible electrode system, the OCP is also referred to as the equilibrium electrode potential. Otherwise, it is called the rest potential, or the corrosion potential, depending on the system being studied.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>open circuit potential</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09058"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09064"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09077"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>OCP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09078 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09078">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrode whose potential changes with an applied potential.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A non-polarizable electrode is an electrode that holds its potential essentially constant by efficiently allowing electric current to pass. This is a desirable characteristic for a reference electrode.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polarizable electrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09079 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09079">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measuring instrument for electric current that controls the potential difference between a working electrode and a reference electrode and measures the electric current between a working electrode and an auxiliary electrode.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:label>potentiostat</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09132"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09080 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09080">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Means of making electrolytic connection between two half cells without introducing a significant liquid junction potential.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A typical construction is a tube of an inert material (e.g. agar agar) filled with a solution containing an electrolyte with approximately equal ion mobilities of the cation and the anion (e.g., \(\ce{KNO3}\), \(\ce{KCl}\)), with the ends of the tube immersed in the electrolyte solution of the half cells.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>salt bridge</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09081 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09081">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrochemical cell with a working electrode, reference electrode, and auxiliary electrode.Schematic of a three-electrode cell. 1 - working electrode, 2 - reference electrode, 3 - auxiliary electrode. The potential between 1 and 2 is set while passing and measuring current between 1 and 3.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A potentiostat can be used to maintain a potential difference between the working and reference electrodes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Electric current flows between the working and auxiliary electrodes. Electrode potential may be measured between the working and reference electrodes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>three-electrode cell</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09058"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09079"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09082 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09082">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Range of electrode potentials of a given working electrode in a given electrolyte, where the electric current from reactions of the electrode or electrolyte is negligible compared with the current from reactions of the system under investigation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Because the working potential range is a function of a specific electrode/electrolyte system, only approximate ranges can be given for generic systems. See table.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The working potential range is usually limited by the stability of the electrolyte, itself affected by the catalytic properties of the electrode, or by the stability of the electrode.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>working potential range</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09061"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09083 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09083">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrode with smallest dimension of order millimetre or greater.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Electrodes are described by their smallest dimension. Thus, an electrode of \(\pu{1 \upmu m \times 1 mm}\) is termed a ‘microelectrode’.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>macroelectrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09084 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09084">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrode with smallest dimension of less than 1 millimetre and greater than 1 micrometre.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>An IUPAC Technical Report defined microelectrode in terms of its relation to the diffusion layer thickness. Microelectrode is any electrode whose characteristic dimension is, under the given experimental conditions, comparable to or smaller than the diffusion layer thickness, \(\delta\). Under these conditions, a steady state or a pseudo steady state (cylindrical electrodes) is attained. In a short time, when the diffusion layer thickness \(\delta\) is smaller than the characteristic electrode dimension \(r\), an electrode behaves as a regular planar electrode. In contrast, at a long time, when \(\delta\) is large compared to \(r\), an electrode acts as a microelectrode. In defining a microelectrode, important is the dimensionless \(\delta/r\) ratio. An electrode operates as the microelectrode if this ratio much exceeds unity. An absolute \(r\) value is irrelevant in this definition.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Electrode dimensions of micrometres are now routine and the distinction between ‘ultramicro’ and ‘micro’ electrodes is not usually made.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Electrodes are described by their smallest dimension. Thus, an electrode of \(\pu{1 \upmu m \times 1 mm}\) is termed a ‘microelectrode’.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Microelectrodes operate at a current small enough to not perturb the concentration of the solution over the experimental time span.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Nanoelectrodes and microelectrodes are often configured as electrode arrays.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When the smallest dimension of an electrode falls below about \(\pu{25 \upmu m}\), the diffusion layer thickness becomes greater than the dimension of the electrode, which leads to a steady state current given by \(I = zFA m_{0} c_{0}\), where \(z\) is the number of electrons transferred (see electron number of an electrochemical reaction) in the charge transfer step of the species (being positive for an oxidation and negative for a reduction), \(F\) the Faraday constant, \(A\) the electrode surface area, and \(c_{0}\) the bulk concentration of electroactive species. \(m_{0}\) depends on the geometry of the electrode and is, for example, \(4D/\pi r_{0}\) for a disc and \(D/r_{0}\) for a hemisphere, where \(D\) is the diffusion coefficient of the electroactive substance, and \(r_{0}\) the radius of the electrode.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>microelectrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09063"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09074"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09114"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09085 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09085">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrode with smallest dimension of less than 25 micrometre.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>UME</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Electrode dimensions of micrometres are now routine and the distinction between ‘ultramicro’ and ‘micro’ electrodes is not usually made.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Electrodes are described by their smallest dimension. Thus, an electrode of \(\pu{1 \upmu m \times 1 mm}\) is termed a ‘microelectrode’.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>ultramicroelectrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09085"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>UME</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09086 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09086">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrode with smallest dimension less than 1 micrometre</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Nanoelectrodes and microelectrodes are often configured as electrode arrays.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Nanoparticles adsorbed, or otherwise deposited, on a conductor are considered a kind of nanoelectrode.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nanoelectrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09114"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09086"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>NE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09087 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09087">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrode that maintains a constant, but not generally well-defined, potential in an electrochemical cell.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>QRE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Examples are platinum or silver wires used in non-aqueous electrolytes or ionic liquids. Quasi-reference electrodes are calibrated against a reference electrode or by using a redox couple of known standard electrode potential.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>quasi-reference electrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09058"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09087"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>QRE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09088 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09088">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrode made of glassy carbon material with an intertwined graphitic ribbon structure, formed by pyrolysis of a resol precursor at temperatures up to \(\pu{3000^{\circ}C}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>GCE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>vitreous carbon electrode</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Glassy carbon combines glass-like mechanical characteristics with physical properties similar to graphite, owing to its “graphene ribbon” structure. It is extremely resistant to many chemicals, impermeable to gases and liquids, and has good electrical conductivity, high hardness and strength, and biocompatibility. It is one of the most frequently used working electrode materials.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>glassy carbon electrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09088"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09089 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09089">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Graphite electrode of material produced by high temperature deposition of carbon on a ceramic surface and subject to graphitization at \(\pu{3000^{\circ}C}\)</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>HOPGE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>HOPGE is employed as edge plane pyrolytic graphite electrode (EPPG, graphene sheets perpendicular to the electrode surface) or as basal plane pyrolytic graphite electrode (BPPG, graphene sheets parallel to the electrode surface). The properties of these electrodes are different.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>highly oriented pyrolytic graphite electrode</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09089"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>HOPGE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09090 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09090">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Graphite electrode based on a graphitic material impregnated with paraffin having a high melting point.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PIGE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Paraffin impregnation results in decreased porosity and lower charging current and memory effects.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>paraffin impregnated graphite electrode</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09090"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PIGE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09091 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09091">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrode composed of a boron-doped diamond film on an electron-conducting substrate.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>BDDE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Boron is the most widely used dopant to produce conducting diamond electrodes. A BDDE is characterized by a wide working potential range, low noise, a low and stable background current, chemical and mechanical stability, good biocompatibility, and resistance to passivation. BDDEs can be used as substrate materials for various chemical modifications.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>boron-doped diamond electrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09082"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09091"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>BDDE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09092 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09092">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Detection principle for a chemically modified electrode (CME) in which the electrode is modified by a biological recognition element.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:label>biorecognition</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09093 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09093">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Detection principle for a chemically modified electrode (CME) in which the signal originates from accumulation and/or release of an analyte or reagent as well as other electrochemical processes which can result in changes in the mass of the CME.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Simultaneous electrochemistry and microgravimetry can be accomplished by means of an electrochemical quartz crystal microbalance (EQCM, see electrochemical piezoelectric microgravimetry). For use with an EQCM, a CME is fabricated by covering one face of a metallized quartz resonator with a modifying film.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>change of mass</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09162"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09094 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09094">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Detection principle for a chemically modified electrode (CME) in which a charge transfer reaction between a CME and analyte proceeds at a lower overpotential than at the bare electrode. Usually, the effect of electrocatalysis is also an increase of faradaic current.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Electrocatalysis at a CME must be distinguished from redox mediation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrocatalysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09095"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09095 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09095">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Detection principle for a chemically modified electrode (CME) in which an immobilized redox couple can shuttle electrons between the electrode and an analyte, for instance, one with difficult access to the electrode surface because of steric reasons.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Electrocatalysis is accomplished by charge mediation but not all mediation results in electrocatalysis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>redox mediation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09096 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09096">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrode of a composite of carbon powder and a pasting liquid (including mineral oil, Nujol, bromoform, bromonaphthalene).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CPE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The CPE was first described by Adams in 1958. A CPE is typically housed in a Teflon holder, contacted by a conductive wire (occasionally a piston is used to renew the surface by extrusion of the used paste). Problems are long-term stability of the paste and that the binder can influence its properties.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>These electrodes can easily be chemically modified by the incorporation of reagents (electrocatalysts, redox mediators, complexation agents, enzymes, etc.) and by the attachment of micro- or nanoparticles to prepare electrochemical sensors for analytical applications. See for an example using rabbit anti-mouse immunoglobulin in an ELISA assay using a carbon ink electrode.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>carbon paste electrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09072"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09096"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CPE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09097 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09097">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Development of a carbon paste electrode that is screen printed using a carbon/polymer mixture of suitable composition.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>These electrodes can easily be chemically modified by the incorporation of reagents (electrocatalysts, redox mediators, complexation agents, enzymes, etc.) and by the attachment of micro- or nanoparticles to prepare electrochemical sensors for analytical applications. See for an example using rabbit anti-mouse immunoglobulin in an ELISA assay using a carbon ink electrode.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Typical composition of a carbon ink expressed as mass fractions is graphite powder \(\pu{60\%}\) and polystyrene \(\pu{40\%}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>carbon ink electrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09072"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09096"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09098 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09098">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrode made of a solid amalgam of an appropriate metal.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SAE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Liquid and paste amalgams can also be used.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Solid amalgam electrodes are electrochemically similar to the hanging mercury drop electrode. Their main advantages are high hydrogen overpotential (similar to liquid mercury); a wide working potential range; the simple mechanical, chemical, and electrochemical regeneration of the surface; a long lifetime; low toxicity of material (comparable with that of dental amalgams); applicability for field measurements; compatibility with flow-through systems; simple construction, without mobile parts; and easy miniaturization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>solid amalgam electrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09082"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09103"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09098"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SAE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09099 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09099">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Foil, wire or disc electrode made of gold which is easily fabricated into a variety of electrode geometries.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>At positive potentials, \(\ce{Au}\) forms an oxide and/or chemisorbed oxygen layer, while in the presence of complexing anions, such as chloride or cyanide, it readily undergoes oxidation and dissolution, limiting its working potential range and its applications.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>gold electrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09082"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09100 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09100">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Foil, wire, disc, or mesh electrode made of platinum, which is the most commonly used metallic working electrode in electrochemistry.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>\(\ce{Pt}\) has a very small overpotential for hydrogen evolution, which determines the negative potential limit in protic solvents. \(\ce{Pt}\) adsorbs hydrogen, resulting in hydrogen adsorption/desorption waves. However, the positive potential limit in polar aprotic solvents free of oxygen and water is higher than that for any other commonly used electrode materials. In the presence of water and/or oxygen, \(\ce{Pt}\) forms a film of oxide (and/or chemisorbed oxygen), resulting in potentially interfering waves or peaks.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>platinum electrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09101 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09101">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrode in the form of foil, mesh, wire, rod, tube, powder, pellets, or single crystal of silver.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>\(\ce{Ag}\) electrodes were first used in potentiometry for the determination of \(\ce{Cl-}\), \(\ce{Br-}\), \(\ce{I-}\), as well as for \(\ce{Ag+}\) ions, to which they are sensitive.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>silver electrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09127"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09102 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09102">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Liquid metal electrode used in polarography.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A mercury film electrode (MFE) or thin mercury film electrode (TMFE) is formed by coating a conducting substrate, usually glassy carbon, with a layer of mercury (thin film, amalgam, or array of microdrops), often by electrodeposition from a solution containing \(\ce{Hg(II)}\). The electrode area is normally in the range of \(\pu{0.1 to\, 0.5 cm2}\) and mercury film thickness typically ranges from \(\pu{10 to 1000 nm}\), producing a film of large surface area-to-volume ratio that results in a high analyte pre-concentration during the deposition step of anodic stripping voltammetry. Compared to an HMDE, the MFE provides high sensitivity and resolution; however, it is somewhat less reproducible and more prone to interferences from intermetallic formation and surface-active substances.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Liquid mercury is an ideal electrode material for negative potentials because of its high overpotential for hydrogen evolution (electrochemical reduction of hydroxonium cations from solution). However, mercury is readily oxidized, particularly in the presence of anions that form complexes or that precipitate with \(\ce{Hg(I)}\) or \(\ce{Hg(II)}\) ions, and thus it is not suitable for use at positive potentials. The use of liquid mercury has largely been discontinued because of concerns about the toxicity of the element and its compounds.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>mercury electrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09103"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09135"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09152"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09103 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09103">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mercury electrode formed by sequence of mercury drops falling from a small aperture.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DME</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Other kinds of mercury drop electrodes are the static mercury drop electrode (SMDE), in which a drop is held at constant radius before being mechanically knocked off, and the hanging mercury drop electrode (HMDE), in which the whole experiment is performed on a single drop of mercury.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The DME was introduced by Jaroslav Heyrovský (1890–1967). A drop is renewed every few seconds. Many metals amalgamate with mercury so that in the analysis of metal ions in aqueous solution the DME maintains a fresh surface throughout.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dropping mercury electrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09102"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09104 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09104">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrode containing two different metals (e.g. platinum and ruthenium) on its surface (e.g. to modify its electrocatalytic properties).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:label>bimetallic electrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09105 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09105">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Non-faradaic electrode system in which electric field variations in the semiconductor gate region control the magnitude of the source-drain current.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ChemFET</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>chemically-sensitive field effect transistor</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09105"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ChemFET</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09106 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09106">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrode coated with a thin layer of a substance that exhibits particular chemical, electrochemical, and/or optical properties.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CME</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A CME is prepared by the deliberate modification of the surface or bulk matrix material of the electrode with a selected reagent (monomeric or polymeric) that governs its electrochemical properties. Such manipulation of the molecular composition of the electrode aims to improve sensitivity, selectivity, and/or stability, allowing for the tailoring of its response to meet analytical needs.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>For electroanalytical purposes, a CME is designed for use as a predominantly voltammetric or amperometric device, but examples of potentiometric, impedimetric, or microgravimetric sensing devices are known.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The layer may be monomolecular, multimolecular, ionic, or polymeric in structure.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The range of electrode surface properties sought is broader than that of ion-selective electrodes (ISEs), which also involve the rational design of the phase-boundary, partition, and transport properties of membranes on or between electrodes. CMEs also differ from ISEs in that they are generally used voltammetrically or amperometrically, a faradaic (charge transfer) reaction being the basis of measurement, whereas ISEs are generally used in potentiometric mode, the measured quantity being a phase/boundary potential (interfacial potential difference).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The substance is termed a chemical modifier.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>chemically-modified electrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09092"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09094"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09095"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11539"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09106"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CME</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09107 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09107">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chemically-modified electrode in which the products of a reaction between enzymes in an immobilized enzyme layer and the enzyme reaction substrate are electrochemically active and are measured usually by amperometry or potentiometry.Schematic of the mediated oxidation of glucose to gluconolactone by glucose oxidase. The current passed to re-oxidise the mediator is proportional to the amount concentration of glucose.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>enzyme electrode</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>enzyme-modified electrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09106"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09127"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09128"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09108 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09108">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>ISE in which the membrane is a crystalline material prepared from either a single compound or a homogeneous mixture of compounds.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:label>homogeneous crystalline membrane electrodes</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09109 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09109">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>ISE prepared of an active substance, or mixture of active substances, mixed with an inert matrix (such as silicone rubber or \(\ce{PVC}\)), or placed on hydrophobized graphite or conducting epoxy resin, to form a heterogeneous sensing membrane.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:label>heterogeneous crystalline membrane electrodes</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09110 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09110">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Kind of ion-selective membranes used in an ISE which is a support matrix, containing an ion exchanger (either cationic or anionic), a plasticizer solvent, and possibly an uncharged, selectivity-enhancing species, form the ion-selective membrane which is usually interposed between two aqueous solutions. The matrix can be either macroporous (e.g., poly(propylene carbonate) filter, glass frit, etc.) or microporous (e.g., “thirsty” glass or polymeric material such as \(\ce{PVC}\)) yielding with the ion-exchanger and the solvent a “solidified” homogeneous mixture.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>An ISE based on these membranes exhibits a response due to the presence of the selectivity-enhancing species (which exhibit an ion-exchange property) in the membrane.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>non-crystalline membrane</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09119"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09111 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09111">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Kind of ion-selective membranes used in an ISE comprising synthetic cross-linked polymer with fixed sites or thin glass membrane.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>matrix membranes</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>self-supporting membranes</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The chemical composition of the polymer (e.g. polystyrenesulfonate, sulfonated poly(tetrafluoroethylene), poly(vinyl chloride) aminated) or the glass determines the membrane selectivity (see pH-sensitive electrode).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>rigid membranes</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09119"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09121"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09112 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09112">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Kind of ion-selective membranes used in an ISE comprising:  Positively-charged, hydrophobic cations which, when dissolved in a suitable organic solvent and held in an inert support (e.g., poly(propylenecarbonate) filter or \(\ce{PVC}\)), provide membranes which are sensitive to changes in the activities of anions. Negatively-charged hydrophobic anions which, when dissolved in a suitable organic solvent and held in an inert support (e.g., poly(propylene carbonate) filter or \(\ce{PVC}\)), provide membranes which are sensitive to changes of cation activities. Uncharged “carrier” electrodes based on solutions of molecular complexing agents of cations and anions, which can be used for preparing ion exchanger membranes sensitive and selective to certain cations and anions. Hydrophobic ion-pair electrodes of plasticized polymers (e.g., \(\ce{PVC}\)) containing a dissolved hydrophobic ion-pair respond to the ion activities of those components, which are relevant to the ion-pairs incorporated in the membrane. The responses can be Nernstian.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:label>membranes with mobile charged sites</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09119"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09113 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09113">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ion-selective electrode cell in which the ion-selective electrode and reference electrode are combined in a single sensor assembly, thereby avoiding the need for a separate external reference electrode.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:label>combined electrode</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09114 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09114">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Assembly of linked electrodes (usually microelectrodes, ultramicroelectrodes or nanoelectrodes) acting as a single electrode.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>By replacement of macroelectrodes with dimensions in the millimetre or centimetre range by a microelectrode array, the current density can be increased by orders of magnitude, as well as the ratio of faradaic to capacitive currents. When array electrodes are employed as generator-collector systems with two distinct potentials applied, the diffusion distance between electrodes becomes the main factor in controlling the total current.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Interdigitated array electrodes are commonly employed in electrochemical sensors.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrode array</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09072"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09084"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09085"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09086"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09115 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09115">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>pH-sensitive electrode, usually consisting of a bulb, or other suitable form, of special glass attached to a stem of high-resistance glass, complete with an internal reference electrode and internal filling solution system.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>glass electrode</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>pH electrode</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Line notation for the cell is: external reference electrode \(\vert\) external reference electrolyte \(\vert\vert\) test solution \(\vert\) glass membrane \(\vert\) internal reference electrolyte \(\vert\) internal reference electrode.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Other geometrical forms may be appropriate for special applications, e.g., a capillary electrode for the measurement of blood pH.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The bulk of the glass membrane is dry (of about \(\pu{50 \upmu m}\) thickness) with two hydrated layers (of \(\pu{5 to 100 nm}\) thickness) on each side of the membrane that contact the solution.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The hydrated layer acts as an ion-exchanger for the exchange of \(\ce{Na+}\) ions of the membrane with \(\ce{H3O+}\) ions from an aqueous solution. \[|\ce{Si{-}O{-}Na + H+(aq)|Si{-}OH + Na+(aq)}\] The glass electrode is therefore selective to \(\ce{Na+}\) (and any other alkali metal ions).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The membrane potential originates as a Galvani potential difference between the two sides of the glass membrane, which depends on the ratio of the activities of \(\ce{H+}\) ions on both sides of the membrane. In the absence of interfering ions, the membrane potential can be expressed by the Nernst equation \[\begin{align*} E &amp;= K + (RT/F)\ln(a_{\ce{H+}}({\rm{test}})/a_{\ce{H+}}({\rm{internal}})) \\ &amp;= K^{\prime}\!-\!(RT/F)/(\log_{10}({\rm{e}}))\ {\rm{pH(test)}} \end{align*}\] where \(K\) and \(K^{\prime}\) are constants, \(R\) is the gas constant, \(T\) the thermodynamic temperature, \(F\) the Faraday constant, and \(a\) activity. At \(\pu{25^{\circ}C}\) this becomes \(E \approx K^{\prime} - 0.0592\ \pu{pH(test)}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The potential across the membrane is measured between two reference electrodes (usually silver-silver chloride, chloride) and is related to the ratio of hydronium-ion activities by the Nikolsky–Eisenman equation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>glass pH-sensitive electrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09068"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09120"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09121"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09116 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09116">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ion-selective electrode in which the ion-selective membrane is a crystalline material mixed with an inert matrix (such as silicone rubber or polyvinyl chloride), or placed on hydrophobized graphite or conducting epoxy resin, to form a heterogeneous sensing membrane.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>heterogeneous crystalline membrane electrode</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>heterogeneous crystalline membrane ion-selective electrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09119"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09117 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09117">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ion-selective electrode in which the ion-selective membrane is a crystalline material prepared from either a single compound or homogeneous mixture of compounds.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>homogeneous crystalline membrane electrode</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>homogeneous crystalline membrane ion-selective electrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09119"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09118 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09118">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrochemical sensor based on the principle of the field effect transistor (FET) in which the surface of the gate is modified in a way that makes the response ion-selective.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ISFET</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A field effect transistor is a semi-conductor device in which the electric current between two semiconductor electrodes (termed the source and the drain) is controlled by the third electrode, termed the gate. Changes in the concentration of the species in the solution in contact with the gate surface thus control the current between the source and drain.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Not all chemical-sensitive semiconductor devices are based on the field effect transistor (FET), metal oxide semiconductor field effect transistor (MOSFET), or insulated gate field effect transistor (IGFET). Also, not all chemically-sensitive field effect transistors (ChemFETs) are ion-sensitive (ISFETs), but considerations are restricted here solely to ISFETs. Generally, ChemFETs are sensitive to gases and to enzyme substrates as well as to ions. Structurally, the ISFET is very similar to the IGFET.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Symbols and techniques related to the performance of ISFETs with regard to the determination of ions in solution should be in accordance with the recommendations for ISEs.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The output signal of the ISFET is usually a potential difference, the magnitude of which varies with the change in the logarithm of sensed ion activity or concentration in the same way (but not necessarily in sign) as the corresponding ion-selective electrode. The graphical representation of results should be in accordance with previous recommendations for ISEs, that is, the output potential difference should be plotted versus the logarithm of ion activity or concentration so that the slope of the plot is positive for cation responsive devices and negative for anion-responsive ones.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>ion-selective field effect transistor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09072"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09105"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09118"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ISFET</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09119 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09119">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Membrane with controlled permeability to a particular ion covering an electrode, or separating two electrolyte solutions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The potential difference of an ion-selective electrode derives from differences in activity of an ion at each side of the membrane.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>ion-selective membrane</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09108"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09109"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09110"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09111"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09112"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09120 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09120">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Equation describing the contribution of interfering ions, \(i\), to the electrode potential of an ion-selective electrode that is mainly dominated by the primary ion \(\ce{X}\). \[E = {\rm{const}} + \frac{RT}{z_{\rm{X}}F} \ln \left(a_{\ce{X}} + \sum\limits_i K_{{\ce{X}},i}^{\rm{pot}}a_{i}^{(z_{\ce{X}}/z_i)} \right)\] where \(E\) is the measured electrode potential of an ISE when the only variables are activities in the test solution. \(R\) is the gas constant, \(T\) the thermodynamic temperature, \(z_{\rm{X}}\) and \(z_{\rm{i}}\) are the numbers of electrons transferred by the analyte ion \(\ce{X}\) and \(i\), respectively, \(F\) is the Faraday constant, \(a_{\rm{X}}\) and \(a_{\rm{i}}\) the activity of \(\ce{X}\) and \(i\), respectively, and \(K_{\ce{X},i}^{\rm{pot}}\) is the potentiometric selectivity coefficient for interfering ion \(i\) with respect to the primary ion \(\ce{X}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The Nikolsky–Eisenman equation is an extension of the Nernst equation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Nikolsky–Eisenman equation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09063"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09068"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09121 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09121">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Potentiometric electrochemical sensor that measures pH as a linear function of electrode potential.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Measurement principles or methods include the use of an ion-selective electrode (see glass pH-sensitive electrode), ion-selective field effect transistor, metal-metal oxide electrodes, or redox electrode (e.g. hydrogen electrode, quinhydrone electrode).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>pH-sensitive electrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09072"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09095"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09115"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09118"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09122 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09122">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Enzyme-modified electrode, or an electrode modified by a substrate, cofactor or inhibitor, in which the activity of a product of the enzyme reaction is measured by an ion-selective electrode.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:label>potentiometric enzyme electrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09107"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09123 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09123">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ion-selective electrode cell that is responsive to an analyte gas.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In electrochemical literature the term ‘gas electrode’ includes potentiometric gas sensors and the classical, redox-equilibrium-based gas electrodes, such as the hydrogen electrode: \(\ce{Pt(s)}\,|\,\ce{H2(g)\,|\,\ce{H+(aq)}}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The ion-selective membrane of the gas sensor is in contact with a thin film of solution that is separated from the bulk of the sample solution by a gas-permeable membrane or an air gap. This intermediate solution interacts with the analyte gas that penetrates through the membrane or air gap, which results in a change of a measured constituent (e.g., \(\ce{H+}\) activity) of the intermediate solution. This change is then sensed by the ion-selective electrode and is related to the partial pressure of the gaseous species in the sample.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>These electrodes respond both to the partial pressure of the gas (\(\ce{H2}\) or \(\ce{Cl2}\)) and to the ionic activities of related species (\(\ce{H+}\) or \(\ce{Cl-}\)).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>potentiometric gas sensor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09119"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09124 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09124">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>A disc electrode that is embedded in the centre of a cylinder which rotates in solution around the longitudinal cylinder axis.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RDE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A second annular working electrode may be positioned concentric with the disc to make a rotating ring-disc electrode (RRDE). This arrangement is largely used in mechanistic studies.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The current is directly proportional to the electrode area and the electrode is uniformly accessible.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The limiting current is given by the Levich equation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>rotating disc electrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09140"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09124"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>RDE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09125 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09125">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>A disc electrode with a thin circular jet of fluid impinging on its centre.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The current is not directly proportional to the electrode area.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The limiting current is given by \[I_{\rm{lim}} = 1.59\ kz Fr^{3/4} d^{-1/2} V_{\rm{f}}^{3/4} \upsilon^{-5/12} D^{2/3} c\] where 1.59 is a numerical factor that arises from the mathematical solution to the convective-diffusion system, \(k\) is an empirical factor with values between 0.90 and 0.96, \(z\) the electron number of an electrochemical reaction, and \(F\) the Faraday constant, \(r\) is the radius of the disc electrode (\(\rm{cm}\)), \(d\) is the diameter of the impinging jet (\(\rm{cm}\)), \(V_{\rm{f}}\) is the volume flow rate (\(\pu{cm3 s-1}\)), \(\nu\) the kinematic viscosity (\(\pu{cm2 s-1}\)), and \(D\) the diffusion coefficient of the electroactive species (\(\pu{cm2 s-1}\)) of concentration \(c\) (\(\pu{mol cm-3}\)).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>wall-jet electrode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09063"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09126 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09126">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Potentiometry in which the potential is measured with time following a change in applied current.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The change in applied current is usually a step, but cyclic current reversals or linearly increasing currents are also used.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>chronopotentiometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09127"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09127 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09127">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrochemical measurement principle where the potential difference between an indicator electrode and a reference electrode is measured.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For measurements using ion-selective electrodes, the measurement is made under equilibrium conditions what means that the macroscopic electric current is zero and the concentrations of all species are uniform throughout the solution. The indicator electrode is in direct contact with the analyte solution, whereas the reference electrode is usually separated from the analyte solution by a salt bridge. The potential difference between the indicator and reference electrodes is normally directly proportional to the logarithm of the activity (concentration) of the analyte in the solution (Nernst equation).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>potentiometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09068"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09080"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09128 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09128">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrochemical measurement principle based on measurement of current at a controlled applied potential.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Amperometry can be distinguished from voltammetry by the parameter being controlled (electrode potential \(E\)) and the parameter being measured (electrode current \(I\) which is usually a function of time - see chronoamperometry).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In a non-stirred solution, a diffusion-limited current is usually measured, which is proportional to the concentration of an electroactive analyte.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The current is usually faradaic and the applied potential is usually constant.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The integral of current with time is the electric charge, which may be related to the amount of substance reacted by Faraday’s laws of electrolysis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>amperometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09075"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09129"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09148"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09129 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09129">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Amperometry in which the current is measured as a function of time after a change in the applied potential.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>If the potential step is from a potential at which no current flows (i.e., at which the oxidation or reduction of the electrochemically active species does not take place) to one at which the current is limited by diffusion (see diffusion-limited current), the current obeys the Cottrell equation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>chronoamperometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09069"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09128"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09130 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09130">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Voltammetry in which a sinusoidal alternating potential of small amplitude (\(\pu{10 to 50 mV}\)) of constant frequency (\(\pu{10 Hz to 100 kHz}\)) is superimposed on a slowly and linearly varying potential ramp. The resulting alternating current is plotted versus imposed DC potential. The obtained AC voltammogram is peak-shaped.Waveform for AC voltammetry. The applied potential as a function of time is a sinusoidal alternating potential superimposed on a linearly increasing ramp.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In cyclic AC voltammetry, a reverse potential scan is added to the usual forward potential scan.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The phase angle between the alternating current and imposed DC potential is measured in the case of AC voltammetry with phase detection.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>AC voltammetry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09148"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09131 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09131">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Empirically-determined quantity defined by the equation \[{\cal Q} = (1/Ac)\Delta Q/\Delta (t^{1/2})\] where \(A\) is the electrode surface area, \(c\) is the bulk concentration of the electroactive substance, \(Q\) is the charge passed, and \(\Delta Q/\Delta(t^{1/2})\) is the slope of a plot of \(Q\) against \(t^{1/2}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The SI unit of \(\cal Q\) is \(\pu{A s^{1/2} mol-1 m}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>chronocoulometric constant</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09074"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09132 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09132">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Empirically-evaluated quantity defined by the equation \[{\cal T} = I\tau^{1/2}/(A c_{B}) = j\tau^{1/2}/c_{\ce{B}}\] where \(I\) is the electric current, \(\tau\) is the transition time, \(A\) is the electrode surface area, \(\ce{B}\) is the bulk concentration of the electroactive substance \(\ce{B}\), and \(j=I/A\) is the current density.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The SI unit of \(\cal T\) is \(\pu{A s^{1/2} mol-1 m}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>chronopotentiometric constant</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09074"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09133 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09133">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement of electric current in voltammetry.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The usual objective is to minimise the sampling time. With modern instrumentation, the interval depends on the speed of a sample-and-hold device coupled with analog-to-digital (A-to-D) conversion.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>current sampling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09148"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09134 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09134">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Voltammetry in which the electric current is recorded as the electrode potential is varied with time cyclically between two potential limits, normally at a constant scan rate.Cyclic voltammetry. (a) Variation of applied potential with time. (b) Cyclic voltammogram of current against applied potential for a reversible redox couple.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CV</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>cyclic linear scan voltammetry</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Cyclic voltammetry is frequently used for the investigation of mechanisms of electrochemical/electrode reactions. The current-potential curve may be modelled to obtain reaction mechanisms and electrochemical parameters.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Normally the initial potential is chosen where no electrode reaction occurs and the switching potential is greater (more positive for an oxidation or more negative for a reduction) than the peak potential of the analyte reaction.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The initial potential is usually the negative or positive limit of the cycle but can have any value between the two limits, as can the initial scan direction. The limits of the potential are known as the switching potentials. See figure a.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The plot of current against potential is termed a cyclic voltammogram. Usually peak-shaped responses are obtained for scans in both directions. See figure b.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>cyclic voltammetry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09146"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09148"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09149"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09134"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CV</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09135 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09135">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Linear scan voltammetry with slow scan rate in which a dropping mercury electrode is used as the working electrode. See figure.Electric current as a function of applied potential for DC polarography, showing oscillations of current as the dropping mercury electrode grows and is detached.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>classical polarography</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>If the whole scan is performed on a single growing drop, the technique should be called single drop scan voltammetry. The term polarography in this context is discouraged.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>This is the oldest variant of polarographic techniques, introduced by Jaroslav Heyrovský (1890–1967).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Usually the drop time is between 1 and \(\pu{5 s}\) and the pseudo-steady-state wave-shaped dependence on potential is called a polarogram. If the limiting current is controlled by diffusion, it is expressed by the Ilkovič equation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>DC polarography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09103"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09139"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09148"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09136 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09136">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Voltammetry in which small potential pulses (constant height \(\pu{10 to 100 mV}\), constant width \(\pu{10 to 100 ms}\)) are superimposed onto a linearly varying potential or onto a staircase potential ramp. The current is sampled just before the onset of the pulse (e.g. \(\pu{10 to 20 ms}\)) and for the same sampling time just before the end of the pulse. The difference between the two sampled currents is plotted versus the potential applied before the pulse. Thus, a differential pulse voltammogram is peak-shaped.(a) Potential waveform for differential pulse voltammetry (not to scale). Typical values for pulse parameters are $\tau$=1 s, $t_{\rm{p}}$=50 ms, $E_{\tau}$=50 mV, $E_{\rm{p}}$=2 mV. Sampling interval indicated by horizontal curly brackets is typically 15 ms. (b) Differential pulse voltammogram. $I$ is the difference between currents sampled at ‘2’ and ‘1’.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DPV</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Differential pulse polarography is differential pulse voltammetry in which a dropping mercury electrode is used as the working electrode. A pulse is applied before the mechanically enforced end of the drop and the current is sampled twice: just before the onset of the pulse and just before its end. The pulse width is usually \(\pu{10 to 20\%}\) of the drop life. The drop dislodgement is synchronized with current sampling, which is carried out as in DPV.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The ratio of faradaic current to charging current is enhanced and the negative influence of charging current is partially eliminated in the same way as in normal pulse voltammetry (NPV). Moreover, subtraction of the charging current sampled before the application of the pulse further decreases its negative influence. Due to the more enhanced signal (faradaic current) to noise (charging current) ratio, the limit of detection is lower than with NPV. See figure.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The sensitivity of DPV depends on the reversibility of the electrode reaction of the analyte.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>differential pulse voltammetry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09103"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09142"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09148"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09136"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>DPV</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09137 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09137">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>The relationship that describes the electrode potential as a function of the current of a reversible redox system (reversible electrode reaction) in the steady state in voltammetry \[E = E^{\circ\prime} - \frac{RT}{zF} \ln \left(\frac{D_{\rm{red}}}{D_{\rm{ox}}}\right)^{s} \pm \frac{RT}{zF} \ln\!\left(\frac{I_{\rm{d,lim}} - I}{I}\right)\] where \(E^{\circ\prime}\) is the formal electrode potential, \(z\) the electron number of an electrochemical reaction, \(D_{\rm{red}}\) and \(D_{\rm{ox}}\) the diffusion coefficients of the reduced and oxidized forms of the electroactive substance, respectively, \(I_{\rm{d,lim}}\) is the limiting diffusion current, and \(I\) is the current at the potential being applied. The value of the exponent \(s\) is ½ for a stationary or dropping mercury electrode, ⅔ for a hydrodynamic electrode (see hydrodynamic voltammetry), or 1 for a microelectrode. In the equation, the last term is added for reduction and subtracted for oxidation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The equation is named after Jaroslav Heyrovský (1890–1967) and Dionýz Ilkovič (1907–1980).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Heyrovský–Ilkovič equation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09063"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09065"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09084"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09103"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09138"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09138 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09138">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Voltammetry with forced flow of the solution towards the electrode surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A linear potential scan, at sufficiently slow scan rates so as to ensure a steady state response, is usually applied.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Mass transport of a redox species enhanced by convection in this way results in a greater electric current. Convective mass transfer occurs up to the diffusion-limiting layer, within which the mass transfer is controlled by diffusion. Electroactive substance depletion outside the diffusion layer is annulled by convective mass transfer, which results in steady-state sigmoidal wave-shaped current-potential curves.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The forced flow can be accomplished by movement either of the solution (solution stirring, or channel flow), or of the electrode (electrode rotation or vibration).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>hydrodynamic voltammetry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09070"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09124"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09140"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09148"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09139 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09139">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Equation relating electric current at a dropping mercury electrode under purely diffusional control, \(I_{{\rm{d,}}t}\), the amount concentration of an electroactive substance at the drop surface \(c\), and the concentration in bulk solution \(c_{\rm{0}}\), \[I_{{\rm{d}},t} = -k\,z\,F\,D^{1/2}q_{\rm{m}}^{2/3} t^{1/6}(c - c_{0})\] where \(k\) is a numerical constant, $z$ the electron number of an electrochemical reaction, \(F\) the Faraday constant, \(D\) the diffusion coefficient of the electroactive substance, \(q_{\rm{m}}\) the mass flux of mercury at the dropping electrode, and \(t\) the time elapsed from the beginning of the drop.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>At sufficiently large overpotentials, the surface concentration \(c\) will fall to zero, giving a limiting diffusion current. \[I_{\rm{d,lim}} = k\,z\,F\,D^{1/2} q_{\rm{m}}^{2/3} t^{1/6} c_{0}\] Mostly, the average current (averaged over drop time and thus drop size) is calculated, when \(t\) becomes the drop time \(t_{\rm{d}}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The value of \(k\) depends on the units used for quantities in the equation (\(D\), \(q_{\rm{m}}\), \(t\), \(c\)), and whether the limiting current or the current averaged over the life of a drop is calculated. For \(D\) in \(\pu{cm2 s-1}\), \(q_{\rm{m}}\) in \(\pu{g s-1}\), \(t\) in \(s\), and \(c\) in \(\pu{mol cm-3}\), the constant \(k\) is 708 (limiting current in \(\pu{A}\)) and 607 (average current in \(\pu{A}\)).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Ilkovič equation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09063"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09103"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09144"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09140 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09140">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Equation relating limiting current at a rotating disc electrode (see hydrodynamic voltammetry) to the amount concentration of an electroactive substance in bulk solution \(c_{0}\), \[I_{\rm{lim}} = k\,z\,F\,A\,D^{2/3} \omega^{1/2}  u^{−1/6} c_{0}\] where \(k\) is a numerical factor, \(z\) the electron number of an electrochemical reaction, \(F\) the Faraday constant, \(A\) the electrode surface area, \(D\) the diffusion coefficient of the electroactive substance, \(\omega\) the angular velocity (rotation rate), and \( u\) the kinematic viscosity of the solution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The equation is named after Veniamin Grigorievich Levich (1917–1987).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The value of \(k\) depends on the units used for quantities in the equation (\(D\), \(A\), \(\omega\), \(\nu\), \(c\)), and is 1.554 for \(A\) in \(\pu{cm2}\), \(D\) in \(\pu{cm2 s-1}\), \(\omega\) in \(\pu{Hz}\), \(\nu\) in \(\pu{cm2 s-1}\), \(c_{0}\) in \(\pu{mol cm-3}\), and \(I_{\rm{lim}}\) in \(\pu{A}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Levich equation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09063"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09074"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09124"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09138"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09141 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09141">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Voltammetry in which the electric current is recorded as the electrode potential is varied linearly with time. See figure.Linear scan voltammogram of current against applied potential for a reversible redox couple.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>LSV</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>linear-sweep voltammetry</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>LSV corresponds to the first half cycle of cyclic voltammetry.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The peak current is expressed by the Randles–Ševčík equation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The scan is usually started at a potential where no electrode reaction occurs.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>linear-scan voltammetry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09134"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09143"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09148"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09149"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09141"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>LSV</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09142 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09142">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Voltammetry in which potential pulses of amplitude increasing by a constant increment and with a pulse width of \(\pu{2 to 200 ms}\) are superimposed on a constant initial potential. The current is sampled at the end of the pulse and then plotted versus the potential of the pulse. Sigmoidal wave-shaped voltammograms are obtained. See figure.(a) Waveform for normal pulse voltammetry (not to scale). Typical values for pulse parameters are \(\tau = \pu{1 s}\), \(t_{\rm{p}} = \pu{50 ms}\), \(E_{\rm{p}} = \pu{2 mV}\), \(E_{\rm{step}} = \pu{2 mV}\). Sampling interval indicated by horizontal curly brackets is typically \(\pu{15 ms}\). (b) Normal pulse voltammogram.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NPV</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Normal pulse polarography is NPV in which a dropping mercury electrode is used as the working electrode. A pulse is applied just before the mechanically enforced end of the drop. The pulse width is usually \(\pu{10 to 20\%}\) of the drop time. The drop dislodgment is synchronized with current sampling, which is carried out just before the end of the pulse, as in NPV.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The current is sampled just before the end of the pulse, when the charging current is greatly diminished. In this way, the ratio of faradaic current to charging current is enhanced and the negative influence of charging current is partially eliminated. Due to the improved signal (faradaic current) to noise (charging current) ratio, the limit of detection is lowered.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The sensitivity of NPV is not affected by the reversibility of the electrode reaction of the analyte.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>normal pulse voltammetry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09148"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09149"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09142"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>NPV</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09143 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09143">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Equations describing the peak current \(I_{\rm{p}}\) in linear scan voltammetry and cyclic voltammetry as a function of the amount concentration, \(c_{\rm{0}}\), of electroactive species in bulk solution. For a reversible charge transfer: \[I_{\rm{p,rev}} = k\,z\,F\,A\,c_0 (z\,F\,D u/RT)^{1/2}\] for irreversible charge transfer: \[I_{\rm{p,irrev}} = k^{\prime}(\alpha z^{\prime})^{1/2} zFA{c_0}(FD u/RT)^{1/2}\] where \(k\) and \(k^{\prime}\) are numerical constants, \(\alpha\) is the charge transfer coefficient (usually assumed to be close to 0.5), \(z\) the electron number of an electrochemical reaction, \(z^{\prime}\) the number of electrons transferred before the rate determining step, \(F\) the Faraday constant, \(R\) the gas constant, \(T\) the thermodynamic temperature, \(A\) the electrode surface area, \(D\) the diffusion coefficient, and \( u\) the scan rate.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>At \(T = \pu{298.15 K}\), for reversible charge transfer \(k = 0.446\), and for irreversible charge transfer, \(k^{\prime} = 0.496\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>For \(A\) in \(\pu{cm2}\), \(D\) in \(\pu{cm2 s-1}\), \(\nu\) in \(\pu{cm2 s-1}\), and \(c_{0}\) in \(\pu{mol cm-3}\), and \(I_{\rm{p}}\) in \(A\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Randles–Ševčík equations</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09063"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09134"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09141"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09144 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09144">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mass of a drop of liquid metal in polarography at the instant when it is detached from the tip of the capillary divided by the drop time \(t_{\rm{d}}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>mass flux</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The liquid is usually mercury.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>rate of flow of liquid metal in polarography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09135"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09145 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09145">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>DC polarography with current sampling at the end of each drop life mechanically enforced by a knocker at a preset drop time value. The current sampling and mechanical drop dislodge are synchronized.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>TAST polarography</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In this way, the ratio of faradaic current to double layer charging current is enhanced and the negative influence of charging current is partially eliminated. Due to the improved signal (faradaic current) to noise (charging current) ratio, the limit of detection is lowered.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sampled DC polarography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09133"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09135"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09146 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09146">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>The rate of change of applied potential with time.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>potential scan rate</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>potential sweep rate</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>sweep rate</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Scan rate has SI unit \(\pu{V s-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>scan rate</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09147 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09147">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Voltammetry in which a square-wave potential waveform is superimposed on an underlying linearlyhyphen‐varying potential ramp or staircase ramp. The current is sampled just before the end of the forward-going pulse and of the backward-going pulse and the difference of the two sampled currents is plotted versus the applied potential of the potential or staircase ramp. The square-wave voltammogram is peak-shaped. See figure.(a) Waveform for square wave voltammetry (not to scale). Typical values for pulse parameters are \(\tau = \pu{10 ms}\), \(t_{\rm{p}} = \pu{5 ms}\), \(E_{\rm{\tau}} = \pu{25 mV}\), \(E_{\rm{step}} = \pu{4 mV}\). Sampling interval indicated by horizontal curly brackets is typically \(\pu{1 ms}\). (b) Square wave voltammogram of the difference between currents sampled at ‘2’ and ‘1’ against applied potential.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Osteryoung square-wave voltammetry (OSWV)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SWV</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Most instruments show plots of the current at the end of the forward-going pulse and of the backward-going pulse vs. the potential, as well as their difference. This can give valuable information on the kinetics of the electrode reaction and the electrode process.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The sensitivity of SWV depends on the reversibility of the electrode reaction of the analyte.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>square-wave voltammetry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09148"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09149"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09147"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SWV</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09148 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09148">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrochemical measurement principle in which the electrode potential of a working electrode is controlled and the electric current measured.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:label>voltammetry</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09149 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09149">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Current-potential curve in a voltammetry experiment.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:label>voltammogram</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09070"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09147"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09148"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09150 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09150">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Transfer of an analyte from a test solution to an electrode surface as the first step in stripping voltammetry.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>accumulation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Analyte accumulation can be achieved by the following methods:electrodeposition of metal atoms onto a solid electrode,reduction of metal ions on a mercury electrode and dissolution of the formed metal atoms in the mercury (amalgam formation),precipitation of sparingly soluble mercuric, mercurous, or silver salts on mercury or silver electrodes,precipitation of metal oxides, hydroxides, or other insoluble salts on an inert (usually noble metal or carbon) electrode,chemical reaction of an analyte with an electrode material,adsorption of electroactive organic substances,adsorption of complexes of metal ions with organic ligands,synergistic adsorption of complexes,anion-induced adsorption of metal ions,surface complexation of metal ions,ion-exchange reactions at surface-modified electrodes,extraction by ion transfer across the interface of two immiscible electrolyte solutions,(m) reaction by biological recognition processes involving nucleic acids, antibodies, enzymes, etc.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>accumulation in stripping voltammetry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09155"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09151 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09151">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Stripping voltammetry involving pre-concentration by adsorption of the analyte (in contrast to electrochemical accumulation).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>AdSV</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A peak-shaped adsorptive stripping voltammogram is obtained. Peak current depends on time of accumulation, mass transport of analyte (stirring), scan rate and mode (linear or pulse), and analyte concentration in solution.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>AdSV is usually employed for analysis of organic compounds or metal complexes with organic ligands. Stripping is done by means of an anodic or a cathodic voltammetric scan (linear or pulse), during which the adsorbed compound is oxidized or reduced.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>adsorptive stripping voltammetry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09146"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09149"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09150"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09155"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09151"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>AdSV</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09152 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09152">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Stripping voltammetry in which material accumulated at the working electrode is electrochemically oxidized in the stripping step.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ASV</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A peak-shaped anodic stripping voltammogram is obtained. Peak current depends on time of accumulation, mass transport of analyte (stirring), scan rate and mode (linear or pulse), and analyte concentration in solution.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A solid electrode (see metal electrode), carbon paste or composite electrode, bismuth film electrode, mercury film electrode, or static mercury drop electrode (see mercury electrode) may be used.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>anodic stripping voltammetry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09096"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09102"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09146"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09149"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09150"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09155"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09152"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ASV</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09153 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09153">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Stripping voltammetry in which material accumulated at the working electrode is electrochemically reduced in the stripping step. See figure.Applied potential against time during the accumulation and stripping steps in cathodic stripping voltammetry.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CSV</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A peak-shaped cathodic stripping voltammogram is obtained. Peak current depends on time of accumulation, mass transport of analyte (stirring), scan rate and mode (linear or pulse), and analyte concentration in solution.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>cathodic stripping voltammetry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09146"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09149"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09150"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09155"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09153"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CSV</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09154 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09154">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Two-step electrochemical measurement in which 1) material is accumulated at an electrode and 2) the material is removed by chemical reaction or electrochemically at constant current with measurement of electrode potential. The time between changes in potential in step two is related to the concentration of analyte in the solution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PSA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Historically for the analysis of metal ions, mercury ions were added to the test solution to form a mercury amalgam when reduced. Alternatively, a hanging mercury drop electrode (HMDE) or mercury film electrode (MFE) was used and the oxidizing agent added after amalgam formation. However, the toxicity of mercury and its compounds have all but precluded the present-day use of mercury.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The accumulation is similar to that used in stripping voltammetry.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The stripping potentiogram shows staircase curves of potential as a function of time. Frequently, the first derivative is displayed (\({\rm{d}}E/{\rm{d}}t = f(t)\)), as this produces peak-shaped signals. The time between transitions (peaks) is proportional to the concentration of analyte in the test solution.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>potentiometric stripping analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09102"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09103"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09150"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09155"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09154"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PSA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09155 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09155">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Two-step electrochemical measurement in which 1) material is accumulated at an electrode and 2) the amount of an accumulated species is measured by voltammetry. The measured electric current in step 2 is related to the concentration of analyte in the solution by calibration.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Anodic stripping voltammetry (ASV) was historically used to measure concentrations of metal ions in solution using cathodic accumulation with mercury to form an amalgam. Due to the toxicity of mercury and its compounds, inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry and inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry have frequently replaced ASV at mercury electrodes in the laboratory, often sacrificing the probing of speciation and lability in complex matrices. Mercury has now been replaced by non-toxic bismuth or antimony as films on a solid electrode support (such as glassy carbon) with equally good sensitivity and detection limits.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Because the accumulation (pre-concentration) step can be prolonged, increasing the amount of material at the electrode, stripping voltammetry is able to measure very small concentrations of analyte.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Often the product of the electrochemical stripping is identical to the analyte before the accumulation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Stripping voltammetry is a calibrated method to establish the relation between amount accumulated in a given time and the concentration of the analyte in solution.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Types of stripping voltammetry refer to the kind of accumulation (e.g. adsorptive stripping voltammetry) or the polarity of the stripping electrochemistry (anodic, cathodic stripping voltammetry).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>stripping voltammetry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09060"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09148"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09150"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09151"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09152"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09153"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09156 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09156">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Direct coulometry at controlled potential in which the electric charge passed after the application of a potential step perturbation is measured as a function of time (\(Q{-}t\) curve).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Chronocoulometry provides the same information that is provided by chronoamperometry, since it is based on the integration of the \(I{-}t\) curve. Nevertheless, chronocoulometry offers important experimental advantages, such as (i) the measured signal usually increases with time and hence the later parts of the transient can be detected more accurately, (ii) a better signal-to-noise ratio can be achieved, and (iii) other contributions to overall charge passed as a function of time can be discriminated from those due to the diffusion of electroactive substances.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>chronocoulometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09129"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09161"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09157 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09157">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measuring instrument to obtain the electrical charge passed in an experiment, or to produce a known amount of substance in a titration.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Modern instruments for measuring trace water by Karl Fischer titration use the passage of a measured charge to produce iodine from iodide solution. They are known as Karl Fischer coulometers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The silver coulometer is a primary reference measurement procedure for charge and current. By weighing the mass of silver deposited in a known time at constant current, the charge and current are calculated from Faraday’s laws of electrolysis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>coulometer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09075"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09158"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09159 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09159">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrochemical measurement principle in which the electric charge required to carry out a known electrochemical reaction is measured. By Faraday’s laws of electrolysis, the amount of substance is proportional to the charge.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Coulometry used to measure the amount of substance is a primary reference measurement procedure not requiring calibration with a standard for a quantity of the same kind (i.e. amount of substance).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The coulometric experiment can be carried out at controlled (constant) potential (see direct coulometry at controlled potential) or controlled (constant) current (see direct coulometry at controlled current).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>coulometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09075"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09160"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09161"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09160 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09160">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coulometry at an imposed, constant electric current in the electrochemical cell.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Direct coulometry at controlled current is usually carried out in convective mass transfer mode. The end-point of the electrolysis, at which the current is stopped, must be determined either from the inflection point in the \(E{-}t\) curve or by using visual or objective end-point indication, similar to volumetric methods. The total electric charge is calculated as the product of the constant current and time of electrolysis or can be measured directly using a coulometer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The advantage of this method is that the electric charge consumed during the electrode reaction is directly proportional to the electrolysis time. Care must be taken to avoid the potential region where another electrode reaction may occur.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>direct coulometry at controlled current</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09058"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09157"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09159"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09161 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09161">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coulometry at a preselected constant potential of the working electrode.Electric current against time showing the representation of the electric charge in direct coulometry at controlled potential as the area under the graph.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Direct coulometry at controlled potential is usually carried out in convective mass transfer mode using a large surface working electrode. Reference electrode and auxiliary electrode are placed in separate compartments. The total electric charge is obtained by integration of the \(I{-}t\) curve or can be measured directly using a coulometer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In principle, the end point at which \(I = 0\), i.e. when the concentration of species under study becomes zero, can be reached only at infinite time. However, in practice, the electrolysis is stopped when the current has decayed to a few percent of the initial value and the charge passed at infinite time is calculated from a plot of charge \(Q(t)\) against time \(t\). For a simple system under diffusion control \(Q_{t} = Q_{\infty}[1 - \exp(-DAt/V\delta)]\), where \(Q_{\infty} = \lim_{t \to \infty}Q(t)\) is the total charge passed at infinite time, \(D\) is the diffusion coefficient of the electroactive species, \(A\) the electrode area, \(\delta\) the diffusion layer thickness, and \(V\) the volume of the solution. See figure.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>direct coulometry at controlled potential</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09129"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09132"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09156"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09157"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09159"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09162 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09162">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrogravimetry using an electrochemical quartz crystal microbalance.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The change of mass is, for rigid deposits, linearly proportional to the change of the resonance frequency of the quartz crystal, according to the Sauerbrey equation. For non-rigid deposits, corrections must be made.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrochemical piezoelectric microgravimetry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09163"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09164"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09163 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09163">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrochemical cell in which the working electrode is located on one surface of a piezoelectric quartz crystal, the oscillation frequency of which is measured.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>EQCM</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>electrochemical quartz crystal nanobalance (EQCN)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The change in resonant frequency (proportional to mass changes) is measured during electrochemical processes, such as linear scan voltammetry or cyclic voltammetry, giving simultaneous information on changes in mass and charge transfer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The electrode may be chemically modified.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrochemical quartz crystal microbalance</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09058"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09134"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09141"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09162"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09163"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>EQCM</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09164 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09164">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Gravimetry in which the material to be weighed is obtained by electrochemical reaction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The electrolysis can be performed by controlling current or electrode potential or, in the simple two-electrode arrangement, controlling the potential difference between the two electrodes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When the precipitate has a known stoichiometric composition, electrogravimetry is a primary reference measurement procedure not requiring calibration with a standard for a quantity of the same kind (i.e. amount of substance).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrogravimetry</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09165 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09165">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Titration in which the electric conductivity of a solution is measured as a function of the amount of titrant added.Conductimetric titration curve of conductance ($G$) against volume of titrant. The end-point of the titration is indicated by a change in slope of the graph.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The equivalence-point is obtained as the intersection of linear parts of the conductance \(G\), versus titrant volume \(V\), curve (see figure).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The method can be used for deeply coloured or turbid solutions. Acid-base and precipitation reactions are most frequently used.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The method is based on replacing an ionic species of the analyte with another species, corresponding to the titrant or the product with significantly different conductance.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>conductometric titration</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09166 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09166">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement principle in which the electric conductivity of a solution is measured.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The conductivity of a solution depends on the concentration and nature of ions present.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>conductometry</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09167 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09167">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrochemical measurement principle based on the measurement of the dielectric constant of a sample resulting from the orientation of particles (molecules or ions) that have a dipole moment in an electric field.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Dielectrometric titrations use dielectrometry for the end-point detection.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The method is used to monitor the purity of dielectrics, for example to detect small amounts of moisture.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dielectrometry</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09168 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09168">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrochemical measurement method of the complex impedance of an electrochemical system as a function of the frequency of a small amplitude (normally \(\pu{5 to 10 mV}\)) sinusoidal voltage perturbation superimposed on a fixed value of applied potential or on the open circuit potential.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>EIS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Impedimetric sensors are based on measurement of a concentration-dependent parameter taken from analysis of the respective electrochemical impedance spectra, or from the impedance magnitudes at a chosen fixed frequency.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The sinusoidal current response lags behind the sinusoidal voltage perturbation by a phase angle \(\phi\). Resistances (e.g. to charge transfer) give a response in phase with the voltage perturbation; capacitances (e.g. double layer) give a response \(\pu{90\!^{\circ}}\) out of phase; combinations of resistances and capacitances give phase angles between 0 and \(\pu{90\!^{\circ}}\). Plots of the out of phase vs. the in phase component of the impedance for all the frequencies tested are called complex plane (or Nyquist) plots. Plots of the phase angle and the magnitude of the impedance vs. the logarithm of perturbation frequency are called Bode diagrams. Complex plane plots are the more commonly used for electrochemical sensors.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrochemical impedance spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09077"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09168"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>EIS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09169 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09169">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement principle in which the complex electric impedance of a system is measured, usually as a function of a small amplitude sinusoidal electrode potential.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:label>impedimetry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09168"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09170 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09170">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrochemical techniques (potentiometry, amperometry, voltammetry, coulometry, conductimetry) used for analyte detection in flowing systems.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Measurement in flowing systems (e.g. flow injection analysis) should be differentiated from batchwise determination in individual fractions obtained after separation (e.g., high performance liquid chromatography, capillary zone electrophoresis).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrochemical detection in flowing systems</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09127"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09128"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09148"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09159"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09166"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09171 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09171">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement methods combining spectrometric and electrochemical measurements.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109"/>
        <rdfs:label>spectroelectrochemistry</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09172 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09172">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of electron emission spectroscopy that utilizes Auger electrons emitted from the target following excitation with an electron beam or X-ray beam.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>AES</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Auger electron spectroscopy</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>An electron beam in the energy range \(\pu{2 keV}\) to \(\pu{30 keV}\) is often used for excitation of the Auger electrons. Auger electrons can also be excited with X-rays, ions, and other sources, but the term Auger electron spectroscopy, without additional qualifiers, is usually reserved for electron-beam-induced excitation. Where an X-ray source is used, the Auger electron energies are referenced to the Fermi level, but, where an electron beam is used, the reference may either be the Fermi level or the vacuum level. Conventionally, spectra may be presented in the direct or differential forms.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Chemical species are identified from the energies of the emitted Auger electrons and the typically low energies of the emitted electrons and resulting escape depth mean the technique is particularly sensitive for surface species analysis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Most AES is performed under high vacuum or ultra-high vacuum.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Auger spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09172"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>AES</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09173 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09173">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Detection of an Auger electron and a core-level photoelectron, emitted from a solid irradiated by mono-energetic X-rays, within a sufficiently short time that the Auger electron shall arise from the decay of the particular core-hole associated with the photoelectron.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>APECS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>Auger photoelectron coincidence spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09173"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>APECS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09174 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09174">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method in which an electron spectrometer is used to measure the energy, intensity, and/or energy broadening distribution of quasi-elastically scattered electrons from a solid or liquid surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>EPES</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>An electron beam in the energy range \(\pu{100 keV}\) to \(\pu{3 keV}\) is often used for this kind of spectroscopy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>EPES is often an auxiliary method of Auger electron spectroscopy and reflection electron energy loss spectroscopy (REELS), providing information on the composition of the surface layer. EPES is suitable for the experimental determination of the electron inelastic mean free path, the electron differential elastic scattering cross section, and the surface excitation parameter.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In general, electron sources with energy spreads that are less than \(\pu{1 keV}\) are required to provide adequate information.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>elastic peak electron spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09172"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09246"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09327"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09329"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09174"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>EPES</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09175 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09175">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method to elucidate composition and structure of the outermost atomic layers of a solid material, in which principally monoenergetic neutral atoms or singly charged probe ions are used to sputter atoms or ions out of the solid surface, their energy being recorded at one or more angle of scattering.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>elastic recoil detection</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>elastic recoil detection analysis</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In the analysis, it is assumed that the recoiled atom or ion is from a single binary elastic scattering event. The energy is the complement of the primary ion scattered ion energy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The scattered primary ions can be removed from the spectrum by a suitable choice of angle of scattering.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>This method is often combined with rutherford backscattering spectrometry, as they share many concepts and instrumental items.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>direct recoil spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09178"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09378"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09176 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09176">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method to elucidate the composition and structure of the very outermost atomic layers of a solid material, in which principally monoenergetic, singly-charged probe ions scattered from the surface are detected and recorded as a function of their energy, angle of scattering, or both.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>LEIS, LEISS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>LEIS(S) is a form of ion beam analysis in which the probe ions, typically \(\ce{He+}\) or \(\ce{Ne+}\), have energies in the range \(\pu{0.1 keV}\) to \(\pu{10 keV}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The acronym usually has only one “S”.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>low-energy ion scattering spectrometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09378"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09176"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>LEIS, LEISS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09177 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09177">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method to elucidate the composition and structure of the outermost atomic layers of a solid material, in which principally monoenergetic, singly-charged probe ions scattered from the surface are detected and recorded as a function of their energy, angle of scattering, or both.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>MEIS, MEISS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>By using channelling and aligning the incident-ion beam along a crystal axis, the scattering from the substrate can be suppressed, enhancing the signal quality and visibility obtained for amorphous overlayers. By further aligning the detector along a second crystal axis, the double-alignment mode, the scattering from the substrate can be further suppressed, improving the signal quality and visibility for amorphous overlayers to a high level.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In some cases, an angle-sensitive detector is used that allows extensive structure and depth profile (see depth profiling) information to be obtained.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>MEIS is a form of ion beam analysis in which the probe ions, typically protons, have energies in the range \(\pu{100 keV}\) to \(\pu{200 keV}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The acronym usually has only one “S”.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>medium-energy ion scattering spectrometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09244"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09378"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09177"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>MEIS, MEISS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09178 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09178">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method to elucidate composition and structure of layers at the surface of a solid material, in which principally monoenergetic, singly charged probe ions scattered from the surface with a Rutherford cross section are detected and recorded as a function of their energy or angle of scattering, or both.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RBS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>By using channelling and aligning the incident-ion beam along a crystal axis, the scattering from the substrate can be suppressed so that enhanced signal quality and visibility are obtained for amorphous overlayers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>RBS is a form of ion beam analysis in which the probe ions, typically \(\ce{He+}\) but sometimes \(\ce{H+}\), have energies in the range \(\pu{1 MeV}\) to \(\pu{2 MeV}\). In its traditional form, a solid-state energy-dispersive detector is used. In the form of high-resolution RBS, the energy can be reduced to \(\pu{300 keV}\) and a high-resolution (ion optical) spectrometer can be used.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Rutherford backscattering spectrometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09378"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09381"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09178"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>RBS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09179 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09179">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method in which a mass spectrometer is used to measure the ratio of mass to charge and the abundance of ionized entities emitted from a sample in air as a result of a nearby corona discharge in a humid carrier gas.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DAPCI</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Both positive and negative ions are observed with the mechanisms involving protonation through water molecules and ionization through the Penning process.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The carrier gas is usually nitrogen.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>desorption atmospheric pressure chemical ionization mass spectrometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09182"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09190"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09179"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>DAPCI</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09180 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09180">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method in which a mass spectrometer is used to measure the ratio of mass to charge and the abundance of ionized entities emitted from a sample in air as a result of desorption by a jet of hot solvent vapour and ionization by ultraviolet light.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DAPPI</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Both positive and negative ions are observed.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The desorption mechanism is thermal.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>desorption atmospheric pressure photoionization mass spectrometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09181"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09184"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09185"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09188"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09180"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>DAPPI</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09181 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09181">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method in which a mass spectrometer is used to measure the ratio of mass to charge and the abundance of ionized entities emitted from a sample in air as a result of the bombardment by ionized solvent droplets generated by pneumatically assisted electrospray ionization.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DESI</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>DESI is one of the few surface analysis methods designed to analyse materials without exposure to vacuum. It is used for complex molecules, organic molecules, and biomolecules. In vivo analysis is claimed to be possible.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Water and methanol are often used as the solvents to create the droplets. Acids and alkalis are added to control the solution pH.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>desorption electrospray ionization mass spectrometry</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09181"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>DESI</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09182 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09182">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method in which a mass spectrometer is used to measure the ratio of mass to charge and the abundance of ionized entities emitted from a sample in air as a result of the interaction with a beam containing metastable, excited atoms heated to about \(\pu{500^{\circ}C}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DART</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Both positive and negative ions are observed, although the mechanism for positive ions can be through protonation involving water molecules and the process for negative ions through Penning ionization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>DART is the registered trademark of JEOL, USA, Inc.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The beam atoms usually used are helium, nitrogen, or neon.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>direct analysis in real time</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09190"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09182"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>DART</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09183 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09183">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Secondary-ion mass spectrometry analysis with primary ion current density sufficiently high for use in the analysis of sample surface layers in the depth direction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Dynamic SIMS is often simply termed SIMS.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dynamic secondary-ion mass spectrometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09184 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09184">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method in which a mass spectrometer is used to measure the ratio of mass to charge quotient and the abundance of ionized entities emitted from a sample in air via ablation by a focused pulsed laser while simultaneously directing an electrospray jet through the desorbed plume to enhance sample ionization.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ELDIMS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Both positive and negative ions are observed.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The desorption mechanism is thermal.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>With the addition of a matrix on the sample, this is termed matrix-assisted laser desorption electrospray ionization (MALDESI).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>With the use of an infrared laser for ablation, this has sometimes been termed laser ablation electrospray ionization (LAESI).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrospray laser desorption ionization mass spectrometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09180"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09181"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09185"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09184"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ELDIMS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09185 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09185">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method in which a mass spectrometer is used to measure the ratio of mass to charge and the abundance of ionized entities emitted from a sample in air as a result of an inert gas being passed over the sample to pick up the molecules, which are subsequently ionized by ionized solvent droplets generated by pneumatically assisted electrospray ionization.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>EESI</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Both positive and negative ions are observed.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>extractive electrospray ionization mass spectrometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09180"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09181"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09184"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09188"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09185"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>EESI</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09186 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09186">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Variant of static secondary-ion mass spectrometry in which the intensities for each mass in two spectra from the same area, recorded with different beam energies or different bombarding ions, are ratioed to each other and the result is used to scale one of the spectra to generate a new spectrum.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>As with static SIMS, the ion areic dose during measurement is restricted to less than \(\pu{E16}\) ions per \(\pu{m2}\) to an extent that depends on both the material of the sample and the size of the molecular fragments being analysed.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The G-SIMS spectrum enables the mass of whole molecules on the surface to be determined more readily than in static SIMS.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The “G” in G-SIMS originally indicated the gentleness of the process generated.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>G-SIMS</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09193"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09223"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09187 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09187">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method in which a mass spectrometer is used to measure the ratio of mass to charge and the abundance of ionized entities emitted from a sample in air as a result of laser ablation and electrospray ionization.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>LAESI</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Both positive and negative ions are observed with the mechanisms involving protonation through water molecules and ionization through the Penning process.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The laser wavelength is usually in the infrared region.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>laser ablation electrospray ionization mass spectrometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09180"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09181"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09184"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09185"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09188"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09187"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>LAESI</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09188 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09188">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method in which a mass spectrometer is used to measure the ratio of mass to charge and the abundance of ionized entities from a sample that has been mixed with a matrix whose role is enhancing the ion yield produced by laser ablation and electrospray ionization.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>MALDESI</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Both positive and negative ions are observed.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The matrix, e.g. glycerol, allows efficient absorption of the laser energy and assists the desorption of the analyte molecules in the ejected charged droplets.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>matrix-assisted laser desorption electrospray ionization mass spectrometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09180"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09181"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09184"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09185"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09187"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09188"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>MALDESI</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09189 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09189">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method in which a time-of-flight mass spectrometer is used to measure the ratio of mass to charge and the abundance of ions emitted, as a result of a short pulse of laser illumination, from a sample whose analyte is contained in a matrix that assists the formation of ions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>MALDI</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>MALDI is used to analyse non-volatile polar biological and organic macromolecules, as well as polymers, to masses of over \(\pu{3000 ku}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The matrix used for assisting the ion emission needs a strong absorbance at the laser wavelength and a low enough mass to sublime. Examples of matrices for \(\pu{337 nm}\) wavelength laser light are 2,5-dihydroxybenzoic acid (DHB), 3,5-dimethoxy-4-hydroxycinnamic acid (sinapinic acid), and α-cyano-4-hydroxycinnamic acid (CHCA).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionization mass spectrometry</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09189"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>MALDI</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09190 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09190">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method in which a mass spectrometer is used to measure the ratio of mass to charge and the abundance of ionized entities emitted from a sample in air as a result of the bombardment by a focused plasma.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PADI</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Both positive and negative ions are observed, although the mechanism for positive ions can be through protonation involving water molecules and the process for negative ions through Penning ionization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The beam atoms usually used are helium, nitrogen, air, or argon.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The energy of the plasma ions is very low.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>plasma assisted desorption ionization mass spectrometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09182"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09190"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PADI</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09191 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09191">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mode of desorption electrospray ionization mass spectrometry with reactive chemicals within the spray solution to enhance the analytical specificity.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The method has been used to detect chemical warefare agents, as well as natural products in marine alga.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>reactive desorption electrospray ionization mass spectrometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09181"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method in which a focused beam of primary ions produces secondary ions by sputtering from a solid surface. The secondary ions are analysed by mass spectrometry.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SIMS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>SIMS is, by convention, generally classified as either dynamic, in which the material surface layers are continually removed as they are being measured, or static, in which the ion areic dose during measurement is restricted to less than \(\pu{E16}\) ions \(\pu{m2}\) in order to retain the surface in an essentially undamaged state.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>secondary-ion mass spectrometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09183"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09193"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SIMS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09193 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09193">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Method of secondary-ion mass spectrometry using low current densities for analysis of sample surface components, in contrast with dynamic secondary-ion mass spectrometry, which is used for analysis of components in the depth direction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SSIMS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Ions are usually detected by a time of flight mass spectrometer (TOF-SIMS).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The ion areic dose during measurement is restricted to less than \(\pu{E16}\) ions per \(\pu{m2}\) to an extent that depends on both the material of the sample and the size of the molecular fragments being analysed.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When the sample is an organic molecule in solid form, the term is often changed to organic SIMS or molecular SIMS.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>static secondary-ion mass spectrometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09183"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09223"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09193"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SSIMS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09194 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09194">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionization mass spectrometry using a combined liquid and particulate matrix.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SALDI</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The first particulate matrix to be used was graphite. More recently, nano particle matrices have been used. Ethane-1,2-diol (ethylene glycol) is a typical liquid.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>surface-assisted laser desorption/ionization mass spectrometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09189"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09194"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SALDI</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09195 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09195">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Attenuated total reflection spectroscopy in which the infrared absorption spectrum of a sample in contact with a prism is measured.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ATRIRS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>ATRIRS has particular application in the surface chemical analysis of solid particle films immersed in aqueous solution.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Most ATRIR work is carried out with an angle of incidence of \(\pu{45\!^{\circ}}\) inside a \(\ce{ZnSe}\) or diamond prism, both with a refractive index of 2.4. With such optical conditions, total internal reflection is achieved with an effective sample refractive index less than about 1.7 for almost all powdered samples.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>attenuated total reflection infrared spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09195"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ATRIRS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09196 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09196">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method in which the infrared absorption spectrum of light reflected and transmitted from powdered samples is measured.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>diffuse reflectance infrared Fourier transform spectroscopy</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Used for the analysis of weakly-reflective surfaces in gas phase or vacuum.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>diffuse reflection-absorption infrared spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09197 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09197">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method for the thickness of transparent surface films and layers and for the refractive index of a reflecting material.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Ellipsometry may utilise monochromatic light, while spectroscopic ellipsometry employs a range of frequencies.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Radiation that is linearly polarized oblique to the surface becomes elliptically polarized after reflection at non-normal incidence. The ellipticity is obtained from measurements of the intensity and polarization of the incident and the reflected radiation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>ellipsometry</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09198 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09198">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>X-ray absorption fine structure spectroscopy in the energy region from the core-level edge to an energy several hundred $\pu{eV}$ greater.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>EXAFS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>EXAFS has been used as an alternate term for the superordinate concept of XAFS.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Extended X-ray absorption fine structure results mainly from the single scattering of the photoelectron from surrounding atoms and is exhibited as oscillations in the absorption cross section with increasing photon energy. Analysis of the oscillations provides information, through bond lengths, on the local chemical environment of the emitting atom.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>extended X-ray absorption fine structure spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09212"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09198"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>EXAFS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09199 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09199">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method in which the elastically scattered intensity of X-rays from a reflecting surface is measured for small-angle deflections at grazing incidence.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>GISAXS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>GISAXS provides structural information for the surface or for particles on the surface, with lengths scales between several nm and several hundred nm.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The grazing incidence angle depends on photon energy and material and is typically less than \(\pu{2\!^{\circ}}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>grazing-incidence small-angle X-ray scattering analysis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09199"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GISAXS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09200 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09200">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>X-ray reflectometry in which significant scattered intensity arises from imperfections in the specimen, the scattered intensity falling outside the specular condition (see specular X-ray reflectometry).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>diffuse X-ray reflectometry</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Imperfections generating diffuse XRR include surface and interface roughnesses, film defects, and inhomogeneities. Quantitative estimates of these can be made by modelling.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>off-specular X-ray reflectometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09204"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09214"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09201 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09201">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Spectroscopy based on reflection-absorption.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>infrared reflection-absorption spectroscopy</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>RAIRS allows improved discrimination between species at the surface and those in the bulk.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>RAIRS is most sensitive at metal surfaces with grazing incidence.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>RAIRS is usually employed with the sample in a vacuum or gas.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>RAIRS may be employed with polarization modulation to minimize background absorption.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>reflection-absorption infrared spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09202 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09202">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method in microscopy in which spectroscopic data may be collected with the detector closer to the sample than the optical wavelength employed.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SNOM</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>near-field optical microscopy</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>near-field scanning optical microscopy (NSOM)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Includes near-field infrared, Raman, and fluorescence spectroscopies.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>scanning near-field optical microscopy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09202"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SNOM</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09203 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09203">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method in which the elastically scattered intensity of X-rays is measured for small-angle deflections.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Angular scattering is usually measured within the range \(\pu{0.1\!^{\circ}}\) to \(\pu{10\!^{\circ}}\). This provides structural information on macromolecules, as well as periodicity on length scales typically larger than \(\pu{5 nm}\) and less than \(\pu{200 nm}\) for ordered or partially ordered systems.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The X-ray source can be a synchrotron, in which case the term synchrotron radiation small-angle X-ray scattering (SRXAS) is occasionally encountered.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Wide-angle X-ray scattering (WAXS) is an analogous technique, similar to X-ray crystallography, in which scattering at larger angles, which is sensitive to periodicity on smaller length scales, is measured.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>small-angle X-ray scattering</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09204 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09204">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mode of X-ray reflectrometry in which the angle between the detected beam and the sample surface is equal to that of the incident beam and the sample surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In this case, the angle scattering \(2 \theta\) is twice the incidence angle, \(\omega\). The detected, scattered X-ray intensity is measured as a function of either \(\omega\) or \(2 \theta\) or the scattering vector, \(q_{\rm{z}}\). The intensity data are usually presented as a function of \(q_{\rm{z}}\) or \(\omega\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Specular XRR is the plainary mode for XRR and is often meant when the term XRR is used.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>specular X-ray reflectrometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09200"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09214"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09205 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09205">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method from non-linear optics in which a structurally-ordered sample at an interface between two phases is irradiated by single frequency visible and variable frequency infrared pulses of light to generate a sum frequency vibrational spectrum of the interfacial species without any bulk phase contribution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SFGS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>vibrational sum frequency spectroscopy (VSFS)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Closely related to second harmonic generation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sum frequency generation spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09205"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SFGS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09206 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09206">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extended X-ray absorption fine structure spectroscopy in which, instead of measuring X-ray absorption, electron emission arising from that absorption is measured.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SEXAFS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Emitted electrons include Auger electrons and secondary electrons.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The detection of emitted electrons provides greater surface sensitivity than is generally found with extended X-ray absorption fine structure measurements.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>surface extended X-ray absorption fine structure spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09198"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09206"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SEXAFS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09207 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09207">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method giving enhanced infrared absorption in the measured infrared spectrum of a sample adsorbed on a roughened thin metal substrate. The metal substrate is deposited onto a prism and data is collected by total internal reflection.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SEIRAS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>SEIRAS enhancements can be \(\pu{E2}\) or greater.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>surface-enhanced infrared spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09207"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SEIRAS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09209 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09209">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy (SERS) by which the Raman spectrum is significantly enhanced under the conditions that, whilst undergoing SERS, the molecules are excited by radiation that is in resonance with electronic transitions in the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SERRS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Enhancements of up to and greater than \(\pu{E10}\) have been measured.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The acronym SERRS is used for both surface-enhanced resonant Raman scattering and spectroscopy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>surface-enhanced resonant Raman spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09208"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09209"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SERRS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09210 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09210">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of Raman spectroscopy combining surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy and scanning using an atomic force microscope or scanning tunnelling microscope to produce maps of a surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>TERS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>TERS is one of several near-field optical spectroscopic techniques.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>tip enhanced raman spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09208"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09210"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>TERS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09211 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09211">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method in which an X-ray spectrometer is used to measure the energy distribution of fluorescence X-rays emitted from a surface irradiated by primary X-rays under the condition of total reflection.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>TXRF</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>total reflection X-ray fluorescence spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09440"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09211"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>TXRF</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09212 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09212">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method to measure the absorption of X-rays at energies near and above (typically several hundred \(\pu{eV}\) greater) an absorption edge, over which fine structure (modulation of the X-ray absorption coefficient) can be detected.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>XAFS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Usefully sharp absorption edges are commonly observed in X-ray absorption spectra, although broader increases can be observed for some inner-shell excitations with short lifetimes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>XAFS includes both extended X-ray absorption fine structure spectroscopy and X-ray absorption near-edge spectroscopy. It involves transitions from a core-level to an unoccupied orbital or band and mainly reflects the local atomic structure and bonding (SEXAFS) and the density of the unoccupied electronic states (XANES).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>XAFS measurements usually start some \(\pu{10 eV}\) before the core-level binding energy (the absorption edge) of the emitting atoms, because in many cases pre-edge features are used to identify chemical bonds [example: \(\uppi^{\ast}\) resonances (excitation into lowest unoccupied molecular orbitals) in C K-edge spectra of polymer samples].</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>XAFS spectra are best recorded when a highly intense beam of X-rays from a synchrotron is used along with a high resolution double crystal or curved crystal spectrometer. Detectors include ionization chambers, scintillation counters, and solid state detectors.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>X-ray absorption fine structure spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09198"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09213"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09213 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09213">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>X-ray absorption fine structure spectroscopy in the energy region near (within approximately \(\pu{100 eV}\)) an absorption edge.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>XANES</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>near-edge extended X-ray absorption fine structure spectroscopy (NEXAFS)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Usefully sharp absorption edges are commonly observed in X-ray absorption spectra, although broader increases can be observed for some inner-shell excitations with short lifetimes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>XANES measurements usually start some \(\pu{10 eV}\) before the core-level binding energy (the absorption edge) of the emitting atoms, because in many cases pre-edge features are used to identify chemical bonds, e.g. \(\uppi^{\ast}\) resonances (excitation into lowest unoccupied molecular orbitals) in C K-edge spectra of polymer samples.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>XANES results from interference in multiple scattering of low-energy photoelectrons by surrounding atoms. It provides information on the local coordination environment, oxidation state, molecular orbitals, hybridization, and band structure (unoccupied electronic states).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>X-ray absorption near-edge spectroscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09212"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09213"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>XANES</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09214 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09214">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method for surface and interface roughness and density, layer thickness of thin layers, and electron density profile achieved by reflecting X-rays from a flat sample and analysing the reflected intensity as a function of angle.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>XRR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Monochromatic X-rays of low divergence are required. Many layers can be modelled, with thicknesses between about \(\pu{1 nm}\) and a few \(\pu{\upmu m}\), and (subject to conditions) fitted to the data. The surface needs to be flat over the beam footprint of typically \(\pu{10 mm}\) to \(\pu{40 mm}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>X-ray reflectometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09204"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09436"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09214"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>XRR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09215 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09215">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method based on the interference of X-rays that occurs near the Bragg condition at the surface of a crystalline solid, which leads to intensity maxima in yields of photoelectrons as the Bragg condition is scanned by tilting the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>XSW</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>For defect-rich substrates, such as metal single crystals, a normal-incidence or back-reflection geometry may be used. Instead of rocking the crystal in space, the energy of the incident beam is tuned through the Bragg condition.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Intensity nodes are scanned from one lattice plane to the next as the sample is tilted. Atomic positions in the crystalline lattice can be determined from comparisons of measured scans of photoelectron intensity versus tilt angle with calculated scans from dynamical scattering theory.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>X-ray standing waves</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09215"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>XSW</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09216 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09216">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coefficient for an element by which the measured intensity for that element is divided to yield the atomic concentration or atomic fraction of the element present in the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Sensitivity factors depend on parameters of the excitation source, the spectrometer, and the orientation of the sample to these parts of the instrument. Sensitivity factors also depend on the matrix being analysed. Sensitivity factors also depend on the matrix being analysed. In secondary ion mass spectrometry this has a dominating influence.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The choice of atomic concentration or atomic fraction should be made clear.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The source of sensitivity factors should be given to ensure that the correct matrix factors or other parameters are used.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The type of sensitivity factor utilized should be appropriate for the formulae used in the quantification process and for the type of sample analysed, e.g. for homogeneous samples or segregated layers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>absolute elemental sensitivity factor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09263"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09217 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09217">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Surface region of a material under particle bombardment where the chemical state or physical structure is modified by the effects of the bombardment.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For silicon bombarded by \(\pu{4 keV}\) \(\ce{O2+}\) at near-normal incidence, after sputtering for a sufficient time to reach a steady state, the surface is converted to stoichiometric \(\ce{SiO2}\) to a depth of around \(\pu{15 nm}\), with lower oxygen concentrations at greater depths. At \(\pu{2 keV}\), this is reduced to \(\pu{7 nm}\), these thicknesses being approximately twice the projected range.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The observed interface width (depth resolution) in secondary-ion mass spectrometry can be greater or smaller than the altered-layer thickness, depending on the analyte and bombarding-ion species.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>altered layer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09245"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09271"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09218 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09218">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Two-dimensional region of a sample surface measured in the plane of that surface, from which the entire analytical signal or a specified fraction of that signal is detected.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>analysis area</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09219 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09219">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Two-dimensional region of a sample surface at the analytical point, but set in the plane at right angles to the spectrometer axis, from which the entire analytical signal or a specified fraction of that signal is detected.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>analysis area</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09220 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09220">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sample preparation in which a sample is mechanically polished at an angle to the original surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>This angle can often be less than \(\pu{1\!^{\circ}}\), so that depth information with respect to the original surface is transformed to lateral information.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>angle lapping</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09272"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09221 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09221">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Angle between the trajectory of a particle or photon, as it leaves a surface, and the local or average surface normal.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>emission angle</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In XPS and other techniques, the take-off angle, which is referred to the sample surface, is quoted.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The particular surface normal needs to be specified.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>angle of emission</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09296"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09222 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09222">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Areic dose introduced into a solid in time interval \(t\) divided by \(t\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For a stationary parallel beam, the areic dose rate equals the flux times \(\cos\ \theta\), where \(\theta\) is the angle of incidence of the beam.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>areic dose rate</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09223"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09223 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09223">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number, \(N\), of energetic particles of a specified type introduced into a solid through a surface area \(A\) divided by \(A\). \(D = N/A\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>dose</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>For a discussion of areic dose in relation to ion-implanted reference materials.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>For a stationary parallel beam, the areic dose equals the fluence times \(cos\ \theta\), where \(\theta\) is the angle of incidence of the beam to the surface normal. Therefore, the use of ‘fluence’ for ‘dose’ is not recommended.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In some texts, the term dose density is used, but the term dose (more correctly areic dose) is more widespread. The term dose has been defined very differently in the fields of radiation and the medical sciences. Some have taken the total amount of particle radiation impacting the surface to be the dose, while others consider the amount divided by the area of the surface to be the dose. Here, dose is taken to be the latter. Dose density and dose, where they occur, are to be taken as the areic dose.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The energetic particles are atoms or atom clusters, which can be electrically charged or neutral, and the surface area \(A\) is the geometric surface area.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>areic dose</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09224 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09224">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Energy required to increase a surface area at thermodynamic equilibrium divided by that increase in area.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>surface energy</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit \(\pu{J m^{–2}}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>This term has no relation to surface energy approximation, used in energetic-ion analysis and Rutherford backscattering spectrometry.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>areic surface energy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09178"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09225 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09225">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Migration of sample atoms due to energy transfer with incident particles in the surface region.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>atomic mixing</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09232"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09238"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09226 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09226">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process in which an electron, tunnelling from the conduction band of a solid, neutralizes an incoming ion and an electron is ejected from a surface atom.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The ejected electron can be emitted into the vacuum.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Auger neutralization</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09227 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09227">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electric charge of specified polarity in a beam passing in time interval \(t\), divided by \(t\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For beams in which the instantaneous current varies periodically with time, the time interval \(t\) is an integral number of periods.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>average beam current</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09228 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09228">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Energy of a particle from the primary beam after it has undergone a backscattering collision and escaped from the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>backscattering energy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09229 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09229">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Procedure in which the sample is abraded by a sphere in order to expose compositional changes in layers below the original surface, with the intent that the depth of those layers can be related to the lateral position in the crater created by the abrasion.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>ball cratering</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09272"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09230 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09230">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Collision between a moving particle and a second particle in which total kinetic energy and total momentum are conserved.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>elastic scattering</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In elastic scattering interactions, the moving particle can be deflected through angles of up to \(\pu{180\!^{\circ}}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>binary elastic scattering</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09231 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09231">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Empirical rule formulated by W.H. Bragg and R. Kleeman that states that the stopping cross section of a compound sample is equal to the sum of the products of the elemental stopping cross sections for each constituent and its atomic fraction. \[S_{\ce{AB}}(\varepsilon) = xS_{\ce{A}}(\varepsilon) + yS_{\ce{B}}(\varepsilon)\] where \(S_{\ce{AB}}(\varepsilon)\) is the stopping cross section of the compound \(\ce{A}_{x}\ce{B}_{y}\), and \(S_{\ce{A}}(\varepsilon)\) and \(S_{\ce{B}}(\varepsilon)\) are the stopping cross sections of elements \(\ce{A}\) and \(\ce{B}\), respectively.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>Bragg’s rule</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09387"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09232 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09232">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Diffusion-like process in which atoms of material are moved randomly by energy deposited by incident particles slowing down in the sample surface region.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>cascade mixing</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09225"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09238"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09233 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09233">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Preferential motion of energetic particles along the crystal axes of a crystalline solid as the particles move through the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>channeling</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09234 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09234">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Maintenance at a fixed potential, usually near neutrality, of the surface of a non-conducting or poorly conducting sample material under bombardment by primary particles or photons.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Charge neutralization can be accomplished by bombarding the surface with electrons or, more rarely, ions or photons.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>charge neutralization</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09235 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09235">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electric potential of the surface region of an insulating sample, caused by irradiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Different charging potentials can occur on different areas or at different depths in a sample, arising from sample inhomogeneities or non-uniform intensity of the incident flux of radiation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The surface and bulk potentials can differ, for example as a result of band bending, interface dipoles, and charge centres.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>charging potential</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09251"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09236 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09236">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Surface map using signals proportional to the amount of an element in a particular chemical state in the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>chemical map</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09293"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09237 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09237">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Non-ideal focus of an electron or ion optical system for electrons or ions of different energies.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>chromatic aberration</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09238 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09238">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sequential energy transfer between atoms in a solid as a result of bombardment by an energetic species.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>collision cascade</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09225"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09232"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09239 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09239">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chemical composition measured as a function of distance normal to the surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CDP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>compositional depth profile</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09239"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CDP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09240 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09240">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Average depth of the region of a crater from which the measured signal is derived.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The crater depth can be modified by the formation of a reacted layer (e.g. an oxide) following any exposure to the atmosphere or other environments necessary when conducting the crater depth measurement.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The crater is generally formed by ion bombardment in sputter depth profiling and, in this case, can be different from the thickness of sample material removed by sputtering due to dilation of the altered layer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>crater depth</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09244"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09241 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09241">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sample preparation in which the sample is cleaved, cut, or polished in a plane perpendicular to the interface under study, so that associated compositional differences or gradients can be observed in that plane.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>cross-sectioning</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09242 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09242">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Particle fluence above which significant changes in the spectrum or in a stated peak, arising from damage processes, are observed.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>damage limit</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09429"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09243 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09243">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Layer of discrete composition, one atom thick, formed during the growth of material on a substrate.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>These films are often formed during epitaxial growth on single-crystal substrates.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>delta layer</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09244 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09244">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Monitoring of signal intensity as a function of a variable that can be related to distance normal to the surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Signal intensity is usually measured as a function of the sputtering time.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>depth profiling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09239"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09245 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09245">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Parameter that can be used as a coefficient in an analytic fit to a measured compositional depth profile or as a qualitative way of describing that profile.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Depth resolution parameters usually give no indication of distinguishability, but are useful in instrumental evaluation and profile deconvolution.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Parameter definitions should be used consistently.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Standard deviations can be used for any bell-shaped curve. If parameters are measured for a step change in composition, care must be taken that the depth range for the measurements is large enough to ensure that the signal becomes constant with depth on either side of the step.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>depth resolution parameter</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09443"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09246 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09246">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Cross section for binary elastic scattering.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>elastic scattering cross section</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09230"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09247 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09247">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Irradiation of a sample with low-energy electrons in order to change or stabilize the charging potential.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>electron flooding</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09235"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09248 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09248">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Surface map using signals proportional to the amount of an element present in a sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>elemental map</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09293"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09249 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09249">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Change in the position of the surface as a result of particle or photon irradiation divided by time of irradiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Erosion rate can be deduced from surface profilometer measurements of a crater after analysis. In this case, the effects of the altered layer and post-profile oxidation need to be considered.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The rate can be measured as a velocity.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Where the erosion is caused by sputtering, initially the erosion rate can be less than the sputtering rate as a result of the retention of sputtering particles.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>erosion rate</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09286"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09250 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09250">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Effect occurring in insulators or semi-conductors where internal electric fields cause the migration of sample atoms.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>field-induced migration</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09251 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09251">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>\(\mathit{\Phi} = {\rm{d}}N/{\rm{d}}t\), where \(N\) is number of particles of a specified type and \(t\) is time.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For a parallel beam, fluence rate and flux density are equivalent measures.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{s^{-1}}\). For a beam of charged particles (ions) the flux is expressed in ampere, i.e. \(F \times ze\), where \(z\) is the charge on a particle and \(e\) the elementary charge (\(\pu{1.602176634E-19 C}\)).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>flux of a beam of particles</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09252 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09252">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ion beam system used for machining small regions with sub-micron precision.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>FIB</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>FIB-machined surfaces can have an ion-damaged surface that needs to be removed.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In general, FIBs use a liquid metal ion source to generate a finely focused ion beam with diameters typically in the range \(\pu{7 nm}\) to \(\pu{300 nm}\) and of sufficient flux, typically \(\pu{4 pA}\) to \(\pu{20 pA}\), to machine small items for study by Auger electron spectroscopy, secondary-ion mass spectrometry, or transmission electron microscopy in an economic time. They are also used to manufacture scanning probe microscopy tips, those for atom force microscopy having radii down to \(\pu{2 nm}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>focused ion beam system</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09172"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09260"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09252"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>FIB</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09253 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09253">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of atoms and ions of a particular species sputtered from a sample divided by the total number of atoms and ions sputtered from the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>fractional sputtering yield</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09265"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09299"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09401"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09410"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09413"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09254 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09254">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Three-dimensional representation of the spatial distribution of a particular elemental or molecular species, as indicated by emitted secondary ions or electrons, as a function of depth or material removed by sputtering.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>image depth profile</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09422"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09255 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09255">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Kinetic energy of particles on impact with a sample surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For primary-ion beams in secondary-ion mass spectrometry (SIMS), the ion impact energy is given by the difference in electric potential between the ion source and the sample surface multiplied by the charge on the ion. In some SIMS systems, the beam energy is given for the source potential with respect to ground, but the sample potential need not be at ground. The impact energy takes account of any sample potential.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Use of the qualifier “impact” indicates that this is the energy of the particles striking the surface.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>impact energy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09256 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09256">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number, \(N_{\rm{imp}}\), of energetic particles of a specified type incident on a solid within a surface area \(A\) and stopped within the solid divided by \(A\). \(D_{\rm{imp}} = N_{\rm{imp}}/A\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Particles that are not stopped within the solid are either backscattered or transmitted.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>implanted areic dose</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09257 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09257">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Maximum depth, normal to the surface, from which useful information is obtained.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>information depth</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09258 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09258">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Maximum radius of a circular region, in the plane of the surface, from which useful information is obtained.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>information radius</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09259 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09259">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Depth resolution in a sample arising from parameters of the instrument.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In sputter depth profiling, these parameters involve the system alignment and can include the ion species, energy, and angle of incidence, as well as the option to rotate the sample while sputtering.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>This concept is used in the secondary-ion mass spectrometry, Auger electron spectroscopy, and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>instrumental depth resolution</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09172"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09244"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09245"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09260 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09260">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Directed flux of charged atoms or molecules.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>ion beam</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09251"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09261 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09261">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Injection of ions into a sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>ion implantation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09262 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09262">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Plot of output signal intensity from a spectrometer, signal intensity from another detector, or processed intensity information from the available software along a line corresponding to a line on the sample surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The line is most often an x- or y-linescan from a rectangular raster, but, in more sophisticated systems, might be in any arbitrary direction.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>line scan</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09274"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09263 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09263">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Factors, arising from the composition of the matrix, for multiplying the quotient of the measured intensity and the appropriate sensitivity factor in formulae to determine the composition using surface analytical techniques.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In methods such as Auger electron spectroscopy, the matrix factor is determined in part by the composition of the sub-surface material and in part by the composition of the analysis volume in the sample.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>matrix factor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09172"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09264 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09264">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Average depth normal to the surface from which specified particles or radiations escape.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>mean escape depth</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09265 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09265">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of atoms and ions of a particular species sputtered from a sample divided by the total number of incident particles.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>partial sputtering yield</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09253"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09299"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09401"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09410"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09413"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09266 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09266">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Energy value corresponding to the intensity maximum in a direct spectrum or to the intensity minimum (i.e. the negative excursion) for a differential spectrum.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For the differential spectrum in Auger electron spectroscopy (AES), the modulation, or differentiating amplitude should be given.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Peak energies for the differential spectrum in AES have greater kinetic energy than those for the direct spectrum.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The energy value can relate to the peak envelope for a group of overlapping peaks or to the positions of constituent peaks obtained by peak synthesis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>peak energy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09172"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09319"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09320"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09267 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09267">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Total ionization cross section for an incident photon of a given energy interacting with a material to produce one or more photoelectrons from all sub-shells that are energetically accessible.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>photoionization cross section</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09357"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09268 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09268">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Excitation of valence-band electrons in a solid in which collective oscillations are generated.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>bulk plasmon</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>volume plasmon</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Plasmon excitations are often observed as characteristic energy loss peaks associated with other peaks in the spectrum, such as those of any elastically scattered primary electrons, photoelectron peaks, Auger electron peaks, and ionization edges.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Plasmons are prominent in some materials and not others.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Two types of plasmon are commonly observed: bulk plasmons, associated with material remote from the surface, and surface plasmons, associated with material at the surface. When the term plasmon is used without a qualifier, the term refers to the bulk plasmon. Occasionally, interface plasmons can be observed that are associated with interfaces. Bulk plasmon energies depend on the electronic structure of the material and are roughly proportional to the square root of valence-band density. Surface plasmon energies are typically between \(\pu{50\%}\) and \(\pu{90\%}\) of bulk plasmon energies.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>plasmon</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09315"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09269 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09269">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Change in equilibrium surface composition of the sample, which can occur when sputtering multicomponent samples.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>preferential sputtering</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09270 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09270">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Directed flux of beam particles or photons incident on a sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>primary beam</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09251"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09271 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09271">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Distance from the surface at which an energetic ion or atom comes to rest in the sample, projected along the direction of the beam.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Calculations usually deal with the mean or average projected range for a large number of ions or atoms of the same species and the same energy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Used in energetic-ion analysis, Rutherford backscattering spectrometry, and secondary-ion mass spectrometry.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>projected range</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09178"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09273"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09272 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09272">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sample preparation in which a sample is polished by a cylinder in order to expose compositional changes below the original sample surface, with the intent that the depth of these layers can be related to the position on the surface created by the cylinder.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>radial sectioning</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09273 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09273">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Standard deviation of the projected ranges of energetic ions or atoms of a given energy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Used in energetic-ion analysis, Rutherford backscattering spectrometry, and secondary-ion mass spectrometry.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>range straggling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09178"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09271"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09274 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09274">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Two-dimensional pattern generated by the deflection of a primary beam.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Commonly used rasters cover square or rectangular areas.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>raster</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09275 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09275">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Deposition of sputtered sample material back onto the sample surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>redeposition</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09276 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09276">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In Auger electron spectroscopy (AES), secondary-ion mass spectrometry, and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy, the spectrometer response function divided by the response function of a reference instrument, or the average for several such instruments, as a function of energy (AES, XPS, etc.) or mass (SIMS).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RISR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The RISR can be used to relate spectra from one instrument to spectra from another when using similar excitation sources and geometries.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>relative instrument spectral response function</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09172"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09283"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09276"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>RISR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09277 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09277">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Resolution of a spectrometer at a given energy, mass, or wavelength divided by that energy, mass, or wavelength.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Designs of spectrometer generally maintain the resolution either to be constant throughout the spectrum or to be proportional to the energy, mass, or wavelength being scanned. For the former, the term resolution is useful, whereas, for the latter, the relative resolution or resolving power is more useful.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In practice, the relative resolution of a spectrometer can be deduced using a source with an emission line of known width, usually chosen to be as narrow as possible.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>It can be convenient to specify the relative energy resolution of an electron spectrometer, the relative mass resolution of a mass spectrometer, or the relative wavelength resolution of an optical spectrometer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The relative resolution is often expressed as a percentage.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The relative resolution of a spectrometer is the reciprocal of the resolving power of a spectrometer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>relative resolution</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09329"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09278 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09278">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sputtering rate of a sample divided by the sputtering rate of a reference sample sputtered under the same conditions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>relative sputtering rate</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09286"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09279 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09279">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electric potential applied to the whole or part of the sample, referenced to the electric potential of the sample holder.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>sample bias</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09281"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09280 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09280">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Change in the electrical potential in the sample or on the sample surface caused by particle or photon bombardment.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>sample charging</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09281 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09281">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electric potential difference of the sample referenced to ground.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For insulators, the sample voltage is assumed to be the same as that of the sample holder if an effective charge neutralizing device is used.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The sample voltage can be pulsed or constant, depending on the type of instrument.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sample voltage</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09279"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09282 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09282">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Solid angle allowing particles or photons to be transmitted from a point on the sample to the detector.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>solid angle of analyser</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09283 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09283">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of particles detected with a spectrometer divided by the number of such particles per solid angle and per interval of the dispersing parameter available for measurement as a function of the dispersing parameter.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>spectrometer response function</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09284"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09284 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09284">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of particles transmitted by the analyser divided by the number of such particles per solid angle and per interval of the dispersing parameter (e.g. energy, mass, or wavelength) available for measurement as a function of the dispersing parameter.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>analyser transmission function</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>spectrometer transmission function</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09283"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09285 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09285">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Compositional depth profile obtained when the surface composition is measured while material is removed by sputtering.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SDP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In some analytical methods, such as secondary ion mass spectrometry, the sputtering is often accomplished by the ion beam used for analysis, but in other methods an ion beam might need to be added.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sputter depth profile</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09260"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09285"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SDP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09286 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09286">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Amount, mass, or number of entities of sample material removed, as a result of particle bombardment, per unit time.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{mol s^{-1}}\), \(\pu{kg s^{-1}}\), \(\pu{s^{-1}}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sputtering rate</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09249"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09287 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09287">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of atoms and ions sputtered from a sample divided by the total number of incident primary particles.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>sputtering yield</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09288 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09288">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electric potential \(\pu{4.500 eV}\) above the Fermi level.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The Fermi level is an absolute level to which electron kinetic energies can be accurately referenced. Historically, in Auger electron spectroscopy, the electron energies have not been referenced to the Fermi level but, instead, have been referenced to the instrument vacuum level. This level varies from instrument to instrument and does not provide a consistent reference level. However, most reported Auger electron kinetic energies have been referenced to the vacuum level, and most analysts are familiar with the variations that occur from one instrument to another for energies referenced in this way. By convention, the standard vacuum level is defined, as above, to be a consistent reference level close to the value for typical instrument vacuum levels. Energies referenced to the standard vacuum level are consistent and are within approximately \(\pu{1 eV}\) of those referenced to individual instrument vacuum levels.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>standard vacuum level</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09172"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09300"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09289 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09289">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>State of the sputtering process in which important operational and analytical parameters are unchanging over a meaningful timescale.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Generally, steady state and stoichiometric sputtering are equivalent but, in profiling dilute delta layers in semiconductors, for example, the sputtering can be at a steady state while the constituents being studied are not being sputtered in their stoichiometric ratio.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>steady-state sputtering</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09243"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09291"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09290 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09290">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Steady-state surface composition produced by sputtering a homogeneous sample under non-varying conditions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>equilibrium surface composition sputtering</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>steady-state surface composition sputtering</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09291 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09291">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>State of the sputtering process in which the relative amounts of the elemental components sputtered from a sample are equal to their stoichiometry within the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For most homogeneous materials, stoichiometric sputtering is attained after the sputter removal of a few nanometres from the surface.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>stoichiometric sputtering</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09289"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09292 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09292">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>For an interface, the difference between the amount of component actually present in the system and that which would be present (in a reference system) if the bulk concentration in the adjoining phases were maintained up to a chosen geometrical dividing surface (Gibbs dividing surface).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For a solid/liquid interface in which no component of the liquid phase penetrates into the solid, the surface excess (or adsorption) of component \(i\) is defined as; \(n_{i}^{\upsigma} = n_{i} - V^{\rm{l}} {c}_{i}^{\rm{l}}\), where \(n_{i}\) is the total amount of \(i\) in the system, \(V^{\rm{l}}\) is the volume of an arbitrarily chosen amount of bulk liquid (in the framework of the so-called algebraic method), and \({c}_{i}^{\rm{l}}\) is its bulk concentration in the liquid.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>surface excess amount</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09293 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09293">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Two- or three-dimensional representation of the sample surface where the information at each point in the representation, given by a brightness or colour or as a length in a third dimension, is related to the output signal from a detector or processed intensity information from the available software.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>surface map</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09236"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09248"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09294 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09294">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Collective motion of electrons on an interface of two phases which have different permittivity, of which one must have electrical conductivity to sustain the collective motion.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Collective motion of electrons is induced by an injection of energetic particles, for example photons, electrons, ions, or alpha particles.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Metals such as silver and gold are well known examples of materials exhibiting surface plasmon behaviour.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>surface plasmon</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09295 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09295">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Partitioning of a species from the bulk of a material to the surface as a result of kinetic or thermodynamic effects.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>surface segregation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09296 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09296">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Angle between the trajectory of a particle as it leaves a surface and the local or average surface plane.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In the past, ‘take-off angle’ has sometimes been used erroneously to mean angle of emission.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The particular surface plane needs to be specified.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The take-off angle is the complement of the angle of emission.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>take-off angle</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09221"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09297 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09297">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Parameter describing the apparent work function of a solid that controls the electronic current density, \(J\), emitted from a conductor at temperature \(T\). \(W = -kT\ln(J/AT^{2})\), where \(k\) is the Boltzmann constant and \(A\) is the Richardson constant.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For polycrystalline solids, the thermionic work function will be close to the minimum work function of the crystallites present.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Richardson constant, \(A = (4\uppi m_{\rm{e}} k^{2} e)/h^{3} \approx \pu{1.202E6 A m-2 K-2}\) where \(m_{\rm{e}}\) is the mass of the electron, \(e\) the elementary charge, and \(h\) is the Planck constant.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>thermionic work function</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09345"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09298 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09298">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Contrast in a surface map or image arising from the topography of the sample surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Topographic contrast can change after ion sputtering.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Topographic effects can modify the interaction between the primary beam and the sample, making the interpretation of electron or ion yield data more complex than otherwise.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>topographic contrast</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09293"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09299 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09299">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of ions of both signs sputtered from a sample divided by the number of incident particles.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>total ion yield</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09253"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09265"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09401"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09410"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09411"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09413"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09300 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09300">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Energy level of an electron positioned at rest just outside a solid, i.e. with zero kinetic energy with respect to the sample surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>local vacuum level</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The vacuum level depends on the nature of the surface; for example, it is different between surface crystal planes \(\ce{Si}[001]\) and \(\ce{Si}[111]\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>‘Just outside’ has also been written as “a few nanometers”, and is taken as a distance where there is no effect of image forces but that is sufficient for the electron to experience the full impact of the surface dipole.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>vacuum level</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09301 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09301">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electric potential of the vacuum at a point in space.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In electron spectroscopy, the point in space is taken at a sufficiently large distance outside the sample, such that electric fields caused by different work functions of different parts of the surface are zero or extremely small.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>This level is not experimentally accessible.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>vacuum level at infinity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09300"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09302 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09302">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Volume sputtered from a sample divided by number of incident particles.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Volume yield is useful for expressing the amount sputtered in organic layers where molecules can be easily fragmented and values of the molecular sputtering yield can be much more variable between samples than the volume yield.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>volume yield</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09253"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09265"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09401"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09410"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09413"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09303 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09303">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Energy width of the principal characteristic X-ray line.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy the X-ray linewidth usually refers to that of the X-ray source.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>X-ray linewidth contributes to the photoelectron peak widths.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>X-ray linewidth</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09304 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09304">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Device used to eliminate photons of energies other than those in a narrow energy or wavelength band.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy using \(\ce{Al}\) X-rays, the monochromator is usually aligned close to the \(\ce{Al}\) \(\ce{K}\upalpha_{1}\) energy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>X-ray monochromator</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09305 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09305">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy, measurement of the charging potential of a particular sample from a comparison of the experimentally measured \(\ce{C 1s}\) binding energy, arising from adsorbed hydrocarbons on the sample, with a standard binding energy value.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A nominal value of \(\pu{285.0 eV}\) is often used for the binding energy of the relevant \(\ce{C 1s}\) peak, although some analysts prefer specific values in the range \(\pu{284.6 eV}\) to \(\pu{285.2 eV}\), depending on the nature of the substrate. This method does not determine the true charging potential, since the true binding energy of the adsorbed hydrocarbons is not known.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Different sample charging potentials can occur on different areas on the surface, or at different depths, arising, for example, from sample inhomogeneities or non-uniform intensity of the incident-radiation flux.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>adventitious carbon referencing</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09235"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09332"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09306 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09306">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy factor which characterizes the intensity distribution, \(L(\gamma)\), of photoelectrons ejected by X-rays from isolated atoms at an angle \(\gamma\) from the incident X-ray direction. In the dipole approximation, \(L(\gamma) = 1 + ½\beta [3(\sin^{2}\gamma)/2-1]\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>This formula relates to gases and is modified by the effects of elastic scattering when applied to solids. At the magic angle, \(L(\gamma) = 1\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>asymmetry parameter</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09307 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09307">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Plot of intensity of Auger electrons as a function of the electron kinetic energy, usually as part of the energy distribution of detected electrons.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The Auger electron spectrum can be presented in either direct spectrum or differential spectrum formats.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When excited by incident electrons, the energy distribution of detected electrons, often measured between \(\pu{0 eV}\) and \(\pu{2500 eV}\), contains Auger electrons, backscattered (primary) electrons, and secondary electrons. The entire distribution is sometimes referred to as an Auger electron spectrum.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Auger electron spectrum</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09311"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09319"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09320"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09308 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09308">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Kinetic energy of a narrow Auger electron peak in a spectrum minus the kinetic energy of the most intense X-ray photoelectron peak from the same element.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The Auger parameter is sometimes called the final state Auger parameter (see initial state Auger parameter).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The Auger parameter is useful for evaluating the relaxation energy of the ionized matrix atom associated with the generation of a core hole for those Auger transitions between core levels, which have similar chemical shifts.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The Auger parameter is useful for separating chemical states for samples in which charging causes uncertainty in the binding energy measurement or in which the binding energy shift is inadequate to identify the chemical state.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The value of the Auger parameter depends on the energy of the X-rays, which therefore needs to be specified.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Auger parameter</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09339"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09343"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09350"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09309 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09309">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Emission of an Auger electron in which additional spectator holes are present in the initial state or the final state) for the transition.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>Auger vacancy satellite</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09333"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09337"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09356"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09310 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09310">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coefficient, proportional to the intensity, calculated for an element in an average matrix, by which the measured intensity for that element is divided in calculations to yield the atomic concentration or atomic fraction of the element present in the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>AMRSF</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The choice of atomic concentration or atomic fraction should be made clear.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>average matrix relative sensitivity factor</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09310"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>AMRSF</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09311 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09311">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electron, originating in the incident beam, which is emitted after interaction with the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>By convention, an electron with energy greater than \(\pu{50 eV}\) can be considered a backscattered electron.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>By convention, the incident beam is often called the primary beam and the backscattered electrons are often referred to as the backscattered primary electrons.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>backscattered electron</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09270"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09312 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09312">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In Auger electron spectroscopy, factor equal to the total Auger-electron current arising from ionizations in the sample caused by both primary electrons and backscattered electrons divided by the Auger-electron current arising directly from the primary electrons.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>‘Backscattering correction factor’ is preferred to the contraction ‘backscattering factor’.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>backscattering correction factor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09172"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09311"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09313 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09313">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In Auger electron spectroscopy, Auger-electron current arising from ionizations in the sample caused by backscattered electrons divided by Auger-electron current arising directly from the primary electrons.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>backscattering fraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09172"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09311"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09314 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09314">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of electrons emitted from the sample with energies greater than \(\pu{50 eV}\) divided by the number of electrons incident at a given energy and angle of incidence.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>backscattering coefficient</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>backscattering yield</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09312"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09359"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09423"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09315 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09315">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Inelastic scattering of electrons in solids that produces a non-uniform energy loss spectrum determined by the characteristics of the material.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>characteristic electron energy losses</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09316 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09316">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement of charging potential of a sample in order to correct the measured energies so that those energies correspond to a sample with no surface charge.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Charge referencing is often conducted using adventitious carbon referencing, using internal carbon referencing or by gold decoration, or by referencing to the substrate when grounded.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Different charging potentials can occur on different areas or at different depths in a sample, arising, for example, from sample inhomogeneities or the non-uniform intensity of the incident flux of radiation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>charge referencing</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09235"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09251"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09305"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09317 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09317">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>State of an atom arising from its chemical interaction with neighbouring atoms in a molecule, compound, solid, liquid, or gas that leads to a characteristic energy or feature observable in electron spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>chemical state</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09318 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09318">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy, plot of the measured kinetic energy of a sharp Auger electron peak versus the binding energy of a photoelectron peak for the same element.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Wagner plot</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Plots are usually made for a given element that can exist in different chemical states. Such plots are helpful in defining the state for an unknown sample, where measurements of the binding energy alone are inadequate.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The binding energy is usually plotted on the abscissa, with values decreasing towards the right.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>chemical state plot</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09319 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09319">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Differential of a direct spectrum with respect to energy by an analogue electrode modulation method or by the numerical differentiation of that spectrum.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The modulation amplitude (for example in unit \(\pu{eV}\)) or the number of points and the type of differentiating function should be given.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Used in Auger electron spectroscopy and, rarely, in X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>differential spectrum</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09172"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09320"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09320 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09320">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Spectrum of intensity of electrons transmitted and detected by a spectrometer with a dispersing energy analyser as a function of energy, \(E\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>By convention, direct spectra in X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy are often presented in constant analyser energy mode, in which the spectrum approximates the true spectrum, whereas, in Auger electron spectroscopy, spectra are often presented in constant retardation ratio mode, in which the spectrum approximates to \(E\) times the true spectrum.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In retarding field energy analysers, which do not have a dispersing element, the direct spectrum can be obtained from the first differential of the collected current with respect to the retarding energy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>direct spectrum</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09172"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09321 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09321">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electron or ion optical method of steering a spectrometer axis to align with the impact area of the primary beam at all points of a raster scan on the sample surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>dynamic emittance matching</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09274"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09322 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09322">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Parameter that, when introduced in place of the electron inelastic mean free path into an expression derived for Auger electron spectroscopy and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy on the assumption that elastic scattering effects are negligible for a given quantitative application, will correct that expression for elastic scattering effects.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Since there are different uses of this term, it is recommended that users specify clearly the particular application and the definition of the parameter for that application (e.g. by giving a formula or by providing a reference to a particular source).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The effective attenuation length can have different values for different quantitative applications of AES and XPS. However, the most common use of effective attenuation length is in the determination of overlayer-film thicknesses from the measurement of the changes of overlayer and substrate Auger-electron or photoelectron signal intensities after the deposition of a film or as a function of the emission angle. For emission angles of up to about \(\pu{60\!^{\circ}}\) (with respect to the surface normal), it is often satisfactory to use a single value of this parameter. For greater emission angles, the effective attenuation length can depend on this angle.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>effective attenuation length</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09172"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09221"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09246"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09327"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09323 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09323">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Equation relating the kinetic energy of an electron, emitted from a conductor illuminated by monochromatic photons, and the photon energy and work function of the surface from which the photoelectron is emitted. \(½m_{\rm{e}}  u_{\rm{max}}^{2} = h u - \phi_{0}\), where \(m_{\rm{e}}\) is the mass of an electron, \( u_{\rm{max}}\) the maximum velocity of the emitted electron, \(h\) the Planck constant, \( u\) the frequency of the illuminating photons, and \(\phi_{0}\) the photoelectric work function of the surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Einstein equation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>Einstein photoelectric equation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09345"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09324 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09324">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Peak in the electron spectrum, produced by quasi-elastically scattered electrons detected by an electron spectrometer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>All electrons that are scattered by atoms can be elastically scattered in the centre-of-mass frame, but energy losses that are typically less than \(\pu{1 eV}\) might be observed in the laboratory frame. These losses are generally significantly less than the measured energy width of the electrons in a primary-electron beam. Historically, and more generally, the scattering has been called “elastic”; however, the term quasi-elastic is now often used if the small change in energy that occurs on scattering is important.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The energy and the energy broadening of the quasi-elastic peak are influenced by the recoil of the scatterer atoms, the energy distribution of the primary (incident) electrons, the scattering geometry, the acceptance geometry, and the response function of the electron spectrometer. The intensity of the elastic peak depends on the electron differential elastic scattering cross section and on the cross section for inelastic electron scattering at the particular beam energy of the primary-electrons and in the given scattering geometry, including the probability of surface excitations.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>elastic peak</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09174"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09246"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09329"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09334"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09325 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09325">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measuring instrument for measuring the number of electrons, or an intensity proportional to that number, as a function of the electron kinetic energy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>An electron energy analyser is part of an electron spectrometer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electron energy analyser</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09329"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09326 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09326">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Energy spectrum of electrons from a nominally monoenergetic source emitted after inelastic interactions with the sample, often exhibiting peaks due to specific inelastic loss processes (see characteristic electron energy losses and plasmon).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The electron energy loss spectrum obtained using an incident-electron beam of about the same energy as a peak obtained in Auger electron spectroscopy or X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy approximates to the energy loss spectrum associated with that peak.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The electron energy loss spectrum, measured with an incident-electron beam, is a function of beam energy, angle of incidence of the beam, angle of emission, and the electronic properties of the sample.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electron energy loss spectrum</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09172"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09221"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09268"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09315"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09327 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09327">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Average distance that an electron with a given energy travels between successive inelastic collisions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>electron inelastic mean free path</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09328 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09328">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In Auger electron spectroscopy and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy, measurement method for kinetic energy distribution by retarding the emitted electrons before or within the electron energy analyser.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>electron retardation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09172"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09325"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09329 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09329">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measuring system, the essential part of which is an electron energy analyser.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The term electron spectrometer can be used either as a synonym for electron energy analyser or to describe a more complex instrument based on an electron energy analyser and additional electron-optical components. Occasionally, the term is used to describe a complete working system with an energy analyser, possible electron-optical components, an electron detector, excitation sources, vacuum pumps, control electronics, and a data-processing system. The meaning will normally be made clear by the context.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electron spectrometer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09325"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09330 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09330">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In dynamic secondary-ion mass spectrometry, coefficient for an element by which the measured intensity of a mass peak for that element, divided by the measured intensity of a mass peak for the matrix, is multiplied to yield the atomic concentration of the element present in the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Matrix terms are strong, and the matrix, bombarding species, incident-ion energy, and angle of incidence, as well as the spectrometer operating conditions, all affect relative elemental sensitivity factors significantly.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The elemental relative sensitivity factor can be obtained by dividing the relative isotopic sensitivity factor by the isotope abundance of the detected isotope ion.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>elemental relative sensitivity factor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09183"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09419"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09331 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09331">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Plasmon excited as an electron travels through a condensed medium.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The probability describing the number of extrinsic plasmons excited by the electron depends on the path length traversed and, for typical applications of Auger-electron spectroscopy and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy of a homogeneous sample, follows a Poisson distribution for the parameter \(s/\lambda\), where \(s\) is path length and \(\lambda\) is electron inelastic mean free path.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>extrinsic plasmon</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09268"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09327"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09342"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09332 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09332">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process for establishing the binding energy scale for a particular sample by assigning the kinetic energy corresponding to the Fermi level, as determined by analysis of the spectrum obtained from X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy or ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy, as the point of zero binding energy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>Fermi level referencing</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09361"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09333 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09333">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Energy state of an atom after a particular Auger, X-ray, or photoemission process.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>final energy state</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09337"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09338"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09334 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09334">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Intensity distribution in a spectrum for particles originally at one energy but which are emitted at lower energies due to one or more inelastic scattering processes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>inelastic background</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>For Auger electron spectroscopy and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy, the inelastic background associated with a particular Auger electron or photoelectron peak has been approximated by a measured electron energy loss spectrum for which the incident-electron energy is close to the energy of the peak. Simple linear backgrounds have also been used, but these are much less accurate, except for the XPS analysis of insulators.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>inelastic electron scattering background</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09172"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09326"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09335"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09355"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09360"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09335 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09335">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Subtraction of a chosen inelastic electron scattering background from a measured spectrum in Auger electron spectroscopy and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The inelastic background associated with a particular Auger electron or photoelectron peak has been approximated by a measured electron energy loss spectrum for which the incident-electron energy is close to the energy of the peak.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>inelastic electron scattering background subtraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09172"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09326"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09334"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09336 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09336">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Cross section for inelastic scattering by an electron traversing a material.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>inelastic scattering cross section</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09246"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09337 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09337">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Core-hole excited state of an atom prior to Auger transition or to X-ray emission.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>initial energy state</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09340"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09338 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09338">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ground state of an atom prior to photoelectron emission.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>initial energy state</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09339 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09339">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>\(\beta = 3E_{\rm{B}} + E_{\rm{K}}\), where \(E_{\rm{B}}\) and \(E_{\rm{K}}\) are, respectively, the binding energy of a photoelectron peak and the Fermi level referenced (see Fermi level referencing) kinetic energy of an Auger electron peak, each involving the same initial core level of the same element.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The initial-state Auger parameter is useful for evaluating the change in the atomic core potential contribution to changes in binding energy between two environments, provided the Auger transition is between core levels that have similar binding energy shifts.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>This parameter has no relation to the asymmetry parameter, which is also given the symbol \(\beta\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>initial-state Auger parameter</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09306"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09308"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09332"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09343"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09340 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09340">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Auger transition in which at least one of the final electron vacancies is localized in valence levels or molecular orbitals of atoms adjacent to the atom in which the initial vacancy occurred.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>interatomic Auger process</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09341 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09341">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Energy shift observed in core-level photoelectron spectroscopy arising from the changed bonding and/or from the displacements of atoms from their bulk positions near an interface between two materials.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>interface core-level shift</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09358"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09342 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09342">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Plasmon excited at the same time as the excitation of a core-level photoelectron or an Auger electron associated with the same locality as the excitation of the photoelectron or Auger electron.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>intrinsic plasmon</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09268"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09331"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09343 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09343">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sum of the Fermi level referenced (see Fermi level referencing) kinetic energy of a narrow Auger electron peak in the spectrum and the binding energy of the most intense photoelectron peak from the same element.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The modified Auger parameter is the sum of the Auger parameter and the energy of the X-rays responsible for the measured photoelectron peak. Unlike the Auger parameter, it does not depend on the energy of the X-rays.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>modified Auger parameter</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09308"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09332"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09339"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09344 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09344">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mean kinetic energy of detected particles in the energy-dispersive portion of an electron energy analyser.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>pass energy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09325"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09345 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09345">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Minimum photon energy required to liberate an electron from the surface of a conducting solid by absorption of that photon.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>photoelectric work function</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09297"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09346 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09346">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Photoelectron peaks in a spectrum resulting from photoemission induced by characteristic minor X-ray lines associated with the X-ray spectrum of the anode material.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>satellite peaks</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>photoelectron X-ray satellite peaks</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09348"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09347 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09347">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Removal of photoelectron X-ray satellite peaks from a spectrum.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For unmonochromated \(\ce{Al}\) and \(\ce{Mg}\) X-rays, the satellites usually removed are \(\ce{K}\upalpha_{3,4}\) and \(\ce{K}\upalpha_{5,6}\). More sophisticated subtraction methods also remove the \(\ce{K}\upalpha_{2}\), \(\ce{K}\upalpha^{\prime}\), and \(\ce{K}\upbeta\) satellites.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>photoelectron X-ray satellite subtraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09346"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09348 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09348">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Emission of electrons from atoms or molecules caused by the photoelectric effect.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>photoemission</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09349 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09349">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In electron spectroscopy, coefficient, proportional to the intensity measured for a pure sample of an element, by which the measured intensity for that element is divided in calculations to yield the atomic concentration or atomic fraction of the element present in the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PERSF</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Sensitivity factors depend on parameters of the excitation source, the spectrometer, and the orientation of the sample to these parts of the instrument. The numerical values of the sensitivity factors can also depend on the method used to measure the peak intensities.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The choice of atomic concentration or atomic fraction should be made clear.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The source of sensitivity factors should be given to ensure that the correct matrix factors or other parameters are used. Matrix factors are significant and should be used with pure-element relative sensitivity factors.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The type of sensitivity factor utilized should be appropriate for the formulae used in the quantification process and for the type of sample analysed, for example homogeneous samples or segregated layers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>pure-element relative sensitivity factor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09263"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09310"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09349"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PERSF</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09350 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09350">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Energy associated with intra-atomic or extra-atomic electronic readjustment to the removal of an atomic electron, so as to minimize the energy of the final state of the system.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>relaxation energy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09333"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09352 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09352">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Aperture in the electron or ion optical system restricting the detected signal to a small area of the sample surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>selected-area aperture</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09353 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09353">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Multi-electron process in which two or more electrons are emitted, partitioning between them the excess kinetic energy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Shakeoff leads to a continuum background intensity at kinetic energies below that of the parent peak in the electron spectrum. This is in contrast to shakeup, which leads to peak structure, also at kinetic energies below that of a parent peak.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>shakeoff</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09354"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09354 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09354">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Multi-electron process in which an atom is left in an excited state following a photoionization or Auger electron process, so that the outgoing electron has a characteristic kinetic energy slightly less than that of the parent photoelectron.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Shakeup peaks are usually observed within \(\pu{10 eV}\) of the parent peak. However, for gases where the background is low, shakeup peaks have been identified at kinetic energies up to \(\pu{100 eV}\) less than that of the parent peak.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>shakeup</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09353"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09355 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09355">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In Auger electron spectroscopy and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy, background calculated to fit the measured spectrum at points at greater and lesser kinetic energy than the peak or peaks of interest, such that the background contribution at a given kinetic energy is in a fixed proportion to the total peak area above that background for greater kinetic energies.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Fitting to the background can be made by averaging over a given number of energy channels.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Shirley background</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09172"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09360"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09356 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09356">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electronic configuration of an atom containing an electronic vacancy that can be present during processes, such as Auger electron and X-ray photoelectron emission, but is not created or destroyed in the process.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>spectator hole</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09348"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09357 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09357">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Cross section for an incident photon interacting with a material to produce one or more photoelectrons from a given sub-shell.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Photoionization from one sub-shell can lead to shake-up or shakeoff of electrons from other shells.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sub-shell photoionization cross section</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09267"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09353"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09354"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09358 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09358">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Energy shift observed in core-level photoelectron spectroscopy arising from the reduced coordination of the surface or near-surface atoms compared to bulk atoms.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Surface core level shifts are often observed for single crystals and nanoparticles and depend on the crystal surface exposed. Shifts can be to lower or higher binding energy, depending on the band structure of the bulk solid. For the outermost atom layer, the shift can be as high as \(\pu{0.4 eV}\). In X-ray photoelectron emission, the sampling depth is generally many atom layers when photoelectrons are detected with emission angles at, or close to, the surface normal. The shift for the outermost atom layer is thus seen most clearly at near-grazing emission angles. Separate energy shifts can be detected for the second and third atom layers from the surface.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>surface core-level shift</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09348"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09359 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09359">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Total number of electrons emitted from a sample divided by the total number of electrons incident at a given energy and angle of incidence. \(\sigma = \delta + \eta\), where \(\delta\) is the secondary-electron yield and \(\eta\) is the backscattering coefficient.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The total secondary-electron yield is often simply called the secondary-electron yield. This leads to confusion with the term of that name, which is restricted to secondary electrons with energies less than or equal to \(\pu{50 eV}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>total secondary-electron yield</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09314"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09423"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09360 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09360">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In Auger electron spectroscopy and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy, intensity distribution of the background due to inelastic scattering obtained from the differential inelastic scattering cross section with respect to energy loss and the three-dimensional distribution of the emitting atoms in the surface region.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A number of classes of atomic distributions can be used together with different differential inelastic scattering cross sections. The atomic distribution and the inelastic scattering cross section should be specified.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The Tougaard background is usually calculated to match the measured spectrum over a wide energy range that excludes the peak region and the spectral region extending to approximately \(\pu{50 eV}\) less kinetic energy than the peaks of interest. The measured spectrum should be corrected for the spectrometer response function of the measuring instrument before the calculation of the Tougaard background.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Tougaard background</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09172"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09283"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09336"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09355"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09361 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09361">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Method of establishing the kinetic energy scale in which the zero point corresponds to an electron at rest at the vacuum level.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>vacuum level referencing</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09300"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09332"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09362 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09362">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>X-ray photoelectron energy distribution arising from the excitation of electrons from the valence band of the sample material.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>valence-band spectrum</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09363 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09363">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Backscattering spectrum recorded with the analysing beam aligned with crystallographic axes or planes of the sample that produce channelling.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>aligned incidence spectrum</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09364"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09364 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09364">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Plot of backscattering yield versus backscattering energy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>backscattering spectrum</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09365 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09365">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrostatic or electromagnetic device used to generate pulses of ions from a continuous ion beam.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The beam chopper can be used to define the pulse length and hence the mass resolution in a time-of-flight mass spectrometer and it can also be used to select particular ions in a beam that contains more than one species.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>beam chopper</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09260"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09393"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09366 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09366">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In ion-scattering spectrometry, increase in the spectrometer detection system response above the background signal, which can be attributed to the binary elastic scattering of an incident ion by a surface atom of a particular mass.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>binary elastic scattering peak</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09230"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09367 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09367">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Experimental arrangement wherein the atom rows or planes of a single-crystal target are aligned parallel to a vector from the sample to the detector.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>blocking geometry</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09368 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09368">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Stopping cross section arising from energy transfer to the electrons of the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The maximum of the nuclear stopping cross section occurs at energies of the order of \(\pu{1 keV}\) per nucleon, whereas that of the electronic stopping cross section occurs at above \(\pu{100 keV}\) per nucleon. The absolute value of the electronic stopping cross section maximum is significantly greater than that for the nuclear stopping cross section.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electronic stopping cross section</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09387"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09369 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09369">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Values of the backscattering energy for an element, or for an isotope, that is located at the surface of a sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>energy edge</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09228"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09370 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09370">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Simplification of calculations involving the energy of an ion passing through a solid sample, where the energy of the ion at the surface is used in place of a properly averaged energy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>This approximation is used to determine the energy at which scattering cross sections (see elastic scattering cross section, inelastic scattering cross section) or stopping cross sections, or both, are evaluated.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>surface approximation energy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09246"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09336"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09387"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09371 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09371">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Cross section of an atom for elastic scattering that is greater than the Rutherford cross section due to partial penetration of a nucleus in the sample by the incident particle.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>enhanced elastic cross section</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09381"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09372 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09372">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measured response of the energy filtering and detection system as a consequence of bombarding the sample material with an ion beam, usually presented as the ordinate of an ion-scattering spectrum.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>experimental scattered-ion intensity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09260"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09373"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09373 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09373">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Plot of the intensities of ions, scattered from a sample, as a function of the scattered-ion energy divided by the incident-ion energy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>ion-scattering spectrum</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09382"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09390"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09374 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09374">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Particle energy after an elastic collision divided by the energy before the collision, in the laboratory frame of reference.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The symbol \(K\) is often used for the kinematic factor and might have a subscript added in ion-scattering spectrometry or Rutherford backscattering spectrometry measurements, denoting the target atom as either, say, \(K_{\ce{Si}}\) or \(K_{28}\). The subscript for atomic mass is preferred, since the isotope is correctly identified.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>kinematic factor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09178"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09375 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09375">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Cross section at a given beam energy and emission direction of the detected product for a particular nuclear reaction per atom.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit \(\pu{m2}\). Commonly used unit barn, \(\pu{1 barn} = \pu{E-28 m2}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nuclear reaction cross section</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09376 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09376">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Stopping cross section arising from energy transfer to atomic nuclei of the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The maximum of the nuclear stopping cross section occurs at energies of the order of \(\pu{1 keV}\) per nucleon, whereas that of the electronic stopping cross section occurs at above \(\pu{100 keV}\) per nucleon. The absolute value of the electronic stopping cross section maximum is significantly greater than that of the nuclear stopping cross section.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nuclear stopping cross section</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09387"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09377 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09377">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Counts in a backscattering spectrum arising from two or more separate events that occur so closely in time that the signals are not resolved by the detection system and cause counts to be recorded in erroneous channels.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>pileup</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09364"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09378 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09378">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ion intentionally produced by an ion source and directed onto the sample surface at a known angle of incidence and a known energy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>probe ion</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09379 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09379">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Backscattering spectrum recorded with the analysing beam incident on the sample in a direction such as to produce no channelling.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>random incidence spectrum</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09364"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09380 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09380">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nuclear reaction that has a narrow peak in the nuclear reaction cross section as a function of energy, the nuclear reaction cross section at the peak being so much larger than the nuclear reaction cross sections at adjacent energies on either side of the peak that essentially all the particles detected from the reaction are due to the peak.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>resonance reaction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09375"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09381 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09381">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Elastic scattering cross section calculated using classical mechanics and a Coulomb potential.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The resulting cross section formula was first derived by Rutherford.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Rutherford cross section</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09178"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09246"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09371"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09382 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09382">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Kinetic energy of a primary ion after a collision.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Following binary elastic scattering, the kinetic energy of the primary or probe ions, \(E_{\rm{s}}\), is given by \(E_{\rm{s}} = E_{0}[M_{0}/(M_{0} + M_{1})]^{2} \{\cos\theta + [(M_{0}/M_{1})^{2} - \sin^{2}\theta]^{½}\}^{2}\) where \(E_{0}\) is the kinetic energy of the incident probe ion prior to scattering; \(M_{0}\) is the mass of the probe ion; \(M_{1}\) is the mass of the target atom; \(\theta\) is the angle between the initial and final velocity vectors for the probe ion, as determined from a common origin in the laboratory coordinate system, expressed as a value between \(\pu{0\!^{\circ}}\) and \(\pu{180\!^{\circ}}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>scattered-ion energy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09378"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09414"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09383 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09383">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Scattered-ion energy divided by energy of the incident probe ion prior to a collision.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>scattered-ion energy ratio</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09378"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09382"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09384 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09384">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Response arising from electrons in an atom causing an apparent reduction in the coulomb potential of the nucleus.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In ion-beam analysis, when the incident ion is far from the target nucleus, the atom in which the nucleus sits looks neutral to the ion as a result of screening. The screening reduces the scattering cross section slightly from the Rutherford cross section (about \(\pu{1\%}\) per 50 increase in the target atomic number for \(\pu{2 MeV}\) \(\ce{He}\)). The effect becomes more pronounced and increasingly uncertain as the energy decreases and, for low-energy ion scattering spectrometry, the cross section is no longer really well-known.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>screening</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09176"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09381"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09385 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09385">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Factor by which the Rutherford cross section is reduced as a result of screening in a given experiment.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>screening function</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09381"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09384"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09386 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09386">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Energy loss of a particle scattered at a given depth in the sample, and detected at a given angle, by the product of the atomic density of the sample atoms and the depth of scattering.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>stopping cross section factor</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09387 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09387">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Rate of energy loss of a particle with distance along its trajectory in a sample divided by the atomic density of sample atoms for a sample of infinitesimal thickness.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>SI unit: \(\pu{J m2}\) or \(\pu{J m2 kg^{-1}}\). Common unit \(\pu{eV m2}\) or \(\pu{eV m2 kg^{-1}}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The atomic density is usually taken as the number density, \(N\), but sometimes as the mass density, \(\rho\). The stopping cross section is thus given either by \(S(E)\equiv (1/N){\rm{d}}E/{\rm{d}}x\) or by \(S(E)\equiv (1/\rho){\rm{d}}E/{\rm{d}}x\), where \({\rm{d}}E/{\rm{d}}x\) is the rate of loss of energy \(E\) with distance \(x\) along the particle trajectory. Note that \({\rm{d}}E/{\rm{d}}x\) is often called the stopping power, although it is not in units of power. This inconsistency for the term stopping power leads to its deprecation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>stopping cross section</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09388 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09388">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ion moving with a velocity exceeding the Bohr speed of \(c/137\), where \(c\) is the velocity of light.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For argon and gold, this criterion requires energies greater than \(\pu{1 MeV}\) and \(\pu{5 MeV}\), respectively.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>swift ion</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09389 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09389">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Energy resolution or depth resolution measured in a backscattering spectrum for a monoenergetic incident-ion beam.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>system resolution</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09245"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09364"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09390 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09390">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Calculated intensity for probe ions scattered into a specified solid angle at a given direction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>theoretical scattered-ion intensity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09378"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09391 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09391">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In energetic-ion analysis and Rutherford backscattering spectrometry, sample whose thickness is sufficiently small that the variation in energy of particles backscattered from atoms of each constitutive element is small with respect to the system resolution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>thin target</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09178"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09389"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09392 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09392">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Action to prevent secondary ions from travelling through the mass spectrometer and being detected.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>This action is usually made by pulsing one of the relevant electrode potentials in time-of-flight mass spectrometers to deflect ions of a selected mass range in which intense peaks occur, so that those masses are not detected and thus do not cause unwanted detector saturation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>analyser blanking</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09422"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09393 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09393">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reduction in the spread of arrival times of an ion pulse by reduction in the speed of the leading ions or acceleration of those at the trailing edge of the pulse.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>This procedure can degrade the optimum focus of the ion beam, since different ions experience different fields.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>beam bunching</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09260"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09394 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09394">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Device to shorten the time length of a pulse of ions so that they arrive at a point over a reduced time interval.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The point is usually the sample surface.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>buncher</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09395 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09395">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Atomic fraction or concentration of impurity species in a homogeneous matrix below which the secondary-ion mass spectrometry can safely be assumed to be linear with composition.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>dilute limit</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09396 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09396">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Cross section for the loss of intensity of an ion signal observed as a result of the bombardment by primary ions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Generally, the larger the molecule, the larger the disappearance cross section.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>It is often assumed that the material being studied is present as a monolayer; however, this is an experimentally measured parameter and a value can be obtained irrespective of the precise form of the material under study. The disappearance cross section has practical significance and might or might not be simply related to the damage cross section.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The ion signal used is usually that for a large or characteristic fragment of the molecule, such as a cationized ion, protonated ion, or deprotonated ion from the molecule, at a surface.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>disappearance cross section</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09414"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09397 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09397">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In secondary-ion mass spectrometry, sputter depth profiling involving two ion guns.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In time-of-flight mass spectrometers, one beam is used with a short on-time for the secondary ion mass spectrometry analysis, while a second is used during the period when the first is off and the mass analysis has been completed in each cycle. The second gun provides the ions for sputter removal of the sample to form the depth profile. This combination allows practical sputtering rates to be achieved and the profiling to be optimized separately from the optimization for the SIMS analysis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Two similar ion guns can be used in opposite azimuths of the sample to reduce the development of topography.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dual-beam profiling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09244"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09286"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09398 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09398">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In secondary-ion mass spectrometry, electric field above a sample, operational during ion emission from the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The extraction field can be pulsed or constant, depending on the type of instrument.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>extraction field</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09399 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09399">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electric potential, referenced to the sample, of the electrode defining the field above the sample and used to facilitate introduction of emitted ions into the mass spectrometer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In pulsed-extraction mode, the extractor voltage will be pulsed to the high value required to extract ions emitted from the sample by the primary-ion pulse for at least the time period necessary for the heaviest ion to pass through the extractor electrode and will then be reduced to the extraction bias voltage until the next pulse is required.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>This voltage, together with the separation distance between the sample and the extractor electrode, defines the extraction field.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>extractor voltage</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09398"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09414"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09400 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09400">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Secondary ion mass spectrometry in which the primary-ion beam is replaced by a fast-atom beam.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>FAB-SIMS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>fast-atom bombardment secondary ion mass spectrometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09414"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09400"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>FAB-SIMS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09401 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09401">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of ions of a particular species sputtered from a sample divided by number of particles of that species sputtered from that sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>fractional ion yield</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09253"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09265"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09299"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09402 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09402">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Power for the ratio of mass intensities in two mass spectra measured for two different primary ion conditions, used to generate the G-SIMS spectrum.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A value of 13 has been found to be effective for the g index in generating G-SIMS spectra and a continuous variation between 0 and 40 for analysis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The different primary ion conditions can be two, well-separated, energies for the same primary ion or two different primary ions, usually at the same beam energy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>g index</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09186"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09414"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09403 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09403">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Development of G-SIMS in which the g index is varied in order to deduce the linkages between product ions and precursor ion.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>G-SIMS-FPM</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>G-SIMS with fragmentation pathway mapping</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09186"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09402"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09403"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>G-SIMS-FPM</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09404 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09404">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Intensity of analyte ion divided by intensity of matrix ion, both corrected for isotopic abundance.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>ion beam ratio</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09405 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09405">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ion gun utilizing a liquid-metal Taylor cone as the source of the primary ions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>LMIG</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The Taylor cone generates ion sources of very high brightness. This design enables the gun to provide beam diameters in the range \(\pu{50 nm}\) to \(\pu{1 \upmu m}\) for ion beam energies in the range \(\pu{5 keV}\) to \(\pu{30 keV}\), the smallest diameters generally being at the higher energies.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>liquid-metal ion gun</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09414"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09405"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>LMIG</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09406 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09406">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measured mass minus reference value for that mass.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In practice, mass accuracy is expressed as a fractional error.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>mass accuracy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09420"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09407 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09407">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Intensity in a mass spectrum arising from ions that spontaneously fragment between emission and detection.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In reflectron time-of-flight mass spectrometers, the decay of metastable ions leads to broad peaks at a mass dependent on the drift energy and reflector voltage. Good design minimizes these background signals.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>metastable background</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09418"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09408 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09408">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ion or neutral particle that was part of a larger molecular structure and contains information about that structure.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>molecular fragment</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09409 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09409">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Image of a surface formed from ions characteristic of a particular molecule.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>molecular image</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09293"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09429"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09410 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09410">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of negatively-charged secondary ions sputtered from a sample divided by total number of incident primary particles.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>negative-ion yield</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09299"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09401"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09413"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09422"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09411 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09411">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of ions of a particular species sputtered from a sample divided by the number of incident particles.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>partial ion sputtering yield</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>partial ion yield</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09253"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09265"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09299"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09401"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09410"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09413"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09412 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09412">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ion or neutral particle composed of three or more atoms.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>polyatomic fragment</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09413 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09413">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of positively-charged secondary ions sputtered from a sample divided by the total number of incident primary particles.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>positive-ion yield</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09299"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09401"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09410"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09422"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09414 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09414">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ion extracted from a source and directed at a sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>primary ion</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09378"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09422"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09415 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09415">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of ion pulse cycles per second in secondary ion mass spectrometry.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>pulse rate</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09421"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09416 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09416">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Full width at half maximum of the time distribution of the pulse of ions generated by a beam chopper and, optionally, the use of beam bunching in secondary ion mass spectrometry.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The pulse width is usually measured using the \(\ce{H+}\) ion. The velocity of \(\ce{H+}\) ions, for a given extraction field, is larger than that of other ions. The width in time of the \(\ce{H+}\) pulse thus provides a more reliable value for the width of the extraction pulse.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>pulse width</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09393"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09398"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09417 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09417">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction field around the sample that is pulsed to the working value for extracting ions for the time necessary for operation of a time-of-flight mass spectrometer, but is otherwise at a low value.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>This is the usual mode in time-of-flight secondary ion mass spectrometry systems, either for studying insulators, where charge neutralization is established while the extraction field is off, or for depth profiling using a second ion beam while the extraction field is off.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>pulsed extraction field</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09244"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09398"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09418 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09418">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electric potential set on the reflector electrode of a reflectron mass spectrometer with the zero referenced, such that an ion emitted from the sample with an energy numerically equal to the reflector voltage would just be on the point of reflection or transmission by the reflector electrode.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>reflector voltage</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11544"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09419 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09419">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In dynamic secondary-ion mass spectrometry, coefficient for an element by which the measured intensity for an isotope of that element, divided by the measured intensity for a matrix ion, is multiplied to yield the atomic concentration of that isotope of the element present in the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Matrix terms are strong, and the matrix, bombarding species, incident-ion energy, and angle of incidence, as well as the spectrometer operating conditions, all affect relative elemental sensitivity factors significantly.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>relative isotopic sensitivity factor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09183"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09420 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09420">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mass accuracy divided by reference mass.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>relative mass accuracy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09406"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09422 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09422">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ion ejected from a sample surface as a result of energy and momentum transfer from a primary ion.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>secondary ion</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09414"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09424 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09424">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of secondary ions as a function of angle of emission.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>secondary-ion angular distribution</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09425 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09425">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of secondary ions as a function of kinetic energy at a specified collection angle.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>secondary-ion energy distribution</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09422"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09426 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09426">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of ions sputtered from a sample divided by the number of ions incident with a given mass, energy, charge, and angle of incidence.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>secondary-ion yield</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09427 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09427">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of ion pulses incident at each pixel in an image for one raster frame in secondary ion mass spectrometry.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>shots per pixel</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09274"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09428 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09428">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Two-dimensional surface map of the spatial distribution of the amount of a particular secondary ion emitted from within a specific area of the sample in secondary ion mass spectrometry.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>SIMS ion image</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09293"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09422"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09429 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09429">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ion fluence above which any significant changes in the spectrum, arising from beam damage, are observed in secondary ion mass spectrometry.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Classically, a limit of \(\pu{E12}\) ions per \(\pu{cm2}\), or \(\pu{E16}\) ions per \(\pu{m2}\) is taken as the limit not to be exceeded in static secondary ion mass spectrometry. This limit is based on one incident ion for each \(\pu{1000}\) surface atoms.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>For imaging, the total molecular signal can be used, and here the limit can be greater and reach \(\pu{100}\) times the limit given in Note 1.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>For large molecules, the damage cross section and disappearance cross section are both generally larger than for small molecules, leading to a static limit less than \(\pu{E12}\) ions per \(\pu{cm2}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>static limit</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09430 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09430">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Registration of a particle by a time-to-digital converter in secondary ion mass spectrometry.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The arrival time for each ion providing a pulse at the detector is registered by the time-to-digital converter (TDC). This is a stop event. TDCs might only record a fixed number of stop events during the time following each primary-ion pulse, for example 512 or 1024, other events being lost.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>stop event</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09414"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09431 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09431">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Probability of a defined charged or neutral species being produced, by consumption of a defined parent atomic or molecular configuration at a surface, as a result of sputtering in secondary ion mass spectrometry.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>transformation probability</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09432 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09432">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Depth profile in secondary-ion mass spectrometry where the depth over which significant changes occur is less than \(\pu{10 nm}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>ultra-shallow depth profile</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09433 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09433">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In secondary ion mass spectrometry, number of ions of a particular isotope detected divided by the number of atoms of the same element sputtered from the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>useful ion yield</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09434 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09434">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Image resolution in secondary ion mass spectrometry obtained in practice.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The image resolution is poorer than the primary-ion beam diameter as a result of either the need to maintain the damage level below a limit set by the integrity of the data or the need to record sufficient signal when the sample is being consumed during analysis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>useful spatial resolution</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09435 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09435">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Distance over which the transmitted X-ray intensity falls by \(1/e\), where \(e\) is the base of the natural logarithm.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>absorption length</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09436 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09436">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Area on the specimen irradiated by X-rays in X-ray reflectrometry.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In typical commercial equipment, the beam width is \(\pu{200 \upmu m}\), so at \(\pu{0.3\!^{\circ}}\) incident angle, the beam footprint is \(\pu{38 mm}\) long; at \(\pu{1\!^{\circ}}\), it is \(\pu{11.5 mm}\) and at zero incidence angle it is infinite. For \(\pu{100 \upmu m}\) incident beam width, these values are halved.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The beam footprint along the beam direction can be reduced by the use of a suitable knife-edge diaphragm mounted in a plane that is normal to the sample surface and whose normal, in turn, aligns with the beam azimuth. The knife-edge is parallel to, and adjusted to be close to, the surface at the point where the X-ray beam centre strikes the sample surface. This closeness limits the footprint size but also reduces the measured signal intensity.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>beam footprint</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09214"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09437 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09437">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Fraction of the incident X-ray beam in X-ray reflectrometry missing the specimen.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>beam spill-off</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09214"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09438 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09438">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>X-ray energy at which there is a significant increase of absorption and for which the increased absorption occurs at a greater energy than a core-level binding energy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For many elements, there is a significant increase of absorption when the X-ray energy is equal to the electron-binding energy for a sub-shell. A delayed onset occurs for some elements and sub-shells when the corresponding increase of absorption occurs, instead, at an energy larger than the sub-shell binding energy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>delayed onset</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09439 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09439">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Plane containing the source, detector, incident, and specularly reflected X–ray beams in X-ray reflectrometry.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>dispersion plane</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09214"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09440 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09440">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Condition where the glancing angle for the incident X-rays is at or less than the critical angle and the X-rays are either reflected or absorbed in a region very close to the surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The reflected intensity is close to \(\pu{100\%}\) of the incident intensity and the transmitted intensity is zero.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>total reflection of X-rays</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09441 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09441">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Vector in reciprocal space describing the direction of propagation of X-rays and equal in magnitude to \(2\uppi/\lambda\), where \(\lambda\) is the wavelength of the X-rays.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In different fields of study, the wave vector is taken as \(2\uppi/\lambda\) or as \(1/\lambda\). In X-ray reflectometry the former is usually used.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>wave vector</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09442 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09442">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>X-ray absorption coefficient at an energy just above an absorption edge divided by that at an energy just below the edge.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:comment>X-ray absorption spectra can have complex shapes for X-ray energies in the vicinity of photoionization thresholds, and a well-defined edge is not always observed at the threshold.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>X-ray jump ratio</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09443 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09443">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In secondary-ion mass spectrometry, Auger electron spectroscopy, and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy, distance over which a change in signal intensity from \(\pu{16\%}\) to \(\pu{84\%}\), or from \(\pu{84\%}\) to \(\pu{16\%}\), is measured at the junction of two dissimilar matrices, the thicknesses of which are more than six times that distance</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>depth resolution</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The change in signal intensity should be quoted with the observed interface width.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>observed interface width</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09172"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09192"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09245"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09444 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09444">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Resonant interaction between the natural frequency of a surface plasmon and incident light of the same frequency.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0404"/>
        <rdfs:label>surface plasmon resonance</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09294"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09445 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09445">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Interfacial agent comprised of molecules possessing two or more functional groups, each of which exhibits preferential interactions with the various types of phase domains in a composite.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>adhesion promoter</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09446 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09446">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Image of a mask pattern that is projected onto the photoresist coated wafer by an optical system.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>aerial image</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09495"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09523"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09447 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09447">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Thermal treatment of a solid polymer material at a fixed or changing temperature that leads to desired changes in its physical structure and properties without involving complete melting or dissolution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Annealing may be carried out by exposure of a crystalline polymer to a poor solvent or to its vapors.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Annealing of a crystalline polymer is usually carried out by keeping the polymer at temperatures just below its melting point.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In a crystalline polymer, the process of annealing leads to reorganization and involves an increase of order in existing crystallites, an increase in degree of crystallinity, and changes to more stable polymorphs.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>annealing</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09448 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09448">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Annealing via the exposure of a substrate to solvent vapor.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Solvent vapor annealing is most commonly used in lithography to process block copolymer (BCP) thin films, often leading to a more equilibrated structure.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>solvent vapor annealing</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09449 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09449">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Annealing in which a heating step is used to activate chemical processes in a resist that depend on diffusion.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Thermal annealing is frequently employed in photolithography and in block copolymer lithography.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>thermal annealing</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09454"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09517"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09450 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09450">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Material used in lithography or in other technologies that is applied to a surface in order to reduce reflection.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ARC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>anti-reflective coating</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09503"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09450"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ARC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09451 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09451">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Proportional relationship between the width and height of an image produced by exposing a resist.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>aspect ratio</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09495"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09452 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09452">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>High-resolution scanning probe microscopy that maps a surface with a demonstrated resolution of the order of fractions of a nanometer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>AFM</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>scanning force microscopy (SFM)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Such tools for microscopy may also be used for nanoscale pattern generation of thin organic films on substrates.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>atomic force microscopy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09452"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>AFM</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09453 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09453">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Base added to a chemical amplification resist to neutralize low levels of acid that might diffuse into nominally unexposed regions and cause blur.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>base quencher</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09565"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09454 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09454">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process in which a thin film of block copolymer is formed into a regular nanoscale pattern.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>block copolymer lithography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09470"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09455 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09455">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Layer placed between a photoresist and a substrate to prevent reflection from the latter resulting in a reduction in photoresist pattern quality, as characterized by resolution, line edge roughness, etc.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>BARC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>bottom anti-reflective coating</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09500"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09455"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>BARC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09456 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09456">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Pattern formation using self-assembly processes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>bottom-up lithography</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09457 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09457">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Solvent in which a resist or additional thin-film component (typically a BARC) is dissolved for the coating process.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>basting solvent</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09455"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09458 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09458">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Dose of radiation required to completely remove a positive-tone resist from a large area.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>clearing dose</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>dose to clear</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>clearance dose</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09477"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09459 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09459">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Hole in a layer of a semiconductor device enabling contact with an underlying semiconductor layer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Contact holes are usually of a diameter less than \(\pu{100 nm}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>contact hole</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09460 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09460">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Maximum of the absolute value of the gradient of a contrast curve taken as a measure of the ability of a resist to differentiate the regions that have been exposed to radiation from those that have not.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>contrast ratio</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>For ease of determination, for positive- and negative-tone resists respectively, contrasts are usually calculated as \[\gamma =\frac{1}{2\log_{10} (D_{\rm{n}}^{0.5}/D_{\rm{n}}^{0})} \quad {\rm{and}} \quad \gamma =\frac{1}{2\log_{10} (D_{\rm{p}}^1 /D_{\rm{p}}^{0.5})}\] in which \(D_{\rm{n}}^{0}\), \(D_{\rm{n,p}}^{0.5}\), and \(D_{\rm{p}}^{1}\) are the incident exposure doses to light or radiation required for incipient gel formation in a negative-tone system, the dose at \(\pu{50\%}\) of the original resist thickness remaining after development for either system, and the clearance dose for a positive-tone system, respectively.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>contrast</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09461"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09468"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09477"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09461 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09461">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Plot of the fraction of the original thickness that remains after the development of a uniformly irradiated resist versus the logarithm of the incident exposure dose.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>contrast curve</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09468"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09477"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09462 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09462">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Length of the smallest geometrical features that can be formed in a lithographic process.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CD</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The critical dimension uniformity (CDU), which is a statistical measurement of the variation in length of the smallest geometrical feature, is often as low as \(3\:\upsigma\) of \(\pu{10\%}\) of the CD.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The final device CD is often notably smaller than the smallest geometrical features that can be formed in a lithographic process, achieved, for example, by etch or slimming processes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>critical dimension</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09462"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CD</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09463 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09463">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Unwanted change in the solubility of a resist that takes place in the absence of exposure.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>dark loss</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09464 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09464">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Imperfection introduced during pattern formation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>image defect</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>defect</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09465 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09465">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Imaged resist without imperfections in pattern.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In a DSA resist, for example, no error is introduced by the self-assembly process.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In a photoresist, for example, no error is introduced by the photochemistry.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>defect-free pattern</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09466 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09466">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Removal of a protecting group which leads to a change in the solubility of a polymeric resist.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Deprotection is a common feature in the performance of most chemical amplification resists.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>deprotection</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09467 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09467">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Tolerance of the placement of the image plane in relation to image quality.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Term used in an exposed and developed photoresist.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>depth of focus</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09495"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09468 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09468">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process in which a developer is used to generate an image by removing the soluble portion of the latent pattern of an exposed resist.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Aqueous-based development is carried out using a \(\pu{0.262 M}\) solution of tetramethylammonium hydroxide in water as developer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Solvent development is carried out using a non-polar solvent as a developer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>development</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09495"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09469 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09469">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Direct write, tip-based patterning method that uses the tip of an AFM probe to act as a pen and deposit molecules on a surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The ink used in dip-pen nanolithography often consists of thiols that self-assemble on surfaces.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dip-pen nanolithography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09515"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09470 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09470">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process in which a thin film of block copolymer is formed into a regular nanoscale pattern on chemically or topographically nanopatterned surfaces (or a combination thereof).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DSA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>directed self-assembly</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09539"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09471 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09471">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Defect, or irregularity, within an ordered structure.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A screw dislocation is a structure in which a helical path is traced around a linear defect (dislocation line) by the atomic planes in the crystal lattice.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>An edge dislocation is a defect where an extra half-plane of atoms is introduced mid-way through the crystal.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In the context of lithography, a dislocation may be found in a block copolymer pattern.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dislocation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09464"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09503"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09472 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09472">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Compound that, when included in a resist formulation, slows the rate of dissolution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A typical example of a resist based on a dissolution inhibitor comprises a diazonaphthoquinone within a phenolic resin.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In lithography, the effectiveness of a dissolution inhibitor is changed after exposure to imaging radiation, which facilitates its application in resist formulations.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dissolution inhibitor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09503"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09473 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09473">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Compound that, when included in a resist formulation, increases the rate of dissolution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>dissolution promoter</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09474 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09474">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Rate at which resist is removed during development.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Usually reported in \(\pu{nm s^{-1}}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dissolution rate</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09468"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09475 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09475">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coating of solution or slurry drawn to uniform film by use of a knife edge or doctor blade.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The term has been borrowed from printing and coating technologies. It is believed that it derives from the blades employed in letter press equipment that were used to wipe so-called ductor rolls, the term &quot;ductor&quot; becoming doctor over time.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>doctor blading</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09476 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09476">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process that increases the thermal-equilibrium concentration of free charge carriers in a material to augment its electrical conductivity using chemical agents or additives (i.e. dopants).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Typical dopants to organic polymers (compounds) are:Oxidants, giving positively charged (p-doped) polymers that act as hole conductors;Reductants, giving negatively charged (n-doped) polymers that mostly act as electron conductors;Strong Brønsted acids, which are indispensable dopants to polymers such as polyaniline.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>doping (p- and n-type)</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09478 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09478">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Lithographic process requiring two exposures to radiation for a single resist layer to form a finished pattern.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>double exposure</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09479 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09479">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Printing of two distinct lines at the edges of a single resist line pattern by means of a deposit-etch process.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>double patterning</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09480 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09480">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Assembly of a layer on a substrate by the use of a solvent-free process, such as vapor deposition.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>dry deposition</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09582"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09481 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09481">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process in which a portion of a latent pattern in an exposed resist is removed to form the image without the use of a liquid developer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>dry development</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09495"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09482 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09482">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Facility of an exposed resist to withstand etching conditions, usually a plasma, determined in comparison to a reference standard.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>etch resistance</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Plasma may be oxygen or halogen-based.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dry-etch resistance</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09483 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09483">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Photoresist capable of being processed in either positive tone (aqueous base development) or negative tone (organic solvent development).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>dual-tone resist</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09484 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09484">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Degree to which the edge of an image appears sharp and precise.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>edge sharpness</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>edge acuity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09495"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09485 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09485">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Creating a pattern by pressing a master die into a malleable material.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>embossing</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09486 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09486">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Growth of crystals on other crystals involving a precisely defined mutual orientation of their lattices.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The process involves heterogeneous nucleation of the growing crystals by the substrate crystal faces.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>epitaxial crystallization</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09487 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09487">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Deposition of a crystalline over layer on a crystalline substrate.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>An ordered spatial relationship existing between the lattices of substrate crystals and those grown by epitaxial crystallization on top of the substrate crystals.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Examples involving polymers are given under the terms homoepitaxy and heteroepitaxy, where if the crystals are of the same substance, but possibly different polymorphs, the former term applies, in contrast to the use of the latter term, which involves crystals of two different substances.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The over layer is called an epitaxial film or epitaxial layer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term epitaxy is sometimes used for epitaxial crystallization. This use is not recommended.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>epitaxy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09486"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09488 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09488">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Treatment to remove substrate to a requisite depth from those areas of a surface no longer protected following the exposure and development of an overlaid resist.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>etch</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09468"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09489 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09489">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process of removal of material, typically of a masked pattern on a semiconductor, by exposing it to a bombardment of ions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>dry etching</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09523"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09490 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09490">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Rate of material removal using either a wet or a dry-etch process.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Usually reported in \(\pu{\upmu m min-1}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>etch rate</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09491 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09491">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process of removal of material, typically of a masked pattern on a semiconductor, by exposing it to a corrosive liquid.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>wet etching</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09523"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09492 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09492">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extent to which a light- or radiation-sensitive material can be overexposed or underexposed and still achieve acceptable imaging.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>exposure latitude</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09493 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09493">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Technique used for the site-specific analysis, deposition, ablation, and micromachining of materials down to dimensions of \(\pu{10}\) to \(\pu{15 nm}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>FIB</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>FIB should not be confused with ion-beam lithography.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>focused ion beam</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09493"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>FIB</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09494 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09494">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>High temperature annealing to solidify any resist material remaining after development in order to provide a durable protective layer for etching.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>hard bake</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09468"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09488"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09495 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09495">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Pattern produced in or on a substrate during a lithographic process.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The term was originally used to describe patterns produced by means of light exposure followed by development, but with the growth in the number of lithographic methods it may now refer to those produced by molding, embossing, or other kinds of patterning processes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>image</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09468"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09485"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09496 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09496">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Visible image produced by an effect of radiation on a substrate followed by subsequent lithographic development.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>developed image</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09468"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09495"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09497 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09497">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Broadening during the development of a lithographic image.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Image blur is measured as the distance between regions of \(\pu{12}\) and \(\pu{88\%}\) maximum exposure.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>image blur</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09468"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09495"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09498 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09498">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>High refractive index liquid medium used in immersion lithography that replaces the air gap between the lens and the resist surface in conventional photolithography.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>immersion fluid</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09508"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09517"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09499 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09499">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Method whereby a film, usually metallic, deposited on a patterned substrate after etch processing, is removed during resist strip, which leaves only that portion of the film that is deposited directly onto the exposed regions of the substrate.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A lift-off process is commonly employed to pattern substrates when using resists that have little or no dry-etch resistance.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>lift-off process</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09488"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09489"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09571"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09500 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09500">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Variations along one edge of a linear resist feature over an extended length.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>LER</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>line edge roughness</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09500"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>LER</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09501 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09501">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Variations in the width of a linear resist feature over an extended length.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>LWR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>line width roughness</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09501"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>LWR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09502 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09502">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Developed lithographic pattern comprising alternating lines with spaces between.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>line-space patterning</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09503 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09503">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process whereby an image is created on an apparently planar surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The term was originally applied within printing technology, but its usage has been extended to embrace the imaging processes of the microfabrication techniques used to make integrated circuits and microelectromechanical systems.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>lithography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09495"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09504 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09504">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Pattern created without recourse to irradiation through a mask of the image.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Typical direct-write imaging systems are electron-beam lithography and ion-beam lithography.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>direct-write lithography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09495"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09505"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09523"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09505 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09505">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Lithography performed by exposing a resist to a beam of electrons.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>e-beam lithography</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>electron beam lithography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09503"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09506 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09506">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electron beam lithography whereby the pattern is written directly onto the photoresist without the use of a mask.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>EBDW</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>electron-beam direct-write lithography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09505"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09523"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09506"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>EBDW</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09507 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09507">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Photolithography performed by exposing a resist to extreme ultraviolet (\(\pu{13 nm}\)) radiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>EUVL</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>extreme ultraviolet lithography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09517"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09507"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>EUVL</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09508 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09508">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Photolithography that provides resolution enhancement by immersing a resist-coated substrate in a fluid during the exposure process.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>immersion lithography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09498"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09517"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09509 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09509">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Patterns created by mechanical deformation of an imprint resist and a subsequent curing process.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>imprint lithography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09510 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09510">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Subset of imprint lithography that allows for the fabrication of nanometer scale patterns.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>nanoimprint lithography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09509"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09511 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09511">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Technique for patterning regular arrays of fine features without the use of complex optical systems or photomasks.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>holographic lithography</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>interference lithography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09523"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09512 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09512">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Lithography for direct patterning of surfaces based on the application of a magnetic field to the substrate using paramagnetic metal masks.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>magnetolithography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09503"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09523"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09513 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09513">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Lithography that does not need a mask of the image to make a pattern.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>maskless lithography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09495"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09503"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09504"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09523"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09514 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09514">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Lithography or pattern formation at a resolution of the order of microns.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>microlithography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09503"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09515 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09515">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Umbrella term that is used to describe all lithographic techniques that allow for the fabrication of nanometer scale patterns.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>nanolithography</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09516 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09516">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Lithography in which material is deposited into the interstices of a 2D array of nanospheres that are subsequently removed to form a pattern.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Vapor deposition is the most commonly used deposition technique.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nanosphere lithography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09503"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09517 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09517">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Lithography performed by exposing a resist to an intense parallel light beam through an image mask.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>optical lithography</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>photolithography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09495"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09503"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09523"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09518 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09518">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Lithographic method in which a microscopic or nanoscopic stylus is moved mechanically across a surface to form a pattern.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>scanning-probe lithography</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09519 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09519">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Family of techniques for fabricating or replicating structures using elastomeric stamps, molds, and conformable photomasks.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>soft lithography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09523"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09520 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09520">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Lithography performed by exposing a resist to X-radiation through an image mask.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>X-ray lithography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09495"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09503"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09523"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09521 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09521">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Lithography performed in three dimensions, typically carried out using nanoimprint patterning or 2-photon lithography.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>3D lithography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09503"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09510"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09522"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09522 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09522">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Photolithography within a thick photoresist film, within which the photochemically-induced solubility change results from the simultaneous absorption of two photons.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>two-photon lithography</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>An instrument such as a confocal microscope can be used for two-photon lithography.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Usually the imaging radiation is of near-IR wavelength.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>2-photon lithography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09517"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09523 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09523">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Transparent substrate pre-patterned with the desired image that is inserted into the incident beam in photo- and X-ray lithography.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>photomask</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A dark field mask is used with a positive tone photoresist and a bright field mask with a negative tone photoresist.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Masks are typically transparent fused silica blanks with a pattern defined in a chrome film using direct-write lithography.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>mask</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09495"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09504"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09520"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09524 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09524">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mask with a pattern defined by a paramagnetic metal.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>magnetic mask</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09523"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09525 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09525">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Increase in error of pattern edge location during pattern formation when the wavelength of light used to expose a resist is greater than the feature size.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>MEEF</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>mask error enhancement factor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09525"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>MEEF</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09526 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09526">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Form of soft lithography that uses micron-scale and smaller relief patterns on a master elastomer stamp to form images on the surface of a substrate through conformal contact.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Stamps fabricated from polydimethylsiloxane are often used to make patterns of self-assembled monolayers on a metal, such as gold.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>microcontact printing</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09495"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09519"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09527 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09527">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process whereby structures of micrometre scale and smaller are fabricated.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>microfabrication</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09528 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09528">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Multi-disciplinary subject concerned with the behavior, precise control, and manipulation of fluids that are geometrically constrained within a small, typically sub-millimeter, scale channel.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>microfluidics</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09529 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09529">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Form of soft lithography that uses the relief patterns in a master template to make 3-dimensional structures.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Micromolding is used to manufacture parts of micro-objects, such as microfluidic circuits.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Molds are commonly fabricated from an elastomer, such as polydimethylsiloxane.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>micromolding</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09519"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09528"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09530 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09530">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Assembly of small molecules that vitrify, rather than crystallize, when cooled.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Molecular glass photoresists are used in deep- and extreme-UV photolithography.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>molecular glass</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09531 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09531">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Molecule that acts as a device performing a logical operation based on one or more physical or chemical inputs and a single output.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>molecular logic gate</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09532 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09532">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Molecular structure that transfers a pattern to or organizes another molecular scale structure.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>molecular template</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09533 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09533">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Molecular-scale objects that conduct electrical current.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>molecular wires</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09534 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09534">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Empirical observation by Gordon E. Moore, co-founder of Intel, that, over the history of computing hardware, the number of transistors on integrated circuits doubles approximately every two years.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Moore’s law applies more fundamentally to advances in lithographic processing, particularly achievable resolution, since the inception of printed circuit boards.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Moore’s law</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09535 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09535">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Device in which the manufactured size is between \(\pu{1}\) and \(\pu{100 nm}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>nanodevice</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09536 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09536">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Devices that integrate electrical and mechanical functionality at the nanoscale. A miniaturization of microelectromechanical systems (MEMS).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NEMS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>nanoelectromechanical systems</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09536"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>NEMS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09537 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09537">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Application of nanotechnology to microelectronics.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>nanoelectronics</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09542"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09538 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09538">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Particle of any shape with an equivalent diameter of approximately \(\pu{1}\) to \(\pu{100 nm}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>nanoparticle photoresists are used in deep- and extreme-UV photolithography.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nanoparticle</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09539 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09539">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Patterns on a substrate with lesser dimensions of between \(\pu{1}\) to \(\pu{100 nm}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>nanopattern</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09540 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09540">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Study of the interaction of light with particles or substances on a scale less than the wavelength of the light.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>nano-optics</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>nanophotonics</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09541 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09541">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Structure or feature of size between \(\pu{1}\) and \(\pu{100 nm}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>nanostructure</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09542 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09542">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Manipulation of matter on an atomic and molecular scale.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>nanotechnology</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09543 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09543">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Microscopy applied to the investigation of nanostructures that breaks the farfield resolution limit by exploiting the properties of evanescent waves.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NSOM</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>NSOM can be used to make nanopatterns.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>near-field scanning optical microscopy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09539"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09541"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09543"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>NSOM</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09544 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09544">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process for revealing the latent image in a resist in which the exposed regions are less soluble in the developer than the unexposed regions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NTD</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>negative-tone development</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09544"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>NTD</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09545 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09545">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measure of the information content of the aerial image and represents an energy (intensity) gradient at the position of the nominal line edge.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NILS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>normalized image slope</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>normalized image log slope</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09446"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09545"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>NILS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09546 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09546">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Destruction of pattern during and after development caused by the surface tension of the development solvent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>pattern collapse</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09468"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09547 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09547">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Transfer of the pattern of a developed lithographic image to an underlying substrate by processes such as etching.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>pattern transfer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09488"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09495"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09548 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09548">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Compound that on exposure to radiation gives rise to a proton.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PAG</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>photoacid generator</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09548"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PAG</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09549 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09549">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Compound that on exposure to radiation will release an active ionic or radical species.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PAC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>photoactive compound</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09549"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PAC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09550 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09550">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Periodic structures consisting of a dielectric material that shows strong interaction with light.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>photonic crystal</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09551 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09551">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Science and technology of the production, transmission, absorption, sensing, and direction of light.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>photonics</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09552 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09552">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Method for making a lithographic pattern smaller than that deemed possible given the wavelength of the imaging radiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A current objective of directed self-assembly is to use the process with conventional lithography to double the pitch.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>pitch division</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09470"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09503"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09553 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09553">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coating used to create a level surface during lithographic processing.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>This layer is often organic and may be called an organic planarizing layer (OPL).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>planarising layer</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09554 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09554">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process to deposit a solid film on a substrate resulting from plasma-induced reactions of precursor compounds, either in the gaseous state or on the film surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PECVD</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A RF frequency or DC discharge generated by two electrodes inducing a plasma from a gas occupying the space between.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Examples of PECVD films are \(\ce{SiO2}\) films via PECVD of dichlorosilane or silane and oxygen precursors or tetraethoxysilane in an oxygen or oxygen-argon plasma, silicon nitride formed from silane and ammonia or nitrogen, and amorphous silicon from monosilane or tetrachlorosilane, as well as polymers from vinyl monomers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>plasma enhanced chemical vapor deposition</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09554"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PECVD</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09555 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09555">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Plasma processing used in the fabrication of integrated circuits that generates volatile products from the elements of the material etched and the reactive species generated by the plasma.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A variation of this method that removes a single layer of atoms is known as atomic layer etching (ALE).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>plasma etch</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09556 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09556">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process for revealing the latent image in a resist in which the exposed regions are more soluble in the developer than are the unexposed regions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>positive-tone development</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09557 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09557">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Thermal annealing step following the coating of a substrate with a resist material.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PAB</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>post-apply bake</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09447"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09557"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PAB</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09558 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09558">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Thermal annealing step following the exposure of a resist to imaging radiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PEB</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>post bake</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>post-exposure bake</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09447"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09558"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PEB</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09559 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09559">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Pause between successive patterning steps in lithographic processing.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PED</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>post-exposure delay</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09559"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PED</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09560 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09560">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Non-reactive group temporarily used to transform a reactive group into one that does not react under conditions where the non-protected group reacts.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In photoresists, protecting groups may also alter solubility (e.g., tBOC protection of hydroxy groups in chemically amplified poly(4-hydroxystyrene) resists).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Trimethylsilylation is a typical transformation used to protect reactive functional groups, such as hydroxy or amino groups, from their reaction with the propagating species in an anionic polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>protecting group</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09561 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09561">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Widening of the exposure dose distribution, and hence the developed pattern, caused by the scattering of the primary beam electrons within the resist and substrate.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>proximity effect</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09477"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09562 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09562">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Rectangular pattern of line-by-line scanning for image capture used in electron beam and ion-beam lithography.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>raster scan</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09495"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09505"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09583"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09563 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09563">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Etching technology used in microfabrication that uses chemically reactive plasma to remove material from a substrate.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RIE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>reactive ion etching</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09563"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>RIE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Material, usually a polymer, that when irradiated either undergoes a marked change in solubility in a given solvent or is ablated.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A resist polymer under irradiation either forms patterns directly or undergoes chemical reactions leading to pattern formation after subsequent processing.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In a positive-tone resist, also called a positive-working resist, the material in the irradiated area not covered by a mask is removed on development, which results in an image with a pattern identical with that on the mask. In a negative-tone resist, also called a negative-working resist, the non-irradiated area is subsequently removed, which results in an image with a pattern that is the complement of that on the mask.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>resist</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09468"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09495"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09523"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09565 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09565">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Resist that undergoes solubility change upon exposure of photoacid generator, followed by thermally activated acid catalyzed deprotection.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CAR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>chemically amplified resist</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09466"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09548"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09565"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CAR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09566 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09566">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Resist optimized for use under electron beam irradiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>e-beam resist</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>electron-beam resist</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>electron beam resist</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09567 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09567">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Resist optimized for use under ion beam irradiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ion-beam resist</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>ion beam resist</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09568 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09568">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Resist comprising more than one active component.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>multi-component resist</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09569 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09569">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Resist optimized for use under X-ray irradiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>X-ray resist</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09570 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09570">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Topography of a resist pattern in cross section.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>resist profile</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09571 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09571">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Subsequent removal of the remaining resist that covers the protected regions of a substrate during development and etch processing.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>resist strip</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09468"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09488"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09572 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09572">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Noise which originates from the discrete nature of elementary charge.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Shot noise decreases the resolution of an image in e-beam lithography.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>shot noise</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09495"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09505"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09573 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09573">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Introduction of a substituted silyl group (\(\ce{R3Si}\)) to an alcohol, carboxylic acid, amine, thiol, or phosphate, commonly as a protecting group.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>silation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Trimethylsilylation used to protect reactive functional groups, such as hydroxy or amino groups, from their reaction with growing anionic species in anionic polymerization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>silylation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09574 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09574">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Short, low temperature annealing step following the coating of a wafer with a photoresist.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The purpose of the soft bake is to (1) drive out remaining coating solvent from the resist layer; (2) improve the adhesion of the resist to the substrate; and (3) anneal the shear stresses introduced during spin-coating.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>soft bake</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09447"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11545"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09575 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09575">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Three-dimensional template comprising the negative pattern used to leave an impression on the surface of a malleable material.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>stamp</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09576 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09576">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Molecular system comprising two or more self-assembled molecular, or molecular and ionic, entities held together by means of non-covalent intermolecular binding interactions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>supramolecular species</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>supramolecular structure</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>supramolecule</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The dimensions of supramolecular assemblies can range from nanometers to micrometers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The use of the term supramolecular polymer is discouraged.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>While a supramolecular assembly may comprise only two molecular or molecular/ionic entities, for example a DNA double helix or a host-guest complex, the term is more commonly used to denote larger constructs, typically with rod-like, sheet-like, or spherical structures.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>supramolecular assembly</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09577 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09577">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measure of the texture of a surface that is quantified by vertical deviations from its ideal location.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>surface roughness</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09578 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09578">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sinusoidal variation in the lithographic clearance dose arising from changes in the phase difference between incoming and outgoing radiation that is induced by variations in resist thickness.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>swing curve</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09458"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09579 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09579">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Protective layer spun directly on top of a resist.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In immersion lithography, a topcoat serves as barrier for chemical diffusion between the liquid medium and the photoresist.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>topcoat</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09508"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09580 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09580">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Pattern formation using lithographic (also embossing) methods.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>top-down processing</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>top-down patterning</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09485"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09581 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09581">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Lithographic process in which the exposed region of resist is silylated and the image then formed through reactive oxygen etch.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>TSI</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Oxygen plasma is used to convert any silicon to \(\ce{SiO2}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Usually a chemically amplified photoresist is used.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>top-surface imaging</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09488"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09495"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09564"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09581"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>TSI</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09582 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09582">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Growth of solid materials on a substrate by deposition from the gas phase.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>vapor deposition</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09583 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09583">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Pattern formation using a directed electron beam or ion beam that skips from region to region requiring exposure but is otherwise turned off.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-1215"/>
        <rdfs:label>vector scan</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09562"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09584 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09584">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Projection formula for macromolecules in which horizontal lines denote polymer backbone bonds. At each individual backbone carbon atom, horizontal lines represent bonds directed below the projection plane and vertical lines represent bonds directed above that plane.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0409"/>
        <rdfs:label>rotated Fischer projection</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09585 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09585">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Pair of adjacent constitutionally equivalent stereogenic units that are of like configuration.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0409"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Usage of the term meso diad for describing structures with a symmetrically constituted connecting group is no longer acceptable because of the potential for confusion with the similar term used in organic chemistry to describe small molecules.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>m diad</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09586 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09586">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Pair of adjacent constitutionally equivalent stereogenic units that are of unlike configuration. Figure 2: Diagrammatic representations of the configurational repeating unit or stereorepeating unit (upper), and the diad sequence (lower) for isotactic poly(oxypropylene), both as a rotated Fischer projections (left) and as a zigzag projection (right). [image2]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0409"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Usage of the term racemo diad for describing structures with a symmetrically-constituted connecting group is no longer acceptable because of the potential for confusion with the similar term used in organic chemistry to describe small molecules.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>r diad</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09587 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09587">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Regular macromolecule having a configurational repeating unit comprising four configurational base units, the first two stereogenic units of which are identical and enantiomorphic to the last two.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2019-0409"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A heterotactic polypropylene can be depicted by a rotated Fischer Projection (top) and zigzag projection (bottom) as follows. The configurational repeating unit, a mrm tetrad, is shown within brackets. The configurational repeating unit can be equally well defined as a rmr tetrad. </rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A macromolecule consisting only of m and r diads in alternating sequence (i.e. mr and rm triads) is heterotactic. The configurational repeating unit consists of four configurational base units.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The prefix ht- is recommended to indicate a heterotactic macromolecule.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>heterotactic macromolecule</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09585"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09586"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09588 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09588">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Asymmetric polymerization in which, starting from a mixture of enantiomeric monomer molecules, only one enantiomer is polymerized.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-1007"/>
        <rdfs:label>asymmetric enantiomer-differentiating polymerization</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09589"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09589 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09589">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymerization that proceeds in an unsymmetrical manner in terms of chirality under the influence of chiral features present in one or more components of the reaction system.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-1007"/>
        <rdfs:label>asymmetric polymerization</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09590 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09590">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>The Bernoullian distribution is a discrete distribution having two possible outcomes labelled by \(n = 0\) and \(n = 1\), in which \(n = 1\) (&apos;success&apos;) occurs with probability $p$ and \(n = 0\) (&apos;failure&apos;) occurs with probability \(q = 1 - p\), where $0 \lt p \lt 1$. It is a special case of a binomial distribution corresponding to a random distribution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-1007"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Since enantiomers have the same reactivity, this statistic applies to units in the case of the polycondensation of mixtures of (\(R\)) and (\(S\)) lactic acid, and to pairs of units in the case of the ring-opening polymerization of mixtures of (\(S,S\)) and (\(R,R\)) lactides.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Bernoullian distribution</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09591 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09591">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Conversion of a substance into biomass by biochemical processes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-1007"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Lactic acid-based polyesters are not biodegradable, but they can be bioassimilated, since the ultimate degradation by-products are lactic acid metabolites.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>bioassimilation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09592 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09592">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Composed of or derived from, in whole or in part, biological products derived from the biomass (including plant, animal, and marine or forestry materials).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-1007"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Lactic acids are obtained from the fermentation of natural products, such as corn or sugars, or from oil-based chemistry. Only the lactic acid-based polymers derived from natural sources are biobased.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>biobased</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09593 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09593">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Qualifier for a substance or device that undergoes biodegradation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-1007"/>
        <rdfs:comment>As hydrolytic degradation leads to low molar mass end-products that can be biodegraded, excreted via kidneys and lungs after biomineralisation, or biorecycled by living organisms or micro-organisms, lactic acid-based polyesters are bioresorbable.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>High molecular mass lactic acid-based polyesters are not degraded by animal cells or environmental micro-organisms. Therefore, the use of the term biodegradable is inappropriate and misleading, unless biodegradation under particular conditions is proved. The correct qualification is &quot;hydrolytically degradable&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>biodegradable</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09596"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09594 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09594">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Surface degradation resulting from the action of cells.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-1007"/>
        <rdfs:comment>High molecular mass lactic acid-based polyesters are not bioerodible.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Some non-human enzymes like proteinase K can cause erosion of lactic acid-based polyesters due to surface enzymatic degradation of lactic acid-based polyesters.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When hydrophobic compounds are present in a lactic acid-based polymeric matrix, water uptake can be so low that surface hydrolysis is faster than the bulk hydrolysis and causes erosion that can be confused with enzymatic erosion or bioerosion.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>bioerosion</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09595 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09595">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Qualifier for actions or substances that are connected to living systems.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-1007"/>
        <rdfs:comment>This qualifier can be used for lactic acid-based polyesters when appropriate according to origin or application.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>biorelated</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09596 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09596">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Qualifier used to indicate that a compound or a device is bioresorbed, i.e., totally eliminated or bioassimilated by an animal or a human body.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-1007"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Lactic acid-based polyesters are bioresorbable after preliminary hydrolytic degradation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>bioresorbable</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09597 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09597">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process of biological decomposition of organic matter performed by microorganisms, mostly bacteria and fungi.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-1007"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Lactic acid-based polyesters can be composted industrially provided that they are first hydrolytically degraded at a temperature above the glass transition temperature \(T_{\rm{g}}\), otherwise degradation is very slow.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Packaging made of semi-crystalline lactic acid-based polyesters may not be compostable in a home compost bin.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>composting</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09598 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09598">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Qualifier to a substance that can undergo physical and/or chemical deleterious changes of some properties, especially of integrity, under stress conditions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-1007"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Lactic acid-based polyesters are degradable polymers and more precisely hydrolytically degradable polymers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>degradable</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09599 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09599">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Degradation or biodegradation occurring at different rates depending on the location within a matrix.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-1007"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Heterogeneous degradation is observed when the soluble degradation by-products that participate in the autocatalysis of the hydrolytic degradation of lactic acid-based polyester matrices are degraded faster than they diffuse outside.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>heterogeneous degradation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09600 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09600">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Degradation that occurs at the same rate regardless of the location within a polymeric item.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-1007"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Homogeneous degradation occurs in aqueous media when soluble degradation by-products that participate in the autocatalyzed hydrolysis diffuse faster out of the matrix than they degrade inside. Homogeneous degradation is also observed in air or in a medium that is not a solvent for degradation by-products, because these products cannot diffuse to such outer media.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>homogeneous degradation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09601 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09601">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Copolymer formed of constitutional repeating units that are constitutionally identical but configurationally different.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-1007"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In transesterification and hydrolytic degradation reactions of lactic-acid stereocopolymers, the intrachain ester bonds in \(m\), \(r\), and heterochiral short sequences exhibit different reactivity.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>stereocopolymer</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09602 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09602">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Constitutional repeating unit of connectivity three or more connected at least to three other constitutional repeating units.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In addition, a branch constitutional repeating unit may also be connected to one or more end-groups.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>branch constitutional repeating unit</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09603 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09603">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Constitutional unit in a dendrimer molecule, from which the dendron(s) emanate(s).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In a hyperbranched macromolecule obtained by the \({\rm{AB}}_{x}\mbox{-}\rm{type}\) monomer + \({\rm{B}}_{y}\mbox{-}\rm{type}\) monomer polycondensation approach, where \(\rm{A}\) and \(\rm{B}\) refer to mutually reactive groups, the terms core molecule (not acceptable) and core unit refer to the \({\rm{B}}_{y}\mbox{-}\rm{type}\) unit, even though the structure of the macromolecule does not comprise dendrons.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Occasionally, a dendrimer molecule may consist of two dendrons directly connected together.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The structure of the core unit may be identical to that of the constitutional repeating units.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>core unit</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09605"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09611"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09604 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09604">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Parameter characterizing the branched architecture of a hyperbranched polymer, which is equal to unity for a polymer that contains no linear or semi-dendritic constitutional repeating unit and is equal to 0 for a polymer that contains no dendritic or semi-dendritic constitutional repeating unit.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Several definitions of the degree of branching have been proposed for hyperbranched polymers obtained by polycondensation of \({\rm{AB}}_2\mbox{-}\rm{type}\) monomers and for those obtained by polycondensation of more complex monomer systems. The definition by Hölter et al. is: \(D_{\rm{br}} = 2 x_{\rm{D}}/(2 x_{\rm{D}} + x_{\rm{L}})\), where \(x_{\rm{D}}\) and \(x_{\rm{L}}\) are the mole fractions of dendritic and linear units, respectively.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The degree of branching of a dendrimer is equal to one. However, a polymer with the degree of branching equal to one is not necessarily a dendrimer. It can be a hyperbranched polymer, since the degree of branching does not per se take into account the regularity and symmetry of the molecules that compose it.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The degree of branching of a linear polymer is equal to 0.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>degree of branching</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09610"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09618"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09622"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09629"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09605 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09605">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Molecule consisting of one or more dendrons emanating from a single constitutional unit.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:comment>An example of a formula of a dendrimer molecule is given in the figure in core unit.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Occasionally, a dendrimer molecule may consist of two dendrons directly connected together.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dendrimer molecule</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09603"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09611"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09606 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09606">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Block macromolecule in which at least one block is a dendron.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:label>dendritic block macromolecule</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09611"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09607 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09607">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Constitutional repeating unit of connectivity $c \ge 3$ connected to $c$ other constitutional repeating units.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Dendritic constitutional repeating units belong to the class of branch constitutional repeating units.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Examples of dendritic constitutional repeating units of connectivity $c = 3$ connected to three other constitutional repeating units are given in figures in dendron direction and hyperbranched oligomer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dendritic constitutional repeating unit</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09602"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09611"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09612"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09616"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09608 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09608">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Graft macromolecule in which at least one of the grafts contains a dendron.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A dendritic graft macromolecule is sometimes referred to as a dendronised macromolecule.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dendritic graft macromolecule</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09611"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09609 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09609">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Macromolecule containing at least one dendron.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:label>dendritic macromolecule</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09611"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09610 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09610">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Substance composed of dendritic macromolecules.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:label>dendritic polymer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09606"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09611 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09611">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Part of a molecule with only one free valence, comprising exclusively dendritic and terminal constitutional repeating units and in which each path from the free valence to any end-group comprises the same number of constitutional repeating units.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A dendrimer molecule comprising only one dendron is sometimes referred to as dendron, monodendron or functionalised dendron. The use of the terms ‘dendron’ or ‘monodendron’ in the meaning of molecule or substance is not acceptable.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>For the purpose of determining the nature of constitutional repeating units the free valence is treated as a connection to a CRU.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In a dendron, macrocycles of constitutional units are absent. Example of a regular dendron, highlighting dendritic constitutional repeating units, terminal constitutional repeatingunits, end-groups, the dendron direction and the free valence. The preferred constitutional repeating unit is chosen such that the branch points are at its end in the dendron direction. </rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dendron</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09605"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09607"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09612"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09630"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09612 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09612">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>For a constitutional repeating unit of a dendron, the direction from the free valence to the end-groups.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:comment>See the figure in dendron.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dendron direction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09611"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09613 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09613">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Core unit of a dendrimer molecule that comprises only one dendron</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:label>focal unit</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09603"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09605"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09611"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09614 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09614">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Set of constitutional repeating units separated from the free valence of a dendron by the same number of constitutional repeating units.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Generations are numbered consecutively starting from the constitutional repeating unit bearing the free valence. Example of a four-generation dendron, highlighting constitutional repeating units of generations 1 to 4 and the end-groups. The preferred constitutional repeating unit is chosen such that the branch points are at its end in the dendron direction.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>generation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09611"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09615 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09615">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Highly branched macromolecule, the constitutional repeating units of which are in substantial part branch constitutional repeating units and terminal constitutional repeating units.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For the purpose of determining the type of constitutional repeating units (dendritic, semi-dendritic, terminal or linear CRUs) in a hyperbranched macromolecule, the core unit should be treated as a CRU.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Hyperbranched macromolecules, unlike dendrimer molecules, usually comprise a number of randomly distributed linear constitutional repeating units. Examples of hyperbranched molecules with a core unit. Terminal, linear and dendritic constitutional repeating units and the core unit are circled. The preferred constitutional repeating unit is chosen such that the branch points are at its end in the dendron direction.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>hyperbranched macromolecule</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09602"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09603"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09605"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09607"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09610"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09622"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09629"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09630"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09616 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09616">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Substance composed of hyperbranched oligomer molecules.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:label>hyperbranched oligomer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09617"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09617 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09617">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Highly branched oligomer molecule, the constitutional repeating units of which are in substantial part branch constitutional repeating units and terminal constitutional repeating units.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:label>hyperbranched oligomer molecule</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09602"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09615"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09630"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09618 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09618">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Substance composed of hyperbranched macromolecules.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;dendrigraft&quot; is sometimes used for hyperbranched polymers of low molar-mass-dispersity. The use of &quot;dendrigraft&quot; is not acceptable.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>hyperbranched polymer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09615"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09619 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09619">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Substance composed of irregular dendrimer molecules.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:label>irregular dendrimer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09620"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09620 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09620">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Dendrimer molecule that contains either different types of regular dendrons or at least one irregular dendron.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:label>irregular dendrimer molecule</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09605"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09621"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09628"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09621 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09621">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Dendron composed of either different types of constitutional units, different types of end-groups or any combination of these.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:label>irregular dendron</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09611"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09622 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09622">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Constitutional repeating unit connected to exactly two other constitutional repeating units.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A linear constitutional repeating unit may be connected to one or more end-groups.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>End-groups attached to non-terminal constitutional repeating units are sometimes termed side groups. The use of the term side group in this meaning is not acceptable.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>linear constitutional repeating unit</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09615"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09623 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09623">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of constitutional repeating units on the path from the free valence to any end-group of a dendron.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The number of generations \(G\) is a cardinal number. A dendron with \(G = n\) generations of constitutional repeating units should be called \(n\mbox{-}\rm{generation}\) dendron. The terms &quot;dendron of \(n^{\rm{th}}\) generation&quot; or &quot;\(n^{\rm{th}}\) generation dendron&quot; are not acceptable.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>number of generations</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09611"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09624 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09624">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of generation of the dendrons of a regular dendrimer molecule.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:label>number of generations</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09611"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09623"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09627"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09625 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09625">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of generations of the hypothetical dendrimer molecule that would present the same ratio of constitutional repeating units to core unit.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In hyperbranched polymers obtained by the \({\rm{AB}}_{x}\mbox{-}\rm{type}\) monomer + \({\rm{B}}_{y}\mbox{-}\rm{type}\) monomer polycondensation approach, where \(\rm{A}\) and \(\rm{B}\) represent mutually reactive groups, the number of pseudo-generations \(n\) can be calculated by solving the equation: \[r = y(x^n - 1)/(x - 1)\], where \(r\) is the mole ratio of \({\rm{AB}}_{x}\mbox{-}\rm{type}\) monomer to \({\rm{B}}_{y}\mbox{-}\rm{type}\) monomer in the initial monomer mixture. See the figure for an example of \({\rm{AB}}_2\) and \({\rm{B}}_{3}\mbox{-}\rm{type}\) monomers (\(x = 2\) and \(y = 3\)). Idealized representation of an aliphatic hyperbranched polyester macromolecule obtained by one-step bulk polyesterification of 3-hydroxy-2-(hydroxymethyl)-2-methylpropanoic acid (\({\rm{AB}}_{2}\mbox{-}\rm{type}\) monomer) and 2-ethyl-2-(hydroxymethyl)propane-1,3-diol (\({\rm{B}}_{3}\mbox{-}\rm{type}\) molecule). </rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The number of pseudo-generations can be a decimal number, while the number of generations of a dendrimer molecule is an integer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The use of the terms generation and number of generations for a hyperbranched polymer is not acceptable.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>number of pseudo-generations</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09603"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09605"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09614"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09618"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09624"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09626 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09626">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Substance composed of regular dendrimer molecules.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:label>regular dendrimer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09627"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09627 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09627">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Dendrimer molecule comprising only identical regular dendrons.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A dendrimer molecule comprising only one dendron is a regular dendrimer molecule.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>regular dendrimer molecule</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09605"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09611"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09628"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09628 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09628">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Dendron composed of only one type of constitutional repeating units and of only one type of end-groups.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:label>regular dendron</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09611"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09629 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09629">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Constitutional repeating unit of connectivity \(c \ge 4\) connected to more than two but less than \(c\) other constitutional repeating units.Example of a hyperbranched macromolecule part with a semi-dendritic constitutional repeating unit of connectivity \(c = 4\) (circled), connected to three other constitutional repeating units and to one hydroxymethyl end-group. The preferred constitutional repeating unit is chosen such that the branch points are at its end in the dendron direction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:label>semi-dendritic constitutional repeating unit</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09630 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09630">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Constitutional repeating unit connected to only one other constitutional repeating unit.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1217"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A terminal constitutional repeating unit may be connected to one or more end-groups.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>terminal constitutional repeating unit</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09611"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09615"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09631 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09631">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Net attractive interaction between an electrophilic region associated with a chalcogen atom in a molecular entity and a nucleophilic region in another, or the same, molecular entity.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0713"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ChB</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A typical chalcogen bond is denoted by the three dots in \(\ce{R–Ch···A}\), where \(\ce{Ch}\) is the \(\ce{ChB}\) donor, being any chalcogen atom (possibly hypervalent) having an electrophilic (electron-poor) region, \(\rm{R}\) is the remainder of the molecular entity \(\ce{R–Ch}\) containing the \(\ce{ChB}\) donor, and \(\rm{A}\) is the \(\ce{ChB}\) acceptor and is typically a molecular entity possessing at least one nucleophilic (electron-rich) region.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Chalcogen atoms can concurrently form one or more than one chalcogen bond.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Chalcogen atoms of a molecular entity give rise to a variety of interactions with different electronic and geometric features. The term chalcogen bond must not be used for interactions where the chalcogen (frequently oxygen) functions as a nucleophile. The attractive intermolecular interaction between the oxygen atoms of an alcohol or a carboxylic acid and the acidic proton of another alcohol or carboxylic acid molecule is not a chalcogen bond, it is a hydrogen bond; the attractive intermolecular interaction between 1,4-dithiane and iodine atoms of diiodine or triiodomethane is not a chalcogen bond, it is a halogen bond; the attractive intermolecular interaction between bismuth and selenium atoms of tris(selenophen-2-yl)-bismuthane is not a chalcogen bond, it is a pnictogen bond (PnB); and the attractive intramolecular interactions between tin and sulphur atoms in crystalline 2,10-dichloro-2,10-dimethyl-2,10-distanna-6-thiaundecane is not a chalcogen bond, it is a tetrel bond (\(\ce{TtB}\)).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>chalcogen bond</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09631"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ChB</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09632 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09632">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sub-field of analytical chemistry dealing with analysis of biomolecules.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Bioanalytical chemistry covers the identification (identity, sequence), characterization (properties such as polarity and charge, structure, folding, intermolecular interactions), and quantification and monitoring (stability, dynamics, fragmentation, degradation, metabolism, and others).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>bioanalytical chemistry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09633"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09633 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09633">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Molecule of biological origin.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Modern bioanalytical chemistry shifts its attention to the analysis and investigation of biomacromolecules, such as nucleic acids, proteins, polysaccharides, and others.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Most biomolecules are organic compounds present in living organisms. They may also include exogenous molecules modified by metabolism. Some typical biomolecules representing subjects of bioanalytical chemistry are amino acids, peptides, polypeptides, proteins, nucleotides, nucleic acids, carbohydrates, glycoconjugates, lipids, antibodies, haptenes, and receptors, as well as primary and secondary metabolites.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>biomolecule</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09632"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09809"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09634 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09634">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Macromolecule (including proteins, nucleic acids, and polysaccharides) formed by living organisms.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>From a biological standpoint, a biomacromolecule can also be defined as a biomolecule of high relative molecular mass.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Non-natural biomacromolecule-like synthetic molecules (such as poly(lactic acid)), and nucleic acid analogues (such as peptide nucleic acids or locked nucleic acids) are also used in bioanalysis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>biomacromolecule</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09714"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09635 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09635">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Fluid that surrounds the foetus in the amniotic sac.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A specimen is obtained by a technique called amniocentesis, in which a long needle is inserted into the amniotic sac through the abdominal wall, through which fluid is withdrawn.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>amniotic fluid</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09636 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09636">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Fluid that circulates through the heart, arteries, capillaries, and veins.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Circulating blood is a heterogeneous fluid consisting of a clear, slightly yellow liquid (native plasma) and three groups of suspended, formed elements: red blood cells, or erythrocytes (about \(\pu{99\%}\) of the blood cells), white blood cells, or leukocytes, and blood platelets, or thrombocytes. When no anticoagulant is added, normal blood withdrawn from the circulation forms a clot due the polymerization of fibrinogen to fibrin. On standing, the clot retracts, expressing serum which differs from plasma chiefly in that it contains no fibrinogen.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>blood</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09637"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09637 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09637">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Any cellular element of the blood, including erythrocytes, leucocytes, and platelets.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>haematopoietic cell</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>hematocyte</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>hemocyte</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A blood cell is produced by hematopoiesis and is normally found in blood. In mammals, these fall into three general categories: red blood cells (erythrocytes), white blood cells (leukocytes), and platelets (thrombocytes).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>blood cell</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09636"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09638 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09638">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of red blood cells and white blood cells per unit volume in a specimen of venous blood.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>blood cell count</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09636"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09637"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09639 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09639">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Red cell phenotypes classified by their antigenic structural characteristics, which are under the control of various allelic genes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The cell membrane properties that provide the specific antigenicity of the blood groups are called agglutinogens, as they agglutinate or clump in the presence of their specific antibody.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>blood group</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09640 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09640">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Liquids originating from the bodies of living or dead humans, animals, and other organisms.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>They include fluids that are excreted or secreted from the body.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>body fluids</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09641 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09641">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Clear, colourless fluid that fills spaces within and around the central nervous system.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CSF</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>CSF originates from plasma by a biological ultrafiltration process. Specimens are obtained by a lumbar puncture (i.e. a spinal tap).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>cerebrospinal fluid</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09641"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CSF</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09642 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09642">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Blood contained in the vessels of the umbilical cord at the time of birth.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Cord blood contains stem cells.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>cord blood</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09643 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09643">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Yellowish, slightly basic fluid circulating through the lymphatic system.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Lymph is similar to plasma and contains white blood cells.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>lymph</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09637"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09644 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09644">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Blood present in such small amounts that its presence can be ascertained only by chemical analysis or by spectroscopic or microscopic examination; particularly the blood found in stools.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>occult blood</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09636"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09645 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09645">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Blood obtained from parts of the body that are located at some distance from the heart.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>peripheral blood</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09636"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09646 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09646">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sample of blood, serum, or plasma from which all proteins have been removed by chemical or physical denaturation, dialysis, ultrafiltration, or solvent extraction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>protein-free filtrate</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09636"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09647 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09647">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Clear, viscous secretion from the parotid, submaxillary, sublingual, and smaller mucous glands in the cavity of the mouth.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Saliva contains many important substances, including electrolytes, mucus, antibacterial compounds and various enzymes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>saliva</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09648 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09648">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Soft outer covering of vertebrates.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>skin</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09649 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09649">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Seminal fluid containing male reproductive cells.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>sperm</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09650 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09650">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Fluid excreted by the sweat glands in the skin of mammals during perspiration.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>sweat</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09651 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09651">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Clear salty liquid that is secreted by the lachrymal gland of the eye to lubricate the surface between the eyeball and eyelid and to wash away irritants.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>tears</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09652 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09652">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Group of biological cells that perform a specific function.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A cellular organizational level intermediate between cells and complete organs.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>tissue</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09653 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09653">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Fluid containing metabolic products that is excreted by the kidneys, stored in the bladder, and normally discharged by way of the urethra.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>urine</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09654 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09654">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Discipline encompassing the development and utilisation of computational tools to store, analyse, and interpret biological data.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Among the most important bioinformatics tools are specialised databases with genetic and protein sequences accompanied by related data (biological function, cell localisation, etc.) and including search, alignment, and sorting algorithms or graphical modelling.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>bioinformatics</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09655 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09655">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Component of biological origin that translates information from the biochemical domain into a physical or chemical domain with a defined selectivity.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>biochemical receptor</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>biocomponent (at a biosensor)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A biocatalytic recognition element is based on a biocatalytic reaction. Five types of biocatalyst are commonly used: (a) Enzyme (mono-or multi-enzyme): the most common and well-developed recognition system; (b) whole cells (micro-organisms, such as bacteria, fungi, eukaryotic cells or yeast) or cell organelles or particles (mitochondria, cell walls); and (c) tissue (plant or animal tissue slice). (d) Ribozymes, and (e) DNAzymes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A biocomplexing or bioaffinity recognition element is based on the binding interaction of the analyte with macromolecules or organised molecular assemblies that have either been isolated from their original biological environment or engineered.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The main purpose of the biological recognition element is to provide a biosensor or other system with a high degree of selectivity for the kind of quantity to be measured.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Typical biological recognition elements include biocatalysts, immune receptors, nucleic acids, and others. Biologically-derived recognition elements, such as oligopeptides, oligosaccharides, and peptide nucleic acids are also of great interest.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>biological recognition element</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09656 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09656">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Decomposition of organic materials (e.g. starch, sugars, proteins, lignin, hemicelluloses, etc.) with specific enzymes or microbes in which the enzyme converts a high relative molecular mass compound into lower relative molecular mass species.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>enzymatic decomposition</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09633"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09658 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09658">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Breaking down of a cell.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A fluid containing the contents of lysed cells is called a &quot;lysate&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Lysis may be caused by physical, (bio)chemical, or biological means.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>lysis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09659 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09659">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Two-dimensional arrangement of a device for analysis of sub-microlitre sample volume.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A collection of molecules spatially addressed on a surface within features that have micrometer dimensions. Planar device (flat plane) used for off-line determination and identification of biocompounds.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>This screening format is a direct offshoot of genomic microarray technologies and makes use of ultra-low-volume miniaturization provided by nanodispensing technologies.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>microarray</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09812"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09832"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09660 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09660">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Test of the purity of the sample that will be subjected to detailed biochemical analysis.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Microelectrophoresis belongs to methods used for sample quality testing.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sample quality control</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09747"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09661 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09661">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Strength of association between a single antibody and a single epitope of antigen.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>binding affinity</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Affinity of the immunochemical reaction: \[\ce{Antibody + Antigen -&gt; Antibody{-}Antigen}\] is characterised by the affinity (binding, association) constant \[K_{\rm{A}} = [\ce{Antibody{-}Antigen}]/[\ce{Antibody}][\ce{Antigen}]\]</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The average binding constant reflects a population of antibody molecules (polyclonal antibody).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>affinity</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09662 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09662">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Type of agglutination in which particulate and soluble antigen compete for soluble antibody.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Typically, it is soluble antigen in the test medium which reacts first with the soluble antibody, inhibiting the agglutination of indicator particles. With viral hemagglutination inhibition assays, host antibodies resulting from a specific infection are the most common forms of agglutination inhibition assays. In this case, viral-specific antibodies block the sites on the virus that agglutinate erythrocytes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>agglutination inhibition</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09663 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09663">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Part of the structure of an antigen molecule that is responsible for specific interaction with antibody molecules evoked by the same or a similar antigen.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>epitome</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>antigenic determinant</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09664 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09664">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Net affinity of all binding sites of all antibodies in the antiserum under specified physicochemical reaction conditions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>avidity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09661"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09665 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09665">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Amount of a ligand that binds to a receptor expressed in operational units.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Binding capacity is determined by saturating the receptor and then measuring the amount of complex forming ligand. Parameters like pH and ionic strength influence the capacity value, which will be changed depending on the experimental conditions.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Operational units are defined in contrast to the quantitative mass units of the affinity constant.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>binding capacity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09661"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09666 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09666">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coating of an immunospecific surface (e.g. serum, tissues, cells, biological macromolecules, biosensor surface) to form an active cover with selectivity or an inert layer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Blocking is a pre-treatment method with blocking agents (e.g. monovalent Fab fragment, i.e. the antigen-binding fragment of an immunoglobulin molecule; mono or divalent antibodies; organic ligands; metals).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>blocking</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09634"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09652"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09667 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09667">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Amount of analyte bound by a label divided by amount of unbound (free) analyte</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>B/F</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>bound/free ratio</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09667"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>B/F</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09668 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09668">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Array of serum proteins (some of which are enzymes) that become sequentially activated after the first member of the series is activated by either antigen-antibody complexes or microbial products.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>complement</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09669 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09669">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Conjugate that has an enzyme bound covalently.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>enzyme conjugate</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09670 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09670">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Agglutination reactions in which the particles used are erythrocytes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Hemagglutination may be either direct, in which erythrocyte antigens are reactants, or indirect (passive) for coated antigen or, in the case of reverse (passive) assays, coated antibody. One of the most common uses of hemagglutination is to quantitate the number of hemagglutinating viruses (cf. influenza) or their soluble hemagglutinating surface subunits. It can also be used for blood typing.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>hemagglutination</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09671 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09671">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Technique that uses antibodies (or other specific ligands in related techniques) to identify target proteins among a number of unrelated protein species.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The techniques allow the detection of proteins from complex mixtures separated by gel chromatography and then transferred to a membrane. After incubation of the attached product from membrane with labelled antibody, the target proteins become visible.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>immunoblotting</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09672 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09672">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>The extent to which the immunoassay responds only to (all subsets of) a specific analyte and not to other substances of similar structure that are present in the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>specificity</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>immunochemical specificity</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09673 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09673">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Product of an antigen-antibody reaction that may also contain components of the complement.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Formation of the immunocomplex is characterised by the binding constant</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>immunocomplex</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09661"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09668"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09674 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09674">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Classification of immunoglobulins based on antigenic and structural differences of the heavy peptide chain.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Immunoglobulin subclass (isotype) represents the subdivision of an immunoglobulin class based on structural and antigenic differences in the heavy peptide chain. Four human \(\ce{IgG}\) subclasses and two \(\ce{IgA}\) subclasses have currently been recognised; \(\ce{IgM}\) subclasses have been postulated; \(\ce{IgD}\) and \(\ce{IgE}\) subclasses are unknown.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>There are five classes: \(\ce{IgG}\), \(\ce{IgA}\), \(\ce{IgM}\), \(\ce{IgD}\), and \(\ce{IgE}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>immunoglobulin class</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09675 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09675">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Isolation and concentration of an antigen by forming a precipitate with an antibody.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Immunoprecipitation requires that the antibody be coupled to a solid substrate at some point in the procedure.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>immunoprecipitation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09676 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09676">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Kinetic property of the immune response caused by an antigen.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>immunoreactivity</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09677 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09677">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Biosensor where one of the immunointeracting partners (an antibody or an antigen as the analyte) is immobilised on the surface of a physicochemical transducer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The generated signal either directly reflects the amount of formed immunocomplex or corresponds to the amount of label.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>immunosensor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09673"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09678 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09678">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Agglutination in which antigen is first immobilised artificially onto the particulate surfaces, either by physical adsorption or by chemical and/or immunochemical linkers.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>passive agglutination</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Antigen-loaded particles can be used to detect the presence of the corresponding specific agglutinins in sample. Agglutination results by cross-linking of the antigen-bearing particles onto an extensive antigen-antibody lattice (i.e. in detectable agglutination of the particles).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>indirect agglutination</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09679 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09679">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Migration of the sample zone driven by capillary forces in a porous carrier strip.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>During migration the dissolved tracer becomes distributed between capture and control zones with immobilised antibodies.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The amount of the label compound in both zones depends on the concentration of the compound in the studied sample.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>lateral flow test</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09680 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09680">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Arising from a single clone of cells.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In the case of immunoglobulin, &quot;monoclonal&quot; refers to its origin.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Usually, the monoclonal antibody is of a single immunoglobulin class containing only one light chain type of either the K or L variety. It also refers to all antibody molecules having identical physical-chemical characteristics and antibody specificity. Monoclonal antibodies have very restricted structural diversity and they are homogeneous compared with polyclonal antibodies.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>monoclonal</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09681 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09681">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Immunoreactivity of an antiserum with its designated antigen.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In practice, true monospecificity to naturally occurring antigens does not occur in antisera produced by the immunization of the intact animal. An attempt is made to reduce the level of unwanted specificities below that which will interfere with the intended use of a particular immunochemical test.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>monospecificity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09672"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09676"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09682 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09682">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Arising from multiple, different clones.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Polyclonal antibodies (pAbs) are antibodies that are secreted by different B cell lineages within the body. They are a collection of immunoglobulin molecules that react against a specific antigen, each identifying a different epitope.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polyclonal</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09683 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09683">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Modification of the indirect agglutination assay, where the particles are coated with antibody.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>These antibody-laden particles become probes for detecting specific antigens in the sample material. The presence of the relevant antigen will result in the agglutination of particles.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>reverse passive agglutination</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09684 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09684">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Immunoassay using chemical or immunochemical binding of analyte to a solid phase carrying antibody and the immunochemical binding of a second (labelled) reagent to the analyte.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>two-site, noncompetitive immunoassay</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>This is one of the most common assays in clinical practice.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sandwich immunoassay</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09685 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09685">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process of producing multiple copies of a deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) sequence by the repeated copying of a DNA segment.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>amplification of DNA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>amplification of deoxyribonucleic acid</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09686 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09686">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Pair of two nucleobases, consisting of one purine and one pyrimidine base, held together by hydrogen bonds.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>bp</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA), guanine typically pairs with cytosine and adenine typically pairs with thymine. In ribonucleic acid (RNA), adenine typically pairs with uracil.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>base pair</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09710"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09686"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>bp</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09687 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09687">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Method for determining the nucleotide sequence.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>base sequence analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09712"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09688 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09688">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Computer algorithm that finds regions of local similarity between deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) or protein sequences.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>BLAST</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>basic local alignment search tool</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09688"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>BLAST</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09689 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09689">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Specialised deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) technology for the production of multiple exact copies of a single gene or another segment of DNA.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>cloning</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09690 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09690">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) molecule into which another DNA fragment of appropriate size can be integrated without the loss of its capacity for self-replication.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>cloning vector</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09691 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09691">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nucleotide sequence that can form a double-stranded structure with another deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) sequence by following base-pairing (see base pair) rules.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>complementary sequence</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09686"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09712"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09692 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09692">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>High-quality, low-error, gap-free deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) sequence of an entire genome of an organism.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>complete genome sequence</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09693 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09693">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Alteration in the deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) chemical structure resulting from interactions with physical or chemical agents occurring in the environment, generated in the organisms as by-products of metabolism, or used as therapeutics.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DNA damage</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>damage to DNA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Terms &quot;(product of) DNA damage&quot; (lesion, adduct) and &quot;mutation&quot; should not be intermingled. Mutations refer to hereditable changes in DNA sequence—substitutions, deletions, or insertions of (one or more) standard base pair(s) and are not synonyms to &quot;base mismatches&quot;. Importantly, DNA damage and its repair can promote the occurrence of mutations.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The main types of DNA damage include the interruption of the sugar-phosphate backbone (strand breaks) or the release of bases due to hydrolysis of N-glycosidic bonds (resulting in abasic sites), inter- and intra-strand crosslinks (such as pyrimidine dimers), and a variety of nucleobase lesions (adducts) resulting from reactions of DNA with a broad range of oxidants, alkylating compounds, and other agents.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>damage to deoxyribonucleic acid</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09694 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09694">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Loss of a part (one or more base pairs) of deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>deletion</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09686"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09695 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09695">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Type of pentose sugar that is incorporated in the structure of deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>deoxyribose</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09696 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09696">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Breaking and/or separation of large deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) molecules into smaller pieces (fragments).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DNA fragmentation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>fragmentation of DNA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>DNA fragmentation is also a consequence of damage to DNA.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>DNA fragmentation is usually achieved via cleavage with restriction endonucleases at specific sites or non-specifically by, for example, sonication.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>deoxyribonucleic acid fragmentation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09704"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09697 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09697">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Fraction of deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) having fragmented DNA.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DFI</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DNA fragmentation index</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>deoxyribonucleic acid fragmentation index</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09697"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>DFI</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09698 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09698">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ratio of longer fragments to shorter deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DNA integrity index</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>DNA strand integrity is measured by quantitative PCR (QPCR) using a real-time system.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Integrity index may be clinically usefully used as surrogate for detection of tumours.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>deoxyribonucleic acid integrity index</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09719"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09699 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09699">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Defect in the double stranded deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) structure in which the DNA double helix contains one (or more) non-standard base pairs.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DNA mismatch</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>mismatch in DNA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>multi-base mismatch</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>single mismatch</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>One mismatch is known as a single mismatch. Multiple mismatches are called multi-base mismatches.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>deoxyribonucleic acid mismatch</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09686"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09703"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09700 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09700">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Identification of individuals by matching characteristics of their genome deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) sequence.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DNA profiling</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Characteristics used include the frequency (or size) of tandem repeats in the genome.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>DNA profiles are at a lower resolution than a full genome DNA sequence, but this is adequate for forensic purposes, such as in parental testing and criminal investigations.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>deoxyribonucleic acid profiling</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09701 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09701">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Synthesis of new deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) strands using existing DNA as a template.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DNA replication</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>replication of DNA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>deoxyribonucleic acid replication</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09702 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09702">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Determination of nucleotide sequence (the deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) primary structure).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DNA sequencing</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>sequencing of DNA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Next-generation sequencing refers to non-Sanger-based high-throughput DNA sequencing technologies where millions of DNA strands can be sequenced in parallel.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>deoxyribonucleic acid sequencing</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09712"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09703 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09703">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>The twisted shape of two complementary strands of deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The complementary chains are held together by hydrogen bonds between pairs of the complementary nucleobases. The helical conformation is promoted by several relatively weak interactions, including hydrophobic (stacking) ones between the bases.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The double helix is the most frequent DNA structure formed by two complementary DNA strands. It can also be formed by two complementary strands of ribonucleic acid (RNA), one DNA and one RNA strand, as well of any of these with synthetic nucleic acids analogues, such as locked nucleic acid, LNA, or peptide nucleic acid, PNA.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>double helix</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09710"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09714"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09704 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09704">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Enzyme that cleaves nucleic acids by hydrolysing phosphodiester bonds between nucleotides within a nucleic acid molecule.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>endonuclease</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09705 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09705">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) molecule of foreign origin that has been introduced into an organism.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>exogenous DNA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>exogenous deoxyribonucleic acid</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09706 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09706">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Enzyme that cleaves nucleotides from free ends of a linear nucleic acid molecule.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>exonuclease</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09707 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09707">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process for converting a gene’s coded information into biomolecules present and operating in the cell.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>gene expression</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09708 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09708">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Technique involving hybridization of nucleic acids  used to study gene expression at the level of ribonucleic acid (RNA).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The Northern blot approach is preferred to Southern blot and Western blot.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Northern blot</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09707"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09728"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09789"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09709 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09709">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Single stranded (ss) oligonucleotides (mainly deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) or ribonucleic acid (RNA)) originating from in vitro selection, which, starting from random sequence libraries, optimise the nucleic acids for high-affinity binding to a given target.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In vitro selection is an iterative method mainly known as Systematic Evolution of Ligands by EXponential enrichment (SELEX).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nucleic acid aptamers</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09710 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09710">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Purine or pyrimidine nitrogenous base occurring in deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) or ribonucleic acid (RNA).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>nucleobase</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09717"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09718"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09711 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09711">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Product of chemical modification of a nucleobase.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>nucleobase lesion</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09693"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09710"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09712 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09712">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Order of nucleotides in a deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) or ribonucleic acid (RNA) molecule.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>base sequence</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>nucleotide sequence</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09713 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09713">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sequence of deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) or ribonucleic acid (RNA) nucleotides, located between the start-code sequence (initiation codon) and the stop-code sequence (termination codon), which is transcribed and translated to generate a protein.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ORF</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>open reading frame</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09713"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ORF</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09714 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09714">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Synthetic deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) mimic, which contains 2-aminoethylglycine linkages instead of the negatively charged phosphodiesteric backbone of oligonucleotides.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PNA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The PNA probes are particularly convenient for the detection of single-base mismatches (point mutations, SNPs), because the stability of DNA-PNA duplexes is strongly influenced by a single-base mismatch.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>peptide nucleic acid</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09699"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09714"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PNA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09715 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09715">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Enzyme that catalyses the synthesis of nucleic acids on pre-existing nucleic acid templates.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>polymerase</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09716 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09716">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Short pre-existing polynucleotide chain attached to a complementary stretch in template deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) to which new deoxyribonucleotides can be added by DNA polymerase.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>primer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09715"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09717 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09717">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nitrogen-containing, double-ring, basic compound that occurs in nucleic acids.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>purine</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09710"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09718 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09718">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nitrogen-containing, single-ring, basic compound that occurs in nucleic acids.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>pyrimidine</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09710"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09719 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09719">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymerase chain reaction to quantify target nucleotide sequences of interest.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>qPCR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>quantitative PCR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>quantitative polymerase chain reaction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09719"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>qPCR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09720 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09720">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Polymerase chain reaction where data are collected and monitored in real-time as the reaction proceeds.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>real-time PCR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Digital PCR is a new alternate method to conventional real-time PCR and quantitative PCR for absolute quantification and rare allele detection. Digital PCR works by partitioning a sample of deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) or complementary DNA into many individual, parallel PCR reactions; some of these reactions contain the target molecule (complementary DNA, positive) while others do not (negative). A single molecule can be amplified a million-fold or more.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>real-time polymerase chain reaction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09719"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09721 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09721">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) molecules joined together using recombinant deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) technology.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>recombinant DNA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>recombinant deoxyribonucleic acid</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09722 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09722">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process by which genetic material is broken and joined to other genetic material, which can lead to a rearrangement of genetic sequences.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>recombination</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09723 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09723">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Variation between individuals in deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) fragment sizes cut by specific restriction enzymes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RFLP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>restriction fragment length polymorphism</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09723"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>RFLP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09724 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09724">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Enzyme used to form a complementary deoxyribonucleic acid sequence (cDNA) from a ribonucleic acid (RNA) template.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>reverse transcriptase</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09725 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09725">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Method for determining a nucleotide sequence of deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) based on incorporating chain-terminating dideoxynucleotides.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The method is named after Frederick Sanger (1918–2013, awarded the Nobel Prize in 1958 and 1980).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Sanger sequencing</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09712"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09726 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09726">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Method used for determining the order of bases in long DNA using sequencing of deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) broken up randomly into numerous small segments.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>shotgun method</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09727 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09727">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Variant of nucleotide sequence of deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) in which the purine or pyrimidine base of a single nucleotide is replaced by another such base.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SNP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>point mutation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>SNP is the most common type of change in DNA.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>SNPs occur normally throughout a human DNA once in every 300 nucleotides on average, which means there are roughly 10 million SNPs in any typical human genome. They can act as biological (diagnostic) markers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>single nucleotide polymorphism</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09712"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09717"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09718"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09727"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SNP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09728 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09728">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Technique based on deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) hybridization used for the detection of a DNA fragment of specific nucleotide sequence.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>Southern blot</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09712"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09729 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09729">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Interruption of the sugar-phosphate backbone of a polynucleotide chain.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>strand break</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09730 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09730">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Multiple consecutive copies of the same nucleotide sequence on a chromosome.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Tandem repeat sequences can be used as markers in physical mapping e.g. by DNA profiling.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>tandem repeat sequence</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09700"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09712"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09731 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09731">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Enzyme that directs the replication of telomeres.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>telomerase</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09732"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09732 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09732">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Repetitive nucleotide sequences present at the ends of a chromosome that protect the chromosome from deterioration.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In polymer sciences, an oligomer obtained by telomerization is often termed a telomer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>telomere</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09733 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09733">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Vector used to clone large deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) fragments.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>YAC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>yeast artificial chromosome</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09690"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09733"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>YAC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09734 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09734">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process occurring during gene expression that allows the generation of multiple protein molecules from one gene. It generally involves the processing of messenger ribonucleic acid (mRNA) via elimination of introns.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>alternative splicing</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09735 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09735">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Approach to identify proteins and characterise their amino acid sequences or post-translational modifications by sequential combination of proteolysis (enzymatic proteolysis and chemical proteolysis) prior to mass spectrometric analysis of the resulting proteolytic peptides.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In shotgun proteomics, the proteins in a mixture are digested, usually by a specific enzyme, and the resulting peptides are separated by liquid chromatography and identified by tandem mass spectrometry.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>bottom-up proteomics</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09726"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09776"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09786"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09736 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09736">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chemical method for C-terminal sequencing of proteins and peptides starting at their C-terminus.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>C-terminal analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09737"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09737 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09737">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>End of an amino acid chain (protein or polypeptide), terminated by a carboxylic group.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>C-terminus</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09738 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09738">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Bioinformatics analysis of proteomic mass spectrometric data used in order to identify a protein or its post-translational modification. It results in a list of candidates presented mostly in order of the decreasing statistical significance of the hit.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>database searching</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09654"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09739 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09739">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement method of two-dimensional gel electrophoresis used to study protein expression regulation by the differential staining of multiple gels representing different states of a proteome, preferably with fluorescent dyes (e.g. \(\ce{Cy{3}}\), \(\ce{Cy{5}}\), \(\ce{Cy{2}}\)).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DIGE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The resulting stained gels are analysed using 2D image analysis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>difference gel electrophoresis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09788"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09739"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>DIGE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09740 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09740">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chemical reduction of protein disulfide bonds.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Reductants, such as tributylphosphine (TBP), 2-sulfanylethan-1-ol (BME), or dithiothreitol (DTT) are used.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>disulfide bond cleavage</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09741 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09741">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Method of N-terminal analysis in which the N-terminal amino acid residue is labelled and cleaved from the peptide without affecting other peptide bonds in the peptide chain.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The method is named after Pehr Victor Edman (1916–1977).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Edman degradation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09753"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09742 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09742">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ion fragmentation technique in mass spectrometry-driven proteomics based on the transfer of low energy electrons to the molecules selected for fragmentation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ETD</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>ETD is particularly effective for analysis of post-translational modification.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electron-transfer dissociation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09780"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09742"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ETD</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09743 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09743">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ion fragmentation technique in mass spectrometry-driven proteomics that is a combination of electron-transfer dissociation (ETD) and higher-energy collision dissociation (HCD) in Kingdon trap instruments (Orbitrap™).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>EthcD</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The dual fragmentation allows generation of rich MS/MS spectra for peptide sequencing and post-translational modification (PTM) analysis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electron-transfer/higher-energy collision dissociation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09742"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09748"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09743"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>EthcD</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09744 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09744">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Soft ionisation technique in mass spectrometry-driven proteomics.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ESI</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Electrospray ionisation is optimal for ionising liquid eluates from separation methods, as well as for the direct introduction of liquid samples to fragmentation in a mass spectrometer. Ionisation is made by positive or negative mode, depending on the ability of the molecules to form cations or anions. During the ionisation process, a biomacromolecule obtains a number of charges roughly proportional to its relative molecular mass. Also, non-covalent protein complexes may be ionised with ESI.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrospray ionisation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09634"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09780"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09744"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ESI</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09745 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09745">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Field of study in proteomics that deals with the determination of changes in protein expression and regulation in response to different stimuli, internal or external. It also includes the analysis of post-translational modifications (e.g. phosphorylation, glycosylation, etc.), their identification, and their localisation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>differential proteomics</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>expression proteomics</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09746 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09746">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Field of study in proteomics studying protein interactions on different levels: protein-protein interactions (interactomics), the formation of protein complexes, and the interaction of proteins with other biomolecules.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>functional proteomics</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09633"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09780"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09872"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09747 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09747">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Separation technique used to separate or fractionate proteins and larger peptides according to their relative molecular mass using an electric field in polyacrylamide gel (PAGE).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PAGE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>1D PAGE has two basic modes: a denaturing (or SDS) PAGE to estimate relative molecular masses and a native PAGE to separate proteins as their native structures.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>gel electrophoresis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09747"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PAGE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09748 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09748">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ion fragmentation technique in mass spectrometry-driven proteomics used mostly in Kingdon trap (Orbitrap™) instruments. It does not have low mass cut-off of collision-induced dissociation (CID) and therefore is used for the tandem mass tag of stable isotope labels.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>HCD</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>higher-energy collision dissociation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09780"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09748"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>HCD</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09749 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09749">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Separation technique used to separate or fractionate proteins and peptides in a gel (or similar type of media) according to their isoelectric point.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>IEF</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>IEF is typically the first dimension of two-dimensional gel electrophoresis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>isoelectric focusing</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09747"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09749"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>IEF</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09750 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09750">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Protein analysis where an unknown protein is chemically or enzymatically cleaved into peptide fragments whose masses are determined by mass spectrometry. The peptide masses are compared to peptide masses calculated for known proteins in a database and analysed statistically to determine the best match.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>mass mapping</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>peptide mass fingerprinting</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>mass fingerprinting</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09751 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09751">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mass spectrometric method used for protein/peptide identification based on protein sequencing.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Using peptide ion fragments, the peptide sequence is read and a list of hit candidates is obtained in combination with database searching.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>MS/MS data based identification</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09738"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11547"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09752 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09752">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Non-gel step-wise multidimensional technique for separating and identifying individual components of complex protein and peptide mixtures. It combines fractionation of proteolytic peptides in steps on a strong cation exchanger (SAX) using ion exchange chromatography (IEC); each step uses higher elution force. Then, each fraction is separated on reversed phase liquid chromatography and analysed using mass spectrometry.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>MudPIT</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>multidimensional protein identification technology</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09752"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>MudPIT</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09753 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09753">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chemical method for N-terminal sequencing of proteins and peptides starting at their N-terminus.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>N-terminal analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09741"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09754"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09754 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09754">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>The end of an amino acid chain (protein or polypeptide), terminating in a free primary amine group.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>N-terminus</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09755 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09755">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Separation technique used to fractionate proteins and peptides into discrete liquid fractions according to their isoelectric point.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>OFFGEL™</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>off-gel isoelectric focusing</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09749"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09755"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>OFFGEL™</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09756 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09756">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Protein/peptide sequencing approach using overlapping complementary information on a sequence acquired using proteolysis in bottom-up proteomic analysis by at least two different proteolytic approaches.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>off peptide product ions</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09735"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11547"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09757 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09757">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Analytical process that derives an amino acid sequence of a peptide from its tandem mass spectrum without the assistance of a sequence database.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>It is defined in contrast to protein/peptide identification using a database search. An advantage of de novo sequencing is its applicability to both known and novel peptides from organisms with unsequenced genome.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>peptide de novo sequencing</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09758 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09758">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Product ion which originates in a peptide precursor ion fragmentation process.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Peptide fragment ions are generated in pairs according to the kind of mechanism that dissociates the chemical bonds around the peptide bond.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>peptide fragment ion</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09760"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09759 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09759">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mass spectrometric technique that serves to generate peptide fragment ions through the fragmentation of peptide precursor ions via different physicochemical processes in an ion source.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>This approach based on the top-down proteomics is an alternative to bottom-up proteomics. The main advantages include the potential for \(\pu{100\%}\) sequence coverage of the protein and improved detection of post-translational modifications.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>peptide fragmentation technique</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09735"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09758"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09760"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09786"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09760 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09760">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Peptide ion generated during the ionisation of peptides in an ion source or during tandem mass spectrometry of an intact protein in top-down proteomic analysis, selected for further analysis by means of tandem mass spectrometry.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Once generated, a peptide fragment ion may become a precursor ion for tandem mass spectrometry of higher orders (\({\rm{MS}}^{n}\), \(n = 3\)).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>peptide precursor ion</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09786"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09761 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09761">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Study of the set of peptides in an organism, its peptidome.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>It uses similar approaches and methods as proteomics.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>peptidomics</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09780"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09762 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09762">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ensemble of two or more different associated proteins, each with a specific function, linked by non-covalent protein-protein interactions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Protein complexes are required for most biological processes. Together they form molecular machines that perform biological functions.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Proteins with quaternary structure are not considered as protein complexes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>protein complex</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09763 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09763">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Set of methods serving to purify the intact protein complex for further analysis.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The methods used may employ single step affinity purification or tandem affinity purification (two affinity purification steps). These approaches include the direct application of an epitope on a bait protein for immunopurification or the genetic insertion of an affinity tag to bait protein, both used to co-purify the prey components of the protein complex.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>protein complex purification</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09762"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09764 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09764">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Collection of protein sequences from several sources, including translations from annotated genetic coding regions, as well as original protein and peptide sequences.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A protein database is fundamental for the determination of protein structure and function via bioinformatics.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>protein database</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11547"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09765 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09765">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Physicochemical process by which a polypeptide folds into its characteristic and functional three-dimensional structure from a random coil.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>protein folding</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09766 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09766">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Methodical approach to simplify a complex mixture of proteomic sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>protein separation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Target compounds can either be separated into individual protein/peptides or fractionated into groups with similar physicochemical properties (polarity, charge, relative molecular mass, etc.). Different analytical separation methods are deployed to achieve this effect: liquid chromatography, gel electrophoresis, isoelectric focusing, affinity chromatography, etc.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>protein fractionation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09747"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09749"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09767 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09767">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process which happens during tandem mass spectrometry of intact proteins.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>This process is used in top-down proteomics. It generates specific peptide fragment ions from a selected protein precursor ion.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>protein fragmentation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09758"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09786"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09768 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09768">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Analytical approach leading to the identity of a protein.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The identity of the protein is given in respect to the proteoform by its primary structure, post-translational modification, or tertiary structure.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>protein identification</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09774"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09785"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09769 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09769">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Supramolecular interacting system, such as a metabolic circuit, intracellular signal transduction, or cell-to-cell communication.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>These systems are operated under process control strategies involving integrated feedback control. The input and output of the circuits or modules are coordinated to assure the normal or adaptive function of the cell or organism.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>protein machine</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09770 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09770">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Series of processes conducted to isolate either the whole proteome or a subset from a biological sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Protein purification involves many different methods, such as chromatography, immunopurification, immunoprecipitation, etc.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>protein purification</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09675"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09771 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09771">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Analytical methods and related techniques to measure the protein content.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Absolute quantification of proteins (AQUA) is performed via measurements on constituent proteolytic peptides using chemically synthesised isotope-labelled peptides as surrogate internal standards for mass spectrometry.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Protein quantification can be further divided into relative quantification and absolute quantification. Basic analytical methods used in proteomics to quantify protein content are immunoanalysis (see Western blotting), mass spectrometry or densitometry of differential staining. Especially mass spectrometry offers many techniques based either on stable isotope labelling or label-free approaches.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>protein quantification</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09789"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09772 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09772">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Equipment designed for the automatic determination of amino acid sequences of peptides using a method of N-terminal analysis.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>protein sequencer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09741"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09753"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09773 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09773">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Technique used to visualise proteins separated on electrophoretic slab gels.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Typically, three types of staining are commonly used: silver staining, which is highly sensitive and non-quantitative; Coommassie Brilliant Blue staining, which is less sensitive than silver staining and quantitative; and fluorescent dye staining, which is highly sensitive and quantitative.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>protein staining</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09774 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09774">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>All of the different molecular forms in which the protein product of a single gene can be found, including changes due to genetic variations, alternatively spliced ribonucleic acid (RNA) transcripts, and post-translational modifications.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>proteoform</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09775 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09775">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Field of biological research in-between proteomics and genomics using proteomic information (mostly derived from mass spectrometry) to improve gene annotations.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>proteogenomics</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09780"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09776 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09776">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Protein cleavage to generate proteolytic peptides for bottom-up proteomic analysis, either enzymatic or chemical.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Both enzymatic and chemical proteolysis may be sequence-specific or unspecific. Proteolysis is specific where cleavage appears at a few specific sequence motives (e.g. proteolysis by trypsin), or unspecific, being connected to wide range of sequence motives (e.g. proteolysis by chymotrypsin).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>proteolysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09735"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09777 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09777">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Exposure of a sample to proteolytic reactions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>proteolytic digestion</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09776"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09778 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09778">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Group of enzymes that cleave the polypeptide into shorter oligopeptides (peptides) and amino acids.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>proteolytic enzyme</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09779 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09779">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Peptide generated via proteolysis.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>proteolytic peptide</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09776"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09780 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09780">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Complex and comprehensive study of proteomes, particularly proteins, and the way they are expressed, modified, involved in metabolism, as well as of their interactions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>proteomics</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09781 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09781">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Type of protein/peptide quantification, where the relative amounts of one protein in different samples is determined, i.e. the ratio of the content of a protein/peptide in sample 1 to sample 2.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>relative quantification</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09782 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09782">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Techniques which serve to quantify protein content by means of mass spectrometry. The principle lies in the introduction of a mass tag to an internal standard protein/peptide containing different stable isotopes than the protein/peptide in the sample. Mass resolved signals originating in the internal standard and the sample are read individually and evaluated.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In the case of a complex sample, the use of multiple reaction monitoring allows for the resolution of multiple overlapping signals.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>stable isotope labelling</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09783 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09783">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Field in proteomics which focuses on higher orders of the protein structure, mainly tertiary or quaternary.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>structural proteomics</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09780"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09785"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09784 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09784">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Approach in proteomics focused on the selected proteins of a sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In a targeted analysis, the mass spectrometer is programmed to analyse a preselected group of proteins. This can be achieved using a technology called selected reaction monitoring (SRM; also referred to as multiple reaction monitoring, MRM), whereby assays are developed on a triple quadrupole instrument to detect fragment-ion signals arising from unique diagnostic peptides representing each of the targeted proteins.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Targeted proteomics achieves the highest sensitivity and throughput for hundreds or thousands of samples in regard to the intended aspect (namely target fragments).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>targeted proteomics</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09780"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09785 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09785">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Arrangement of all atoms of a protein molecule or a subunit of a protein molecule in space, without regard to its relationship with neighbouring molecules or subunits.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The tertiary structure represents a three-dimensional fold/organisation of a polypeptide chain, one containing specific arrangements of secondary structures (see secondary structure of a protein).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>tertiary structure of a protein</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09786 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09786">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Approach to identify proteins and characterise their amino acid sequences or post-translational modifications either by direct mass spectrometric analysis of an ionised protein molecule or by sequential combination of protein/peptide fragmentation in the ion source or mass analyser with ion trapping mass spectrometers.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>top-down proteomics</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09735"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09787 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09787">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Serine protease with highly specific primary endoproteolytic activity that serves to generate proteolytic peptides in bottom-up proteomics analysis.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>It may be modified by reductive methylation or glycosylation to increase its thermal stability.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>trypsin</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09735"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09788 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09788">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Technique used to separate proteins using isoelectric focusing in a gel. The proteins are then further separated in the second dimension using denaturing PAGE.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>2D PAGE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>2D PAGE greatly enhances the capacity of the separation and facilitates further analysis, such as the difference gel electrophoresis or the mass spectrometric analysis of gel separated proteins.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>two-dimensional gel electrophoresis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09747"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09749"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09788"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>2D PAGE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09789 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09789">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Immunoanalytical technique for the identification of polypeptides separated using gel electrophoresis. The polypeptide is transferred by blotting to a membrane, where it undergoes specific interaction with primary antibody that is specific to the protein of interest. The peptides are detected as complexes by a label and/or enzymatic reaction, which may be conjugated to the primary antibody directly or via a separate (secondary) antibody.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>Western blot</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09708"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09728"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09790 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09790">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sample pretreatment to degrade complex metabolites in the presence of oxygen; a method for amplifying the concentration of native metabolites in solution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>cultivation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09791 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09791">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Metabolomic workflow composed of sampling, metabolism quenching, metabolite extraction, metabolite analyses, data analysis, and interpretation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>design of experiment</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09792 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09792">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Complete set of metabolites inside a cell (intracellular metabolites).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>endometabolome</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09793 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09793">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process removing compounds and their metabolites from the body, usually through the kidneys (via urine) or in the feces. Unless excretion is complete, the accumulation of foreign substances can adversely affect normal metabolism.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>excretion</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09794 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09794">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Complete set of metabolites outside the cell (extracellular environment).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>exometabolome</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09795 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09795">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Qualitative analysis of the endometabolome.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>fingerprinting</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09792"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09796 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09796">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In vivo measurements of metabolic fluxes and integration of the results into stoichiometric network models.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>fluxomics</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09797 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09797">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Qualitative analysis of the exometabolome.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>footprinting</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09794"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09798 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09798">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Glycoside of glucuronic acid.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Glucuronides of xenobiotics (such as drugs, toxic substances, etc.) are formed to excrete them from the organism.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>glucuronide</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09793"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09824"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09799 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09799">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>All metabolic products of the main compound (e.g. phase 1 and phase 2 metabolites) with the routes of metabolism and the excreted metabolites in body fluids, e.g. in urine or blood.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>metabolic pathway</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09800 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09800">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Physical or chemical methodology used for rapidly breaking all biochemical processes in metabolomic samples.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>metabolism quenching</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09804"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09801 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09801">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Unique pattern characterizing the metabolism in a given living phase that does not attempt to identify or quantify all the metabolites.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>metabolite fingerprinting and footprinting</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09795"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09797"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09802 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09802">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Strategy to identify and quantify a particular set of metabolites belonging to a specific metabolic pathway or class of compounds.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>metabolite profiling</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09803 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09803">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Complete set (from \(\pu{50}\) to \(\pu{1000}\) metabolites) of organic or inorganic compounds of low relative molecular mass, present in the cell or organism and which participate in the metabolic reactions required for growth, maintenance, and normal functioning.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The metabolome forms a large network of metabolic reactions where outputs from one enzymatic chemical reaction are inputs to other chemical reactions.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>metabolome</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09804 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09804">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Complex and comprehensive study of metabolome; identification and quantification of the small molecule metabolic products of a biological system (cell, tissue, organ, biological fluid, or organism) at a specific point in time.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>An essential part of systems biology in addition to genomics, transcriptomics, proteomics, interactomics, and fluxomics.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>metabolomics</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09780"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09796"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09803"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09872"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09805 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09805">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement of the dynamic multiparametric metabolic response of a living system to pathophysiological stimuli or genetic modification.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>This term is sometime misapplied to refer to the whole of &quot;metabolomics&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>metabonomics</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09804"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09806 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09806">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Metabolite directly involved in the normal life processes of each cell.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>primary metabolite</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09807 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09807">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Activated form of monosaccharide linked to a nucleotide to be available in a high-energy state for glycosyl transfer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>nucleotide sugar</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>activated nucleotide sugar</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09826"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09808 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09808">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Hydroxy group that originates from the aldehydic or ketonic group of a monosaccharide after ring closure.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>glycosidic hydroxy group</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A functional group in one of two stereoisomers of a cyclic saccharide that differs only in its configuration at the hemiacetal or hemiketal carbon, which is also called the anomeric carbon. An anomer is a special type of epimer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The group is sometimes called, incorrectly, &quot;anomeric hydroxyl group&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>anomeric hydroxy group</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09829"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09830"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09809 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09809">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Monosaccharide, oligosaccharide and polysaccharide substances, as well as substances derived from a monosaccharide by reduction of the carbonyl group (alditol), by oxidation, including the oxidation of one or more terminal groups of a carboxylic acid, or by replacement of one or more hydroxy groups by a hydrogen atom, an amino group, a thiol group, or by similar heteroatomic groups. This term also includes derivatives of these of these compounds.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>glycan</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The term carbohydrate was applied originally to monosaccharides, in recognition of the fact that their empirical composition can be expressed as \(\ce{C_{m}(H2O)_{n}}\). However, the term is now used generically in a wider sense.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>carbohydrate</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09810 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09810">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Field of glycoproteomics that studies that the changes in the structures and expression levels of glycoproteins under different conditions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>comparative glycoproteomics</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09821"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09822"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09811 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09811">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Field of proteoglycomics that studies the changes in the structures and expression levels of proteoglycans under different conditions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>comparative proteoglycomics</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09833"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09812 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09812">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Glycans attached to a surface of a microarray.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Glycan microarrays are used for the characterization of lectins and other glycan-binding proteins via specific interactions with labelled glycan-binding proteins.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Specific lectin–glycan interactions are also used for the characterization of glycans in the opposite set-up (see lectin microarray, glycan profiling).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>glycan microarray</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09659"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09813"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09831"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09832"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09813 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09813">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Determination of the diversity of glycan structures expressed on glycoconjugates or cells.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>glycan profiling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09832"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09814 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09814">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Protein that recognises and binds to specific glycans and mediates their biological function.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>carbohydrate-binding protein</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>glycan-binding protein</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09831"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09815 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09815">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Specific form of a glycoprotein containing a distinct glycan structure per glycosylation site.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Natural glycoprotein consists of a population of glycoforms resulting from variable glycan structure and/or glycan attachment site occupancy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>glycoform</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09821"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09816 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09816">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Gene encoding an enzyme or other functional protein for glycan synthesis.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Proteins for glycan synthesis include glycosyltransferases, sugar-nucleotide synthases, sugar-nucleotide transporters, and sulfotransferases (see glycosylation, activated nucleotide sugars).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term should not be confused with glycogen (an energy-storage glucose polysacharide in animals and fungi).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>glycogene</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09807"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09827"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09817 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09817">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Compound containing a glycan covalently linked to a lipid aglycon.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;lipid&quot; comprises several types of compounds. Therefore, different classes of glycolipides are described. For example, typical glycolipids in higher organisms are glycosphingolipids.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>glycolipid</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09818 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09818">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Complete set of glycans and glycoconjugates produced by a species, tissue, or cell in a specified time, space, and environment.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Glycome is an analogue of the terms genome, proteome, and transcriptome.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The terms, as for example &quot;sialome&quot;, are sometimes used for the specific subclasses of the glycome.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>glycome</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09819 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09819">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Complex and comprehensive study of the glycome.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>&quot;Glycomics&quot; is an analogue of the terms genomics or proteomics. The terms, as for example &quot;fucanomics&quot; or &quot;galactanomics&quot;, are sometimes used for the specialised fields of glycomics.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>glycomics</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09780"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09818"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09820 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09820">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Carbohydrate part of a glycoconjugate or a glycoside.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>glycone</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09809"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09824"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09821 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09821">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Protein with one or more covalently bound glycans.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>glycoprotein</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09831"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09822 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09822">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Comprehensive analysis of glycoproteins.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Glycoproteomics studies the glycosylated subset of the proteome, including sites of glycosylation and glycan structures.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>glycoproteomics</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09821"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09827"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09823 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09823">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Enzyme that catalyses the hydrolysis of glycosidic linkages.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Endoglycosidase hydrolyses internal glycosidic linkages, while exoglycosidase removes terminal monosaccharide from the non-reducing end of a glycoside.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>glycosidase</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09825"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09824 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09824">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mixed acetal formally arising by the elimination of water between the hemiacetal or hemiketal hydroxy group of a sugar and a hydroxy group of a second compound. Glycan containing at least one glycosidic linkage to another glycan or an aglycon.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The bond between the two components is called a glycosidic bond.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>glycoside</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09825"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09829"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09830"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09825 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09825">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Linkage of a monosaccharide to another residue via the anomeric hydroxy group.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>glycosidic bond</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>glycosidic linkage</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09808"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09826 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09826">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Monosaccharide ring without the anomeric oxygen.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>glycosyl</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09827 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09827">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Covalent attachment of a saccharide to a protein, lipid, polynucleotide, saccharide, or other organic compound, generally catalysed by enzymes glycosyltransferases.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>glycosylation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09828"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09828 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09828">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Enzyme that catalyses the transfer of a saccharide from a nucleotide di(mono)phosphate-activated sugar to an acceptor molecule.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>glycosyltransferase</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09807"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09827"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09834"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09829 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09829">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Compound having the general formula \(\ce{RCH(OH)(OR^{\prime})}\) (\(\ce{R^{\prime}}  eq \ce{H}\)).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A hemiacetal results from the nucleophilic addition of a hydroxy group to a carbonyl functionality of an aldehyde, as in ring closure of an aldose.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>hemiacetal</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09808"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09830"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09830 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09830">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Compound having the general formula \(\ce{R2C(OH)OR^{\prime}}\) (\(\ce{R^{\prime}}  eq \ce{H}\)).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A hemiketal results from the nucleophilic addition of a hydroxy group to a carbonyl functionality of a ketone, as in ring closure of a ketose.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>hemiketal</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09829"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09831 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09831">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Saccharide binding protein of non-immune origin.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Some lectins participate in immune response, but they are not products of primary immune response. Lectins also do not display catalytic activity towards their ligands; the binding of carbohydrate is reversible and highly specific.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>lectin</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09821"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09832 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09832">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Lectins with distinct saccharide-binding specificity arranged on a microarray platform.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Lectin microarray is used for the characterization of glycans (see glycan profiling) via specific interactions with labelled glycans.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The specific lectin–glycan interactions are also used for the characterization of lectins in the opposite set-up (see glycan microarray).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>lectin microarray</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09659"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09812"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09813"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09831"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09833 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09833">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Systematic study of the structure, expression, and function of proteoglycans.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>proteoglycomics</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09834 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09834">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Term frequently applied to monosaccharides and lower oligosaccharides.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>sugar</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09835 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09835">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Treatment with alkaline solution (e.g. hydrazine, \(\ce{KOH}\), \(\ce{NaOH}\)) useful for the cleavage of fatty acids.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>alkaline hydrolysis</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The resulting (partially or fully) O-deacylated and (fully) N-deacylated products enable the determination of the fatty acid substitution positions by mass spectrometry.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>alkaline degradation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09836 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09836">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Intermediate structural region between the O-polysaccharide and lipid A moiety of lipopolysaccharides (LPS).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>core oligosaccharide</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09840"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09837 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09837">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Diverse group of lipid molecules synthesised by chain elongation of an acetyl-CoA primer with malonyl-CoA (or methylmalonyl-CoA) groups.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>FA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The fatty acyl category is further divided into multiple subclasses, including fatty acids and conjugates, icosanoid, fatty alcohols and esters, glycans, and others.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>fatty acyls</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09837"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>FA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09838 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09838">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Esters, ethers, and other O-derivatives of glycerol with alkyls or alkenyls, including mono-, di-, and tri-substituted glycerols.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>GL</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>glycerolipids</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09838"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GL</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09839 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09839">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Lipids with glycerol backbone esterified with one or two fatty acids in position \(\ce{C-1}\) and/or \(\ce{C-2}\) and with phosphoric acid at position \(\ce{C-3}\). The phosphate group is usually esterified with an alcohol (choline, ethanolamine, serine, inositol, glycerol) to form the &apos;lipid head group&apos;.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>GP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>phospholipids</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>GPs are ubiquitous in nature and are key components of the membrane lipid bilayers of cells.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>glycerophospholipids</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09839"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09840 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09840">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Glycolipid component of lipopolysaccharides (LPSs) that is integrated into the bacterial cell wall and is responsible for the beneficial (immunostimulatory) or toxic (immunopathological) effects of the molecule.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The archetypical (Enterobacteriaceae) structure of lipid A consists of a \(\beta\)-1,6-linked d-glucosamine (GlcN) or 2,3-diamino-2,3-dideoxy-d-glucose (GlcN3N) disaccharide (or a mixture of them), which is acylated by varying numbers (usually four to seven) of ester- and amide-linked acyl groups (often termed primary and secondary fatty acids). Phosphate groups and other glycosyl substituents may be linked at the \(\ce{C-1}\) and \(\ce{C-4^{\prime}}\) positions. Lipid A is linked to the core oligosaccharide at the \(\ce{C-6^{\prime}}\) position of the distal sugar unit of lipid A.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>lipid A</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09841 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09841">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Round-shaped intracellular inclusions consisting of hydrophobic cores (mainly triacylglycerols, sterol esters, occasionally free fatty acids or squalene) covered by a phospholipid monolayer containing a specific set of proteins.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>adiposomes</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>lipid bodies</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>lipid particles</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>oil bodies</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>oleosomes</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Originally considered as lipid storage compartments, lipid droplets are currently recognised as organelles with high metabolic activity. The defective formation and/or function of lipid droplets is involved in various pathogenic conditions, such as obesity, cardiovascular diseases, type 2 diabetes, or metabolic syndrome.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>lipid droplets</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09839"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09842 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09842">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Isolation of lipids in their native state from samples of different origin.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Usually based on the use of organic solvents (methanol, chloroform) to facilitate lipid partitioning between aqueous and organic phases.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>lipid extraction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09843 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09843">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Lipidomics technique conferring unbiased information about total lipids within a particular cell type, tissue, or body fluid.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>lipid profiling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09846"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09844 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09844">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Changes in the lipid molecules composition after completion of their biosynthesis.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In glycerophospholipids, remodelling includes changes in the composition of fatty acids (for example by acyl chain exchange catalysed by transacylases) or the introduction of unsaturated double bonds (catalysed by desaturases), or by the synthesis of new lipid species or degradation of existing ones. In phosphoinositides, it can involve phosphorylation/dephosphorylation processes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Lipid remodelling is important for the adaptation of biological membranes to changing environmental conditions and physiological demands.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>lipid remodelling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09839"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09845 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09845">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Complete set of lipid species and their metabolites present within a particular cell type, tissue, or body fluid.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>It is estimated that lipidome of a eukaryotic cell may contain over \(\pu{100000}\) individual molecular species of various lipids.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>lipidome</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09846 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09846">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Systematic and comprehensive study aimed at the identification, profiling, and quantification of lipidome.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In a broader context, lipidomics includes system-level analysis of subcellular localization and the biological activities of individual lipids.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Lipidomics is a lipid-targeted metabolomics approach aimed at a comprehensive analysis of lipids in biological systems.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>lipidomics</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09804"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09845"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09847 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09847">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Lipopolysaccharide produced by specific wild-type bacteria that give a smooth-colony appearance when cultured on agar plates.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>LOS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>LOSs are composed of lipid A and a saccharide moiety, limited to an oligosaccharide core only.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The lack of O-polysaccharide chains in LOS structures is an inherent property of bacteria.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>lipooligosaccharide</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09840"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09847"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>LOS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09848 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09848">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Study of the structure-activity relationship of bacterial lipopolysaccharides.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>lipopolysaccharidomics</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09849 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09849">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Planar structure spontaneously formed by amphipathic lipids in a polar (aqueous) environment.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Formation of a lipid bilayer is driven by the interaction of the polar heads of lipids with water molecules while minimizing the exposure of the hydrophobic parts of lipids to water. Lipid bilayers represent the basic structure of biological membranes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>membrane bilayer</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09850 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09850">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Dynamic, nanometre-sized, highly ordered, membrane domains enriched in sterols and sphingolipids. They contain specific sets of membrane proteins that are sorted into rafts by their specific physical properties and specific lipid-protein interactions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In functional terms, membrane rafts represent highly specialised membrane domains involved in various cellular processes, such as transmembrane transport, membrane trafficking, cell signalling, or virus assembly.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>membrane rafts</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09860"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09861"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09851 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09851">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Specific structure of lipid molecules (glycerophospholipids) in aqueous environments and membranes as a function of the ratio between the cross-sections of their polar heads and hydrophobic tails.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Lipids’ ability to self-assemble in dynamic macrostructures in an aqueous medium is driven by the amphiphilic nature of lipid molecules, which tend to aggregate so that the hydrophobic (tails) and the hydrophilic (heads) parts of the lipid are well separated and the area of the dividing surface is held by the hydrophobic effect.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The shape of a membrane lipid depends on the relative size of its polar headgroup and apolar tails. In cases in which the headgroup and lipid backbone have similar cross-sectional areas, the molecule has a cylindrical shape (phosphatidylcholine and phosphatidylserine). Lipids with a small headgroup, such as phosphatidylethanolamine, are conical shaped. By contrast, when the hydrophobic part occupies a relatively smaller surface area, the molecule has the shape of an inverted cone (lysophosphatidylcholine and, to some extent, sphingomyelin).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>molecular shape of lipids</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09839"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09852 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09852">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Part of a lipopolysaccharide that consists of a polysaccharide chain extending outwards from the bacterial cell surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>O-polysaccharide</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09853 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09853">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Assemblies of lipids and proteins in blood plasma transporting lipids between tissues.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>They are structurally very similar to intracellular lipid droplets. They are classified according to their size and buoyant density into five types with different functions: chylomicrons, very low density lipoproteins (VLDL), intermediate lipoproteins (IDL), low density lipoproteins (LDL), and high density lipoproteins (HDL).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>plasma lipoproteins</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09854 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09854">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Fatty acids with more than one double bond.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PUFA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The double bonds usually have cis (Z) configuration and are methylene-interrupted.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>polyunsaturated fatty acids</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09854"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PUFA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003012"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09855 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09855">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Lipids containing a linear chain of isoprenoid groups.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Prenol lipids are synthesised from the five-carbon precursors isopentenyl diphosphate and dimethylallyl diphosphate. Both precursors are produced mainly via the mevalonic acid pathway.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>prenol lipids</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09855"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09856 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09856">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Proteins with covalently linked lipids, e.g. fatty acid or glycophosphatidylinositol (GPI).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The term ‘lipoprotein’ is also occasionally used to describe such compounds, but to avoid confusion this might be better reserved for the non-covalently linked lipid-protein complexes of the type found in plasma (plasma lipoproteins).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>proteolipids</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09853"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09857 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09857">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Biomolecules where fatty acids are directly bound to a saccharide backbone, thus forming structures similar to glycerolipids or glycerophospholipids, with saccharide substituting for the glycerol.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SL</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>saccharolipids</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09838"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09839"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09857"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SL</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09858 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09858">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Methodological approach based on the direct analysis of the total lipid extracts from cells or tissues without an online chromatographic separation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>shotgun lipidomics</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09859 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09859">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Lipid molecules involved in intracellular signalling and the regulation of cell death, immune response, or inflammation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>lipid mediators</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Broadly defined term referring to any lipid messenger that binds a protein target, such as a receptor, kinase or phosphatase, which in turn mediates the effects of these lipids on specific cellular responses.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Signalling lipids include diacylglycerol, phosphatidic acid, phosphatidylinositol-4,5-bisphosphate (the source of polyphosphorylated inositols), arachidonic acid-derived mediators (icosanoids, prostaglandins and leukotrienes), sphingolipid-derived mediators (ceramides, sphingosine, sphingosine-1-phosphate), or cholesterol-derived steroid hormones (androgens, estrogens, progestogens, mineralocorticoids, glucocorticoids).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>signalling lipids</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09860 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09860">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Complex family of lipids that contain a sphingoid base backbone and a fatty acyl chain.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Together with glycerophospholipids and sterols, sphingolipids are the major lipid components of cellular membranes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sphingolipids</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09837"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09839"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09860"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09861 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09861">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Structural derivatives of tetracyclic alcohols with a hydroxyl group in the position \(\ce{C-3}\) and an aliphatic side chain in the position \(\ce{C-17}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ST</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>This category primarily comprises sterols: polycyclic compounds of isoprenoid origin and their various derivatives, including steroids, bile acids, and sterol esters. Sterols, along with glycerophospholipids and sphingolipids, are important constituents of cellular membranes. In addition, sterol lipids act as hormones (steroids) or fat emulsifiers (bile acids).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sterol lipids</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09839"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09860"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09861"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ST</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09862 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09862">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Lipids (mainly glycerophospholipids, sterols and sphingolipids) involved in the formation of biological membranes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>structural lipids</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09839"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09860"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09861"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09863 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09863">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Analytical technique focusing on the quantification of specific lipids of interest.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>targeted lipidomics</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09864 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09864">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measure of the number of double bonds in fatty acids moieties of phospholipids, calculated as the fraction of each unsaturated fatty acid multiplied by the number of double bonds within that fatty acid.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>UI</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>UI is indicative of membrane physical properties (e.g. fluidity, curvature, or flexibility).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>unsaturation index</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09864"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>UI</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09865 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09865">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Specifically-designed single-stranded nucleic acid with a defined (known) nucleotide sequence immobilised on a surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>An analogous principle, based on immobilised nucleic acid probes, has been applied in studies of the interactions of nucleic acids with proteins or other targets. See also nucleic acid aptamers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Capture probes are utilised as a recognition element to test the nucleotide sequence of target deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) or ribonucleic acid (RNA) in the sample solution by using DNA or RNA hybridization.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>capture probe</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09709"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09712"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09714"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09866 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09866">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Method for studying deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA)–protein and/or protein–protein interactions in the context of chromatin, suitable for the identification of binding sites of proteins in genome DNA, etc. This method combines chemical cross-linking, the isolation of specific protein conjugates by immunoprecipitation, and polymerase chain reaction (PCR) amplification of co-precipitated DNA.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>chromatin immunoprecipitation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09675"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09685"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09867 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09867">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Formation of a covalent bridge between two biomacromolecules or between two segments of the same biomacromolecule using reactive substances (e.g. formaldehyde, bifunctional aldehydes such as glutaraldehyde, or cisplatin) or photochemical processes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Cross-linking is used to study protein oligomerization, as well as protein–protein and nucleic acid–protein interactions</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>cross-linking</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09634"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09866"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09868 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09868">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Cytogenetic technique used to detect and localise the presence or absence of specific deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) sequences on chromosomes. It uses fluorescent probes that only bind to those parts of the chromosome with which they show a high degree of sequence complementarity.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>FISH</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>FISH is often used for finding specific features in DNA for use in genetic counselling, medicine, and species identification. FISH can also be used to detect and localise specific ribonucleic acid (RNA) targets (messenger ribonucleic acid (mRNA), long non-coding ribonucleic acid (lncRNA) and mitochondrial ribonucleic acid (miRNA)) in cells, circulating tumour cells, and tissue samples. In this context, it can help define the spatial-temporal patterns of gene expression within cells and tissues.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>fluorescent &lt;em&gt;in situ&lt;/em&gt; hybridization</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09868"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>FISH</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09869 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09869">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process in which molecules are bound within a minor or major groove of the deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) double helix.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>groove binding</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09703"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09870 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09870">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Assay with specifically designed single-stranded deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) probe with a defined (known) nucleotide sequence usually immobilised on a surface (in such a case, the nucleic acid probe is called the capture probe).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The probe is used as a recognition element to test for the nucleotide sequence within the target DNA in the sample solution. If target DNA contains a sequence complementary to the probe, a hybrid double-stranded DNA (dsDNA) is formed.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>hybridization assay</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09712"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09865"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09871 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09871">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Virtual map depicting protein–protein interactions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The interactome is the most visible outcome of functional proteomics.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>interactome</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09746"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09872 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09872">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Complex and comprehensive study of the interactions between various proteins and between protein and other molecules within a cell, such as nucleic acids and metabolites, as well as the consequences of these interactions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>interactomics</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09873 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09873">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Interactions between (parts of) two biomolecule chains (e.g. base pairing in deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) double helix, or the interactions between two polypeptide chains, or between a DNA and a protein molecule).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>intermolecular interactions</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09633"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09703"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09874 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09874">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Interactions between (neighbouring or distant) segments within the same biomolecule chain (e.g. base pairing in hairpin or monomolecular tetraplex structures of deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) or ribonucleic acid (RNA), the interactions stabilizing the secondary or tertiary structures of proteins, etc.).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>intramolecular interactions</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09633"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09785"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09875 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09875">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Interactions between biomolecules or within a biomolecule (e.g. hydrogen bonds, electrostatic interactions, and van der Waals forces) taking part in the stabilization of proper biomolecule structure and intermolecular interactions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>non-covalent interactions</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09633"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09873"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09876 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09876">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Binding of a protein to deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) independent of the nucleotide sequence or the conformation of the DNA, often with a strong contribution of electrostatic interaction between the negatively charged sugar-phosphate backbone of the DNA and basic amino acid residues of the protein.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>non-specific binding</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09712"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09877 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09877">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Formation of specific or non-specific complexes between deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) or ribonucleic acid (RNA) and proteins, mediated and stabilised by a network of non-covalent interactions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Nucleic acid–protein interactions are critical for basic cellular functions such as replication, transcription, translation, recombination, DNA repair etc. as well as for fine regulation of them. Proteins may bind more tightly to specific sequences in the nucleic acid or non-specifically, in which case their affinity is independent of sequence.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nucleic acid–protein interaction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09661"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09722"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09875"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09878 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09878">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Association of one protein with one or more other proteins to form either homo- or heteromeric proteins or protein complexes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PPI</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Such associations are common in biological systems and are responsible for the regulation of numerous cellular functions in addition to the mediation of disease morphology, where aberrant interactions play a significant role.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>protein–protein interaction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09878"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PPI</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09879 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09879">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Approach used for studying protein–protein interactions analogous to immunoprecipitation, using (instead of antibody) one of the interacting proteins (the &apos;bait&apos;) immobilised at a surface to capture the other protein (the &apos;prey&apos;).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:label>pull-down assay</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09675"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09878"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09880 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09880">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Specifically designed single-stranded deoxyribonucleic acid (ssDNA) (alternatively, ribonucleic acid (RNA) or a synthetic nucleic acid analogue such as locked nucleic acid (LNA) or peptide nucleic acid (PNA)) with a defined (known) nucleotide sequence used to detect complementary DNA or RNA sequence in a sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>signalling probe</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Reporter probe is usually labelled (radioactively, immunologically, or by fluorescent, electroactive, or other species to produce a specific signal upon hybridization).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Similar to capture probes, nucleic acid signalling probes (or nucleic acid aptamers) can be applied in studies of nucleic acid interactions with proteins and other ligands.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>reporter probe</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09709"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09712"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09714"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09881 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09881">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Binding of proteins to specific sites in deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) based on the precise recognition of nucleotide sequence and/or conformation of the DNA.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Sequence-specific DNA binding is typical for restriction enzymes, transcription factors and other proteins involved in cellular regulation, etc.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>specific binding</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09712"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09882 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09882">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Attractive interaction between planar aromatic rings, in nucleic acids being one of the critical forces stabilizing double- and multistranded structures.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Stacking interactions are involved in binding of intercalators to DNA.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>stacking interactions</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09883 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09883">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Method for the determination of strand breaks and DNA fragmentation in cells based on labelling and quantification of free 3′-OH ends of deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>TUNEL</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The abbreviation is for terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase dUTP nick end labelling (TUNEL).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Tunel test</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09696"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09729"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09883"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>TUNEL</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09884 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09884">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Molecular biology method for studying protein–protein or protein–deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) interactions in cellular (yeast or bacterial) milieu.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2016-1120"/>
        <rdfs:comment>This method involves a transcription factor split into two separate domains (DNA binding and transcription activating), one of which is fused with one of the tested proteins (the &quot;bait&quot;) and the other with another tested protein (the &quot;prey&quot;), as well as with a reporter gene construct. Only upon physical interaction between bait and prey is the expression of the reporter gene observed.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>two hybrid system</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09878"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09885 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09885">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Form in which the stationary phase is used.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>chromatographic bed</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09886 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09886">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Stationary phase which has been immobilized on the support particles or on the inner wall of the column, e.g. by a physical attraction (coated stationary phase), by chemical bonding (bonded stationary phase), or by in situ polymerisation (cross-linked stationary phase) after coating.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>immobilized stationary phase material</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>immobilized stationary phase</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09887 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09887">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Partition chromatography which uses two immiscible liquid phases and no solid support. One liquid acts as the stationary phase and the other as the mobile phase.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CCC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The method allows continuous elution of the mobile phase and on-line monitoring of the effluent.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The method employs a variety of systems using either gravity or centrifugal force to retain the stationary phase in the column.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>countercurrent chromatography</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09887"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CCC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09888 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09888">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Separation of enantiomeric species due to the enantioselectivity of their interaction with the chiral selector(s) of a chromatographic (electrophoretic) system.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>chiral chromatography</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>chiral electrophoresis</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>enantioselective electrophoresis</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>enantioselective chromatography</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09889 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09889">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Liquid chromatography for the separation of macromolecules and particles based on the difference in the speed of the mobile phase flow between the column axis and column wall and the specific size-dependent distribution of particles across the column cross-section.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>hydrodynamic chromatography</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09890 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09890">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Liquid chromatography in which the molecules are partitioned between a water-enriched layer, which is immobilised on a hydrophilic (polar) stationary phase, and a less-polar mobile phase, containing water and organic solvent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>HILIC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>hydrophilic-interaction liquid chromatography</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09890"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>HILIC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09891 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09891">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chromatography in which molecules are separated by interactions between their hydrophobic moieties and hydrophobic sites on the surface of the stationary phase.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>HIC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>High salt concentrations are used in the mobile phase and separations are effected by changing the salt concentration. Gradients are run by decreasing the salt concentration over time.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>hydrophobic-interaction chromatography</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09891"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>HIC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09892 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09892">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Exclusion chromatography in which ionized compounds are excluded from the pores of the support and elute first, while weakly ionized and non-ionic compounds elute later.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>ion-exclusion chromatography</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09893 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09893">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Liquid chromatography in which the mobile liquid phase contains an ionized additive (an ion-pair reagent) that can form an ion pair with an analyte molecule.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Separations occur due to the difference in the ability to form ion pairs and/or in the distribution coefficients of the ion pairs between the mobile and stationary phases.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>ion-pair chromatography</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09894 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09894">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chromatography based on the different ability of substances to form complexes with metal ions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>ligand exchange chromatography</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09895 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09895">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reversed-phase liquid chromatography which uses a mobile phase containing a surfactant above its critical micellar concentration (cmc).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>micellar chromatography</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09896 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09896">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chromatography in which the composition of the mobile phase remains constant during elution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>isocratic analysis</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>isocratic elution</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09897 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09897">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Inner diameter of the column tubing.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The column radius \(r_{\rm{c}} = d_{\rm{c}}/2\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>column diameter</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09898 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09898">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Length of that part of the column tube that contains the stationary phase.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>column length</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09899 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09899">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Cross-sectional area of the empty tube: \(A_{\rm{c}} = \pi r_{\rm{c}}^{2} = \pi (d_{\rm{c}}/2)^{2}\), where \(d_{\rm{c}}\) is the column diameter.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>cross-sectional area of the column</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09897"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09900 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09900">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Average thickness of the liquid stationary phase film coated on the inside wall of the column tubing of an open-tubular column.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>liquid-phase film thickness</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09901 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09901">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Column packed with a single piece of solid material (monolith) composed of interconnected skeletons (forming macropores) and interconnected flow paths (mesopores) as a stationary phase material.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>monolithic column</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09902 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09902">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Width of a peak (\(f + b\)) divided by twice the width of the front of the peak (\(f\)), measured at \(\pu{10\%}\) of the peak height (see figure). \[A_{\rm{s}} = \frac{(f + b)}{2f}\] Peak asymmetry (schematic). \(R_{\rm{D}}\) is the response of the detector.Peak asymmetry (schematic). \(R_{\rm{D}}\) is the response of the detector.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For a symmetrical peak \(A_{\rm{s}} = 1\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Some pharmacopoeia definitions of peak asymmetry measure the value at \(\pu{5\%}\) of the peak height.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>asymmetry factor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09905"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09903 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09903">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Peak width at peak base intercepted by tangents to the chromatogram drawn at the inflection points on either side of the peak. Represented by the line AB in the peak height figure.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>base width</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>peak width at base</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09904 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09904">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Peak width at \(\pu{50\%}\) of the peak height that terminates at the intersections with the peak.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The peak width at half height must never be called the &quot;half width&quot; because that has a completely different meaning.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The peak width at half height of a Gaussian peak is about 2.355 times the standard deviation of the modelled Gaussian. See figure in peak height.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>peak width at half height</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09905 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09905">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Width of the back of a peak (\(b\)) divided by the width of the front (\(f\)) of the peak measured at \(\pu{10\%}\) of the peak height. See figure in asymmetry factor.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>tailing factor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09902"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09906 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09906">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Column length (\(L\)) divided by effective plate number (\(N_{\rm{eff}}\)): \[H_{\rm{eff}} = \frac{L}{N_{\rm{eff}}}\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>height equivalent to one effective plate</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>effective plate height</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09898"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09907"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09907 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09907">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number indicative of column performance, calculated from the ratios of measures of adjusted retention and peak width: \[\eqalign{&amp; N_{\rm{eff}} = \left(\frac{V_{\rm{R}}^{\prime}}{\sigma} \right)^{2} = \left(\frac{t_{\rm{R}}^{\prime}}{\sigma} \right)^{2} \cr &amp; N_{\rm{eff}} = 16 \left(\frac{V_{\rm{R}}^{\prime}}{w_{\rm{b}}} \right)^{2} = 16 \left(\frac{t_{\rm{R}}^{\prime}}{w_{\rm{b}}} \right)^{2} \cr &amp; N_{\rm{eff}} \approx 5.545 \left(\frac{V_{\rm{R}}^{\prime}}{w_{\rm{h}}} \right)^{2} \approx 5.545 \left(\frac{t_{\rm{R}}^{\prime}}{w_{\rm{h}}} \right)^{2}}\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>number of effective plates</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The plate number is related to the effective plate number and retention factor (\(k\)) \(N = N_{\rm{eff}}{\left(\frac{{k + 1}}{k} \right)^2}\)</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>These expressions assume a Gaussian (symmetrical) peak.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Units for the quantities being divided must be consistent so that their ratio is dimensionless: i.e. if the numerator is a time, then peak width must also be expressed in terms of time</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>\(V_{\rm{R}}^{\prime}\): adjusted retention volume; \(t_{\rm{R}}^{\prime}\): total retention time; \(\sigma\): standard deviation of the Gaussian peak; \(w_{\rm{b}}\): peak width at base; \(w_{\rm{h}}\): peak width at half height; \(\rm{8 \ln 2} ≈ 5.545\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>effective plate number</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09903"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09904"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10039"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09908 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09908">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In liquid chromatography plate height divided by average particle diameter. \[h = H/d_{\rm{p}}\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For open-tubular columns \(h\) is the plate height divided by the column diameter, \(h = H/d_{\rm{c}}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>reduced plate height</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09897"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09909 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09909">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sensor that responds to components in the effluent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>detector</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09911 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09911">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Capillary electrophoresis in which the background electrolyte contains substances capable of specific, often biospecific, interactions with the analytes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ACE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CAE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>affinity capillary electrophoresis</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Biospecific compounds can be already in the sample (receptor-acceptor formation under applied electric field, or the bioselective compounds may be attached on the capillary inner wall before the analysis starts. They are bound covalently or dynamically on the surface.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>capillary affinity electrophoresis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09913"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09911"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CAE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09912 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09912">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Liquid chromatography, where the movement of the mobile phase through a capillary, filled, packed, or coated with a stationary phase, is achieved by electroosmotic flow (which may be assisted by pressure).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CEC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The retention time is determined by a combination of electrophoretic migration and chromatographic retention.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>capillary electrochromatography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09963"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09912"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CEC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09913 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09913">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Separation method based solely on the differences in the electrophoretic mobilities of charged species in capillaries.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The background electrolyte may be an aqueous or non-aqueous solution.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The basic kind of CE is capillary zone electrophoresis in which separation takes place in the homogeneous solution of the background electrolyte on the basis of different electrophoretic mobilities of analytes in a free solution.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The electrolytes can contain additives, which can interact with the charged species and alter their electrophoretic mobilities.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The use of the term capillary electrophoresis (CE) as a collective term for all capillary electromigration techniques is not recommended, as some of these techniques involve separation mechanisms other than electrophoresis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>capillary electrophoresis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09911"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09913"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09914 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09914">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Capillary sieving electrophoresis in which the capillary is filled with a gel.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CGE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>capillary gel electrophoresis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09917"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09914"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CGE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09915 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09915">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrophoretic technique for the separation of analytes according to their isoelectric points by an electric field along a pH gradient formed in a capillary.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CIEF</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>capillary isoelectric focusing</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09915"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CIEF</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09916 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09916">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrophoretic separation technique in a discontinuous buffer system in which analytes migrate according to their electrophoretic mobilities, forming a chain of adjacent zones moving with equal speed (velocity) between two solutions, leading and terminating electrolytes, bracketing the mobility range of the analytes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CITP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>capillary isotachophoresis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09916"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CITP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09917 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09917">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrophoretic separation technique, which takes place in a capillary containing a medium in the background electrolyte that retains molecules and particles based on their size and shape.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CSE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The medium may be an entangled polymer network.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>capillary sieving electrophoresis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09917"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CSE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09918 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09918">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Length of the capillary from the sample introduction point to the detection point.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>effective length of the capillary</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09919 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09919">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Separation method based on a combination of electrophoresis and interactions of the analytes with additives (e.g. surfactants), which form a dispersed phase moving at a different speed (velocity). In order to achieve separation, either the analytes or this secondary phase should be charged.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>electrokinetic capillary chromatography</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment> This is not totally compatible with the definition of chromatography.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Surfactants must be at concentrations above their critical micelle concentration for being able to form a dispersed phase – otherwise they will just act as ion-pair reagents.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrokinetic chromatography</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09920 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09920">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Injection of the analytical sample by applying a potential across the sample and capillary and using the electroosmotic flow to draw an aliquot into the end of the capillary tube. The sample is then replaced by the running electrolyte for the separation to take place.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>electrokinetic injection</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09921 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09921">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Motion of a liquid through a capillary (or membrane or plug) because of the application of an electric field across the capillary (or membrane or plug).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>electro-osmosis</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>electroosmosis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09922 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09922">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Effective length of the capillary (\(L_{\rm{eff}}\)) divided by the electroosmotic hold-up time (\(t_{\rm{eo}}\)). \[\boldsymbol{v}_{\rm{eo}} = L_{\rm{eff}}/t_{\rm{eo}}\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In capillary electromigration, this velocity is positive, by convention, in the direction from the sample introduction end of the capillary to the detection end, otherwise negative.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electroosmotic velocity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09918"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09921"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09923 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09923">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Volume flow rate divided by electric field strength through the whole plug or capillary.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>electroosmotic volume flow</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>electroosmotic volume flow rate</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09924 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09924">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Methods that use differences in migration behaviour in spatially defined regions of the capillary of different field strengths (conductivities).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Electrostacking methods can be used to concentrate dilute samples.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electrostacking</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09925 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09925">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Introduction of the analytical sample into a capillary by a pressure difference between the ends of the capillary.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>hydrodynamic injection</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09926 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09926">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Biochemical methods for separation and characterization of proteins based on electrophoresis and reaction with antibodies.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>immunoelectrophoresis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09927 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09927">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrokinetic chromatography, in which the secondary phase is a micellar dispersed phase in the capillary.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>micellar electrokinetic capillary chromatography</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>micellar electrokinetic chromatography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09919"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09928 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09928">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Electrokinetic chromatography, where a microemulsion is employed as the dispersed phase.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>microemulsion electrokinetic capillary chromatography</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>microemulsion electrokinetic chromatography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09919"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09929 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09929">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Capillary electrophoresis, which uses water-free organic solutions and modifiers to produce ions in solution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NACE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Typical modifiers are acetic acid, sodium acetate, ammonium acetate, ammonium chloride and ammonium hydroxide (non aqueous).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Typical organic solvents are methanol, ethanol, propanol, and acetonitrile.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>non-aqueous capillary electrophoresis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09913"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09929"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>NACE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09930 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09930">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In isotachophoresis a sample is introduced between a zone of fast leading electrolyte and a zone of slow terminating electrolyte.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Usually, the leading and terminating electrolytes have a common counter-ion, but the co-ions are different.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sample introduction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09931 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09931">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Flow field-flow fractionation in which only one channel wall is permeable, meaning there is one channel inlet for the flow during the run and some of the channel flow is directed through the accumulation wall.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Advantages of AsFlFFF include that the upper channel wall can be made of a transparent material, allowing the accumulation and sample migration to be viewed. Sample focusing can be achieved during relaxation, resulting in a narrower sample band being produced, resulting in lower band broadening.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In a uniform width channel this would result in the channel flow decreasing dramatically along the channel length. In order to minimize this, a trapezoid shape of the separation channel has been developed for AsFlFFF. In general, the channel geometry significantly affects the separation efficiency in AF4.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Three different channel designs were evaluated using polystyrene latex nanoparticles. The channel breadth was held constant for one (rectangular) profile, and was reduced either linearly (trapezoid profile) or exponentially (exponential profile) along the length for the other two channels. Com-pared with a rectangular channel, trapezoid and exponential profiles provided better performance of AsFlFFF and allowed band broadening to be reduced.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>asymmetrical flow field-flow fractionation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09935"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09932 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09932">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mechanism of field-flow fractionation of sub-micron particles that relies on Brownian motion.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>BFFF</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>BFFF mode</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NFFF mode</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>normal FFF</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>normal FFF mode (NFFF)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>BFFF mode requires a specific sample injection procedure described as stop-flow injection or a relaxation step. A relaxation time is needed to equilibrate the sample under the influence of a force field and Brownian diffusion in the absence of flow. The injected sample is forced to concentrate in a thin layer adjacent to the accumulation wall (channel bottom).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Two main factors influencing the sample behaviour in BFFF mode are the properties of particles characterized by the diffusion coefficient and the strength of the cross-field applied.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When the flow is switched on, elution begins with smaller particles, which are distributed further away from the accumulation wall and thus protrude further into the faster flow streams; larger particles elute later.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Brownian field-flow fractionation mode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09934"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09943"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09932"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>BFFF</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09933 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09933">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Field-flow fractionation in a long (usually \(\pu{10 m}\) or more) rotating coiled column under the action of asymmetrical centrifugal field.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CTFFF</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>CTFFF can be attributed to non-conventional sedimentation field-flow fractionation (SdFFF), which enables the sample mass to be increased to gram levels.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The complex asymmetrical force field is generated in planetary centrifuges where the column drum rotates around its own axis and at the same time revolves around the central axis of the centrifuge.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>coiled-tube field-flow fractionation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09934"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09938"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09933"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CTFFF</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09934 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09934">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Separation based on the retention and elution of macromolecular, colloidal, and particulate matter achieved by the combined action of the non-uniform flow speed (velocity) profile of a carrier fluid (mobile phase) and a physical force field (e.g. gravitational, centrifugal, electrical) applied at right angles to a thin (\(\pu{0.05 mm}\) to \(\pu{0.5 mm}\)) channel (see figure). Basic principle of field-flow fractionation.Basic principle of field-flow fractionation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>FFF</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Although similar to liquid chromatography, FFF requires no stationary phase, so the separation channel contains no packing material and only physical interactions are involved in the separation process.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>FFF is applicable to the fractionation and characterization of colloidal and solid particles, polymers, and biological cells in the size range from \(\pu{1 nm}\) to \(\pu{50 \upmu m}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The sample mass injected is usually less than \(\pu{1 mg}\) to avoid overloading.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>field-flow fractionation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09943"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09934"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>FFF</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09935 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09935">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Field-flow fractionation in which the physical force field is a nonspecific hydrodynamic force field across the channel, generated by a secondary mobile phase cross-flow in addition to the normal channel flow.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Like sedimentation field-flow fractionation (SdFFF), FlFFF can be used for submicron particles and macromolecules using the Brownian field-flow fractionation mode or micron size particles utilizing the hyperlayer mode.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The sample components are driven by the cross-flow toward the accumulation wall, which consists of porous frit covered with a permeable membrane.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>This technique can be applied to the size fractionation of different synthetic polymers, liposomes, proteins, viruses, dissolved organic matter, humic and fulvic acids as well as their complexes with trace metals, diesel soot particles, marine colloids, heterogeneous and arbitrarily shaped natural colloidal particles, gold colloid standards starting from 5 nm.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>flow field-flow fractionation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09932"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09934"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09937"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09938"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09936 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09936">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Field-flow fractionation in which the physical force field is the Earth’s gravitational field.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>GFFF</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>GFFF can fractionate particulate matter in the micrometre size range (large microorganisms, blood cells, sediments, etc).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Since the Earth’s gravitational field produces relatively weak forces, this mode is only appropriate for micron size particles, hence the separation is an example of hyperlayer focusing field-flow fractionation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>gravitational field-flow fractionation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09934"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09937"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09936"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GFFF</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09937 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09937">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mechanism of field-flow fractionation of particles greater than \(\pu{1 \upmu m}\) in diameter relying on hydrodynamic lift forces near the channel wall to move particles into faster flowing streams.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>HyFFF</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>hyperlayer field-flow fractionation mode</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>lift-hyperlayer field-flow fractionation mode</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Due to the large size of the particles, Brownian diffusion away from the accumulation wall is assumed to be negligible in this mode.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>HyFFF is a preferred alternative to steric field-flow fractionation. The retention of particles larger than \(\pu{1 \upmu m}\) typically increases with flow rate. Particles are driven away from the wall by a hydrody-namic lift force and elevated into faster flow streams. The particles form thin layers at an equilibrium position, where the external field is balanced by the opposing hydrodynamic lift force, as compared to steric mode, where the thin layer of particles is adjacent to the accumulation wall.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>hyperlayer focusing field-flow fractionation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09934"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09940"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09937"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>HyFFF</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09938 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09938">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Field-flow fractionation in which the physical force field is a centrifugal force field.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SdFFF</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>SdFFF can be applied to a wide range of colloidal and micron size particles and is a powerful method for cell separations, including industrial carbon blacks, silica nanoparticles, silver and gold nano-particles, aquatic sediments, aerosols, and latex beads.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>SdFFF can be successfully applied to the fractionation and characterization (sizing) of submicron particles, starting from about \(\pu{5 nm}\), but this limit depends on the particle density, as the separation is based on the buoyant mass of the particles.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>SdFFF can operate in both the Brownian field-flow fractionation (BFFF) and hyperlayer field-flow fractionation (HyFFF) modes and caution must be exercised if samples span the Brownian-hyperlayer transition point of about \(\pu{1 \upmu m}\), as this can lead to separated fractions containing two particle sizes, smaller particles separated by Brownian mode and larger particles separated by hyperlayer mode.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>SdFFF is performed in a circular flat channel inserted inside a centrifugal basket.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sedimentation field-flow fractionation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09932"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09934"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09937"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09938"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SdFFF</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09939 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09939">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Separation in which laminar flow conditions are established between a liquid containing suspended sample and a carrier liquid, with a physical force field applied across the streams.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SPLITT</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A semi-preparative binary separation can be generated with smaller particles eluting from one outlet and larger particles emerging from the other. Careful manipulation of the inlet and outlet flow rates and the field strength enables the cut-off diameter of the particles to be selected.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Although SPILTT is similar to field-flow fractionation, it should not be referred to as FFF. The separation is achieved by the combined action of controlled flow rates and an applied cross-field.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Fields that have been tested so far include gravitational, centrifugal and magnetic.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>SPLITT enables the separation of samples of masses of the order of grams.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The system has two channel inlets and two outlets. Sample suspension is continuously pumped into one inlet and carrier liquid is pumped into the other inlet. Due to the laminar flow conditions, these two streams do not mix.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>split-flow thin-cell fractionation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09934"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09939"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SPLITT</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09940 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09940">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mechanism of field-flow fractionation of particles greater than \(\pu{1 \upmu m}\) in diameter relying on proximity to the accumulation wall.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>StFFF</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In StFFF the elution order is opposite to that of Brownian field-flow fractionation mode and larger particles move ahead of smaller ones.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>It was originally considered that particles are in contact with the accumulation wall during the entire migration time and consequently the centre of larger particles are situated closer to the centre of the channel in the faster flow streams. This is never achieved in practice due to hydrodynamic lift forces away from the accumulation wall.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>steric field-flow fractionation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09932"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09934"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09940"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>StFFF</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09941 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09941">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Flow field-flow fractionation in which the speed of mobile phase is constant along the length of the channel due to constant channel width.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The width is constant except for triangular sections at each of the ends of the channel.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Theory and data analysis are simplified by the assumption of constant flow.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>symmetrical flow field-flow fractionation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09935"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09942 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09942">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Field-flow fractionation in which the physical force field is created by a temperature difference across the channel.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>TFFF</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>One side of the channel is heated and the other side is cooled. A relatively small temperature difference between the top and bottom walls of the channel provides a high thermal gradient.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>TFFF has been mainly used for the separation of lyophilic synthetic polymers, including ultrahigh molecular weight polymers and microgels. However, some reports of other types of macromolecules and particles, including aqueous solvents, are present in the literature.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>thermal field-flow fractionation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09934"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09942"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>TFFF</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09943 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09943">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Method of introduction of the mobile phase (upper or lower) into the rotating coil (from the head or the tail). Of four combinations, only two elution modes should be used in high-speed counter-current chromatography, i.e. either the lower phase from the head toward the tail or the upper phase from the tail toward the head.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>elution mode</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09944"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09944 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09944">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Counter-current chromatography system using a multilayer coil separation column, which undergoes a type-J synchronous planetary motion (see figure).Type-J planetary motion of a multilayer coil separation column. The column holder rotates about its own axis and revolves around the centrifuge axis at the same angular velocity (ω) in the same direction. This planetary motion prevents twisting of the bundle of flow tubes, allowing continuous elution through a rotating column without risk of leakage and contamination.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>HSCCC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The method covers analytical (microgram) to preparative (\(\pu{10 g}\)) scale separations usually in a few hours at a high partition efficiency up to a few thousand theoretical plates (see number of theoretical plates).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>high-speed counter-current chromatography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09887"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09944"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>HSCCC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09945 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09945">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Volume of the liquid mobile phase contained in the column in counter-current chromatography \[V_{\rm{M}} = V_{\rm{C}} - V_{\rm{S}}\] where \(V_{\rm{C}}\) is the total volume of the column and \(V_{\rm{S}}\) is the volume of the liquid stationary phase in the column.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>mobile-phase volume</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09887"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09946 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09946">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Separation column used for high-speed counter-current chromatography.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The multilayer coil is prepared from a long piece of polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) tubing by coaxially winding it around a spool-shaped holder, making multiple coiled layers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Usually, three different inner diameter sizes of tubing are used: ca. \(\pu{1 mm}\) for analytical, \(\pu{1.6 mm}\) for semipreparative, and \(\pu{2.6 mm}\) for preparative separations. It provides efficient mixing of the two phases and a high retention of the stationary phase under a high flow rate of the mobile phase.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>multilayer coil</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09944"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09947 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09947">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Counter-current chromatography that produces a train of fused rectangular solute peaks called pH-zones, each with its specific pH. Each zone contains highly concentrated solute, often near saturation level, and is separated from the neighbouring zone by a narrow strip called a mixing zone, which contains a mixture of impurities.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>pH-zone-refining counter-current chromatography</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09887"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09948 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09948">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Most important reagents in pH-zone-refining counter-current chromatography. A retainer is added to the stationary organic phase to retain the analytes and the eluter added to the mobile aqueous phase to elute the analytes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Trifluoroacetic acid and triethylamine are typical retainers for the separation of acidic and basic compounds, respectively, where the stationary phase typically is the organic phase. The retainer determines the analyte concentration in the stationary phase. NH4OH and HCl are commonly used as the eluter for the separation of acidic and basic compounds, respectively. The eluter always stays in the aqueous mobile phase to serve as a counter ion. pH-zone-refining CCC can also be performed using the organic phase as the mobile phase (normal displacement mode). In this case, the retainer becomes the eluter to elute the analytes, and the eluter becomes the retainer to retain the analytes in the aqueous stationary phase.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>retainer and eluter</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09947"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09949 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09949">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Volume of the stationary phase, which stays permanently within the column in counter-current chromatography.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:comment>\(V_{\rm{s}}\) is one of the most important parameters that determines the peak resolution.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>retention volume of the stationary phase</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09887"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09950 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09950">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Centrifuge that provides type-J synchronous planetary motion to the column holder, the axis of which is parallel to the centrifuge axis (see figure in high-speed counter-current chromatography).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>type-J coil planet centrifuge</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09944"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09951"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09951 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09951">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Unique type of planetary motion (so named because the flow tubes form a letter J figure, as shown in the figure in high-speed counter-current chromatography) of the column holder, which rotates around its own axis once during one revolution around the central axis of the centrifuge in the same direction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The system prevents the flow tubes from twisting during column rotation, and produces an excellent mixing and high retention of the stationary phase in a multilayer coil separation column.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>type-J synchronous planetary motion</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09944"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09952 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09952">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chiral selector that has been added as a component of a mobile phase or electrophoretic medium.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>chiral additive</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09953 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09953">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Type of two-dimensional chromatography in which all the effluent from the first separation step is transferred in fractions to a second separation step.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>comprehensive chromatography</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The multiplier (\(\times\)) is used to denote the two separation steps, e.g. \(GC \times GC\) or \(LC \times SFC\) .</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>comprehensive two-dimensional chromatography</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09954 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09954">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Type of two-dimensional chromatography in which selected fractions from the first separation step are transferred to a second separation step.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>heart-cut separations</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09955 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09955">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of modulations in a given time interval.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>modulation frequency</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09956 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09956">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Number of modulations for a given first-dimension peak.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>modulation number</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09957 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09957">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Duration of a complete cycle of modulation in a system for comprehensive two-dimensional chromatography.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>modulation time</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Modulation period is the length of a second-dimension chromatogram, i.e. the time between two successive injections into the second column.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>modulation period</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09953"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09958 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09958">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Interface device between the two columns in a system for comprehensive two-dimensional chromatography that accumulates or samples narrow bands from the effluent of the first column for fast re-injection into the second column.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>modulator</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09953"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09959 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09959">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Chromatography in which parts or all of the separated sample components are subjected to additional separation steps.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Multi-dimensional chromatography can be done, for example, by conducting a particular fraction eluting from the column into other columns (system) having different separation characteristics.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>multi-dimensional chromatography</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09960 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09960">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Occurrence of second dimension peaks in subsequent elution sequences, caused by second-dimension retention times that exceed the modulation time in comprehensive two-dimensional chromatography.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>wrap-around</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09953"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09957"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09962 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09962">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Retention volume of a sample component the molecules of which are larger than the largest pores of the particles of the stationary phase.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Ignoring any extra-column volume, \(V_{0}\) is equal to the interparticle volume of the column.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The retention volume of an unretained compound is related to the retention time of an unretained compound (in size-exclusion chromatography) and the flow rate (\(F_{\rm{c}}\)) by \(V_{0} = t_{0} \times F_{\rm{c}}\)</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>These molecules will be eluted first from the column.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>retention volume of an unretained compound</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09963"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10040"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09963 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09963">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Retention volume of a sample component, the molecules of which are smaller than the largest pores of the particles of the stationary phase but larger than the smallest pores.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The corresponding retention time is related to the retention time (in size-exclusion chromatography) (\(t_{\rm{R}}\)) and the flow rate (\(F_{\rm{c}}\)) by \(V_{\rm{R}} = t_{\rm{R}} \times F_{\rm{c}}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>retention volume</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09964 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09964">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Retention volume of a sample component, the molecules of which are smaller than the smallest pores of the particles of the stationary phase.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The total mobile phase volume is related to the total mobile phase time (in size-exclusion chromatography) (\(t_{\rm{t}}\)) and the flow rate (\(F_{\rm{c}}\)) by \(V_{\rm{t}} = t_{\rm{t}} \times F_{\rm{c}}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>total mobile phase volume</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09963"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09965 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09965">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Maximum value of the relative molecular masses of components in a specific polymer solvent system that can enter into the pores of the porous non-adsorbing material used in size-exclusion chromatography.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>molecular-weight exclusion limit</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>For particles with relative molar mass greater than the exclusion limit the separation effect of the size-exclusion chromatography vanishes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>relative molecular-mass exclusion limit</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09966 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09966">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Peak resolution also considering the molar masses (\(M_{1}\), \(M_{2}\)) of the two test compounds: \[R_{\rm{sp}} = \left[\frac{V_{\rm{R1}} - V_{\rm{R2}}}{(w_{\rm{b1}} + w_{\rm{b2}})/2} \right] \cdot \left[ \frac{1}{\log (M_{2}/M_{1})} \right]\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In some areas, the symbol \(R_{\rm{s}}\) is used for the specific resolution. Due to the possibility of confusing it with the general resolution term (see peak resolution), the symbol \(R_{\rm{sp}}\) is suggested here.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The test compounds used for the determination of the specific resolution should have a narrow molecular-mass distribution (the ratio of the mass-average and number-average molecular masses should be equal to or less than about \(\pu{1.1}\)) and differ by a factor of about \(\pu{10}\) in their molar masses.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>specific resolution</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09967 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09967">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Flavans are compounds with a 2-phenyl-3,4-dihydro-2\(H\)-1-benzopyran skeleton (1 in flavonoids), that may be substituted, and include flavan-3-ols (compounds derived from 2-phenyl-3,4-dihydro-2\(H\)-1-benzopyran-3-ol skeleton 2) and flavan-4-ones (compounds derived from 2-phenyl-2,3-dihydro-4\(H\)-1-benzopyran-4-one skeleton 3), which are generically designated as flavanols and flavanones, respectively. Compounds derived from 2-phenyl-3,4-dihydro-2\(H\)-1-benzopyran-3,4-diol skeleton 4 are flavan-3,4-diols, designated leukoanthocyanidins because these colourless compounds have their structure derived from that of an anthocyanidin, specifically they are dehydronated 3,4-dihydroxy-1,2,3,4-tetrahydroanthocyanidins.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2013-0919"/>
        <rdfs:label>flavans</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09968 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09968">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Flavones and 3-hydroxyflavones (flavonols) are compounds with a 2-phenyl-4\(H\)-1-benzopyran-4-one (2-phenyl-4\(H\)-chromen-4-one) skeleton 5.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2013-0919"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;flavonol&quot; is used as a class name for compounds with a 3-hydroxy-2-phenyl-4\(H\)-1-benzopyran-4-one (3-hydroxy-2-phenyl-4\(H\)-chromen-4-one) skeleton 6.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>flavones</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09970 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09970">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Isoflavans are compounds with a 3-phenyl-3,4-dihydro-2\(H\)-1-benzopyran skeleton (see isoflavonoids). They may be substituted.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2013-0919"/>
        <rdfs:label>isoflavans</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09969"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09971 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09971">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Isoflavones are compounds with a 3-phenyl-4$H$-1-benzopyran-4-one (3-phenyl-4$H$-chromen-4-one) skeleton 9. They may be substituted.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2013-0919"/>
        <rdfs:label>isoflavones</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09973 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09973">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Neoflavans are compounds with a 4-phenyl-3,4-dihydro-2$H$-1-benzopyran skeleton (see neoflavonoids). They may be substituted.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2013-0919"/>
        <rdfs:label>neoflavans</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09972"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09974 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09974">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Neoflavones are compounds with a 4-phenyl-2$H$-1-benzopyran-2-one (4-phenyl-2$H$-chromen-2-one) skeleton 11. They may be substituted.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2013-0919"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>4-phenylcoumarins</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>neoflavones</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09975 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09975">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Aurones are compounds derived from (2$Z$)-2-benzylidene-1-benzofuran-3(2$H$)-one [alternative name: (2$Z$)-2-(phenylmethylidene)-1-benzofuran-3(2$H$)-one] (aurone, compound 14).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2013-0919"/>
        <rdfs:label>aurones</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09976 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09976">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Pterocarpans are compounds derived from (6a$R$,11a$R$)-6a,11a-dihydro-6$H$-[1]benzofuro[3,2-$c$][1]benzopyran (15), and their 6a,11a-didehydro derivatives 16 are named coumestans. They are numbered systematically following the numbering rules for fused ring systems.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2013-0919"/>
        <rdfs:label>pterocarpans</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09977 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09977">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Flavonolignans have structures based on flavonoids condensed with \(\ce{C6{-}C3}\) lignan precursors.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2013-0919"/>
        <rdfs:label>flavonolignans</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09978 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09978">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Biflavonoid and other flavonoid oligomers, which occur widely in nature, are exemplified and named in this recommendation (Flv-3.8).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2013-0919"/>
        <rdfs:label>biflavonoids</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Property of a phenomenon, body, or substance, where the property has no size.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>&quot;Nominal property&quot; is sometimes termed &quot;nameable property&quot;, but not here.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The concept &quot;nominal property&quot; is defined as the opposite of &quot;quantity&quot;, i.e. the former concept lacks the essential characteristic of &quot;size&quot; (or &quot;magnitude&quot;). In such cases, ISO 704:2009-6.5.4 allows a &quot;negative definition&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;attribute&quot; has sometimes been used to designate &quot;nominal property&quot;, but not here.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;qualitative property&quot; is also used, but not here as it is ambiguous because &quot;ordinal quantity&quot; is often included under that term.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nominal property</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09982"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09980 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09980">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Defining aspect, common to mutually comparable nominal properties.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>&quot;Kind-of-nominal-property&quot; is sometimes termed &quot;attribute&quot;, but not here.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>kind-of-nominal-property</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09982"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09981 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09981">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Kind-of-nominal-property indicating a class among nominal properties ordered according to nominal property values of a specified nominal property value set without relation to size.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:label>category</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09980"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09996"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09997"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09982 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09982">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Category indicating a class of organisms within a recognized hierarchically structured nominal property value set.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Taxonomy is the science of classification and its applications, usually regarding organisms.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>taxon</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09980"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09981"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09996"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09997"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09983 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09983">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Kind-of-nominal-property with a sort of system and any pertinent sort(s) of component(s).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A dedicated kind-of-nominal-property is not specified to any individual person, in contrast to an individual nominal property, and it cannot have a nominal property value.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In clinical laboratory report forms, dedicated kind-of-nominal-properties may be described according to an internationally recommended NPU syntax from IFCC-IUPAC. The syntax is System(specification)-Component(specification); kind-of-property(specification). The last &quot;specification&quot; might contain information on the examination procedure or the nominal property value set. The initial capital for System and for Component is mandatory.Examples of the NPU syntax:.Blood—Plasma; colour({milky, red, yellow});.Erythrocytes(Blood)—Erythrocyte antigen; blood group({A, B, AB, O}).For examples 1-4 above, individual nominal properties can be written as follows (omitting patient identity and time but including the nominal property value for clarity):Patient—Spinal fluid; colour(visual)=reddish;DNA(Leukocytes)—CYP2D6 gene; sequence variation(allele specific PCR)=*1/*3;Expectoration—Bacterium; taxon(Phadebact coagglutination test)=Mycobacterium tuberculosis;Monocytes(Blood)—Nuclei; shape=oval.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The dedicated kinds-of-nominal-property examples above correspond to examples 1-4 of kind-of-nominal-property.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The patient&quot;s name (or other identifier), location, and date and time of obtaining the sample of the system under consideration shall always be given in the clinical laboratory report.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dedicated kind-of-nominal-property</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09980"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09982"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09990"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09996"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09997"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09984 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09984">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process of experimentally obtaining one or more nominal property values that can reasonably be attributed to a nominal property.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The activity of examination essentially consists in comparing the property considered, i.e. the examinand by way of an examining system, with the property of a &quot;reference&quot; of similar nature. Such a reference may be personal and subjective, such as a person&quot;s memory of a colour, or the reference may be objective, such as a nominal reference material.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The outcome of an examination is an examination result.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>examination</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09985"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09986"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09996"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09999"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10020"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09985 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09985">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nominal property intended to be examined.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The examined value may be obtained indirectly through examinations of other nominal properties, giving the examined value by using an algorithm which may involve measurement results and examination results.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The nominal property value of an examinand may be different from that of the property actually being examined due to changes of the system bearing the property during the examination.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>examinand</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09982"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09984"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09996"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09999"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10000"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09986 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09986">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Set of one or more devices, including any reagent and supply, assembled and adapted to give information used to generate examined values from a nominal property value set.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A human eye may be an essential element of an examining system.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>examining system</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09997"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10000"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09987 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09987">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nominal property that, in an examination, does not affect the nominal property that is actually examined, but affects the relation between the nominal indication on the examining system and the examination result.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A quantity might also influence a nominal property.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>influence nominal property</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09984"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09986"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09991"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09999"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09988 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09988">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Phenomenon serving as a basis of an examination.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The phenomenon can be of a physical, chemical, or biological nature.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>examination principle</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09984"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09989 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09989">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Generic description of a logical organization of operations used in an examination.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The laconic description in an examination method is insufficient to allow an examination with prescribed examination uncertainty, but aids in formulating one or more examination procedures.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>examination method</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09984"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09990"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10004"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09990 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09990">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Detailed description of an examination according to one or more examination principles and to a given examination method and any decision algorithm necessary to obtain an examination result.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>An examination procedure can include a statement concerning a target examination uncertainty.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>An examination procedure specifies the dedicated kind-of-nominal-property involved, any sampling, examining system, nominal reference material(s) needed, and the nominal property value set used. The examination procedure also specifies how many examined values that are necessary to obtain an examination result and how to estimate the expected examination uncertainty.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The information presented in an examination procedure is intended to be operational and should be sufficient for a trained operator to perform an examination satisfactorily.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>examination procedure</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09983"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09984"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09986"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09988"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09989"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09997"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09999"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10000"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10004"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10013"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10020"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09991 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09991">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nominal property value provided by an examining system.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:label>nominal indication</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09986"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09996"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09992 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09992">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nominal indication obtained from an object similar to the one under investigation, except that the nominal property of interest is supposed not to be present, or is not contributing to the nominal indication.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A blank nominal indication is necessary for quality assessment in some examination procedures.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A blank nominal indication shall not be confused with a quantity value below a detection limit.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A blank nominal indication, when the examination procedure is expected to produce a nominal property value, is an erroneous nominal property value.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The concept is often termed &quot;negative control&quot;, but not here.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>blank nominal indication</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09990"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09991"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09996"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09993 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09993">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Condition of examination out of a set of conditions that includes the same examination procedure, same operators, same examining system, same operating conditions, same location, and replicate examinations on the same or similar objects over a short period of time.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:label>repeatability condition</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09984"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09986"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09990"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09994 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09994">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Condition of examination out of a set of conditions that includes the same examination procedure, same location, and replicate examinations on the same or similar objects over an extended period of time, but may include other conditions involving changes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:label>intermediate precision condition</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09984"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09990"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09995 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09995">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Condition of examination out of a set of conditions that includes different locations, operators, examining systems, and replicate examinations on the same or similar objects.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The different examining systems may use different examination procedures.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>reproducibility condition</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09984"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09986"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09990"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09996 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09996">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Feature common to equivalent individual nominal properties.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A nominal property value can consist of a set of items.Example: The colours of the national flag of Switzerland are red and white.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Nominal property values can be words, alphanumerical codes, symbols, etc, but cannot enter into algebraic equations and is not related to a quantity dimension or a measurement unit.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;nominal quantity value&quot; and its short form &quot;nominal value&quot; are used to indicate a concept concerning rounded or approximate quantity values. The term &quot;nominal indication interval&quot; is used to indicate a concept concerning rounded or approximate extreme indications.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nominal property value</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09980"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09982"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09997 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09997">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Set of possible nominal property values for nominal properties of a given kind-of-nominal-property.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A &quot;nominal property value set&quot; can be ordered by convention, for example in alphabetic order of words, or alphanumerical codes identifying nominal property values, or an order reflecting a classification, but neither of these is an ordering by size.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nominal property value set</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09980"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09996"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09998 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09998">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nominal property value used as a basis for comparison with nominal property values of nominal properties of the same kind-of-nominal-property.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A reference nominal property value can be a true nominal property value of an examinand, in which case it is unknown, or a conventional nominal property value, in which case it is known.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>reference nominal property value</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09980"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09985"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09996"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10001"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10002"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09999 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09999">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Set of nominal property values being attributed to an examinand together with any other available relevant information.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>&quot;Examination result&quot; should not be confused with nominal property value set.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>An &quot;examination result&quot; may be expressed as a single examined value and an examination uncertainty. If there is no dispersion of nominal property values attributed to the examinand or if the examination uncertainty is considered to be negligible for a specified purpose, the examination result may be expressed as a single examined value.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>An &quot;examination result&quot; sometimes contains &quot;relevant information&quot; about the set of nominal property values, such that some may be more representative of the examinand than others.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>examination result</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09985"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09996"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09997"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10000"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10002"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10004"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10000 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10000">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nominal property value representing an examination result.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;observed value&quot; is sometimes used for &quot;examined value&quot;, but not here.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>examined value</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09996"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09999"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10001 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10001">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nominal property value consistent with the definition of a nominal property.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:label>true nominal property value</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09996"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10002 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10002">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nominal property value attributed by agreement to a nominal property for a given purpose.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:label>conventional nominal property value</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09996"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10003 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10003">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Fraction of examined values identical to one or more reference nominal property values among all the examined values provided.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Each examined value is either equivalent, or non-equivalent, to the reference nominal property value.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The examination trueness, in contrast to measurement trueness, is a quantity, and is complementary to examination uncertainty.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>examination trueness</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09984"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09997"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09998"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10000"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10004"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10004 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10004">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Fraction of examined values that is different from a reference nominal property value among all the examined values provided.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Examination uncertainty is a part of an examination result.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Examination uncertainty is a quantity that is complementary to examination trueness.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>With exception of the situation where the examination uncertainty is zero, some examined values differ from the reference nominal property value. In a comment to the examination result, the laboratory can provide information about other possible nominal property values, based on the information available.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>examination uncertainty</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09984"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09990"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09996"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09997"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09998"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09999"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10000"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10003"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10005 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10005">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Disagreement between an examined value and a reference nominal property value.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>An examination error is either present or absent.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Examination error is associated with an examined value, but not with an examining system.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Examination error should not be confused with production error or mistake.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>examination error</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09986"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09998"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10000"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10006 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10006">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Closeness of agreement between an examined value and a true nominal property value of an examinand.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Examination accuracy is a feature of an individual examined value, but not of an examining system.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>examination accuracy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09985"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09986"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10000"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10001"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10007 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10007">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Closeness of agreement between nominal indications on an examining system or examined values obtained by replicate examinations on the same or similar objects under specified examination conditions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>&quot;Specified examination conditions&quot; can, for example, be repeatability condition of examination, intermediate precision condition of examination, or reproducibility condition of examination.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A proportion distribution of a set of examined values describes proportions of data belonging to every possible nominal property value.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Examination precision can be expressed numerically by measures of dispersion of examined values such as Index of Qualitative Variation (IQV) and the Shannon&quot;s entropy, under specified examination conditions. IQV varies between 0 (no dispersion, all examined values in a single class) and 1 (two or more classes with identical frequency). Shannon&quot;s entropy varies between 0 (no dispersion, that is all examined values belong to a single category) and a maximum value equal to the logarithm (on basis 2) of the number of all categories. See Example.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Examination precision is a feature of an examining system used with a specified examination procedure.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Examination precision is used to define examination repeatability, intermediate examination precision, and examination reproducibility.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>examination precision</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09984"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09986"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09991"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09997"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10000"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10008 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10008">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Examination precision under repeatability condition of examination.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Examination repeatability is a feature of an examining system used with a specified examination procedure.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Examination repeatability is a quantity.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>examination repeatability</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09986"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09990"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09993"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10007"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10009 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10009">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Examination precision under intermediate precision condition of examination.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Intermediate examination precision is a feature of an examining system used with a specified examination procedure.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Intermediate examination precision is a quantity.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>intermediate examination precision</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09986"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09990"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09994"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10007"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10010 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10010">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Examination precision under reproducibility condition of examination.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Examination reproducibility is a feature of a set of examined values.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Examination reproducibility is a quantity.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>examination reproducibility</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09995"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10000"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10007"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10011 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10011">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Fraction of examined values with examination error that in replicate examinations remains constant or varies in a predictable manner.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Systematic examination error is a feature of an examining system used with a specified examination procedure.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Systematic examination errors for examined values are quantities.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The systematic examination error for examined values can be expressed for a set of examined values as examination bias.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>systematic examination error</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09984"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09986"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09990"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09998"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10000"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10005"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10012"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10012 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10012">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Estimate of a systematic examination error.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Examination bias is a quantity.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>If the proportion distributions of the reference nominal property values and the examined values are the same, there is no examination bias for the examining system. If the distributions differ there is an examination bias.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In some publications on analytical chemistry, &quot;examination bias&quot; is termed &quot;lack of reliability&quot;, but this is not recommended here.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>examination bias</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09986"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09998"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10000"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10011"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10022"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10013 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10013">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Examination uncertainty specified as an acceptable fraction and decided on the basis of the intended use of examination results.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Target examination uncertainty is a quantity.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>target examination uncertainty</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09999"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10004"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10014 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10014">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Subset of the nominal property value set containing the true nominal property value(s) of an examinand with a stated probability, based on the information available.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:label>nominal property value coverage set</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09985"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09997"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10001"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10015 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10015">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Probability that the set of true nominal property values of an examinand is contained within a specified nominal property value coverage set.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:label>nominal property value coverage probability</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09985"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10001"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10014"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10016 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10016">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Property of an examination result whereby it can be related to a reference through a documented unbroken chain of examination calibrations, each contributing to the examination uncertainty.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The ISO Technical report (TR 79:2015) characterizes reference materials for &quot;qualitative properties&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>examination traceability</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09999"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10004"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10021"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10017 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10017">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Comparability of examination results, for nominal properties of a given kind-of-nominal-property, that are examined and traceable to the same reference.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:label>examinational comparability</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09980"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09999"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10018 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10018">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Property of a set of examination results for a specified examinand of a given kind-of-nominal-property that have overlapping nominal property value sets.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The definition of this concept is not analogous to that of &quot;metrological compatibility of measurement results&quot; in the VIM.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Two compatible examination results can be identical.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>examinational compatibility</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09980"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09985"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09997"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09999"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10019 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10019">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Realization of the definition of a given nominal property (2.1), with stated nominal property value (3.1) and associated examination uncertainty (3.9), used as a reference.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A &quot;realization of the definition of a given nominal property&quot; can be provided by an examining system or a nominal reference material.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>examination standard</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09982"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09986"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09996"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10000"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10004"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10020"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10020 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10020">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Material, sufficiently homogeneous and stable with reference to a specified nominal property, that has been established to be fit for its intended use in an examination.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Nominal reference materials with or without assigned reference nominal property values can be used for control of examination precision, whereas only nominal reference materials with assigned reference nominal property values can be used for the control of examination calibration or evaluation of examination trueness.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nominal reference material</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09984"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09998"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10003"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10007"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10021"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10021 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10021">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process that confers to one or more persons or to a device the capacity to provide nominal property values from specified examinations after having examined one or more examination standards.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Examination calibration differs from that of calibration in the VIM, because in examination calibration there is no explicit mention of &quot;nominal indication&quot; and &quot;examination uncertainty&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>examination calibration</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09984"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09996"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10019"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10022 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10022">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Examination procedure accepted as providing examination results fit for their intended use in assessing examination trueness of examined values) obtained from other examination procedures for nominal properties of the same kind-of-nominal-property, in examination calibration, or in characterizing nominal reference materials.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:label>reference examination procedure</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09980"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09984"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09990"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09999"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10000"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10003"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10020"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10021"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10023 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10023">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Reference examination procedure used to obtain an examination result without relation to an examination standard for a nominal property of the same kind-of-nominal-property.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:label>primary reference examination procedure</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09980"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09999"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10019"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10022"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10024 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10024">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Examination standard recognized by signatories to an international agreement and intended to serve worldwide.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:label>international examination standard</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10019"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10025 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10025">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Examination standard recognized by national authority to serve in a state or economy as the basis for assigning nominal property values to other examination standards for the same kind-of-nominal-property.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:label>national examination standard</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09980"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09985"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09990"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09996"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10019"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10020"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10026 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10026">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Examination standard established using a primary reference examination procedure, or created as an artefact, chosen by convention.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:label>primary examination standard</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10019"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10023"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10027 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10027">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Examination standard established through examination calibration with respect to a primary examination standard for a nominal property of the same kind-of-nominal-property.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:label>secondary examination standard</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09980"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10019"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10021"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10026"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10028 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10028">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Examination standard designated for the examination calibration of other examination standards for nominal properties of a given kind-of-nominal-property in a given organization or at a given location.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:label>reference examination standard</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09980"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10019"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10021"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10029 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10029">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Examination standard that is used routinely to calibrate or verify examining systems.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A working examination standard is usually calibrated with respect to a reference examination standard.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>working examination standard</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09986"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10019"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10028"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10030 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10030">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Examination standard, sometimes of special construction, intended for transport between different locations.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:label>travelling examination standard</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10019"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10031 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10031">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Device used as an intermediary to compare examination standards.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Sometimes, examination standards are used as transfer examination devices.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>transfer examination device</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10019"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10032 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10032">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Examination standard based on a reproducible nominal property of a phenomenon or substance.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A nominal property value of an intrinsic examination standard is assigned by consensus and does not need to be established by relating it to another examination standard of the same type.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>intrinsic examination standard</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09996"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10019"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10033 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10033">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Set of operations necessary to preserve the examinational properties of an examination standard within stated limits.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Conservation of an examination standard commonly includes periodic verification of predefined nominal properties or examination calibration, storage under suitable conditions, and specified care in use.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>conservation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09979"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10019"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10021"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10034 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10034">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Examination standard used in examination calibration.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:label>examination calibrator</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10019"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10021"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10035 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10035">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Nominal reference material accompanied by documentation issued by an authoritative body and providing one or more specified nominal property values with examination uncertainty and examination traceability, using valid examination procedures.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2011-0613"/>
        <rdfs:label>certified nominal reference material</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09990"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.09996"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10004"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10016"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10020"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10036 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10036">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Linear velocity of the mobile phase across the average cross-section of the column.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>mobile-phase speed</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10037 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10037">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Total retention time (\(t_{\rm{R}}\)) minus hold-up time (\(t_{\rm{M}}\)). \(t_{\rm{R}}^{\prime} = t_{\rm{R}} - t_{\rm{M}}\)</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:label>adjusted retention time</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10038"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10038 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10038">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Time required to elute a component which is not retained by the stationary phase.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The hold-up time includes any time that the component spends in the sample injector, the detector, and connectors.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The hold-up time is related to the hold-up volume (\(V_{\rm{M}}\)) and flow rate (\(F_{\rm{c}}\)) by \(t_{\rm{M}} = V_{\rm{M}}⁄F_{\rm{c}}\) .</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>hold-up time</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10039 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10039">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Time between sample injection and the emergence of the peak maximum of the sample component of interest.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The total retention time is related to the total retention volume (\(V_{\rm{R}}\)) and flow rate (\(F_{\rm{c}}\)) by \(t_{\rm{R}} = V_{\rm{R}}⁄F_{\rm{c}}\) .</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Total retention time includes the hold-up time.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>total retention time</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10038"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10040 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10040">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Retention time of a sample component the molecules of which are larger than the largest pores of the particles of the stationary phase.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2017-0111"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The retention time of an unretained compound is related to the retention volume of an unretained compound (in size-exclusion chromatography) and the flow rate (\(F_{\rm{c}}\)) by \(t_{0} = V_{0}/F_{\rm{c}}\) </rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>This component will be eluted first from the column.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>retention time of an unretained compound</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10042 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10042">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measured or observed quantity in an experimental design.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:label>response</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10071"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10043 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10043">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Correlation of a variable with itself over a successive time or space intervals (lags).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>If the mean of autocorrelated data is estimated the standard deviation of the mean depends on sampling mode.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>autocorrelation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10044 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10044">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Variance scaling of mean-centered data.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:label>autoscaling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10053"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10064"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10045 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10045">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Data, values of which are one of a fixed number of nominal categories.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Data in a contingency table are categorical.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>categorical data</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10046"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10046 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10046">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Type of table in a matrix format that displays the multivariate frequency distribution of variables.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>cross tabulation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The entries in the cells of a contingency table can be frequency counts or relative frequencies.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>contingency table</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10045"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10047 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10047">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Measurement results on a system arranged in a \(m \times n\) matrix, with \(m\) objects and \(n\) variables.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>By convention a matrix is arranged with \(m\) rows and \(n\) columns.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>For multi-way data the data is arranged in a hypercube of \(m \times n_{1} \times n_{2} \times\) …, where \(n_{1}\), \(n_{2}\) … are the kinds of explanatory variable.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Objects are also called &quot;samples&quot;, but confusion with physical &quot;test samples&quot; in analytical chemistry should be avoided.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Variables are also called features, or explanatory variables.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>data matrix</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10051"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10056"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10048 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10048">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Manipulation of raw data prior to a specified data analysis treatment.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>All data pre-processing methods imply some assumptions about the nature of the variability in the data set. It is important that these assumptions are understood and appropriate for the data set involved.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Aside from the three main categories of data pre-processing methods (mean centering, scaling and transformation), data pre-processing can refer to any other procedures carried out on the raw data, including mass binning and peak selection. In the case of multivariate images, this can also include region-of-interest selection and image filtering or binning.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>More than one data pre-processing method can be applied to the same data set. The order of data pre-processing is important and can affect assumptions made on the nature of variance in the data set.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;pre-processing&quot; is preferred to the term &quot;pre-treatment&quot; to reduce confusion with physical sample preparation or treatment prior to experimental analysis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>data pre-processing</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10053"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10058"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10060"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10049 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10049">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process of synchronizing a data matrix so that it represents the same time shifts.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The method is used in chromatography to align peaks by their retention times.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dynamic time warping</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10050 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10050">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Data used to validate a model.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>validation data</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>&quot;Test data&quot; is also used for data from an unknown sample, and should not be used for &quot;evaluation data&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Evaluation data should be independent of the data used to calibrate or train a model.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>evaluation data</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10077"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10051 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10051">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Variable that influences the value of a response variable, and is used to build models of the response variable.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:label>explanatory variable</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10052 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10052">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Summary of the main characteristics of data, often using graphical methods.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>EDA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>initial data analysis</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Exploratory data analysis is recommended before deciding an approach to chemometric modelling.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>exploratory data analysis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10052"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>EDA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10053 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10053">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Data pre-processing in which the mean value of a variable is subtracted from data across all objects. \[a_{i,j}^{\ast} = a_{i,j} - \frac{1}{n} \sum \limits_{i\,=\,1}^{n} a_{i,j}\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>centering</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Mean centering emphasises the differences between samples rather than differences between the samples and the variable’s origin (zero).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Mean centering is generally applied with other data pre-processing methods.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Mean centering is generally recommended for principal component analysis, partial least squares and discriminant analysis of data, where relative values across the samples are more important than their absolute deviation from zero. Mean centering is not compatible with non-negativity constraints in, for example, multivariate curve resolution.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>mean centering</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10048"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10060"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10100"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10106"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10155"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10054 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10054">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Data pre-processing in which a constant and a multiple of a reference data set is subtracted from data.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>MSC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>multiplicative signal correction</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>MSC is typically used in near-infrared spectrometry to remove effects of non-homogeneous particle size.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>multiplicative scatter correction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10048"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10054"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>MSC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10055 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10055">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Data having two or more variables per object.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The measurements results are often of the same kind of quantity.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>multivariate data</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10056 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10056">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Multivariate data having two or more kinds of explanatory variable per object.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>N-way data</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>For two groups of explanatory variables the data is termed ‘three-way’.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Models that decompose multi-way data include PARAFAC, Tucker3 model.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>multi-way data</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10051"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10055"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10103"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10116"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10057 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10057">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sampling in which the sample locations are selected randomly from the whole population.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The population defines the kind of quantity of the location. For example, in a time series, the quantity giving the location is time, in QSAR the location is a point in design space.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>random sampling</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10058 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10058">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Data not yet subjected to analysis.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>primary data</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Raw data can be an indication obtained from a measuring instrument or measuring system</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>raw data</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10059 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10059">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Defined quantity of material having a boundary which may be physical or temporal.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A number of sampling units may be gathered together, for example in a package or box.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Examples of physical boundaries are capsules, containers, and bottles.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sampling unit</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10060 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10060">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Element-wise division of a data matrix by a scaling matrix.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>weighting</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>scaling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10044"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10047"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10064"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10061 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10061">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Transformation using an approximating function to capture important patterns in data, while removing noise or other fine-scale structures.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:label>smoothing</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10041"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10062 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10062">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sampling in which individual samples are taken at equal intervals in location.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In a time series, the quantity giving the location is time.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The starting point may be assigned randomly within the first stratum.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>systematic sampling</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10063 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10063">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Happenstance data that must be processed, or not processed, as is.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>TILI</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>take-it-or-leave-it data</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10063"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>TILI</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10064 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10064">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Scaling in which the scaling matrix is the standard deviation of each variable across the objects. \[a_{i,j}^{\ast} = \frac{a_{i,j}}{s_{j}}\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A variable occupies a column of the data matrix.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Variance scaling equalizes the importance of each variable in multivariate data.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When used with mean centering variance scaling is known as autoscaling.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>variance scaling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10044"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10047"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10053"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10055"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10060"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10065 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10065">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>List of combinations of effects that are aliased (confounded).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:label>alias structure</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10066"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10066 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10066">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In a fractional-factorial design, effects for which the information obtained are identical.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>confounded effects</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In a two-level design, the product of the coded levels for the aliased effects are equal.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>aliased effects</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10068"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10073"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10067 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10067">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Matrix of runs by factor levels in which each level is denoted by a code that represents the relative magnitude of the level.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>coded design</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>coded experimental design</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10072"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10068 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10068">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coefficient of a term in a response model.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>effect</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>effect of a factor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10075"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10076"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10077"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10078"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10069 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10069">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Matrix with rows representing individual experimental treatments (possibly transformed according to the assumed model) which can be extended by deduced levels of other functions of factor levels.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:label>design matrix</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10072"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10070 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10070">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Factor that is known to have no effect on the response, used in an experimental design, to estimate repeatability standard deviation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:label>dummy factor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10042"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10071"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10071 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10071">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Efficient procedure for planning combinations of values of factors in experiments so that the data obtained can be analyzed to yield valid and objective conclusions.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DoE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>design of experiments</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>An &quot;experimental design&quot; (noun) usually refers to a table giving the levels of each factor for each run.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Experimental design is applied to determine the set of conditions that are required to obtain a product or process with desirable, often optimal properties. A characteristic of experimental design is that these conditions are determined in a statistically-optimal way.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Response surface methodology is considered an important part of experimental design.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>experimental design</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10067"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10082"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10071"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>DoE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10072 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10072">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Value of a factor in an experimental design.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>level</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A design may be designated by the number of levels chosen for each factor, as in &quot;two-level design&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When writing an experimental design the levels are usually coded.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>factor level</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10067"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10071"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10073 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10073">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Experimental design obtained from a full-factorial design in which experiments are systematically removed to fulfill stated statistical requirements.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A design, having \(L^{k}\) (see full-factorial design) experiments, is fractionated to \(L^{k - p}\) experiments where \(p\) is an integer \(\lt k\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A fractional-factorial design is incomplete, but all incomplete designs are not fractional factorial.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The aim of a fractional design is to reduce the number of experiments by confounding low-order effects (e.g. main effect, two-way interaction) with high order interactions, which are assumed to be small.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The choice of design is governed by an alias structure.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>fractional-factorial design</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10065"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10071"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10074"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10080"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10074 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10074">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Experimental design with all possible combinations of factor levels.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>If there are \(k\) factors, each at \(L\) levels, a full-factorial design has \(L^{k}\) runs.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>full-factorial design</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10071"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10072"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10075 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10075">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Effect of a factor where the term is the product of two or more factors.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:label>interaction effect</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10068"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10076"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10078"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10076 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10076">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Effect of a factor where the term is a single factor.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:label>main effect</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10068"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10075"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10078"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10077 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10077">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Equation describing the response as a function of values of the factors.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The model can be based on knowledge of the chemistry or physics of the system, but usually the model is empirical, being linear or quadratic with interaction terms.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>To obtain information about the significance of effects, data is usually mean centered and assessed against a coded experimental design.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>model</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10042"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10053"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10067"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10075"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10076"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10078"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10078 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10078">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Effect of a factor where the term is a factor raised to the power \(n\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:label>&lt;em&gt;n&lt;/em&gt;&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt;-order effect</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10068"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10075"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10076"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10079 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10079">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Minimization or maximization of a real function by systematically choosing the values of real or integer variables from within an allowed set.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:label>optimization</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10080 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10080">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Incomplete experimental design to estimate main effects for which each combination of factor levels for any pair of factors appears the same number of times.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For \(4 \times N\) experiments, \(4 \times N − 1\) main effects and the mean are estimated.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>If less than \(4 \times N − 1\) factors are being studied, dummy factors are inserted which allow estimation of the repeatability standard deviation of the measurement results.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Plackett–Burman designs are typically given for two levels, with a number of experiments that is a multiple of 4 but not a power of 2. (The latter case is a fractional-factorial design).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Plackett−Burman design</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10070"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10071"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10072"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10073"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10076"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10081 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10081">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>One more than the smallest nth-order interaction effect that some main effect is aliased with.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>resolution</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Full-factorial designs have no effects that are aliased and therefore have infinite resolution.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Resolution is used to describe the extent to which fractional-factorial designs create aliased effects.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The resolution is written as a Roman numeral.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>resolution of a design</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10066"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10073"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10074"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10075"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10076"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10082 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10082">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Experimental design in which the response is modelled in terms of one or more factor levels.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Response surface methodology is usually associated with optimization. The model used is typically a quadratic function leading to a maximum or minimum response in the factor space.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;surface&quot; implies two factors and a single response, when a plot of the modelled response as a function of values of the factors leads to a surface in the three-dimensional space. This can be generalized to any number of factors.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>response surface methodology</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10042"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10071"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10072"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10079"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10083 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10083">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Solution to the multivariate decomposition of a data matrix in which iteratively a solution of one output matrix is used to compute the second matrix, after the application of constraints.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ALS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>alternating regression</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>ALS is used to decompose multiple spectra (\(\boldsymbol{X}\)) into concentration (\(\boldsymbol{C}\)) and component spectra (\(\boldsymbol{S}\)). \(\boldsymbol{X} = \boldsymbol{C}\boldsymbol{S}^{\rm{T}}\). Because spectra and concentrations cannot be negative at each iteration negative values are set to zero.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>alternating least squares regression</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10055"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10083"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ALS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10084 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10084">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Stochastic process in which future values are estimated based on a weighted sum of past values.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A process called AR(1) is a first order process, meaning that the current value is based on the immediately preceding value. An AR(2) process has the current value based on the previous two values.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>autoregression</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10085 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10085">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Combination plot of a scores plot as points and loadings plot as vectors for common factors.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Points in the scores plot (objects) that fall on a loadings vector are considered to be characterised by the variable associated with the vector.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The plots are scaled to facilitate interpretation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>biplot</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10096"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10097"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10113"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10086 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10086">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Estimation of parameters by multiple re-sampling from measured data to approximate its distribution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Bootstrapping is an alternative to cross validation in model validation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Multiple resamples of the original data allow calculation of the distribution of a parameter of interest, and therefore its standard error (see example).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Random sampling with replacement is used when the data are assumed to be from an independent and identically-distributed population.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>bootstrapping</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10087 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10087">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Linear combinations of data with the greatest correlation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:label>canonical variables</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10088"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10088 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10088">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Multivariate technique which finds linear combinations of two sets of data that are most highly correlated.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>canonical analysis</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The combinations with the greatest correlation, denoted U1 and V1 are known as the &quot;first canonical variables&quot;. The next canonical functions, U2 and V2 are then restricted so that they are uncorrelated with U1 and V1. Everything is scaled so that the variance equals 1.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The relationship between the canonical variables is known as the canonical function.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>canonical variate analysis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10089 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10089">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Factor analysis in which latent variables are calculated that maximise the correlation with observed variables.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>exploratory factor analysis</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>factor analysis</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The common factors are not unique. Typically factors are rotated so that the factors are more easily interpreted in terms of the original variables.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>common factor analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10093"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10095"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10090 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10090">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Method to assess the appropriateness of a PARAFAC model.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CONCORDIA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>An appropriate PARAFAC model is a model where the components primarily reflect low-rank, trilinear variation in the data.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The principle of the method is to assess the degree of superdiagonality of the model.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>core consistency diagnostic</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10103"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10090"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CONCORDIA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10091 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10091">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Factor analysis applied to categorical data in which orthogonal factors are obtained from a contingency table.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>correspondence analysis</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>correspondence factor analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10093"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10092 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10092">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Factor analysis that follows the change or evolution of the rank of the data matrix as a function of an ordered variable.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>EFA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The changing rank is calculated by principal-component analysis on an increasing data matrix.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The ordering variable may be time.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>evolving factor analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10093"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10106"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10092"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>EFA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10093 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10093">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Matrix decomposition of a data matrix (\(\boldsymbol{X}\)) into the product of a scores matrix (\(\boldsymbol{T}\)) and the transpose of the loadings matrix (\(\boldsymbol{P}^{\rm{T}}\)).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Factor analysis is equivalent to a rotation in data space where the factors form the new axes. This is not necessarily rotation that maintains orthogonality except in the case of PCA.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Factor analysis methods include common factor analysis (also called ‘factor analysis’), principal component analysis, and multivariate curve resolution.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Hence \(\boldsymbol{X} = \boldsymbol{TP}^{\rm{T}} + \boldsymbol{E}\), where \(\boldsymbol{E}\) is a residual matrix.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The number of factors selected in factor analysis is smaller than the rank of the data matrix.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The residual matrix contains data that are not described by the factor analysis model, and is usually assumed to contain noise.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>factor analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10041"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10047"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10089"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10096"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10100"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10106"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10112"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10094 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10094">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Cross validation of a data set of \(N\) objects in which \(N/G\) objects are removed at each iteration of the procedure.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Because the perturbation of the model is larger than in leave-one-out cross validation, the prediction ability of the G-fold cross validation is less optimistic than obtained with leave-one-out cross validation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Objects 1 to \(N/G\) are removed on the first iteration, then objects \(N/G + 1\) to \(2N/G\) after replacement of the first \(N/G\) objects, and so on.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>&lt;em&gt;G&lt;/em&gt;-fold cross validation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10095 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10095">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Variable that is inferred through a mathematical model from other variables that are observed.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>hidden variable</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>latent construct</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A distinction can be made between ‘hidden variable’, which is considered to be an actual variable that is buried in the effects of other variables and noise, and a ‘latent variable’ that is entirely hypothetical.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The factors obtain from common factor analysis are termed latent variables.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>latent variable</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10041"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10089"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10096 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10096">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Projection of a factor onto the variables.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>&quot;Loadings&quot; (plural) refers to a column in the loadings matrix that relates to a particular factor. &quot;loading&quot; (singular) is the particular contribution of a variable in the original space to the factor.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In multivariate curve resolution the term &quot;pure component spectrum&quot; is interchangeable with the term &quot;loading&quot; and is therefore deprecated. The term, in spectroscopy, may be confused with the spectrum for a pure material.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In principal component analysis the loadings are also the cosine angles between the variables and a particular factor.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The loadings on a factor reflect the relationships between the variables on that factor.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>loadings</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10100"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10106"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10112"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10097 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10097">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Plot of one loading against variable number, or two or three loadings against each other.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Usually the loadings associated with the early factors (1, 2, 3) are plotted to reveal relationships among the variables.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>loadings plot</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10085"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10096"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10098 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10098">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Principal-component analysis that incorporates information about measurement uncertainty to develop models that are optimal in a maximum likelihood sense.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>MLPCA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>maximum likelihood principal component analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10106"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10098"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>MLPCA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10099 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10099">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In multivariate calibration the average of the squared deviation of estimated values from the values of evaluation data. For \(N\) evaluation data where \(c_{i}\) is an observed value and \(\hat c_{i}\) is the predicted value. \[E_{\rm{MSEP}} = \frac{\sum\limits_{i\,=\,1}^{i\,=\,N} (\hat c_{i}\,-\, c_{i})^2}{N}\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>mean squared error of estimation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Mean squared error of prediction is the square of root mean squared error of prediction.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>mean squared error of prediction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10050"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10110"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10153"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10100 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10100">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Factor analysis for the decomposition of multicomponent data into a linear sum of chemically-meaningful components when little or no prior information about the composition is available.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>MCR factors are extracted by the iterative minimization of the residual matrix using an alternating least squares approach, while applying suitable constraints, such as non-negativity, to the loadings and scores. MCR can be performed on the data matrix with or without data pre-processing.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>MCR factors are not unique but are dependent on initial estimates, the number of factors to be resolved, constraints applied and convergence criteria. MCR factors are not required to be orthogonal.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>multivariate curve resolution</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10048"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10083"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10093"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10101 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10101">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Iterative decomposition of a data matrix to give principal components.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NIPALS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The algorithm is very fast if only a few principal components are required, because the covariance matrix is not computed.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Writing the model as \(\boldsymbol{X} = \boldsymbol{T}\boldsymbol{P}^{\rm{T}} + \boldsymbol{E}\), the first principal component is computed from a data matrix. The data explained by this PC are then subtracted from \(\boldsymbol{X}\) and the algorithm applied again to residual data. The procedure is repeated until sufficient principal components are obtained.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>non-linear iterative partial least squares</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10047"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10107"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10101"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>NIPALS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10102 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10102">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Projection of objects defined in a multivariate space onto two- or three-dimensional space so that the distances between objects are preserved as well as possible.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NLM</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>An often applied criterion for the mapping error (\(E\)) is the relative squared error between the true distance \(d_{ij}\) and mapped distance \(\delta_{ij}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Several iterative optimization procedures can be applied to minimize \(E\), such as steepest descent.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nonlinear mapping</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10102"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>NLM</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10103 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10103">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Decomposition of a three-way data matrix into the sum of sets of two-way loadings matrices.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>canonical decomposition</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A representation of PARAFAC is \(x_{ijk} = \sum\limits_{r\,=\,1}^R a_{ir}b_{jr}c_{kr} + e_{ijk}\), where \(x_{ijk}\) is \(i\),\(j\),\(k\)-th element of the data matrix, and \(a_{ir}\), \(b_{jr}\), \(c_{kr}\) are the components of the loadings matrices. \(e_{ijk}\) is the \(i\),\(j\),\(k\)-th element of residual matrix.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A schematic representation of the PARAFAC model is </rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>PARAFAC is a special case of the Tucker3 model (see Tucker tri-linear analysis) where the core matrix is the identity matrix, and \(r = s = t = R\) .</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The PARAFAC model is also known as Canonical Decomposition (CANDECOMP).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>parallel factors analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10096"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10116"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10104 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10104">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In multivariate calibration for a prediction set of \(N\) data where \(c_{i}\) is an observed value and \(\hat c_{i}\) is the predicted value. \[E_{\rm{PRESS}} = \sum\limits_{i\,=\,1}^{i\,=\,N} (\hat c_{i} - c_{i})^2\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>residual sum of squares</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>sum of squared errors of prediction</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>sum of squared residuals</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>prediction error sum of squares</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10110"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10153"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10105 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10105">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Discriminant analysis on a multivariate data set that has been subject to principal-component analysis.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PC-DA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>This procedure removes collinearity from the multivariate data and ensures that the new predictor variables, which are PCA scores, are distributed normally.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>principal component-discriminant analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10055"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10106"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10105"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PC-DA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10106 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10106">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Factor analysis in which factors are calculated that successively capture the greatest variance in the data set.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PCA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The factorization is written \(\boldsymbol{X} = \boldsymbol{T}\boldsymbol{P}^{\rm{T}} + \boldsymbol{E}\), where \(\boldsymbol{T}\) is the scores matrix, \(\boldsymbol{P}\) is the loadings matrix and \(\boldsymbol{E}\) is a residual matrix.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The factors are orthogonal and are known as principal-component factors.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term &quot;principal-component analysis&quot; is preferred to the plural &quot;principal-components analysis&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>principal-component analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10093"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10096"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10101"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10107"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10112"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10106"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PCA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10107 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10107">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Orthogonal factors obtained in a principal-component analysis.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>principal component</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>ISO 18115 recommends the use of the term &quot;PCA factor&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The successive factors explain reducing fractions of the variance of the data set, and are written PC1, PC2 …</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>principal-component factor</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10106"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10107"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10108 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10108">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Comparison of shapes of multi-dimensional objects by a series of geometrical transformations to minimise the sum of squared distances between the transformed and target structures while maintaining the internal structure of the objects.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For two objects defined by \(\boldsymbol{X}\) and \(\boldsymbol{Y}\) the manipulation orthogonal rotation/reflection matrix \(\boldsymbol{R}\) Procrustes analysis minimises: \(\parallel\! \boldsymbol{Y} - \boldsymbol{X}\boldsymbol{R}\parallel^{2}\) subject to \(\boldsymbol{R}^{\rm{T}} \boldsymbol{R} = \boldsymbol{R} \boldsymbol{R}^{\rm{T}} = 1\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Ordinary, or classical, Procrustes analysis is when an object is compared to one other object, which may be a reference shape. Generalized Procrustes analysis compares three or more shapes to an optimally-determined mean shape.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Procrustes analysis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10109 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10109">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Root mean square error of prediction when the predicted data is obtained by cross validation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RMSECV</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>root mean square error of cross validation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10110"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10109"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>RMSECV</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10110 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10110">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In multivariate calibration or classification for \(N\) evaluation data where \(c_{i}\) is an observed value and \(\hat c_{i}\) is the predicted value \[E_{\rm{RMSEP}} = \sqrt {\frac{\sum\limits_{i\,=\,1}^{i\,=\,N} (\hat c_{i}\,-\,c_{i})^2}{N}}\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>root mean squared error of estimation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>standard error of estimation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>standard error of prediction</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>For completely independent, normally distributed evaluation data, RMSEP is a measure of the bias of the calibration.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>RMSEP is related to the prediction error sum of squares (PRESS) by \(E_{\rm{RMSEP}} = \sqrt \frac{E_{\rm{PRESS}}}{N}\) .</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When prediction is by cross validation RMSEP may be termed root mean square error of cross validation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>root mean squared error of prediction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10050"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10153"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10111 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10111">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Interactive method to obtain concentrations and pure spectra from spectra of mixtures using directly-measured variables.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SIMPLISMA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A data matrix \(\boldsymbol{D} = \boldsymbol{C} \times \boldsymbol{P}^{\rm{T}} + \boldsymbol{E}\) where \(\boldsymbol{C}\) is a concentration matrix, \(\boldsymbol{P}\) pure spectra of mixture components and \(\boldsymbol{E}\) an error matrix. Pure spectra are estimated \(\boldsymbol{\hat Y}\) which allows projection of a concentration matrix \(\boldsymbol{C^{\ast}}\) from which the data matrix can be reconstructed and compared with the measured spectra. \[\left\{ {\matrix{ {\boldsymbol{\hat P} = \boldsymbol{D}^{\rm{T}} \boldsymbol{C}(\boldsymbol{C}^{\rm{T}}\boldsymbol{C})}^{-1} \cr {\boldsymbol{C^{*}} = \boldsymbol{C}\boldsymbol{\hat P}(\boldsymbol{\hat P}^{\rm{T}}\boldsymbol{\hat P})}^{-1} \cr}} \right. {\rm{then}}\;{\boldsymbol{D}_{{\rm{recon}}}} = \boldsymbol{C^{*}} \times \boldsymbol{\hat P}^{\rm{T}}\]</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Second derivatives of spectra can be used for modelling.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The directly-measured variables are called &quot;pure variables&quot; in the method.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>simple-to-use interactive self-modelling mixture analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10047"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10111"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SIMPLISMA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10112 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10112">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Factor analysis projection of an object onto a factor.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In principal-component analysis, the factors are orthogonal and the scores are an orthogonal projection of the objects onto a factor.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The scores on a factor reflect the relationships between objects for that factor.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The term scores (plural) refers to a whole column in the scores matrix that relates to a particular factor. The term score (singular) is the projection of a particular object onto the factor.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>score</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10093"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10096"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10106"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10113 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10113">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Plot of one score against object number, or two or three scores against each other.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Usually the scores associated with the early factors (1, 2, 3) are plotted to reveal relationships among the objects.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>scores plot</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10085"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10097"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10112"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10114 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10114">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Generic probabilistic meta-heuristic to locate a good approximation to the global optimum of a given function in a large search space, in which there is a slow decrease in the probability of accepting worse solutions as the solution space is explored.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The function \(E(\rm{s})\) to be minimized is analogous to the internal energy of the system in that state. The goal is to bring the system, from an arbitrary initial state, to a state with the minimum possible energy. At each step, the heuristic considers some neighbouring state \(s^{\prime}\) of the current state \(s\), and probabilistically decides between moving the system to state \(s^{\prime}\) or staying in state \(s\). These probabilities ultimately lead the system to move to states of lower energy. Typically this step is repeated until the system reaches a state that is good enough for the application, or until a given computation budget has been exhausted.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>simulated annealing</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10115 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10115">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>A factorization of an \(m \times n\) matrix (\(\boldsymbol{M}\)) such that \(\boldsymbol{M} = \boldsymbol{U}\boldsymbol{\Sigma} \boldsymbol{V}^{T}\), where \(\boldsymbol{U}\) is an \(m \times m\) matrix, \(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\) is a \(m \times n\) matrix and \(\boldsymbol{V}^{\rm{T}}\) is a \(n \times n\) matrix.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SVD</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>If \(\boldsymbol{M}\) is a data matrix with \(m\) objects and \(n\) variables, the matrix \(\boldsymbol{U}\) is the scores matrix, the diagonal of \(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\) contain the square roots of the eigenvalues and \(\boldsymbol{V}\) is the loadings matrix.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>singular value decomposition</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10047"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10096"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10112"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10115"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SVD</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10116 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10116">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Decomposition of a three-way data matrix into a three-way core matrix, and three, two-way loadings matrices.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Tucker3 model</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A graphical representation of the Tucker3 model is </rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A representation of the Tucker3 model is \[x_{ijk} = \sum\limits_{r\,=\,1}^R \sum\limits_{s\,=\,1}^S \sum\limits_{t\,=\,1}^T a_{ir}b_{js}{c_{kt}z_{rst} + e_{ijk}}\] where \(x_{ijk}\) is the data matrix, \(a_{ir}\), \(b_{js}\), \(c_{kt}\) are the loadings matrices, and \(z_{rst}\) is the core matrix. \(e_{ijk}\) is the residual matrix.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Tucker tri-linear analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10103"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10117 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10117">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Inference method from hypothesis to data that supports the hypothesis</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>back chaining</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Backward chaining is used in backward propagation to train an artificial neural network.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>backward chaining</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10118"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10118 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10118">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Supervised classification method for an artificial neural network in which weights of connections between nodes are calculated from the known output layer back to the input layer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>back-propagation learning rule</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>back-propagation of errors</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>backward propagation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10144"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10119 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10119">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Linear discriminant analysis in which variables to build the discriminant functions are removed one at a time to minimise the loss of discrimination, until there is a significant loss.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Interrelationships between variables that have not yet been selected are ignored, and variables already added, which may become largely redundant through subsequent additions, cannot be removed.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Significant loss is tested by an F-test</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>backward stepwise linear discriminant analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10126"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10133"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10120 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10120">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Distance (\(d_{i,j}\)) between two objects (\(i\) and \(j\)) calculated as the sum of the absolute difference between \(k\) variables (\(x\)) that describe the objects. \[d_{i,j} = \sum\limits_{k\,=\,1}^{k\,=\,N} \vert x_{i,k} - x_{j,k}\vert\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Hamming distance</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Manhattan distance</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>taxi distance</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>city-block distance</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10121 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10121">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Linkage criterion in hierarchical clustering in which the distance between two clusters is the distance between those two objects (one from each cluster) that are farthest apart.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Complete linkage tends to find compact clusters of approximately equal size.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>complete linkage</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10129"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10122 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10122">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Tree diagram used to illustrate the arrangement of clusters produced by hierarchical clustering.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The objects are successively grouped (or un-grouped) in each layer of the dendrogram, where the length of the linking lines is proportional to the dissimilarity of the objects.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dendrogram</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10129"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10123 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10123">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Principal-component analysis (PCA) independently performed on each class as a step in classification.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Soft independent modelling of class analogy is an example of the use of disjoint principal component analysis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When a PCA model is obtained using all classes, it is known as conjoint PCA.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>disjoint principal component analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10106"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10143"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10124 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10124">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Distance (\(d_{i,j}\)) between two objects (\(i\) and \(j\)) calculated as the square root of the sum of the squared differences between \(k\) variables (\(x\)) that describe the objects. \[d_{i,j} = \sqrt {\sum\limits_{k\,=\,1}^{k\,=\,N} (x_{i,k} - x_{j,k})^2}\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:label>Euclidean distance</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10125 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10125">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Inference method from data to hypothesis.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In logic, forward chaining is the application of modus ponens (if \(\rm{P}\) implies \(\rm{Q}\), given \(\rm{P}\) then \(\rm{Q}\)).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>forward chaining</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10126 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10126">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Linear discriminant analysis in which variables to build the discriminant functions are introduced one at a time to maximise the discrimination, until there is no significant improvement.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Interrelationships between variables that have not yet been selected are ignored, and variables already added, which may become largely redundant through subsequent additions, cannot be removed.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Significant improvement is usually tested by an F-test</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>forward stepwise linear discriminant analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10119"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10133"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10127 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10127">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Classification in which membership of an object in each possible class is given a weight between zero and one.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In so-called hard cluster algorithms membership of a group can only take values 0 or 1, but in fuzzy clustering any value between 0 and 1 is allowed subject to the sum of all memberships of an object being 1.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>fuzzy clustering</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10128 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10128">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Group of nodes in an artificial neural network between input layer and output layer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:label>hidden layer</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10137"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10129 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10129">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Pattern recognition in which objects are linked together by use of an appropriate measure of distance between pairs of objects, and a linkage criterion, which specifies the dissimilarity of sets as a function of the pairwise distances between objects.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Distance measures include Euclidean distance, Mahalanobis distance and city-block distance.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Linkage criteria include single linkage, complete linkage.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>hierarchical clustering</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10120"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10121"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10124"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10134"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10142"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10148"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10130 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10130">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Unsupervised classification method which partitions objects into \(k\) groups, in which each object belongs to the group with the nearest mean.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Although \(k\)-means clustering is called unsupervised classification the value of \(k\) may be specified.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>&lt;em&gt;k&lt;/em&gt;-means clustering</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10146"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10131 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10131">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Non-parametric supervised classification method for objects based on the closest training examples in the variable space.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>kNN</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>An object is classified by a majority vote of its \(k\)-nearest neighbours, with the object being assigned to the class most common.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When \(k = 1\) the method is known as &quot;nearest neighbour&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>\(k\) is a small positive integer.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>&lt;em&gt;k&lt;/em&gt;-nearest neighbour</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10144"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10131"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>kNN</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10132 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10132">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Type of self-organising map with low dimensional grid.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:label>Kohonen network</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10133 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10133">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Discriminant analysis in which the criterion function is based on the pooled covariance matrix.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>LDA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>linear discriminant analysis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10133"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>LDA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10134 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10134">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Distance (\(d_{i,j}\)) between an object characterised by vector of variables \(\boldsymbol{x}_{i}\) and the centroid of a class \(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{j}\) with covariance matrix \(\boldsymbol{S}\) calculated as \[d_{i,j} = \sqrt {(\boldsymbol{x}_{i} - \boldsymbol{\mu}_{j})^T \boldsymbol{S}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i} - \boldsymbol{\mu}_{j})}\] where \(\boldsymbol{S}\) is the covariance matrix of the variables.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:label>Mahalanobis distance</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10135 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10135">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Fraction of objects incorrectly assigned to a group in supervised classification.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>misclassification frequency</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Misclassification rate is an estimate of the misclassification probability.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Misclassification rate may be calculated for an evaluation data set, or in leave-one-out cross validation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>misclassification rate</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10050"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10144"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10136 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10136">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Classification identifying objects of a specific class amongst all objects, by learning from training data containing only objects of the specific class.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>independent classification</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>unary classification</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The assignment of an object to a group may be in the form of a probability of membership.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>one-class classification</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10137 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10137">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Locations in an artificial neural network that carry values that are calculated from values of connected nodes and weights by a transfer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Nodes are arranged in layers, input, hidden and output.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The value of a node is used in the calculation of nodes in subsequent layers.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>node</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10128"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10138 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10138">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Linear classification in which the criterion function is obtained by partial least squares analysis.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PLS-DA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>PLS-DA can also be useful for exploratory data analysis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>PLS-DA results depend on data pre-processing and choice of parameters and so is believed to be more difficult to implement well than other forms of discriminant analysis such as linear discriminant analysis, quadratic discriminant analysis or regularized discriminant analysis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>partial least squares discriminant analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10048"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10052"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10133"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10139"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10140"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10155"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10138"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PLS-DA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10139 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10139">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Discriminant analysis in which the criterion function is based on the individual within-group covariance matrix.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>QDA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>quadratic discriminant analysis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10139"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>QDA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10140 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10140">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Discriminant analysis in which the criterion function is based on a combination of pooled covariance matrix and the individual within-group covariance matrix.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The covariance matrix (\(\sum_{k}(\lambda)\)) is related to the class covariance matrix (\(\sum_{k}\)) and the pooled covariance matrix (\(\sum_{\rm{pooled}}\)) by \(\sum_{k}(\lambda) = (1 - \lambda)\sum_{k} + \lambda\sum_{\rm{pooled}}\)</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>regularized discriminant analysis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10141 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10141">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Quantity that describes the equivalence of two objects characterised by multivariate data.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>similarity distance</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A similarity index may be in the interval, where 0 is complete dissimilarity and 1 is complete equivalence.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When the term &quot;distance&quot; is used the quantity is some function of the differences of coordinates in the multivariate data space.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>similarity index</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10055"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10120"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10124"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10134"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10145"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10148"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10142 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10142">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Linkage criterion in hierarchical clustering in which the distance between two clusters is the distance between those two objects (one from each cluster) that are closest together.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A drawback of this method is the so-called chaining phenomenon, which refers to the gradual growth of a cluster as one element at a time gets added to it. This may lead to impractically heterogeneous clusters and difficulties in defining classes that could usefully subdivide the data.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>single linkage</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10129"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10143 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10143">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Supervised classification that performs a principal-component analysis on each class, and then an unknown is assigned to the class with which it has the lowest residual variance.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SIMCA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>SIMCA is an example of the use of disjoint principal component analysis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The number of principal-component factors chosen for each class is determined by cross validation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>soft independent modelling of class analogy</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10106"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10107"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10123"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10144"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10143"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SIMCA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10144 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10144">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Classification in which, in a first step, a model is built using data from objects of known classes, and in a second step, the model is applied to new data to assign a class to unknown objects.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Algorithms for supervised classification include discriminant analysis, support vector machine, artificial neural network, Bayesian classifier.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>supervised classification</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10077"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10145 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10145">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Fraction of variables that are considered to agree between two objects.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Tanimoto index</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The Tanimoto index is essentially similar to the Jaccard index. (See https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jaccard_index).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The Tanimoto index is used in drug discovery and QSAR for comparison of structures in large data bases in an efficient way.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The rules for agreement must be defined. For example in an elemental analysis, element concentrations equivalent within \(\pu{20\%}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Tanimoto similarity index</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10146 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10146">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Classification in which no prior information about membership of groups is known.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Algorithms for unsupervised classification include k-means cluster analysis, hierarchical cluster analysis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The number of groups may be specified.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>unsupervised classification</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10129"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10130"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10147 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10147">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Linkage criterion in hierarchical clustering in which the distance between two clusters is the average of all distances between pairs of objects.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>UPGMA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>average linkage</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>unweighted pair group method with arithmetic mean</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10129"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10147"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>UPGMA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10148 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10148">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Linkage criterion in hierarchical clustering in which the within-cluster variance is minimized.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Ward’s method</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The initial cluster distances in Ward’s minimum variance method are the squared Euclidean distance.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Ward’s minimum variance method</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10124"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10129"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10149 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10149">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Regression that minimizes the sum of squared differences between observed values of a variable and the values predicted by a model.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>LS regression</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>LSR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Least squares regression is used as a synonym for ordinary least squares regression. The full term should be used if there is ambiguity about the kind of regression being performed.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>least squares regression</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10154"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10149"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>LSR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10150 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10150">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Least squares regression in which both the response variable and predictor variable have measurement error.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>total least squares regression</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The model for EIV regression is \[\left\{\matrix{c_{i} = \alpha + \beta x_{i}^{*} + \varepsilon_{i} \cr x_{i} = x_{i}^{*} + \eta_{i} \cr}\right.\] where \(x^{*}\) is the true value of the predictor variable, \(c_{i}\) concentration and \(\varepsilon\) and \(\eta\) are errors.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Total least squares regression is performed by singular value decomposition.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When the errors \(\varepsilon\) and \(\eta\) have the same variance the method is called orthogonal regression and minimises the perpendicular distance of a point to the regression line.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>errors-in-variables regression</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10115"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10151 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10151">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In calibration for \(N\) calibration data where \(c_{i}\) is an observed value and \(\hat c_{i}\) is the value predicted by the calibration function. \[E_{\rm{MSEC}} = \frac{\sum\limits_{i\,=\,1}^{i\,=\,N} (\hat c_{i} - c_{i})^2}{N}\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>mean residual sum of squares</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Mean squared error of calibration is the square of the root mean squared error of calibration.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>mean squared error of calibration</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10157"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10152 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10152">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Multivariate calibration in which the coefficients of the regression are calculated directly from the indications and values of standards.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>If \(\boldsymbol{X}^{\rm{T}} \boldsymbol{X}\) has full rank only because of noise, the solution can become unstable.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In \(\boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{b} = \boldsymbol{c} + \boldsymbol{e}\) (where \(\boldsymbol{X}\) is a vector of indications, \(\boldsymbol{b}\) are coefficients of the model, \(\boldsymbol{c}\) is a vector of values of the quantity of interest and \(\boldsymbol{e}\) a vector of errors), \(\boldsymbol{b} = \boldsymbol{X^{+}} \boldsymbol{c}\), where \(\boldsymbol{X^{+}}\) is the pseudo-inverse of \(\boldsymbol{X}\), calculated as \(\boldsymbol{X^{+}} = (\boldsymbol{X}^{\rm{T}} \boldsymbol{X})^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}^{\rm{T}}\)</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>It is assumed that \(\boldsymbol{X}^{\rm{T}} \boldsymbol{X}\) has full rank, i.e. there are more objects than variables, and the variables are independent.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>multilinear least squares regression</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10041"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10153"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10153 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10153">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Calibration in which the indications are multivariate data.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In multivariate regression and calibration the problem is usually posed as a relation between the indications \(\boldsymbol{X}\) (which are multivariate) and the property to be measured \(\boldsymbol{y}\). Note that this is a reversal of the traditional form, \(x\) (concentration)/\(y\ (\rm{g})\) (indication) of linear calibration. \(\boldsymbol{X}\) is a vector of observations (indications), and \(\boldsymbol{c}\) a vector of concentrations. In matrix form: \(\boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{b} = \boldsymbol{c} + \boldsymbol{e}\) where \(\boldsymbol{b}\) are the coefficients of the model and \(\boldsymbol{e}\) is a vector of errors.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Regression establishes the coefficients \(\boldsymbol{b}\) (\(\boldsymbol{X} \boldsymbol{b} = \boldsymbol{c} + \boldsymbol{e}\)) from given indications \(\boldsymbol{X}\), or some factorization of \(\boldsymbol{X}\), and values \(\boldsymbol{c}\), or some factorization of \(\boldsymbol{c}\). Given indications \(\boldsymbol{X}_{\rm{u}}\) from an unknown sample, the quantity value \(c_{\rm{u}}\) can be calculated.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>multivariate calibration</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10055"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10154 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10154">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Least squares regression that minimizes the sum of squared differences between the known values of dependent variable and values predicted by a linear model.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>OLSR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>classical least squares regression</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Assumptions of the model are that error is only in the dependent variable, Normally-distributed, and homoscedastic.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>OLSR is used for calibration and multivariate calibration.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>ordinary least squares regression</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10149"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10153"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10154"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>OLSR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10155 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10155">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Multivariate calibration which finds factors that maximise covariance between two blocks of data.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PLS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>partial least squares</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>PLS finds factors (latent variables) in observed variables \(\boldsymbol{X}\) that explain the maximum variance in the variable(s) \(\boldsymbol{c}\), using the simultaneous decomposition of the two. It removes redundant information from the regression, i.e. factors describing large amounts of variance in the observed data that does not correlate with the predictions.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>PLS may also be used in classification. See partial least squares discriminant analysis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>PLS1 refers to PLS for a single &quot;c&quot; variable. PLS2 is PLS that simultaneously obtains values for two or more &quot;c&quot; variables. Therefore in the equations of Note 2, PLS1 has vectors \(\boldsymbol{c}\) and \(\boldsymbol{q}\) and PLS2 has matrices \(\boldsymbol{C}\) and \(\boldsymbol{Q}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The decompositions are \(\boldsymbol{X} = \boldsymbol{T}_{k}\boldsymbol{P}_{k}^{\rm{T}} + \boldsymbol{E}\) and \(\boldsymbol{C} = \boldsymbol{U}_{k}\boldsymbol{Q}_{k}^{\rm{T}} + \boldsymbol{F}\), and \(\boldsymbol{X^{+}} = \boldsymbol{W}_{k} (\boldsymbol{P}_{k}\boldsymbol{W}_{k})^{-1} (\boldsymbol{T}_{k}^{\rm{T}}\boldsymbol{T}_{k})^{-1} \boldsymbol{T}_{k}^{\rm{T}}\), where \(\boldsymbol{W}\) are weights that maintain orthogonal scores.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When used for multivariate calibration, evaluation data or cross validation may be used to choose the number of PLS factors and assess the accuracy of the prediction (although the same data must not be used to do both). This is important to guard against overfitting.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>partial least squares regression</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10050"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10112"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10138"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10153"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10155"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PLS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10156 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10156">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Multivariate calibration in which a dependent variable is regressed against the scores of a chosen number of factors obtained from principal-component analysis of the predictor variable.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PCR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>PCA decomposes the predictor variable data \(\boldsymbol{X}\) into \(k\) principal component factors \(\boldsymbol{\hat X}_{k} = \boldsymbol{T}_{k}\boldsymbol{P}_{k}^{\rm{T}}\), where \(k\) may be determined by cross validation. The dependent variable \(\boldsymbol{c}\) is then regressed against \(\boldsymbol{\hat X}_{k}\), \(\boldsymbol{c} = \boldsymbol{\hat X}_{k} \boldsymbol{\hat b}\).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The factorization gives orthogonal factors, but no information about the predicted variable \(\boldsymbol{c}\) is used.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>principal components regression</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10106"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10107"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10112"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10152"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10153"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10155"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10156"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PCR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10157 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10157">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In calibration for \(N\) calibration data where \(c_{i}\) is an observed value and \(\hat c_{i}\) is the value predicted by the calibration function. \[E_{\rm{RMSEC}} = \sqrt \frac{\sum\limits_{i\,=\,1}^{i,=\,N} (\hat c_{i} - c_{i})^2}{N}\]</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RMSEC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>standard error of calibration (SEC)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Overfitting results in a small RMSEC, but poor predictive ability.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>root mean squared error of calibration</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10157"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>RMSEC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10158 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10158">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Condition in which a model fails to describe the data adequately.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>underfitting</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Underfitting generally occurs when a model is not sufficiently complex, such as having too few parameters.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>underfitting of a calibration model</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10159 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10159">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Least squares regression in which a nonnegative constant is associated with each value of the dependent variable.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0605"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>WLSR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>If the only source of dispersion in the dependent variable is normally distributed, the weights are the inverse of the variance of each value.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>It is assumed the weights are known exactly.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The nonnegative constants are called weights.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>weighted least squares regression</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10159"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>WLSR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10160 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10160">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Distance from the interface beyond which mass transfer is determined by convection.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>boundary layer thickness</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10161 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10161">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sample volume at which a defined amount of an analyte pumped continuously through an extraction device will begin to be eluted. Typical breakthrough curve indicating the breakthrough volume \(V_{\rm{B}}\) and the sample volume corresponding to the saturation capacity of the sorbent \(V_{\rm{C}}\) (\(V_{\rm{R}}\) corresponds to the chromatographic elution volume and \(\upsigma_{\rm{v}}\) the standard deviation of the derivative of the curve).Typical breakthrough curve indicating the breakthrough volume \(V_{\rm{B}}\) and the sample volume corresponding to the saturation capacity of the sorbent \(V_{\rm{C}}\) (\(V_{\rm{R}}\) corresponds to the chromatographic elution volume and \(\upsigma_{\rm{v}}\) the standard deviation of the derivative of the curve).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Defined amounts are usually given as a volume fraction of the sample volume (e.g. \(\pu{1\%}\), \(\pu{5\%}\) or \(\pu{10\%}\), etc.).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The breakthrough volume, \(V_{\rm{B}}\), for a particular compound and sampling device is determined by its breakthrough curve (see figure).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>breakthrough volume</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10162 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10162">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Transfer of ions from an aqueous phase to an immiscible liquid (or solid) phase by adding an ion of complementary charge to the aqueous phase.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Ion-pair extraction can also be done by adding the complementary-charge containing compound to the organic phase before extraction.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>ion-pair extraction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Condensed phase where extraction of analytes can occur at the surface and in the bulk.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>If extraction occurs exclusively at the surface then adsorbent is the preferred term and if exclusively in the bulk then absorbent should be used.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Sorbent is typically used explicitly to describe a condensed phase where extraction occurs simultaneously by adsorption and absorption (termed sorption), or where the true mechanism has not been established and the use of adsorbent or absorbent would be inappropriate.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sorbent</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10164 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10164">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Component of a multi-phase system into which the target analytes are extracted.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In extractions employing a semipermeable membrane for phase separation the acceptor phase is the phase to which analytes are transferred from the donor phase.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>When the acceptor phase is a liquid the term acceptor solution may be used.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>acceptor phase</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10171"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10290"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10165 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10165">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Continuous extraction with movement of the sample matrix with respect to the extraction phase.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>active sampling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10175"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10166 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10166">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction in which the extraction phase and the sample are confined in an extraction vessel and processed according to a defined procedure.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Term &quot;batch&quot; is also used in extraction to indicate a number of samples extracted sequentially or simultaneously by the same procedure (e.g. extraction of sample batch(es)).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>batch extraction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10167 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10167">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Internal standard that is incorporated in the extraction phase for calibration.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>calibrant in extraction phase</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10175"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10168 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10168">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Tube filled with charcoal typically used to adsorb analytes from the gas or liquid phase.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Activated charcoal was used in early applications, but a wider selection of carbon materials are currently used, with properties tailored to each application.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>charcoal tube</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10169 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10169">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction in which the sample is continuously introduced into the extraction device.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>continuous extraction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10170 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10170">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction in which both phases are continuously added (or changed) and flow (or move) in opposite directions as the extraction progresses.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The phases are either immiscible or separated by a membrane.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>countercurrent extraction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10171 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10171">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In extraction, the sample or sample solution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>donor phase</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10172 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10172">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction in which clean (or recycled) extraction phase is continuously passed through the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The extraction phase can be a gas or a liquid.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dynamic extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10175"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10173 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10173">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Type of extraction where the intent is a complete extraction of the analytes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>exhaustive extraction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10174 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10174">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Calibration employing a simplified sample matrix spiked with target analyte embodying a reference value of the measurand.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>external calibration</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10175 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10175">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Liquid, solid, or gas phase in contact with the sample for the purpose of removal of analytes from the sample matrix.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>extraction phase</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10176 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10176">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Rate at which analytes are transferred from the sample to the extraction phase.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>kinetics of extraction</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>extraction rate</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10175"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10177 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10177">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Solution or gas containing analyte introduced into an extraction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>If feed is used without qualification, the term may be taken to designate the initial liquid phase containing the main solute to be transferred or sample solution in a batch process.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>feed</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10178 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10178">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction in a biphasic system in which the main component of one phase is a highly fluorinated solvent (liquid-liquid extraction) or highly fluorinated sorbent (solid-phase extraction).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>fluorous extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10255"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10179 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10179">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Dissolution of sample components from a solid with a liquid in which the solid phase is not wholly soluble.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>leaching</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10180 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10180">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Calibration employing external calibration in which standard solutions of target are prepared in a solution of analyte-free matrix.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Matrix-matched calibration is used to minimize the matrix effect on the measurement of target measurands.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>matrix-matched calibration</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10181 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10181">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Plate with multiple wells for individual samples.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>multiwell sample plate</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10182 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10182">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Adjustment of extraction parameters to achieve a defined goal.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Many extractions are controlled by several parameters and their interactions. Optimum conditions are more efficiently identified by multivariate methods.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>optimisation of extraction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10183 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10183">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction process driven by diffusion of analytes towards the extraction phase.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>passive sampling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10175"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10184 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10184">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Addition of an electrolyte to an aqueous phase to decrease the distribution ratio of the analyte(s) or matrix.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>salting in</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10186 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10186">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction wherein extractants of increasing strength or different chemical character are used in order to extract analytes of interest.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The most commonly used approaches in elemental analysis are the BCR (now termed Standards, Measurement and Testing Program) and Tessier procedures.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sequential extraction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10187 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10187">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Batch extraction in which the extraction phase attains a constant composition before phase separation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>If the analytes are initially present as a solution in an immiscible liquid phase the process is synonymous with liquid-liquid extraction.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>If the sample is a solid that is partially soluble in the extraction phase then leaching may be a more appropriate term.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>static extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10166"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10175"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10179"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10188 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10188">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>State of a continuous process operating in such a way that the concentration of solutes in exit streams remains constant with respect to time for constant feed concentrations, even though the two phases are not necessarily in thermodynamic equilibrium in any part of the process.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The term equilibrium should not be used to describe this situation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>steady state extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10177"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10189 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10189">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>A passive sampling or active sampling strategy over a relatively long time, during which the amount of analyte(s) extracted is proportional to its average (mean) concentration over that time.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>TWA sampling</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>time-weighted average sampling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10165"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10183"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10190 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10190">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Type of chemical vapour generation wherein volatile hydrides are generated by reaction with (usually) borane complexes in aqueous solution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CHG</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>chemical hydride generation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10190"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CHG</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10191 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10191">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction by a fixed volume of gas continuously recycled through the sample and a sorbent trap by a pump in a closed-circuit arrangement.Typical apparatus for closed-loop stripping analysis.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>closed-loop stripping analysis</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Closed-loop extraction was developed for trace analysis of volatile compounds (at mass concentrations of \(\pu{ng L-1}\)) in drinking water.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>closed-loop extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10201"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10192 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10192">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Device used to concentrate volatile analytes from the gas phase by condensation on a cold surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Typically, cryogenic traps have a low thermal mass to respond rapidly to changes in temperature and are prepared from short lengths of capillary tubing. Sub-ambient temperatures are required for the efficient collection of analytes from a flowing gas stream and above ambient temperatures for evaporation into a flowing gas stream for analysis, usually by gas chromatography or atomic spectrometry.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>cryogenic trap</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10193 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10193">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Apparatus for the extraction of volatile analytes from a flowing gas stream by diffusion to the wall of a collection vessel, where they are retained by chemisorption or strong physisorption by the wall coating.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>denuder device</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Both liquid and solid sorbents are used for trapping.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>There are many design variations.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>denuder</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10194 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10194">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Continuous removal of headspace above a sample by a flow of inert gas over or through the sample until all or a significant portion of the analytes have been removed from the sample. Sample vapours are typically transferred to a sorbent trap or cryogenic trap for collection.Typical apparatus for analysis by dynamic headspace (purge and trap). The gas flow (sparging gas) enters at the bottom of the sample container transporting volatile compounds through the headspace and purge head to the desorption tube for collection.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A typical apparatus for purge-and-trap is shown in the figure below.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Purge-and-trap is a version of dynamic headspace analysis in which volatile compounds are purged from solution by a continuous stream of gas bubbles (sparging gas) and transported through the headspace to a sorbent or cryogenic trap for collection.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dynamic headspace analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10192"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10198"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10201"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10195 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10195">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Separation of close-boiling liquids by fractional distillation in the presence of a low-volatility solvent or salt that selectively interacts with one or more components of the mixture, increasing the difference in vapour pressure of the sample components.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>If the separation solvent forms an azeotrope with one or more sample components the process is called azeotropic distillation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>extractive distillation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10196 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10196">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Thermal desorption where the temperature of a packed tube or cryogenic trap is rapidly increased to transfer condensed or sorbed compounds to the gas phase.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>flash desorption</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10197 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10197">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Small volume of sample in a headspace vessel maintained at a temperature sufficient to cause complete evaporation of analytes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Equilibrium between the condensed and vapor phases no longer exists. As a consequence, the possibility of matrix interferences is reduced and the fraction extracted for low-volatility compounds is increased.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The full-evaporation technique is used with both static headspace analysis and dynamic headspace analysis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>full-evaporation headspace analysis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10198 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10198">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Volume of gas phase above a sample in a closed container.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>headspace</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10199 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10199">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Dynamic gas extraction carried out stepwise, with sequential analysis of a number of headspace samples taken from the same vial.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>At equilibrium the headspace is sampled and the remainder (or most of it) is exhausted and replaced by fresh gas phase. The process is repeated and the original sample concentration estimated from the peak areas for two consecutive extractions. \[A_{\rm{T}} = \frac{A_{1}^{2}}{A_{1} - A_{2}}\] where \(A_{\rm{T}}\) = estimated sum of all partial peak areas corresponding to exhaustive extraction, \(A_{1}\) the peak area obtained from the first headspace fraction, and \(A_{2}\) the peak area obtained in the second (consecutive) headspace fraction.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>External calibration is required to convert peak areas into sample concentration.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>multiple headspace extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10174"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10198"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10200 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10200">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction in which a sample dispersed in water and a water immiscible organic solvent (extraction phase) in separate flasks are heated simultaneously and their vapours co-condensed and separated into two streams in which water is returned to the sample flask and the extraction phase to the organic solvent flask.Apparatus for simultaneous steam distillation-solvent extraction with a lighter-than-water extraction phase. A = aqueous sample solution or slurry; B = immiscible organic solvent and receiving flask for extracted compounds; C = bowl for phase separation; D, E, F, and G are solvent or vapour transfer arms; and H = entry port for additional solvent or alternative sample introduction position for continuous liquid–liquid extraction. Switching the position of flasks A and B allows extraction with a heavier-than-water extraction solvent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A typical apparatus for simultaneous steam distillation-solvent extraction with a lighter-than-water extraction solvent is shown in the figure below.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>simultaneous steam distillation-solvent extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10175"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10201 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10201">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Tube filled with porous sorbent typically used to extract analytes from the gas phase.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The performance of a sorbent tube depends on the sorbent type, temperature, gas flow rate, and desorption time.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sorbent trap</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10202 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10202">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Method of analysis, in which a small aliquot of the gas phase in contact with the sample in a closed container is withdrawn for analysis.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>At equilibrium, the analyte concentration in the gas phase is related to the sample concentration by a partition constant.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The sample vessel is thermostatted.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>static headspace analysis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10203 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10203">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Release of volatile compounds from a sample by heat.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The volatile compounds are typically transported by a gas stream to a sorbent tube or cryogenic trap for collection.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>thermal extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10192"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10201"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10204 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10204">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Dynamic headspace analysis of a flowing sample solution in the form of a thin film on the surface of a heated tube.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>TLHS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Volatile compounds in the sample solution pass into the gas phase and are continuously swept out of the tube for analysis or detection.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>thin-layer headspace analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10194"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10204"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>TLHS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10205 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10205">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Equilibrium system containing two or more solvents forming two phases with a single interface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>biphasic system</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10206 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10206">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction method wherein charged analytes are extracted from an aqueous phase into an organic phase, assisted by a chelating agent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The analyte(s) are usually metal ions.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The chelating agent replaces water molecules in the coordination sphere of the metal ions. Consequently, the resultant metal chelates have a hydrophobic character which facilitates their transfer to the organic phase.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>chelation solvent extraction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10207 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10207">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coacervative extraction at a temperature above the cloud point temperature.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>cloud point extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10208"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10208 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10208">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction by a surfactant-rich, water-immiscible phase formed by adding a dehydrating agent to a colloidal solution of the sample and a surfactant above its critical micelle concentration.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In cloud point extraction a small volume &quot;surfactant-rich&quot; phase enriched in sample components of moderate water solubility and a bulk aqueous phase with dissolved surfactant and highly water-soluble compounds are formed.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>coacervative extraction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10209 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10209">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction in which a water-immiscible extraction solvent is continuously percolated through an aqueous solution and returned to the extraction solvent reservoir.Apparatus for continuous liquid–liquid extraction with a lighter-than-water extraction solvent (left) and a heavier-than-water extraction solvent (right). In each case the extraction solvent is contained in the round bottom flask and the aqueous sample in the vertical glass column.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Continuous liquid-liquid extraction techniques are used when the sample volume is large, the distribution constant is small, or the rate of extraction is slow.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Typical apparatuses are shown below.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Typically, a fixed volume of extraction solvent is recycled through the aqueous sample solution by distillation and condensation, and dripped into the sample solution. A siphon mechanism allows continuous recovery of the extraction solvent based on its relative density.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>continuous liquid-liquid extraction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10210 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10210">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Apparatus for extraction based on a multistage countercurrent distribution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The Craig CCD apparatus consists of a battery of glass vessels designed to allow the mixing, settling, and transfer of extracted phase (sample) and the addition of fresh solvent (extractant) in a repetitive fashion. Movement about the horizontal axis provides gentle mixing of the phases, settling, and decanting of the upper phase. The extraction process is slow but is fully automated.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Craig countercurrent distribution apparatus</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10217"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10211 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10211">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction comprising a cascading series of states where the raffinate is brought into a subsequent stage and contacted with fresh solvent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>crosscurrent extraction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10212 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10212">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Type of continuous multistage extraction equipment in which there is only one interface at which phase separation by settling occurs.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>differential contactor</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10213 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10213">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Solvent used to disperse the extraction solvent as fine droplets throughout the sample solution in dispersive liquid–liquid microextraction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The dispersion solvent is typically a polar organic solvent miscible with water.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dispersing solvent</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10214"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10214 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10214">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction of analyte(s) from an aqueous solution by fine droplets of extraction phase dispersed throughout the solution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DLLME</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The extraction phase can be formed by ion exchange when an ionic liquid is used as the extraction solvent.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The extraction phase is formed by a mixture of extraction and dispersion solvents injected into the sample solution.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The extraction phase is sedimented by centrifugation and collected for analysis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>dispersive liquid-liquid microextraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10175"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10214"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>DLLME</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10215 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10215">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Liquid-liquid distribution process that involves the transfer of ionic species from the extractant to the aqueous phase in exchange for ions from the aqueous phase.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>liquid-ion exchange</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10216 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10216">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Microextraction technique using a liquid as the extraction phase.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>liquid-phase microextraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10175"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11552"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10217 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10217">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Multistage countercurrent separation based on a discontinuous, differential migration process employing stepwise extraction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>multistage countercurrent distribution</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10170"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10218 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10218">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction of analytes from the gas phase or solution by a single drop of extraction solvent.Typical experimental arrangement for single-drop microextraction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SDME</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The extraction solvent with a volume typically \(\pu{\lt 50 \upmu L}\) is suspended as a drop from the tip of a microsyringe needle as shown in the figure below.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The sample is stirred or agitated during extraction. After extraction, the drop of acceptor phase is collected by withdrawing it into the syringe used to support the drop for the final analytical measurement.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Typically, the sample is aqueous and the acceptor phase is an organic solvent, resulting in a two-phase extraction system (\(\ce{aqueous\!-&gt;\!organic}\)). SDME can also be operated in three-phase mode (\(\ce{aqueous\!-&gt;\!organic\!-&gt;\!aqueous}\)), or in the headspace mode (\(\ce{aqueous\!-&gt;\!gas\!-&gt;\!organic or aqueous}\)).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>single-drop microextraction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10218"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SDME</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10219 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10219">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Droplet of organic solvent recovered from the surface of the sample solution by solidification induced by cooling.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>solidified floating organic drop</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10220 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10220">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Microextraction employing a solidified floating organic drop.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SFODME</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The extraction solvent has a melting point near room temperature to facilitate collection after solidification induced by cooling. The solidified solvent is then melted and analysed.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>solidified floating organic drop microextraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10219"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11552"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10220"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SFODME</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10221 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10221">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Physically distinct part of an extraction process in which transfer of analyte(s) occurs, followed by phase separation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>stage</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10222 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10222">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Type of continuous multi-stage liquid-liquid distribution equipment in which each stage has a physically distinct cycle of interphase contact and separation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>There are the same number of phase separation interfaces as there are stages.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>stagewise contactor</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10223 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10223">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Distribution in a biphasic system formed by two aqueous solutions containing structurally incompatible components (two polymers or a polymer and a salt) above a critical concentration.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>two-phase aqueous partition</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10224 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10224">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction in which samples are blended with an abrasive solid sorbent, forming a free flowing powder from which target analytes are recovered by solvent elution.Typical work flow scheme for solid-phase matrix dispersion. (i) Sample is blended with an abrasive sorbent, (ii) free flowing powder containing sample is packed in a short column, and (iii) target analytes are recovered by elution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>MSPD</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A typical workflow scheme for matrix–solid-phase dispersion is shown in the figure below.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>For additional sample clean-up the sorbent/sample mixture is deposited at the head of a solid-phase extraction cartridge containing a suitable sorbent to retard the elution of matrix components.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>matrix-solid phase dispersion</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10224"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>MSPD</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10225 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10225">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Leaching using microwave energy to heat the solvent in contact with the sample or the sample directly.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>MAE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Microwaves are electromagnetic radiation of \(\pu{300\!{–}\! 300,000 MHz}\). They heat the extraction solution by their interaction with dielectric compounds (solvent or sample). Microwave heating is more efficient and rapid than convection and conduction and enhances the migration rate of compounds into the extraction solvent. Microwave-assisted extraction may be performed in a closed vessel with control of temperature and pressure or in an open vessel at atmospheric pressure.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>microwave-assisted extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10179"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10225"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>MAE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10226 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10226">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction by water below its critical point (\(\pu{374.15^{\circ}C}\) and \(\pu{22.064 MPa}\)).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>subcritical water extraction</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>superheated water extraction</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Instrument requirements are similar to pressurized-liquid extraction.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The solvation properties of water become closer to organic solvents at elevated temperatures.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>pressurized hot-water extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10227"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10227 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10227">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Leaching performed at elevated temperature and pressure.Schematic diagram of a typical apparatus for pressurized-liquid extraction. Gas flow is used to displace the extraction solvent from the sample chamber.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A typical apparatus is shown in the figure below.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The sample, in the form of a fine powder or liquid supported on an inert solid, is packed into an extraction vessel and inserted in a flow-through system. The temperature, pressure, solvent type, and static and/or dynamic extraction time are varied to optimize the extraction performance.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>pressurized-liquid extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10179"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10228 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10228">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Leaching with fresh solvent cycled multiple times through a fine solid sample contained in a porous thimble.Typical apparatus for classical Soxhlet extraction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A typical apparatus for classical Soxhlet extraction is shown in the figure below.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The extraction solvent is cycled in a continuous-discrete process by distillation, condensation and siphoning. Extracted material accumulates in the distillation flask.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Soxhlet extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10179"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10229 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10229">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Use of a supercritical fluid to extract analytes from a solid or supported liquid sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SFE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The most common supercritical fluid for extraction is carbon dioxide (critical point = \(\pu{39.9^{\circ}C}\) and \(\pu{73.8 bar}\)). Its extraction properties are varied by changing its density (temperature and pressure) or by addition of polar organic solvents.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The powdered sample, or supported liquid, is packed into a vessel and extracted using static or dynamic extraction conditions. The extracted material is typically recovered by reducing the pressure of the extraction fluid, allowing it to evaporate.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>supercritical fluid extraction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10229"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SFE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10230 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10230">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Streamlined multiresidue method for extraction and clean-up of analytes in complex matrices.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Often used for samples containing pesticide residues.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The general procedure includes micro-scale extraction with acetonitrile, salt-assisted phase separation, and sample clean up using dispersive solid-phase extraction.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>There are several procedural variations of the general method for application to particular compounds or sample matrices.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>QuEChERS (quick, easy, cheap, effective, rugged and safe) extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11552"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10231 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10231">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction in which ultrasound energy is used to increase the extraction rate by enhancing the contact area between the sample and solvent and by promoting efficient agitation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>UAE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Ultrasound energy causes cavitation in solvents, which generates numerous tiny bubbles in liquids and mechanical erosion of solids, including particle rupture. It promotes digestion and slurry formation with solids and homogenization and emulsification with liquids.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Ultrasound energy is applied to samples in a vessel immersed in a water bath or applied more directly using devices, such as horns, probes, or sono-reactors.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>ultrasound-assisted extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10176"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10231"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>UAE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10232 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10232">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process in which the sample (analyte and matrix) is dissolved by an acid.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Acid digestion can also be used to remove a matrix constituent by selective volatilization, e.g. silicon by the use of hydrofluoric acid.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Acid digestion can be performed in closed or open vessels.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>acid digestion</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10233 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10233">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Type of acid digestion performed in a closed vessel at elevated pressure.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The increased rate of decomposition and retention of volatile trace elements is an advantage over open-vessel acid digestion.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>closed-vessel acid digestion</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10232"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10236"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10234 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10234">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sample dissolution with simultaneous matrix simplification.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>digestion</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10235 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10235">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Type of bomb acid digestion with microwave radiation used for sample heating.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>microwave acid digestion</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10236 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10236">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Type of acid digestion performed at ambient pressure wherein the digestion temperature is limited by the boiling point of the acid.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>open-vessel acid digestion</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10232"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10233"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10237 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10237">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Formation of free metal ions in aqueous solution by oxidation under the influence of UV light and possibly an oxidizing agent (e.g. hydrogen peroxide or peroxodisulfate) in order to break down dissolved organic matter and metal complexes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Typical samples are sea water, waste water, drinking water, or fresh water.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>UV photochemical digestion</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10238 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10238">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Short disposable column containing a bed of porous particles immobilized between two porous frits.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The particles are sized to allow sample processing by gravity, gentle suction or low pressure.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>cartridge</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10239 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10239">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Small volume of solvent used to prepare the sorbent for efficient sample processing.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Principal roles include removal of contaminants from the sorbent bed to create the conditions for a stable flow of the sample solution during sampling and to facilitate wetting of hydrophobic sorbent surfaces before applying aqueous samples.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>conditioning solvent</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10240 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10240">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Small volume of strong solvent used to displace target analytes from the sorbent bed.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>elution solvent</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10241 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10241">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sorbent consisting of spherical, micron-sized, agglomerated particles bound together in a rigid and highly porous structure used for the extraction of semi-volatile organic compounds from air in high-volume samplers.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Foamed polyurethane sorbents have a minor use for extracting compounds from water.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Their main attribute is their favourable aerodynamic properties, which permit sampling at high flow rates.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>foamed polyurethane sorbent</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10242 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10242">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Porous material with surface bound monoclonal or polyclonal antibodies capable of selective extraction of target analytes by formation of reversible antibody-antigen (compound) binding complexes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>immunoaffinity sorbents</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A high level of selectivity is possible due to the specificity of antibody-antigen spatial fitting and interactions.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The porous support is chosen to minimize nonspecific matrix interactions to facilitate processing complex biological, environmental and food samples without additional clean up.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>immunosorbents</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10243 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10243">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Adsorbent with site-specific polar and ion exchange functional groups that extract analytes from a gas or liquid phase by their complementary interactions with the surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Compounds with hydrogen-bonding or ionic functional groups are extracted efficiently, while neutral dipolar compounds are extracted to a lesser extent and weakly polarizable compounds to a limited extent.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Typical inorganic oxide adsorbents include silica gel, alumina, magnesium silicate (Florisil), and zirconia.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>inorganic oxide adsorbent</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10244 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10244">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Porous sorbent material  containing fixed ionic sites with a complementary charge to that of the target analyte(s).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Ion-exchange sorbents are usually classified as weak or strong depending on the identity of the ionic group and whether its charge is independent of the sample \(\rm{pH}\) (strong ion-exchanger) or can be varied by changing the sample \(\rm{pH}\) (weak ion-exchanger).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Ionic or ionizable compounds are extracted almost exclusively from water by electrostatic interactions with ion-exchange sorbents.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>ion-exchange sorbent</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10245 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10245">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sorbent with a non-selective surface used almost exclusively to extract analytes from an aqueous solution. The sorbent has a largely passive role in the extraction, with the driving force being the expulsion of the organic contaminants from waterto minimize disruption of the water structure.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>From the gas phase compounds are extracted by non-polar and weakly polar interactions with high surface area sorbents.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Low-specificity sorbents have surfaces with a low concentration of polar functional groups and retain compounds mainly through non-selective dispersion interactions.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Typical low-specificity sorbents are chemically-bonded porous silica, hydrophobic macroporous polymers, and various forms of carbon.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>low-specificity sorbents</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10246 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10246">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sorbent consisting of nanometre-sized, spherical particles with a magnetic or magnetisable core surrounded by a sorbent shell.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Magnetic nanoparticles have a high surface area-to-volume ratio and a low mass. They are easily dispersed in solution. A magnet facilitates recovery of the nanoparticles from the sample solution or suspension.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>magnetic nanoparticle sorbent</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10247 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10247">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Porous sorbent material with surface co-bonded ion exchange and alkyl functional groups.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The dual extraction mechanism allows fractionation of mixtures into a neutral and acidic fraction and a basic fraction by a stepwise change in \(\pu{pH}\) of the eluting solvent.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>mixed-mode sorbent</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10248 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10248">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Porous polymer sorbent with synthetic recognition sites with a high specificity for the sorption of a targeted compound or group of structurally-related compounds.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>MIPS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The imprint is obtained by the co-polymerization of functional and crosslinking monomers in the presence of a template molecule (the analyte) and a porogen. The resultant imprint possesses a steric (size and shape) and chemical (spatial arrangement of complementary functional groups) memory of the template molecule that enable the sorbent to selectively rebind the template molecule from a complex matrix.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>molecularly imprinted polymer sorbent</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10248"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>MIPS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10249 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10249">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Regular array of wells in a single moulded body with a short sorbent bed or disc embedded at the base of each well.Multiwell extraction plate.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Multiwell plates typically contain 24, 96, 256, or 384 wells with volumes varying from about \(\pu{100 \upmu L}\) to \(\pu{10 mL}\). They are used for parallel sample processing in high throughput applications.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The design of a typical multiwall extraction plate is shown in the figure below.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>multiwell extraction plate</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10250 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10250">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Gas-tight syringe with a sorbent-filled needle.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Vertical movement of the plunger allows bidirectional flow of sample through the sorbent bed (active sampling). Alternatively, sampling can occur by diffusion of analytes into the syringe needle (passive sampling).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>needle trap</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10165"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10183"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10251 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10251">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Suspension of micron-sized sorbent particles woven into a glass fibre matrix.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Small discs are self-supporting while larger discs require external support.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>particle-embedded glass fibre disc</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10252 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10252">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Suspension of micron-sized sorbent particles distributed throughout a web of poly(tetrafluroethylene) microfibrils formed into a thin disc of various diameters.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>For general use the flexible discs are supported on a sintered glass disc in a filtration apparatus using suction to generate the desired flow through the membrane.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>particle-loaded membrane disc</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10253 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10253">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Porous material with a biocompatible outer surface and a retentive interior surface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Access to the retentive interior surface is restricted for macromolecules by a physical or chemical diffusion barrier that facilitates elution of macromolecules and extraction of low-mass compounds.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The restricted access barrier can be accomplished by size exclusion, differential partitioning, or other phenomenon.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>restricted-access sorbent</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10254 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10254">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Weak solvent used to displace matrix components from a sorbent without displacing the target analyte(s).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>An optional step, intermediate between sample application and elution.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>rinse solvent</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10255 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10255">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction of analytes from a gas, liquid, or fluid by transfer to a solid sorbent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SPE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Typical uses of solid-phase extraction include isolation, concentration, solvent exchange, and matrix simplification.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>solid-phase extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10255"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SPE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10256 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10256">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Use of a solvent, typically combined with agitation, to remove target analytes from a sorbent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>solvent desorption</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10257 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10257">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Disposable pipette tip with a small packed bed, disc, or monolithic plug of sorbent at its narrow end.Sorbent containing pipette tips in an array for use with a multiwall sample plate.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A micropipettor is used to control sample processing steps. This arrangement facilitates the use of bidirectional flow (the cycling of sample and solvents across the sorbent bed in either direction) for sample processing.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>A typical design of sorbent-containing pipette device for high throughput sample processing is shown in the figure below.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sorbent-containing pipette tip</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10258 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10258">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction from the surface of a solid by sorbent or polymer film in the form of a tape in direct contact with, or in the headspace above, the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The tape is typically a suitably-sized sheet of poly(dimethylsiloxane) polymer, or a piece of particle-loaded membrane, with a large surface area-to-volume ratio.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sorptive-tape extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10198"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10259 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10259">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction of compounds from solution by a sorbent coated magnetic stir bar by rapid stirring (direct immersion) or by exposure to the headspace above a sample (headspace extraction).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Rapid stirring accelerates mass transfer overcoming some of the limitations of diffusion controlled mass transfer in sessile solutions.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Related devices for simultaneous stirring and sampling include rotating disc, stir rod, and stir membrane devices.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>stir bar sorptive extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10198"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10267"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10260 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10260">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Porous sorbent with surface-bound macrocyclic ligands designed for the extraction of metal ions and some anions from aqueous solution.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Extracted ions are recovered by elution with a solution of complexing agent with a higher binding constant than the surface-bound macrocyclic ligand.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The selectivity of the extraction process depends on the stability of the host-guest complex, which can be optimized for individual ions by the choice of the macrocyclic cavity and the type of donor atoms.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>surface-bound macrocyclic sorbent</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10261 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10261">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Porous sorbent with surface-bound phenylboronic acid groups for the selective extraction of bifunctional compounds by reversible formation of 5- or 6-membered covalent cyclic boronic acid complexes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Extraction occurs at a basic \(\pu{pH}\) for compounds with hydrogen-donating functional groups on neighbouring carbon atoms (1,2- and 1,3-). The analytes are released by elution with an acidic aqueous-organic solvent solution.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>surface-bound phenylboronic acid sorbent</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10262 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10262">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction by a matrix-compatible immersion solid-phase microextraction device where the extraction phase and support are chosen to eliminate matrix interferences.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>biocompatible solid-phase microextraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10276"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10263 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10263">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Solid-phase microextraction device where the extraction phase (sorbent) is dispersed in the form of a thin coating on the surface of a blade.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>This technique is typically used as a 96-blade brush in conjunction with a multiwell sample plate.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>blade format</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10181"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10276"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10264 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10264">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sample vessel with sorbent-coated walls.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>coated-vessel format</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10265 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10265">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Solid-phase microextraction in the fibre format with a sorbent temperature maintained below that of the sample matrix during extraction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>cold fibre solid-phase microextraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10266"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10276"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10266 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10266">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction phase in the form of a thin layer supported on a fibre made of inert material, such as fused silica, stainless steel, or nickel-titanium alloy.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>fibre format</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10175"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10267 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10267">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Solid-phase microextraction where the extraction phase is placed in the headspace above the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>headspace solid-phase microextraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10175"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10198"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10276"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10268 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10268">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Solid-phase microextraction where the extraction phase is immersed directly into the sample.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>immersion solid-phase microextraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10175"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10276"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10269 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10269">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>In-tube format device with a sorbent-coated capillary placed inside a needle.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>INCAT</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>in-needle capillary adsorption trap</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10269"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>INCAT</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10270 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10270">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction utilizing a coated open tubular or packed capillary tube as the extraction phase in a flow system.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>in-tube solid-phase microextraction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10271 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10271">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Immersion solid-phase microextraction where the extraction phase is designed to minimize sorption of particles or macromolecules present in the sample matrix.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Sorption of particles and macromolecules by the extraction phase is a potential source of interference in the extraction process or subsequent determination of analytes when sampling occurs by direct contact of the extraction phase with the sample. In some cases this can be addressed by incorporation of specific properties in the extraction phase that minimize matrix-extraction phase interactions for problem matrix components.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>matrix-compatible solid-phase microextraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10175"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10268"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10272 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10272">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Immersion solid-phase microextraction in which the extraction phase is surrounded by a permeable membrane that allows access of analytes to the extraction phase while shielding the extraction phase from matrix components with a potential to interfere in the extraction or subsequent analysis.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>membrane-protected solid-phase microextraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10175"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10268"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10273 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10273">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction phase in the form of sorbent particles.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Particles are typically of a nanometre size and applied in the dispersion mode of extraction.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>particle sorbent</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10175"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10274 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10274">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sorbent-coated disc consisting of a polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) base with an integrated magnet and a sorbent film attached to one side.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>RDSE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Facilitates more rapid stirring than conventional sorbent-coated stir bars (see stir bar sorptive extraction) without deterioration of the sorbent coating, because it is not in contact with the vessel wall.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>rotating disk sorptive extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10259"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10274"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>RDSE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10275 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10275">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Solid-phase microextraction in which the extraction phase is coated on the internal surface of a needle.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SPDE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>solid-phase dynamic extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10276"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10275"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SPDE</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10276 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10276">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Microextraction where the extraction phase is a solid.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The extraction phase can also be a liquid physically coated on a fiber or wire support.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>This term is often narrowly used to describe an extraction process employing a syringe-based sampling device with a short length of small gauge solid fiber or wire coated with sorbent attached to one end of the plunger of the syringe to facilitate bidirectional movement of the sorbent-coated fiber (wire) through the syringe needle for extraction, storage, and desorption of extractants. For extraction the sorbent is exposed to the headspace above the sample or immersed directly into the sample solution. Sample components diffuse into and/or onto the sorbent coating until equilibrium is reached. The extractants are subsequently recovered by thermal desorption into a gas stream (typically in the liner of an injection port of a gas chromatograph) or rinsed from the sorbent coating with solvent for determination by other methods.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>solid-phase microextraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10175"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11552"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10277 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10277">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Solid-phase microextraction where the sorbent is in the form of a thin film on a support or self supported membrane.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>thin film format</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10276"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10278 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10278">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Hollow-fibre liquid-phase microextraction in which the extraction is driven by an electrical potential sustained between the sample solution and the acceptor phase, separated by a supported liquid membrane.Typical experimental arrangement for electromembrane extraction of cations (anions extracted by reversing the polarity). SLM, supported liquid membrane in the form of a porous hollow fiber with solvent-filled pores.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>EME</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A typical experimental arrangement is shown in the figure below.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Electromembrane extraction is typically performed in three-phase mode (\(\ce{aqueous\!-&gt;\!organic\!-&gt;\!aqueous}\)).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The sample is agitated or stirred during extraction.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>electromembrane extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10164"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10280"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10293"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10278"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>EME</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10279 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10279">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Narrow cylindrical tube prepared from a porous polymer.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The internal diameter of the tube is typically \(\pu{500 \upmu m}\) – \(\pu{1.5 mm}\) with walls \(\pu{100 \upmu m}\) to \(\pu{300 \upmu m}\) thick. Fibers are typically prepared from porous poly(propylene), although other materials can be used.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>hollow fibre</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10280 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10280">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Liquid-phase microextraction of analytes from a liquid (or gaseous) sample by a supported liquid membrane in the form of a porous hollow fibre with solvent-filled pores. The analytes are transferred to an acceptor phase contained in the cavity of the hollow fibre.Typical experimental arrangement for hollow-fiber supported liquid membrane (SLM) for liquid-phase microextraction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>HF-LPME</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>A typical experimental arrangement is shown in the figure below.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The acceptor phase can be aqueous, which provides a three-phase extraction system (\(\ce{aqueous\!-&gt;\!organic\!-&gt;\!aqueous}\)), or an organic solvent, providing a two-phase extraction system (\(\ce{aqueous\!-&gt;\!organic}\)).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The sample is agitated or stirred during extraction.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The supported liquid membrane is generally an organic solvent immiscible with water and the sample an aqueous solution.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>hollow-fibre liquid-phase microextraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10164"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10216"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10279"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10293"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10280"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>HF-LPME</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10281 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10281">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction of analytes from a sample by permeation through a membrane.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>membrane extraction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10282 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10282">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Membrane extraction with a sorbent trap for concentration of the permeate.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>MESI</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>MESI is typically used in a system consisting of three components: (1) membrane extraction module; (2) sorbent interface; and (3) gas chromatograph. A flow of gas is used as the stripping phase to transfer permeate to the sorbent trap for concentration and thermal desorption into the gas chromatograph.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>membrane extraction with sorbent interface</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10201"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10281"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10282"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>MESI</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10283 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10283">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Dialysis by a short length of hollow-fibre membrane used for sampling.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Microdialysis is performed mostly in a living biological system.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>microdialysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10279"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10284 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10284">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Membrane in which permeation occurs by processes other than flow through pores.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>non-porous membrane</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10287"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10288"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10285 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10285">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction of analytes from aqueous samples in a multiwell plate with a porous membrane at the base of each well by migration through the solvent-filled membrane pores into an acceptor phase located in each well.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PALME</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>PALME is usually performed in the three-phase mode (\(\ce{aqueous\!-&gt;\!organic\!-&gt;\!aqueous}\)).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The solvent-filled filter pores function as a supported liquid membrane typically based on an organic solvent immiscible with water.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>parallel artificial liquid membrane extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10164"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10181"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10293"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10285"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PALME</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10286 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10286">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Stream of extraction phase entering a dialysis module.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Perfusate is also used to describe the stripping phase in dialysis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>perfusate</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10175"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10287 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10287">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process by which mass transfer occurs through a membrane.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>permeation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10288 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10288">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Membrane in which permeation occurs through its porous structure.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>porous membrane</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10284"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10287"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10289 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10289">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Sample left over in membrane separations after initial contact with the membrane.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>retentate stream</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10290 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10290">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Membrane extraction in which the membrane fully encloses an acceptor phase.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SPMD</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Typically, a membrane filled with acceptor phase and formed into a sealed tube is immersed in the sample. As a passive sampling device, it is used to obtain time-weighted average concentrations for trace contaminants or to isolate them for toxicity assessment. With triolein as an acceptor phase, it is used to estimate the bioconcentration of contaminants in fatty tissues of organisms.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>semipermeable membrane device</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10164"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10281"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10290"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SPMD</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10291 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10291">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>The sample in membrane separations prior to contact with the membrane.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>stream</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10292 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10292">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction phase in a membrane separation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>stripping phase</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10175"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10293 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10293">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Porous membrane with solvent-filled pores.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SLM</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>artificial liquid membrane</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Porous polymeric hollow fibres and filter disks are typically used as support formats for microextraction techniques.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Water immiscible organic solvents are typically employed in the preparation of supported liquid membranes.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>supported liquid membrane</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10279"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10288"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11552"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10293"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SLM</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10294 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10294">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Flow system with extraction device employing a local detector.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The extraction phase is typically collected in an automated fraction collector and introduced into a detector via a robotic device.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>at-line extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10175"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10295 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10295">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction in a mechanical device regulated by feed-back of information without human intervention.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>automated extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10307"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10296 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10296">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction according to manually preselected conditions without further human intervention.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>automatic extraction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10297 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10297">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Fully renewable (disposable) packed sorbent bed for solid-phase extraction in a flow manifold or mesofluidic platform using programmable flow extraction.Bead-injection protocols for automated solid phase microextraction with sorbent renewal for each assay [Reproduced from  https://www.flowinjectiontutorial.com].a) Injection of a metered amount of particles or beads with reactive surfaces in the flow system followed by mechanical trapping or capture with the aid of external energy sources; b) Injection of a metered volume of sample; c) Interaction of analyte(s) with sorptive surfaces; d) Preconcentration of analyte(s) followed by detection of native species or derivatization products; e) Automatic removal of sorptive surfaces.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A typical workflow for bead injection in a flow manifold is shown in the figure below.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>bead injection</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10255"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10276"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10313"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10298 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10298">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Fraction of analytes desorbed from a sample potentially available to organisms.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>environmental availabilty</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Also termed environmental availability.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Bioaccessibility tests are usually performed by operationally defined leaching.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>bioaccessibility</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10179"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10299 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10299">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Leaching of analytes from a solid or semi-solid sample in a flow-through device to evaluate their bioaccessibility.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>dynamic bioaccessibility</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10298"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10300 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10300">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction by flow of sample through or over the extraction phase, or vice versa, to enhance the extraction rate.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The forced flow of matrix or extraction phase is controlled by an external device.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>flow-through extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10175"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10176"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10301 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10301">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Continuous optical interrogation of a packed-sorbent bed in a flow system at any stage of the extraction process prior to analyte elution and sorbent regeneration.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>flow-through solid-phase spectrometry</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10163"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10302 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10302">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coupling of a flow system with extraction device to a detector without flow interruption.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>in-line extraction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10303 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10303">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Batch-type extraction in which the barrel of a syringe pump is used as the device for liquid-phase microextraction or dispersive solid-phase microextraction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>in-syringe microextraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10216"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10276"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10304 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10304">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Automatic batch-type liquid-phase microextraction by sequential injection analysis using an external extraction container (e.g. pipette tip) connected to a multiposition rotary valve.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>lab-at-valve liquid-phase microextraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10316"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11552"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10305 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10305">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Microfluidic platform containing microconduits to facilitate extraction processes with pressure-driven or electroosmotic flow.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>lab-on-a-chip extraction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10306 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10306">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Mesofluidic platform containing microconduits for extraction processes mounted on a multi-position rotary valve.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>LOV</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>lab-on-valve extraction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10306"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>LOV</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10307 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10307">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction using a device with at least one movable part that replaces or supplements human effort in any (or all) steps of the process.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>mechanized extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10295"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10308 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10308">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction device with channel widths from \(\pu{10 \upmu m}\) to \(\pu{2 mm}\) integrated into a flow platform.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Lab-on-valve platforms belong to the group of mesofluidic devices.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>mesofluidic extraction device</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10306"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10309 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10309">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Miniaturized flow injection analysis device for extraction with conduits of a diameter greater than \(\pu{0.1 mm}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>μFIA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>μFIA extraction device</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>microflow injection analysis extraction device</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10309"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>μFIA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10310 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10310">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction device with channel widths from \(\pu{100 nm}\) to \(\pu{10 \upmu m}\) integrated into a flow platform.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>microfluidic extraction device</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10311 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10311">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Programmable flow extraction device with microsolenoid valves for processing microliter sample volumes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>multicommutation flow extraction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10313"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10312 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10312">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Coupling of a flow system with an extraction device to a detector via a discontinuous interface.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A suitable discontinuous interface is a rotary valve with a sample loop.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>on-line extraction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10313 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10313">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Multi-bidirectional flow (including stopped flow) of sample and/or extractant through an extraction device controlled by a pump.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>programmable flow extraction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10314 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10314">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Continuous-flow system attaining steady-state extraction/reaction conditions facilitated by injection of air bubbles. This might be extended to immiscible liquids injected as droplets in the flow system. Related term: segmented flow extraction.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>segmented flow analysis</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10315"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10315 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10315">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Extraction by insertion of the sample solution as equal-size droplets (segments) in a flowing stream of immiscible extraction phase.Typical segmented flow extraction manifold. C, carrier or liquid phase for sample; R, reagent (optional); P, propulsion unit; S, sample; IV, injection valve; MC, mixing coil; DB, displacement bottle (functions as a constant volume pump); ORG, organic solvent; SG, segmenter; EC, extraction coil; PS, phase separator; D, detector (optional); RC, restriction coil; and W, waste.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A typical apparatus for segmented flow extraction is shown in figure below.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The characteristic components of the system are the segmenter (creates the droplets in the solvent stream); the extraction coil (provides sufficient time for transfer of target analytes to the extraction solvent); and the phase separator (reconstitutes the sample solution and extraction solvent into two separate streams).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>segmented flow extraction</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10316 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10316">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Automatic programmable flow extraction through a multi-position rotary valve housing a mesofluidic extraction device.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-0903"/>
        <rdfs:label>sequential injection analysis extraction device</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10308"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10317 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10317">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Ratio of the adult toxic dose to the developmentally toxic dose.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-1202"/>
        <rdfs:label>A/D ratio</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10347"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10318 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10318">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Body cavity between the diaphragm and pelvis that contains the abdominal organs.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-1202"/>
        <rdfs:label>abdominal cavity</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10604"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10319 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10319">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Congenital absence of the eyelids.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-1202"/>
        <rdfs:label>ablepharia</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10552"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10320 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10320">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Premature termination of pregnancy with the death of the embryo or fetus.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-1202"/>
        <rdfs:label>abortion</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10718"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11116"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10321 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10321">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Intentional termination of a pregnancy with death of the embryo or fetus.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-1202"/>
        <rdfs:label>induced abortion</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10718"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11116"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10322 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10322">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Non-intentional termination of pregnancy before the embryo or fetus has developed to the stage of independent viability, or in humans before the 20th week of gestation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-1202"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>miscarriage</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>spontaneous abortion</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10718"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10764"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11116"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11370"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10323 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10323">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Rib arising from a cervical vertebra (cervical rib), or supernumerary rib arising from a thoracic or lumbar vertebra.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-1202"/>
        <rdfs:label>accessory rib</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10506"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11269"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11366"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10324 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10324">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Any gland, other than a gonad, associated with the genital tract, such as the bulbourethral gland and prostate.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-1202"/>
        <rdfs:label>accessory sex gland</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10479"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10775"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11145"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10325 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10325">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Organ or structure other than the gonads that matures at puberty and assists indirectly in sexual reproduction by nurturing and transporting gametes.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-1202"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>secondary sex organ</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>In the human female the accessory sex organs include the Fallopian tubes, uterus, vagina, and the external genitalia.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>In the human male, the accessory sex organs include the epididymis, vas deferens, ejaculatory duct, urethra, seminal vesicles, bulbourethral glands, prostate, and penis.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>accessory sex organ</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10479"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10668"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10706"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10745"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10775"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11145"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11152"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11203"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11217"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11343"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11350"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11352"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11358"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10326 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10326">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Hydrolytic enzyme (EC 3.2.1.52) that acts on ganglioside \(\rm{G}_{\rm{M2}}\), producing N-acetyl-D-galactosamine and ganglioside \(\rm{G}_{\rm{M3}}\).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-1202"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>hexosaminidase A</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Deficiency of this enzyme is associated with Tay–Sachs disease.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>β-&lt;em&gt;N&lt;/em&gt;-acetylhexosaminidase</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11281"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10327 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10327">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Inherited disorder where ossification of cartilage is retarded, especially affecting growth of long bones, resulting in very short limbs and a comparatively large head. Type of dwarfism.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-1202"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Achondroplasia results from a mutation in the fibroblast growth factor receptor FGFR3 gene, increasing its activity in suppressing endochondral ossification.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>achondroplasia</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10656"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11051"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10328 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10328">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Small sac-like cavity in a gland, surrounded by secretory cells. Terminal region of the airways of the lung where gas exchange occurs.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-1202"/>
        <rdfs:label>acinus</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10767"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10329 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10329">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Either of two passages in the ear, one leading to the tympanic membrane (external acoustic meatus), and one for passage of nerves and blood vessels (internal acoustic meatus).</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-1202"/>
        <rdfs:label>acoustic meatus</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11335"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10330 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10330">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Rare congenital disorder that occurs in the human fetus, in which the flat bones in the cranium are either completely or partially absent.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-1202"/>
        <rdfs:label>acrania</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10552"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10569"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10718"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10331 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10331">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Type of cephalic disorder where the top of the skull is pointed or conical due to premature closure of the coronal suture plus any other suture.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-1202"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>acrocephalia</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>oxycephaly</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Acrocephaly should be differentiated from Crouzon syndrome, which involves the maxilla and mandible.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>acrocephaly</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10575"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10332 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10332">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Abnormal enlargement of the hands, feet, and face in adults, caused by overproduction of growth hormone by the pituitary gland.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-1202"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>acromegalia</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>Overproduction of growth hormone by the pituitary gland in children causes gigantism.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>acromegaly</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10766"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10783"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11094"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10333 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10333">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Organelle that develops over the anterior half of the head in the spermatozoon.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-1202"/>
        <rdfs:comment>The acrosome is a cap-like structure derived from the Golgi apparatus.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>acrosome</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11243"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10334 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10334">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Process that occurs in the acrosome of the sperm as it contacts the egg, leading to structural changes that facilitate fusion.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-1202"/>
        <rdfs:label>acrosome reaction</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10333"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10640"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.11243"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10335 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10335">
        <goldbook1:hasStatus>current</goldbook1:hasStatus>
        <dcterms:description>Growth factor of the transforming growth factor \(\upbeta\) (TGF-\(\upbeta\)) superfamily, originally identified as a gonadal factor that stimulated secretion of follicle-stimulating hormone, also involved in many aspects of development including mesodermal induction (see mesenchyme), hematopoiesis, and neuronal differentiation.</dcterms:description>
        <mito:isMentionedBy rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/pac-2015-1202"/>
        <rdfs:label>activin</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10730"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10782"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dx.doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.10789"/>
